PREFACE\par
\par
\par
IMITATION OF HORACE\par
Ep. 20.—B. 1.\par
\par
Methinks, Oh! vain ill-judging Book,\par
I see thee cast a wishful look,\par
Where reputations won and lost are\par
In famous row called Paternoster.\par
Incensed to find your precious olio\par
Buried in unexplored port-folio,\par
You scorn the prudent lock and key,\par
And pant well bound and gilt to see\par
Your Volume in the window set\par
Of Stockdale, Hookham, or Debrett.\par
\par
Go then, and pass that dangerous bourn\par
Whence never Book can back return:\par
And when you find, condemned, despised,\par
Neglected, blamed, and criticised,\par
Abuse from All who read you fall,\par
(If haply you be read at all\par
Sorely will you your folly sigh at,\par
And wish for me, and home, and quiet.\par
\par
Assuming now a conjuror’s office, I\par
Thus on your future Fortune prophesy:—\par
Soon as your novelty is o’er,\par
And you are young and new no more,\par
In some dark dirty corner thrown,\par
Mouldy with damps, with cobwebs strown,\par
Your leaves shall be the Book-worm’s prey;\par
Or sent to Chandler-Shop away,\par
And doomed to suffer public scandal,\par
Shall line the trunk, or wrap the candle!\par
\par
But should you meet with approbation,\par
And some one find an inclination\par
To ask, by natural transition\par
Respecting me and my condition;\par
That I am one, the enquirer teach,\par
Nor very poor, nor very rich;\par
Of passions strong, of hasty nature,\par
Of graceless form and dwarfish stature;\par
By few approved, and few approving;\par
Extreme in hating and in loving;\par
\par
Abhorring all whom I dislike,\par
Adoring who my fancy strike;\par
In forming judgements never long,\par
And for the most part judging wrong;\par
In friendship firm, but still believing\par
Others are treacherous and deceiving,\par
And thinking in the present aera\par
That Friendship is a pure chimaera:\par
More passionate no creature living,\par
Proud, obstinate, and unforgiving,\par
But yet for those who kindness show,\par
Ready through fire and smoke to go.\par
\par
Again, should it be asked your page,\par
“Pray, what may be the author’s age?”\par
Your faults, no doubt, will make it clear,\par
I scarce have seen my twentieth year,\par
Which passed, kind Reader, on my word,\par
While England’s Throne held George the Third.\par
\par
Now then your venturous course pursue:\par
Go, my delight! Dear Book, adieu!\par
\par
M. G. L.\par
\par
Hague,\par
Oct. 28, 1794.\par
