\par
\par
CHAPTER I.\par
\par
\par
——Lord Angelo is precise;\par
Stands at a guard with envy; Scarce confesses\par
That his blood flows, or that his appetite\par
Is more to bread than stone.\par
\par
MEASURE FOR MEASURE.\par
\par
Scarcely had the Abbey Bell tolled for five minutes, and already was\par
the Church of the Capuchins thronged with Auditors. Do not encourage\par
the idea that the Crowd was assembled either from motives of piety or\par
thirst of information. But very few were influenced by those reasons;\par
and in a city where superstition reigns with such despotic sway as in\par
Madrid, to seek for true devotion would be a fruitless attempt. The\par
Audience now assembled in the Capuchin Church was collected by various\par
causes, but all of them were foreign to the ostensible motive. The\par
Women came to show themselves, the Men to see the Women: Some were\par
attracted by curiosity to hear an Orator so celebrated; Some came\par
because they had no better means of employing their time till the play\par
began; Some, from being assured that it would be impossible to find\par
places in the Church; and one half of Madrid was brought thither by\par
expecting to meet the other half. The only persons truly anxious to\par
hear the Preacher were a few antiquated devotees, and half a dozen\par
rival Orators, determined to find fault with and ridicule the\par
discourse. As to the remainder of the Audience, the Sermon might have\par
been omitted altogether, certainly without their being disappointed,\par
and very probably without their perceiving the omission.\par
\par
Whatever was the occasion, it is at least certain that the Capuchin\par
Church had never witnessed a more numerous assembly. Every corner was\par
filled, every seat was occupied. The very Statues which ornamented the\par
long aisles were pressed into the service. Boys suspended themselves\par
upon the wings of Cherubims; St. Francis and St. Mark bore each a\par
spectator on his shoulders; and St. Agatha found herself under the\par
necessity of carrying double. The consequence was, that in spite of all\par
their hurry and expedition, our two newcomers, on entering the Church,\par
looked round in vain for places.\par
\par
However, the old Woman continued to move forwards. In vain were\par
exclamations of displeasure vented against her from all sides: In vain\par
was She addressed with—“I assure you, Segnora, there are no places\par
here.”—“I beg, Segnora, that you will not crowd me so\par
intolerably!”—“Segnora, you cannot pass this way. Bless me! How can\par
people be so troublesome!”—The old Woman was obstinate, and on She\par
went. By dint of perseverance and two brawny arms She made a passage\par
through the Crowd, and managed to bustle herself into the very body of\par
the Church, at no great distance from the Pulpit. Her companion had\par
followed her with timidity and in silence, profiting by the exertions\par
of her conductress.\par
\par
“Holy Virgin!” exclaimed the old Woman in a tone of disappointment,\par
while She threw a glance of enquiry round her; “Holy Virgin! What heat!\par
What a Crowd! I wonder what can be the meaning of all this. I believe\par
we must return: There is no such thing as a seat to be had, and nobody\par
seems kind enough to accommodate us with theirs.”\par
\par
This broad hint attracted the notice of two Cavaliers, who occupied\par
stools on the right hand, and were leaning their backs against the\par
seventh column from the Pulpit. Both were young, and richly habited.\par
Hearing this appeal to their politeness pronounced in a female voice,\par
they interrupted their conversation to look at the speaker. She had\par
thrown up her veil in order to take a clearer look round the Cathedral.\par
Her hair was red, and She squinted. The Cavaliers turned round, and\par
renewed their conversation.\par
\par
“By all means,” replied the old Woman’s companion; “By all means,\par
Leonella, let us return home immediately; The heat is excessive, and I\par
am terrified at such a crowd.”\par
\par
These words were pronounced in a tone of unexampled sweetness. The\par
Cavaliers again broke off their discourse, but for this time they were\par
not contented with looking up: Both started involuntarily from their\par
seats, and turned themselves towards the Speaker.\par
\par
The voice came from a female, the delicacy and elegance of whose figure\par
inspired the Youths with the most lively curiosity to view the face to\par
which it belonged. This satisfaction was denied them. Her features were\par
hidden by a thick veil; But struggling through the crowd had deranged\par
it sufficiently to discover a neck which for symmetry and beauty might\par
have vied with the Medicean Venus. It was of the most dazzling\par
whiteness, and received additional charms from being shaded by the\par
tresses of her long fair hair, which descended in ringlets to her\par
waist. Her figure was rather below than above the middle size: It was\par
light and airy as that of an Hamadryad. Her bosom was carefully veiled.\par
Her dress was white; it was fastened by a blue sash, and just permitted\par
to peep out from under it a little foot of the most delicate\par
proportions. A chaplet of large grains hung upon her arm, and her face\par
was covered with a veil of thick black gauze. Such was the female, to\par
whom the youngest of the Cavaliers now offered his seat, while the\par
other thought it necessary to pay the same attention to her companion.\par
\par
The old Lady with many expressions of gratitude, but without much\par
difficulty, accepted the offer, and seated herself: The young one\par
followed her example, but made no other compliment than a simple and\par
graceful reverence. Don Lorenzo (such was the Cavalier’s name, whose\par
seat She had accepted) placed himself near her; But first He whispered\par
a few words in his Friend’s ear, who immediately took the hint, and\par
endeavoured to draw off the old Woman’s attention from her lovely\par
charge.\par
\par
“You are doubtless lately arrived at Madrid,” said Lorenzo to his fair\par
Neighbour; “It is impossible that such charms should have long remained\par
unobserved; and had not this been your first public appearance, the\par
envy of the Women and adoration of the Men would have rendered you\par
already sufficiently remarkable.”\par
\par
He paused, in expectation of an answer. As his speech did not\par
absolutely require one, the Lady did not open her lips: After a few\par
moments He resumed his discourse:\par
\par
“Am I wrong in supposing you to be a Stranger to Madrid?”\par
\par
The Lady hesitated; and at last, in so low a voice as to be scarcely\par
intelligible, She made shift to answer,—“No, Segnor.”\par
\par
“Do you intend making a stay of any length?”\par
\par
“Yes, Segnor.”\par
\par
“I should esteem myself fortunate, were it in my power to contribute to\par
making your abode agreeable. I am well known at Madrid, and my Family\par
has some interest at Court. If I can be of any service, you cannot\par
honour or oblige me more than by permitting me to be of use to\par
you.”—“Surely,” said He to himself, “She cannot answer that by a\par
monosyllable; now She must say something to me.”\par
\par
Lorenzo was deceived, for the Lady answered only by a bow.\par
\par
By this time He had discovered that his Neighbour was not very\par
conversible; But whether her silence proceeded from pride, discretion,\par
timidity, or idiotism, He was still unable to decide.\par
\par
After a pause of some minutes—“It is certainly from your being a\par
Stranger,” said He, “and as yet unacquainted with our customs, that you\par
continue to wear your veil. Permit me to remove it.”\par
\par
At the same time He advanced his hand towards the Gauze: The Lady\par
raised hers to prevent him.\par
\par
“I never unveil in public, Segnor.”\par
\par
“And where is the harm, I pray you?” interrupted her Companion somewhat\par
sharply; “Do not you see that the other Ladies have all laid their\par
veils aside, to do honour no doubt to the holy place in which we are? I\par
have taken off mine already; and surely if I expose my features to\par
general observation, you have no cause to put yourself in such a\par
wonderful alarm! Blessed Maria! Here is a fuss and a bustle about a\par
chit’s face! Come, come, Child! Uncover it; I warrant you that nobody\par
will run away with it from you—”\par
\par
“Dear aunt, it is not the custom in Murcia.”\par
\par
“Murcia, indeed! Holy St. Barbara, what does that signify? You are\par
always putting me in mind of that villainous Province. If it is the\par
custom in Madrid, that is all that we ought to mind, and therefore I\par
desire you to take off your veil immediately. Obey me this moment\par
Antonia, for you know that I cannot bear contradiction—”\par
\par
Her niece was silent, but made no further opposition to Don Lorenzo’s\par
efforts, who, armed with the Aunt’s sanction hastened to remove the\par
Gauze. What a Seraph’s head presented itself to his admiration! Yet it\par
was rather bewitching than beautiful; It was not so lovely from\par
regularity of features as from sweetness and sensibility of\par
Countenance. The several parts of her face considered separately, many\par
of them were far from handsome; but when examined together, the whole\par
was adorable. Her skin though fair was not entirely without freckles;\par
Her eyes were not very large, nor their lashes particularly long. But\par
then her lips were of the most rosy freshness; Her fair and undulating\par
hair, confined by a simple ribband, poured itself below her waist in a\par
profusion of ringlets; Her throat was full and beautiful in the\par
extreme; Her hand and arm were formed with the most perfect symmetry;\par
Her mild blue eyes seemed an heaven of sweetness, and the crystal in\par
which they moved sparkled with all the brilliance of Diamonds: She\par
appeared to be scarcely fifteen; An arch smile, playing round her\par
mouth, declared her to be possessed of liveliness, which excess of\par
timidity at present represt; She looked round her with a bashful\par
glance; and whenever her eyes accidentally met Lorenzo’s, She dropt\par
them hastily upon her Rosary; Her cheek was immediately suffused with\par
blushes, and She began to tell her beads; though her manner evidently\par
showed that She knew not what She was about.\par
\par
Lorenzo gazed upon her with mingled surprise and admiration; but the\par
Aunt thought it necessary to apologize for Antonia’s mauvaise honte.\par
\par
“’Tis a young Creature,” said She, “who is totally ignorant of the\par
world. She has been brought up in an old Castle in Murcia; with no\par
other Society than her Mother’s, who, God help her! has no more sense,\par
good Soul, than is necessary to carry her Soup to her mouth. Yet She is\par
my own Sister, both by Father and Mother.”\par
\par
“And has so little sense?” said Don Christoval with feigned\par
astonishment; “How very Extraordinary!”\par
\par
“Very true, Segnor; Is it not strange? However, such is the fact; and\par
yet only to see the luck of some people! A young Nobleman, of the very\par
first quality, took it into his head that Elvira had some pretensions\par
to Beauty—As to pretensions, in truth, She had always enough of THEM;\par
But as to Beauty....! If I had only taken half the pains to set myself\par
off which She did....! But this is neither here nor there. As I was\par
saying, Segnor, a young Nobleman fell in love with her, and married her\par
unknown to his Father. Their union remained a secret near three years,\par
But at last it came to the ears of the old Marquis, who, as you may\par
well suppose, was not much pleased with the intelligence. Away He\par
posted in all haste to Cordova, determined to seize Elvira, and send\par
her away to some place or other, where She would never be heard of\par
more. Holy St. Paul! How He stormed on finding that She had escaped\par
him, had joined her Husband, and that they had embarked together for\par
the Indies. He swore at us all, as if the Evil Spirit had possessed\par
him; He threw my Father into prison, as honest a painstaking Shoe-maker\par
as any in Cordova; and when He went away, He had the cruelty to take\par
from us my Sister’s little Boy, then scarcely two years old, and whom\par
in the abruptness of her flight, She had been obliged to leave behind\par
her. I suppose, that the poor little Wretch met with bitter bad\par
treatment from him, for in a few months after, we received intelligence\par
of his death.”\par
\par
“Why, this was a most terrible old Fellow, Segnora!”\par
\par
“Oh! shocking! and a Man so totally devoid of taste! Why, would you\par
believe it, Segnor? When I attempted to pacify him, He cursed me for a\par
Witch, and wished that to punish the Count, my Sister might become as\par
ugly as myself! Ugly indeed! I like him for that.”\par
\par
“Ridiculous”, cried Don Christoval; “Doubtless the Count would have\par
thought himself fortunate, had he been permitted to exchange the one\par
Sister for the other.”\par
\par
“Oh! Christ! Segnor, you are really too polite. However, I am heartily\par
glad that the Condé was of a different way of thinking. A mighty pretty\par
piece of business, to be sure, Elvira has made of it! After broiling\par
and stewing in the Indies for thirteen long years, her Husband dies,\par
and She returns to Spain, without an House to hide her head, or money\par
to procure her one! This Antonia was then but an Infant, and her only\par
remaining Child. She found that her Father-in-Law had married again,\par
that he was irreconcileable to the Condé, and that his second Wife had\par
produced him a Son, who is reported to be a very fine young Man. The\par
old Marquis refused to see my Sister or her Child; But sent her word\par
that on condition of never hearing any more of her, He would assign her\par
a small pension, and She might live in an old Castle which He possessed\par
in Murcia; This had been the favourite habitation of his eldest Son;\par
But since his flight from Spain, the old Marquis could not bear the\par
place, but let it fall to ruin and confusion—My Sister accepted the\par
proposal; She retired to Murcia, and has remained there till within the\par
last Month.”\par
\par
“And what brings her now to Madrid?” enquired Don Lorenzo, whom\par
admiration of the young Antonia compelled to take a lively interest in\par
the talkative old Woman’s narration.\par
\par
“Alas! Segnor, her Father-in-Law being lately dead, the Steward of his\par
Murcian Estates has refused to pay her pension any longer.\par
\par
With the design of supplicating his Son to renew it, She is now come to\par
Madrid; But I doubt, that She might have saved herself the trouble! You\par
young Noblemen have always enough to do with your money, and are not\par
very often disposed to throw it away upon old Women. I advised my\par
Sister to send Antonia with her petition; But She would not hear of\par
such a thing. She is so obstinate! Well! She will find herself the\par
worse for not following my counsels: the Girl has a good pretty face,\par
and possibly might have done much.”\par
\par
“Ah! Segnora,” interrupted Don Christoval, counterfeiting a passionate\par
air; “If a pretty face will do the business, why has not your Sister\par
recourse to you?”\par
\par
“Oh! Jesus! my Lord, I swear you quite overpower me with your\par
gallantry! But I promise you that I am too well aware of the danger of\par
such Expeditions to trust myself in a young Nobleman’s power! No, no; I\par
have as yet preserved my reputation without blemish or reproach, and I\par
always knew how to keep the Men at a proper distance.”\par
\par
“Of that, Segnora, I have not the least doubt. But permit me to ask\par
you; Have you then any aversion to Matrimony?”\par
\par
“That is an home question. I cannot but confess, that if an amiable\par
Cavalier was to present himself....”\par
\par
Here She intended to throw a tender and significant look upon Don\par
Christoval; But, as She unluckily happened to squint most abominably,\par
the glance fell directly upon his Companion: Lorenzo took the\par
compliment to himself, and answered it by a profound bow.\par
\par
“May I enquire,” said He, “the name of the Marquis?”\par
\par
“The Marquis de las Cisternas.”\par
\par
“I know him intimately well. He is not at present in Madrid, but is\par
expected here daily. He is one of the best of Men; and if the lovely\par
Antonia will permit me to be her Advocate with him, I doubt not my\par
being able to make a favourable report of her cause.”\par
\par
Antonia raised her blue eyes, and silently thanked him for the offer by\par
a smile of inexpressible sweetness. Leonella’s satisfaction was much\par
more loud and audible: Indeed, as her Niece was generally silent in her\par
company, She thought it incumbent upon her to talk enough for both:\par
This She managed without difficulty, for She very seldom found herself\par
deficient in words.\par
\par
“Oh! Segnor!” She cried; “You will lay our whole family under the most\par
signal obligations! I accept your offer with all possible gratitude,\par
and return you a thousand thanks for the generosity of your proposal.\par
Antonia, why do not you speak, Child? While the Cavalier says all sorts\par
of civil things to you, you sit like a Statue, and never utter a\par
syllable of thanks, either bad, good, or indifferent!”\par
\par
“My dear Aunt, I am very sensible that....”\par
\par
“Fye, Niece! How often have I told you, that you never should interrupt\par
a Person who is speaking!? When did you ever know me do such a thing?\par
Are these your Murcian manners? Mercy on me! I shall never be able to\par
make this Girl any thing like a Person of good breeding. But pray,\par
Segnor,” She continued, addressing herself to Don Christoval, “inform\par
me, why such a Crowd is assembled today in this Cathedral?”\par
\par
“Can you possibly be ignorant, that Ambrosio, Abbot of this Monastery,\par
pronounces a Sermon in this Church every Thursday? All Madrid rings\par
with his praises. As yet He has preached but thrice; But all who have\par
heard him are so delighted with his eloquence, that it is as difficult\par
to obtain a place at Church, as at the first representation of a new\par
Comedy. His fame certainly must have reached your ears—”\par
\par
“Alas! Segnor, till yesterday I never had the good fortune to see\par
Madrid; and at Cordova we are so little informed of what is passing in\par
the rest of the world, that the name of Ambrosio has never been\par
mentioned in its precincts.”\par
\par
“You will find it in every one’s mouth at Madrid. He seems to have\par
fascinated the Inhabitants; and not having attended his Sermons myself,\par
I am astonished at the Enthusiasm which He has excited. The adoration\par
paid him both by Young and Old, by Man and Woman is unexampled. The\par
Grandees load him with presents; Their Wives refuse to have any other\par
Confessor, and he is known through all the city by the name of the ‘Man\par
of Holiness’.”\par
\par
“Undoubtedly, Segnor, He is of noble origin—”\par
\par
“That point still remains undecided. The late Superior of the Capuchins\par
found him while yet an Infant at the Abbey door. All attempts to\par
discover who had left him there were vain, and the Child himself could\par
give no account of his Parents. He was educated in the Monastery, where\par
He has remained ever since. He early showed a strong inclination for\par
study and retirement, and as soon as He was of a proper age, He\par
pronounced his vows. No one has ever appeared to claim him, or clear up\par
the mystery which conceals his birth; and the Monks, who find their\par
account in the favour which is shewn to their establishment from\par
respect to him, have not hesitated to publish that He is a present to\par
them from the Virgin. In truth the singular austerity of his life gives\par
some countenance to the report. He is now thirty years old, every hour\par
of which period has been passed in study, total seclusion from the\par
world, and mortification of the flesh. Till these last three weeks,\par
when He was chosen superior of the Society to which He belongs, He had\par
never been on the outside of the Abbey walls: Even now He never quits\par
them except on Thursdays, when He delivers a discourse in this\par
Cathedral which all Madrid assembles to hear. His knowledge is said to\par
be the most profound, his eloquence the most persuasive. In the whole\par
course of his life He has never been known to transgress a single rule\par
of his order; The smallest stain is not to be discovered upon his\par
character; and He is reported to be so strict an observer of Chastity,\par
that He knows not in what consists the difference of Man and Woman. The\par
common People therefore esteem him to be a Saint.”\par
\par
“Does that make a Saint?” enquired Antonia; “Bless me! Then am I one?”\par
\par
“Holy St. Barbara!” exclaimed Leonella; “What a question! Fye, Child,\par
Fye! These are not fit subjects for young Women to handle. You should\par
not seem to remember that there is such a thing as a Man in the world,\par
and you ought to imagine every body to be of the same sex with\par
yourself. I should like to see you give people to understand, that you\par
know that a Man has no breasts, and no hips, and no ...”.\par
\par
Luckily for Antonia’s ignorance which her Aunt’s lecture would soon\par
have dispelled, an universal murmur through the Church announced the\par
Preacher’s arrival. Donna Leonella rose from her seat to take a better\par
view of him, and Antonia followed her example.\par
\par
He was a Man of noble port and commanding presence. His stature was\par
lofty, and his features uncommonly handsome. His Nose was aquiline, his\par
eyes large black and sparkling, and his dark brows almost joined\par
together. His complexion was of a deep but clear Brown; Study and\par
watching had entirely deprived his cheek of colour. Tranquillity\par
reigned upon his smooth unwrinkled forehead; and Content, expressed\par
upon every feature, seemed to announce the Man equally unacquainted\par
with cares and crimes. He bowed himself with humility to the audience:\par
Still there was a certain severity in his look and manner that inspired\par
universal awe, and few could sustain the glance of his eye at once\par
fiery and penetrating. Such was Ambrosio, Abbot of the Capuchins, and\par
surnamed, “The Man of Holiness”.\par
\par
Antonia, while She gazed upon him eagerly, felt a pleasure fluttering\par
in her bosom which till then had been unknown to her, and for which She\par
in vain endeavoured to account. She waited with impatience till the\par
Sermon should begin; and when at length the Friar spoke, the sound of\par
his voice seemed to penetrate into her very soul. Though no other of\par
the Spectators felt such violent sensations as did the young Antonia,\par
yet every one listened with interest and emotion. They who were\par
insensible to Religion’s merits, were still enchanted with Ambrosio’s\par
oratory. All found their attention irresistibly attracted while He\par
spoke, and the most profound silence reigned through the crowded\par
Aisles.\par
\par
Even Lorenzo could not resist the charm: He forgot that Antonia was\par
seated near him, and listened to the Preacher with undivided attention.\par
\par
In language nervous, clear, and simple, the Monk expatiated on the\par
beauties of Religion. He explained some abstruse parts of the sacred\par
writings in a style that carried with it universal conviction. His\par
voice at once distinct and deep was fraught with all the terrors of the\par
Tempest, while He inveighed against the vices of humanity, and\par
described the punishments reserved for them in a future state. Every\par
Hearer looked back upon his past offences, and trembled: The Thunder\par
seemed to roll, whose bolt was destined to crush him, and the abyss of\par
eternal destruction to open before his feet. But when Ambrosio,\par
changing his theme, spoke of the excellence of an unsullied conscience,\par
of the glorious prospect which Eternity presented to the Soul untainted\par
with reproach, and of the recompense which awaited it in the regions of\par
everlasting glory, His Auditors felt their scattered spirits insensibly\par
return. They threw themselves with confidence upon the mercy of their\par
Judge; They hung with delight upon the consoling words of the Preacher;\par
and while his full voice swelled into melody, They were transported to\par
those happy regions which He painted to their imaginations in colours\par
so brilliant and glowing.\par
\par
The discourse was of considerable length; Yet when it concluded, the\par
Audience grieved that it had not lasted longer. Though the Monk had\par
ceased to speak, enthusiastic silence still prevailed through the\par
Church: At length the charm gradually dissolving, the general\par
admiration was expressed in audible terms. As Ambrosio descended from\par
the Pulpit, His Auditors crowded round him, loaded him with blessings,\par
threw themselves at his feet, and kissed the hem of his Garment. He\par
passed on slowly with his hands crossed devoutly upon his bosom, to the\par
door opening into the Abbey Chapel, at which his Monks waited to\par
receive him. He ascended the Steps, and then turning towards his\par
Followers, addressed to them a few words of gratitude, and exhortation.\par
While He spoke, his Rosary, composed of large grains of amber, fell\par
from his hand, and dropped among the surrounding multitude. It was\par
seized eagerly, and immediately divided amidst the Spectators. Whoever\par
became possessor of a Bead, preserved it as a sacred relique; and had\par
it been the Chaplet of thrice-blessed St. Francis himself, it could not\par
have been disputed with greater vivacity. The Abbot, smiling at their\par
eagerness, pronounced his benediction, and quitted the Church, while\par
humility dwelt upon every feature. Dwelt She also in his heart?\par
\par
Antonia’s eyes followed him with anxiety. As the Door closed after him,\par
it seemed to her as had she lost some one essential to her happiness. A\par
tear stole in silence down her cheek.\par
\par
“He is separated from the world!” said She to herself; “Perhaps, I\par
shall never see him more!”\par
\par
As she wiped away the tear, Lorenzo observed her action.\par
\par
“Are you satisfied with our Orator?” said He; “Or do you think that\par
Madrid overrates his talents?”\par
\par
Antonia’s heart was so filled with admiration for the Monk, that She\par
eagerly seized the opportunity of speaking of him: Besides, as She now\par
no longer considered Lorenzo as an absolute Stranger, She was less\par
embarrassed by her excessive timidity.\par
\par
“Oh! He far exceeds all my expectations,” answered She; “Till this\par
moment I had no idea of the powers of eloquence. But when He spoke, his\par
voice inspired me with such interest, such esteem, I might almost say\par
such affection for him, that I am myself astonished at the acuteness of\par
my feelings.”\par
\par
Lorenzo smiled at the strength of her expressions.\par
\par
“You are young and just entering into life,” said He; “Your heart, new\par
to the world and full of warmth and sensibility, receives its first\par
impressions with eagerness. Artless yourself, you suspect not others of\par
deceit; and viewing the world through the medium of your own truth and\par
innocence, you fancy all who surround you to deserve your confidence\par
and esteem. What pity, that these gay visions must soon be dissipated!\par
What pity, that you must soon discover the baseness of mankind, and\par
guard against your fellow-creatures as against your Foes!”\par
\par
“Alas! Segnor,” replied Antonia; “The misfortunes of my Parents have\par
already placed before me but too many sad examples of the perfidy of\par
the world! Yet surely in the present instance the warmth of sympathy\par
cannot have deceived me.”\par
\par
“In the present instance, I allow that it has not. Ambrosio’s character\par
is perfectly without reproach; and a Man who has passed the whole of\par
his life within the walls of a Convent cannot have found the\par
opportunity to be guilty, even were He possessed of the inclination.\par
But now, when, obliged by the duties of his situation, He must enter\par
occasionally into the world, and be thrown into the way of temptation,\par
it is now that it behoves him to show the brilliance of his virtue. The\par
trial is dangerous; He is just at that period of life when the passions\par
are most vigorous, unbridled, and despotic; His established reputation\par
will mark him out to Seduction as an illustrious Victim; Novelty will\par
give additional charms to the allurements of pleasure; and even the\par
Talents with which Nature has endowed him will contribute to his ruin,\par
by facilitating the means of obtaining his object. Very few would\par
return victorious from a contest so severe.”\par
\par
“Ah! surely Ambrosio will be one of those few.”\par
\par
“Of that I have myself no doubt: By all accounts He is an exception to\par
mankind in general, and Envy would seek in vain for a blot upon his\par
character.”\par
\par
“Segnor, you delight me by this assurance! It encourages me to indulge\par
my prepossession in his favour; and you know not with what pain I\par
should have repressed the sentiment! Ah! dearest Aunt, entreat my\par
Mother to choose him for our Confessor.”\par
\par
“I entreat her?” replied Leonella; “I promise you that I shall do no\par
such thing. I do not like this same Ambrosio in the least; He has a\par
look of severity about him that made me tremble from head to foot: Were\par
He my Confessor, I should never have the courage to avow one half of my\par
peccadilloes, and then I should be in a rare condition! I never saw\par
such a stern-looking Mortal, and hope that I never shall see such\par
another. His description of the Devil, God bless us! almost terrified\par
me out of my wits, and when He spoke about Sinners He seemed as if He\par
was ready to eat them.”\par
\par
“You are right, Segnora,” answered Don Christoval; “Too great severity\par
is said to be Ambrosio’s only fault. Exempted himself from human\par
failings, He is not sufficiently indulgent to those of others; and\par
though strictly just and disinterested in his decisions, his government\par
of the Monks has already shown some proofs of his inflexibility. But\par
the crowd is nearly dissipated: Will you permit us to attend you home?”\par
\par
“Oh! Christ! Segnor,” exclaimed Leonella affecting to blush; “I would\par
not suffer such a thing for the Universe! If I came home attended by so\par
gallant a Cavalier, My Sister is so scrupulous that She would read me\par
an hour’s lecture, and I should never hear the last of it. Besides, I\par
rather wish you not to make your proposals just at present.”\par
\par
“My proposals? I assure you, Segnora....”\par
\par
“Oh! Segnor, I believe that your assurances of impatience are all very\par
true; But really I must desire a little respite. It would not be quite\par
so delicate in me to accept your hand at first sight.”\par
\par
“Accept my hand? As I hope to live and breathe....”\par
\par
“Oh! dear Segnor, press me no further, if you love me! I shall consider\par
your obedience as a proof of your affection; You shall hear from me\par
tomorrow, and so farewell. But pray, Cavaliers, may I not enquire your\par
names?”\par
\par
“My Friend’s,” replied Lorenzo, “is the Condé d’Ossorio, and mine\par
Lorenzo de Medina.”\par
\par
“’Tis sufficient. Well, Don Lorenzo, I shall acquaint my Sister with\par
your obliging offer, and let you know the result with all expedition.\par
Where may I send to you?”\par
\par
“I am always to be found at the Medina Palace.”\par
\par
“You may depend upon hearing from me. Farewell, Cavaliers. Segnor\par
Condé, let me entreat you to moderate the excessive ardour of your\par
passion: However, to prove to you that I am not displeased with you,\par
and prevent your abandoning yourself to despair, receive this mark of\par
my affection, and sometimes bestow a thought upon the absent Leonella.”\par
\par
As She said this, She extended a lean and wrinkled hand; which her\par
supposed Admirer kissed with such sorry grace and constraint so\par
evident, that Lorenzo with difficulty repressed his inclination to\par
laugh. Leonella then hastened to quit the Church; The lovely Antonia\par
followed her in silence; but when She reached the Porch, She turned\par
involuntarily, and cast back her eyes towards Lorenzo. He bowed to her,\par
as bidding her farewell; She returned the compliment, and hastily\par
withdrew.\par
\par
“So, Lorenzo!” said Don Christoval as soon as they were alone, “You\par
have procured me an agreeable Intrigue! To favour your designs upon\par
Antonia, I obligingly make a few civil speeches which mean nothing to\par
the Aunt, and at the end of an hour I find myself upon the brink of\par
Matrimony! How will you reward me for having suffered so grievously for\par
your sake? What can repay me for having kissed the leathern paw of that\par
confounded old Witch? Diavolo! She has left such a scent upon my lips\par
that I shall smell of garlick for this month to come! As I pass along\par
the Prado, I shall be taken for a walking Omelet, or some large Onion\par
running to seed!”\par
\par
“I confess, my poor Count,” replied Lorenzo, “that your service has\par
been attended with danger; Yet am I so far from supposing it be past\par
all endurance that I shall probably solicit you to carry on your amours\par
still further.”\par
\par
“From that petition I conclude that the little Antonia has made some\par
impression upon you.”\par
\par
“I cannot express to you how much I am charmed with her. Since my\par
Father’s death, My Uncle the Duke de Medina, has signified to me his\par
wishes to see me married; I have till now eluded his hints, and refused\par
to understand them; But what I have seen this Evening....”\par
\par
“Well? What have you seen this Evening? Why surely, Don Lorenzo, You\par
cannot be mad enough to think of making a Wife out of this\par
Grand-daughter of ‘as honest a painstaking Shoe-maker as any in\par
Cordova’?”\par
\par
“You forget, that She is also the Grand-daughter of the late Marquis de\par
las Cisternas; But without disputing about birth and titles, I must\par
assure you, that I never beheld a Woman so interesting as Antonia.”\par
\par
“Very possibly; But you cannot mean to marry her?”\par
\par
“Why not, my dear Condé? I shall have wealth enough for both of us, and\par
you know that my Uncle thinks liberally upon the subject.\par
\par
From what I have seen of Raymond de las Cisternas, I am certain that he\par
will readily acknowledge Antonia for his Niece. Her birth therefore\par
will be no objection to my offering her my hand. I should be a Villain\par
could I think of her on any other terms than marriage; and in truth She\par
seems possessed of every quality requisite to make me happy in a Wife.\par
Young, lovely, gentle, sensible....”\par
\par
“Sensible? Why, She said nothing but ‘Yes,’ and ‘No’.”\par
\par
“She did not say much more, I must confess—But then She always said\par
‘Yes,’ or ‘No,’ in the right place.”\par
\par
“Did She so? Oh! your most obedient! That is using a right Lover’s\par
argument, and I dare dispute no longer with so profound a Casuist.\par
Suppose we adjourn to the Comedy?”\par
\par
“It is out of my power. I only arrived last night at Madrid, and have\par
not yet had an opportunity of seeing my Sister; You know that her\par
Convent is in this Street, and I was going thither when the Crowd which\par
I saw thronging into this Church excited my curiosity to know what was\par
the matter. I shall now pursue my first intention, and probably pass\par
the Evening with my Sister at the Parlour grate.”\par
\par
“Your Sister in a Convent, say you? Oh! very true, I had forgotten. And\par
how does Donna Agnes? I am amazed, Don Lorenzo, how you could possibly\par
think of immuring so charming a Girl within the walls of a Cloister!”\par
\par
“I think of it, Don Christoval? How can you suspect me of such\par
barbarity? You are conscious that She took the veil by her own desire,\par
and that particular circumstances made her wish for a seclusion from\par
the World. I used every means in my power to induce her to change her\par
resolution; The endeavour was fruitless, and I lost a Sister!”\par
\par
“The luckier fellow you; I think, Lorenzo, you were a considerable\par
gainer by that loss: If I remember right, Donna Agnes had a portion of\par
ten thousand pistoles, half of which reverted to your Lordship. By St.\par
Jago! I wish that I had fifty Sisters in the same predicament. I should\par
consent to losing them every soul without much heart-burning—”\par
\par
“How, Condé?” said Lorenzo in an angry voice; “Do you suppose me base\par
enough to have influenced my Sister’s retirement? Do you suppose that\par
the despicable wish to make myself Master of her fortune could....”\par
\par
“Admirable! Courage, Don Lorenzo! Now the Man is all in a blaze. God\par
grant that Antonia may soften that fiery temper, or we shall certainly\par
cut each other’s throat before the Month is over! However, to prevent\par
such a tragical Catastrophe for the present, I shall make a retreat,\par
and leave you Master of the field. Farewell, my Knight of Mount Aetna!\par
Moderate that inflammable disposition, and remember that whenever it is\par
necessary to make love to yonder Harridan, you may reckon upon my\par
services.”\par
\par
He said, and darted out of the Cathedral.\par
\par
“How wild-brained!” said Lorenzo; “With so excellent an heart, what\par
pity that He possesses so little solidity of judgment!”\par
\par
The night was now fast advancing. The Lamps were not yet lighted. The\par
faint beams of the rising Moon scarcely could pierce through the gothic\par
obscurity of the Church. Lorenzo found himself unable to quit the Spot.\par
The void left in his bosom by Antonia’s absence, and his Sister’s\par
sacrifice which Don Christoval had just recalled to his imagination,\par
created that melancholy of mind which accorded but too well with the\par
religious gloom surrounding him. He was still leaning against the\par
seventh column from the Pulpit. A soft and cooling air breathed along\par
the solitary Aisles: The Moonbeams darting into the Church through\par
painted windows tinged the fretted roofs and massy pillars with a\par
thousand various tints of light and colours:\par
\par
Universal silence prevailed around, only interrupted by the occasional\par
closing of Doors in the adjoining Abbey.\par
\par
The calm of the hour and solitude of the place contributed to nourish\par
Lorenzo’s disposition to melancholy. He threw himself upon a seat which\par
stood near him, and abandoned himself to the delusions of his fancy. He\par
thought of his union with Antonia; He thought of the obstacles which\par
might oppose his wishes; and a thousand changing visions floated before\par
his fancy, sad ’tis true, but not unpleasing. Sleep insensibly stole\par
over him, and the tranquil solemnity of his mind when awake for a while\par
continued to influence his slumbers.\par
\par
He still fancied himself to be in the Church of the Capuchins; but it\par
was no longer dark and solitary. Multitudes of silver Lamps shed\par
splendour from the vaulted Roof; Accompanied by the captivating chaunt\par
of distant choristers, the Organ’s melody swelled through the Church;\par
The Altar seemed decorated as for some distinguished feast; It was\par
surrounded by a brilliant Company; and near it stood Antonia arrayed in\par
bridal white, and blushing with all the charms of Virgin Modesty.\par
\par
Half hoping, half fearing, Lorenzo gazed upon the scene before him.\par
Suddenly the door leading to the Abbey unclosed, and He saw, attended\par
by a long train of Monks, the Preacher advance to whom He had just\par
listened with so much admiration. He drew near Antonia.\par
\par
“And where is the Bridegroom?” said the imaginary Friar.\par
\par
Antonia seemed to look round the Church with anxiety. Involuntarily the\par
Youth advanced a few steps from his concealment. She saw him; The blush\par
of pleasure glowed upon her cheek; With a graceful motion of her hand\par
She beckoned to him to advance. He disobeyed not the command; He flew\par
towards her, and threw himself at her feet.\par
\par
She retreated for a moment; Then gazing upon him with unutterable\par
delight;—“Yes!” She exclaimed, “My Bridegroom! My destined Bridegroom!”\par
She said, and hastened to throw herself into his arms; But before He\par
had time to receive her, an Unknown rushed between them. His form was\par
gigantic; His complexion was swarthy, His eyes fierce and terrible; his\par
Mouth breathed out volumes of fire; and on his forehead was written in\par
legible characters—“Pride! Lust! Inhumanity!”\par
\par
Antonia shrieked. The Monster clasped her in his arms, and springing\par
with her upon the Altar, tortured her with his odious caresses. She\par
endeavoured in vain to escape from his embrace. Lorenzo flew to her\par
succour, but ere He had time to reach her, a loud burst of thunder was\par
heard. Instantly the Cathedral seemed crumbling into pieces; The Monks\par
betook themselves to flight, shrieking fearfully; The Lamps were\par
extinguished, the Altar sank down, and in its place appeared an abyss\par
vomiting forth clouds of flame. Uttering a loud and terrible cry the\par
Monster plunged into the Gulph, and in his fall attempted to drag\par
Antonia with him. He strove in vain. Animated by supernatural powers\par
She disengaged herself from his embrace; But her white Robe was left in\par
his possession. Instantly a wing of brilliant splendour spread itself\par
from either of Antonia’s arms. She darted upwards, and while ascending\par
cried to Lorenzo,\par
\par
“Friend! we shall meet above!”\par
\par
At the same moment the Roof of the Cathedral opened; Harmonious voices\par
pealed along the Vaults; and the glory into which Antonia was received\par
was composed of rays of such dazzling brightness, that Lorenzo was\par
unable to sustain the gaze. His sight failed, and He sank upon the\par
ground.\par
\par
When He woke, He found himself extended upon the pavement of the\par
Church: It was Illuminated, and the chaunt of Hymns sounded from a\par
distance. For a while Lorenzo could not persuade himself that what He\par
had just witnessed had been a dream, so strong an impression had it\par
made upon his fancy. A little recollection convinced him of its\par
fallacy: The Lamps had been lighted during his sleep, and the music\par
which he heard was occasioned by the Monks, who were celebrating their\par
Vespers in the Abbey Chapel.\par
\par
Lorenzo rose, and prepared to bend his steps towards his Sister’s\par
Convent. His mind fully occupied by the singularity of his dream, He\par
already drew near the Porch, when his attention was attracted by\par
perceiving a Shadow moving upon the opposite wall. He looked curiously\par
round, and soon descried a Man wrapped up in his Cloak, who seemed\par
carefully examining whether his actions were observed. Very few people\par
are exempt from the influence of curiosity. The Unknown seemed anxious\par
to conceal his business in the Cathedral, and it was this very\par
circumstance, which made Lorenzo wish to discover what He was about.\par
\par
Our Hero was conscious that He had no right to pry into the secrets of\par
this unknown Cavalier.\par
\par
“I will go,” said Lorenzo. And Lorenzo stayed, where He was.\par
\par
The shadow thrown by the Column, effectually concealed him from the\par
Stranger, who continued to advance with caution. At length He drew a\par
letter from beneath his cloak, and hastily placed it beneath a Colossal\par
Statue of St. Francis. Then retiring with precipitation, He concealed\par
himself in a part of the Church at a considerable distance from that in\par
which the Image stood.\par
\par
“So!” said Lorenzo to himself; “This is only some foolish love affair.\par
I believe, I may as well be gone, for I can do no good in it.”\par
\par
In truth till that moment it never came into his head that He could do\par
any good in it; But He thought it necessary to make some little excuse\par
to himself for having indulged his curiosity. He now made a second\par
attempt to retire from the Church: For this time He gained the Porch\par
without meeting with any impediment; But it was destined that He should\par
pay it another visit that night. As He descended the steps leading into\par
the Street, a Cavalier rushed against him with such violence, that Both\par
were nearly overturned by the concussion. Lorenzo put his hand to his\par
sword.\par
\par
“How now, Segnor?” said He; “What mean you by this rudeness?”\par
\par
“Ha! Is it you, Medina?” replied the Newcomer, whom Lorenzo by his\par
voice now recognized for Don Christoval; “You are the luckiest Fellow\par
in the Universe, not to have left the Church before my return. In, in!\par
my dear Lad! They will be here immediately!”\par
\par
“Who will be here?”\par
\par
“The old Hen and all her pretty little Chickens! In, I say, and then\par
you shall know the whole History.”\par
\par
Lorenzo followed him into the Cathedral, and they concealed themselves\par
behind the Statue of St. Francis.\par
\par
“And now,” said our Hero, “may I take the liberty of asking, what is\par
the meaning of all this haste and rapture?”\par
\par
“Oh! Lorenzo, we shall see such a glorious sight! The Prioress of St.\par
Clare and her whole train of Nuns are coming hither. You are to know,\par
that the pious Father Ambrosio (The Lord reward him for it!) will upon\par
no account move out of his own precincts: It being absolutely necessary\par
for every fashionable Convent to have him for its Confessor, the Nuns\par
are in consequence obliged to visit him at the Abbey; since when the\par
Mountain will not come to Mahomet, Mahomet must needs go to the\par
Mountain. Now the Prioress of St. Clare, the better to escape the gaze\par
of such impure eyes as belong to yourself and your humble Servant,\par
thinks proper to bring her holy flock to confession in the Dusk: She is\par
to be admitted into the Abbey Chapel by yon private door. The Porteress\par
of St. Clare, who is a worthy old Soul and a particular Friend of mine,\par
has just assured me of their being here in a few moments. There is news\par
for you, you Rogue! We shall see some of the prettiest faces in\par
Madrid!”\par
\par
“In truth, Christoval, we shall do no such thing. The Nuns are always\par
veiled.”\par
\par
“No! No! I know better. On entering a place of worship, they ever take\par
off their veils from respect to the Saint to whom ’tis dedicated. But\par
Hark! They are coming! Silence, silence! Observe, and be convinced.”\par
\par
“Good!” said Lorenzo to himself; “I may possibly discover to whom the\par
vows are addressed of this mysterious Stranger.”\par
\par
Scarcely had Don Christoval ceased to speak, when the Domina of St.\par
Clare appeared, followed by a long procession of Nuns. Each upon\par
entering the Church took off her veil. The Prioress crossed her hands\par
upon her bosom, and made a profound reverence as She passed the Statue\par
of St. Francis, the Patron of this Cathedral. The Nuns followed her\par
example, and several moved onwards without having satisfied Lorenzo’s\par
curiosity. He almost began to despair of seeing the mystery cleared up,\par
when in paying her respects to St. Francis, one of the Nuns happened to\par
drop her Rosary. As She stooped to pick it up, the light flashed full\par
upon her face. At the same moment She dexterously removed the letter\par
from beneath the Image, placed it in her bosom, and hastened to resume\par
her rank in the procession.\par
\par
“Ha!” said Christoval in a low voice; “Here we have some little\par
Intrigue, no doubt.”\par
\par
“Agnes, by heaven!” cried Lorenzo.\par
\par
“What, your Sister? Diavolo! Then somebody, I suppose, will have to pay\par
for our peeping.”\par
\par
“And shall pay for it without delay,” replied the incensed Brother.\par
\par
The pious procession had now entered the Abbey; The Door was already\par
closed upon it. The Unknown immediately quitted his concealment and\par
hastened to leave the Church: Ere He could effect his intention, He\par
descried Medina stationed in his passage. The Stranger hastily\par
retreated, and drew his Hat over his eyes.\par
\par
“Attempt not to fly me!” exclaimed Lorenzo; “I will know who you are,\par
and what were the contents of that Letter.”\par
\par
“Of that Letter?” repeated the Unknown. “And by what title do you ask\par
the question?”\par
\par
“By a title of which I am now ashamed; But it becomes not you to\par
question me. Either reply circumstantially to my demands, or answer me\par
with your Sword.”\par
\par
“The latter method will be the shortest,” rejoined the Other, drawing\par
his Rapier; “Come on, Segnor Bravo! I am ready!”\par
\par
Burning with rage, Lorenzo hastened to the attack: The Antagonists had\par
already exchanged several passes before Christoval, who at that moment\par
had more sense than either of them, could throw himself between their\par
weapons.\par
\par
“Hold! Hold! Medina!” He exclaimed; “Remember the consequences of\par
shedding blood on consecrated ground!”\par
\par
The Stranger immediately dropped his Sword.\par
\par
“Medina?” He cried; “Great God, is it possible! Lorenzo, have you quite\par
forgotten Raymond de las Cisternas?”\par
\par
Lorenzo’s astonishment increased with every succeeding moment. Raymond\par
advanced towards him, but with a look of suspicion He drew back his\par
hand, which the Other was preparing to take.\par
\par
“You here, Marquis? What is the meaning of all this? You engaged in a\par
clandestine correspondence with my Sister, whose affections....”\par
\par
“Have ever been, and still are mine. But this is no fit place for an\par
explanation. Accompany me to my Hotel, and you shall know every thing.\par
Who is that with you?”\par
\par
“One whom I believe you to have seen before,” replied Don Christoval,\par
“though probably not at Church.”\par
\par
“The Condé d’Ossorio?”\par
\par
“Exactly so, Marquis.”\par
\par
“I have no objection to entrusting you with my secret, for I am sure\par
that I may depend upon your silence.”\par
\par
“Then your opinion of me is better than my own, and therefore I must\par
beg leave to decline your confidence. Do you go your own way, and I\par
shall go mine. Marquis, where are you to be found?”\par
\par
“As usual, at the Hotel de las Cisternas; But remember, that I am\par
incognito, and that if you wish to see me, you must ask for Alphonso\par
d’Alvarada.”\par
\par
“Good! Good! Farewell, Cavaliers!” said Don Christoval, and instantly\par
departed.\par
\par
“You, Marquis,” said Lorenzo in the accent of surprise; “You, Alphonso\par
d’Alvarada?”\par
\par
“Even so, Lorenzo: But unless you have already heard my story from your\par
Sister, I have much to relate that will astonish you. Follow me,\par
therefore, to my Hotel without delay.”\par
\par
At this moment the Porter of the Capuchins entered the Cathedral to\par
lock up the doors for the night. The two Noblemen instantly withdrew,\par
and hastened with all speed to the Palace de las Cisternas.\par
\par
\par
“Well, Antonia!” said the Aunt, as soon as She had quitted the Church;\par
“What think you of our Gallants? Don Lorenzo really seems a very\par
obliging good sort of young Man: He paid you some attention, and nobody\par
knows what may come of it. But as to Don Christoval, I protest to you,\par
He is the very Phoenix of politeness. So gallant! so well-bred! So\par
sensible, and so pathetic! Well! If ever Man can prevail upon me to\par
break my vow never to marry, it will be that Don Christoval. You see,\par
Niece, that every thing turns out exactly as I told you: The very\par
moment that I produced myself in Madrid, I knew that I should be\par
surrounded by Admirers. When I took off my veil, did you see, Antonia,\par
what an effect the action had upon the Condé? And when I presented him\par
my hand, did you observe the air of passion with which He kissed it? If\par
ever I witnessed real love, I then saw it impressed upon Don\par
Christoval’s countenance!”\par
\par
Now Antonia had observed the air, with which Don Christoval had kissed\par
this same hand; But as She drew conclusions from it somewhat different\par
from her Aunt’s, She was wise enough to hold her tongue. As this is the\par
only instance known of a Woman’s ever having done so, it was judged\par
worthy to be recorded here.\par
\par
The old Lady continued her discourse to Antonia in the same strain,\par
till they gained the Street in which was their Lodging. Here a Crowd\par
collected before their door permitted them not to approach it; and\par
placing themselves on the opposite side of the Street, they endeavoured\par
to make out what had drawn all these people together. After some\par
minutes the Crowd formed itself into a Circle; And now Antonia\par
perceived in the midst of it a Woman of extraordinary height, who\par
whirled herself repeatedly round and round, using all sorts of\par
extravagant gestures. Her dress was composed of shreds of\par
various-coloured silks and Linens fantastically arranged, yet not\par
entirely without taste. Her head was covered with a kind of Turban,\par
ornamented with vine leaves and wild flowers. She seemed much\par
sun-burnt, and her complexion was of a deep olive: Her eyes looked\par
fiery and strange; and in her hand She bore a long black Rod, with\par
which She at intervals traced a variety of singular figures upon the\par
ground, round about which She danced in all the eccentric attitudes of\par
folly and delirium. Suddenly She broke off her dance, whirled herself\par
round thrice with rapidity, and after a moment’s pause She sang the\par
following Ballad.\par
\par
THE GYPSY’S SONG\par
\par
\par
Come, cross my hand! My art surpasses\par
    All that did ever Mortal know;\par
Come, Maidens, come! My magic glasses\par
    Your future Husband’s form can show:\par
\par
For ’tis to me the power is given\par
    Unclosed the book of Fate to see;\par
To read the fixed resolves of heaven,\par
    And dive into futurity.\par
\par
I guide the pale Moon’s silver waggon;\par
    The winds in magic bonds I hold;\par
I charm to sleep the crimson Dragon,\par
    Who loves to watch o’er buried gold:\par
\par
Fenced round with spells, unhurt I venture\par
    Their sabbath strange where Witches keep;\par
Fearless the Sorcerer’s circle enter,\par
    And woundless tread on snakes asleep.\par
\par
Lo! Here are charms of mighty power!\par
    This makes secure an Husband’s truth\par
And this composed at midnight hour\par
    Will force to love the coldest Youth:\par
\par
If any Maid too much has granted,\par
    Her loss this Philtre will repair;\par
This blooms a cheek where red is wanted,\par
    And this will make a brown girl fair!\par
\par
Then silent hear, while I discover\par
    What I in Fortune’s mirror view;\par
And each, when many a year is over,\par
    Shall own the Gypsy’s sayings true.\par
\par
\par
“Dear Aunt!” said Antonia when the Stranger had finished, “Is She not\par
mad?”\par
\par
“Mad? Not She, Child; She is only wicked. She is a Gypsy, a sort of\par
Vagabond, whose sole occupation is to run about the country telling\par
lyes, and pilfering from those who come by their money honestly. Out\par
upon such Vermin! If I were King of Spain, every one of them should be\par
burnt alive who was found in my dominions after the next three weeks.”\par
\par
These words were pronounced so audibly that they reached the Gypsy’s\par
ears. She immediately pierced through the Crowd and made towards the\par
Ladies. She saluted them thrice in the Eastern fashion, and then\par
addressed herself to Antonia.\par
\par
THE GYPSY\par
\par
\par
“Lady! gentle Lady! Know,\par
I your future fate can show;\par
Give your hand, and do not fear;\par
Lady! gentle Lady! hear!”\par
\par
\par
“Dearest Aunt!” said Antonia, “Indulge me this once! Let me have my\par
fortune told me!”\par
\par
“Nonsense, Child! She will tell you nothing but falsehoods.”\par
\par
“No matter; Let me at least hear what She has to say. Do, my dear Aunt!\par
Oblige me, I beseech you!”\par
\par
“Well, well! Antonia, since you are so bent upon the thing, ... Here,\par
good Woman, you shall see the hands of both of us. There is money for\par
you, and now let me hear my fortune.”\par
\par
As She said this, She drew off her glove, and presented her hand; The\par
Gypsy looked at it for a moment, and then made this reply.\par
\par
THE GYPSY\par
\par
\par
“Your fortune? You are now so old,\par
Good Dame, that ’tis already told:\par
Yet for your money, in a trice\par
I will repay you in advice.\par
Astonished at your childish vanity,\par
Your Friends all tax you with insanity,\par
And grieve to see you use your art\par
To catch some youthful Lover’s heart.\par
Believe me, Dame, when all is done,\par
Your age will still be fifty one;\par
And Men will rarely take an hint\par
Of love, from two grey eyes that squint.\par
Take then my counsels; Lay aside\par
Your paint and patches, lust and pride,\par
And on the Poor those sums bestow,\par
Which now are spent on useless show.\par
Think on your Maker, not a Suitor;\par
Think on your past faults, not on future;\par
And think Time’s Scythe will quickly mow\par
The few red hairs, which deck your brow.\par
\par
\par
The audience rang with laughter during the Gypsy’s address; and—“fifty\par
one,”—“squinting eyes,” “red hair,”—“paint and patches,” &c. were\par
bandied from mouth to mouth. Leonella was almost choaked with passion,\par
and loaded her malicious Adviser with the bitterest reproaches. The\par
swarthy Prophetess for some time listened to her with a contemptuous\par
smile: at length She made her a short answer, and then turned to\par
Antonia.\par
\par
THE GYPSY\par
\par
\par
“Peace, Lady! What I said was true;\par
And now, my lovely Maid, to you;\par
Give me your hand, and let me see\par
Your future doom, and heaven’s decree.”\par
\par
\par
In imitation of Leonella, Antonia drew off her glove, and presented her\par
white hand to the Gypsy, who having gazed upon it for some time with a\par
mingled expression of pity and astonishment, pronounced her Oracle in\par
the following words.\par
\par
THE GYPSY\par
\par
\par
“Jesus! what a palm is there!\par
Chaste, and gentle, young and fair,\par
Perfect mind and form possessing,\par
You would be some good Man’s blessing:\par
But Alas! This line discovers,\par
That destruction o’er you hovers;\par
Lustful Man and crafty Devil\par
Will combine to work your evil;\par
And from earth by sorrows driven,\par
Soon your Soul must speed to heaven.\par
Yet your sufferings to delay,\par
Well remember what I say.\par
When you One more virtuous see\par
Than belongs to Man to be,\par
One, whose self no crimes assailing,\par
Pities not his Neighbour’s Failing,\par
Call the Gypsy’s words to mind:\par
Though He seem so good and kind,\par
Fair Exteriors oft will hide\par
Hearts, that swell with lust and pride!\par
Lovely Maid, with tears I leave you!\par
Let not my prediction grieve you;\par
Rather with submission bending\par
Calmly wait distress impending,\par
And expect eternal bliss\par
In a better world than this.\par
\par
\par
Having said this, the Gypsy again whirled herself round thrice, and\par
then hastened out of the Street with frantic gesture. The Crowd\par
followed her; and Elvira’s door being now unembarrassed Leonella\par
entered the House out of humour with the Gypsy, with her Niece, and\par
with the People; In short with every body, but herself and her charming\par
Cavalier. The Gypsy’s predictions had also considerably affected\par
Antonia; But the impression soon wore off, and in a few hours She had\par
forgotten the adventure as totally as had it never taken place.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER II.\par
\par
\par
Fòrse sé tu gustassi una sòl volta\par
La millésima parte délle giòje,\par
Ché gusta un còr amato riamando,\par
Diresti ripentita sospirando,\par
Perduto è tutto il tempo\par
Ché in amar non si spènde.\par
\par
TASSO.\par
\par
Hadst Thou but tasted once the thousandth part\par
Of joys, which bless the loved and loving heart,\par
Your words repentant and your sighs would prove,\par
Lost is the time which is not past in love.\par
\par
The monks having attended their Abbot to the door of his Cell, He\par
dismissed them with an air of conscious superiority in which Humility’s\par
semblance combated with the reality of pride.\par
\par
He was no sooner alone, than He gave free loose to the indulgence of\par
his vanity. When He remembered the Enthusiasm which his discourse had\par
excited, his heart swelled with rapture, and his imagination presented\par
him with splendid visions of aggrandizement. He looked round him with\par
exultation, and Pride told him loudly that He was superior to the rest\par
of his fellow-Creatures.\par
\par
“Who,” thought He; “Who but myself has passed the ordeal of Youth, yet\par
sees no single stain upon his conscience? Who else has subdued the\par
violence of strong passions and an impetuous temperament, and submitted\par
even from the dawn of life to voluntary retirement? I seek for such a\par
Man in vain. I see no one but myself possessed of such resolution.\par
Religion cannot boast Ambrosio’s equal! How powerful an effect did my\par
discourse produce upon its Auditors! How they crowded round me! How\par
they loaded me with benedictions, and pronounced me the sole\par
uncorrupted Pillar of the Church! What then now is left for me to do?\par
Nothing, but to watch as carefully over the conduct of my Brothers as I\par
have hitherto watched over my own. Yet hold! May I not be tempted from\par
those paths which till now I have pursued without one moment’s\par
wandering? Am I not a Man, whose nature is frail, and prone to error? I\par
must now abandon the solitude of my retreat; The fairest and noblest\par
Dames of Madrid continually present themselves at the Abbey, and will\par
use no other Confessor.\par
\par
I must accustom my eyes to Objects of temptation, and expose myself to\par
the seduction of luxury and desire. Should I meet in that world which I\par
am constrained to enter some lovely Female, lovely ... as yon\par
Madona....!”\par
\par
As He said this, He fixed his eyes upon a picture of the Virgin, which\par
was suspended opposite to him: This for two years had been the Object\par
of his increasing wonder and adoration. He paused, and gazed upon it\par
with delight.\par
\par
“What Beauty in that countenance!” He continued after a silence of some\par
minutes; “How graceful is the turn of that head! What sweetness, yet\par
what majesty in her divine eyes! How softly her cheek reclines upon her\par
hand! Can the Rose vie with the blush of that cheek? Can the Lily rival\par
the whiteness of that hand? Oh! if such a Creature existed, and existed\par
but for me! Were I permitted to twine round my fingers those golden\par
ringlets, and press with my lips the treasures of that snowy bosom!\par
Gracious God, should I then resist the temptation? Should I not barter\par
for a single embrace the reward of my sufferings for thirty years?\par
Should I not abandon.... Fool that I am! Whither do I suffer my\par
admiration of this picture to hurry me? Away, impure ideas! Let me\par
remember that Woman is for ever lost to me. Never was Mortal formed so\par
perfect as this picture. But even did such exist, the trial might be\par
too mighty for a common virtue, but Ambrosio’s is proof against\par
temptation. Temptation, did I say? To me it would be none. What charms\par
me, when ideal and considered as a superior Being, would disgust me,\par
become Woman and tainted with all the failings of Mortality. It is not\par
the Woman’s beauty that fills me with such enthusiasm; It is the\par
Painter’s skill that I admire, it is the Divinity that I adore! Are not\par
the passions dead in my bosom? Have I not freed myself from the frailty\par
of Mankind? Fear not, Ambrosio! Take confidence in the strength of your\par
virtue. Enter boldly into a world to whose failings you are superior;\par
Reflect that you are now exempted from Humanity’s defects, and defy all\par
the arts of the Spirits of Darkness. They shall know you for what you\par
are!”\par
\par
Here his Reverie was interrupted by three soft knocks at the door of\par
his Cell. With difficulty did the Abbot awake from his delirium. The\par
knocking was repeated.\par
\par
“Who is there?” said Ambrosio at length.\par
\par
“It is only Rosario,” replied a gentle voice.\par
\par
“Enter! Enter, my Son!”\par
\par
The Door was immediately opened, and Rosario appeared with a small\par
basket in his hand.\par
\par
Rosario was a young Novice belonging to the Monastery, who in three\par
Months intended to make his profession. A sort of mystery enveloped\par
this Youth which rendered him at once an object of interest and\par
curiosity. His hatred of society, his profound melancholy, his rigid\par
observation of the duties of his order, and his voluntary seclusion\par
from the world at his age so unusual, attracted the notice of the whole\par
fraternity. He seemed fearful of being recognised, and no one had ever\par
seen his face. His head was continually muffled up in his Cowl; Yet\par
such of his features as accident discovered, appeared the most\par
beautiful and noble. Rosario was the only name by which He was known in\par
the Monastery.\par
\par
No one knew from whence He came, and when questioned in the subject He\par
preserved a profound silence. A Stranger, whose rich habit and\par
magnificent equipage declared him to be of distinguished rank, had\par
engaged the Monks to receive a Novice, and had deposited the necessary\par
sums. The next day He returned with Rosario, and from that time no more\par
had been heard of him.\par
\par
The Youth had carefully avoided the company of the Monks: He answered\par
their civilities with sweetness, but reserve, and evidently showed that\par
his inclination led him to solitude. To this general rule the Superior\par
was the only exception. To him He looked up with a respect approaching\par
idolatry: He sought his company with the most attentive assiduity, and\par
eagerly seized every means to ingratiate himself in his favour. In the\par
Abbot’s society his Heart seemed to be at ease, and an air of gaiety\par
pervaded his whole manners and discourse. Ambrosio on his side did not\par
feel less attracted towards the Youth; With him alone did He lay aside\par
his habitual severity. When He spoke to him, He insensibly assumed a\par
tone milder than was usual to him; and no voice sounded so sweet to him\par
as did Rosario’s. He repayed the Youth’s attentions by instructing him\par
in various sciences; The Novice received his lessons with docility;\par
Ambrosio was every day more charmed with the vivacity of his Genius,\par
the simplicity of his manners, and the rectitude of his heart: In short\par
He loved him with all the affection of a Father. He could not help\par
sometimes indulging a desire secretly to see the face of his Pupil; But\par
his rule of self-denial extended even to curiosity, and prevented him\par
from communicating his wishes to the Youth.\par
\par
“Pardon my intrusion, Father,” said Rosario, while He placed his basket\par
upon the Table; “I come to you a Suppliant. Hearing that a dear Friend\par
is dangerously ill, I entreat your prayers for his recovery. If\par
supplications can prevail upon heaven to spare him, surely yours must\par
be efficacious.”\par
\par
“Whatever depends upon me, my Son, you know that you may command.\par
\par
What is your Friend’s name?”\par
\par
“Vincentio della Ronda.”\par
\par
“’Tis sufficient. I will not forget him in my prayers, and may our\par
thrice-blessed St. Francis deign to listen to my intercession!—What\par
have you in your basket, Rosario?”\par
\par
“A few of those flowers, reverend Father, which I have observed to be\par
most acceptable to you. Will you permit my arranging them in your\par
chamber?”\par
\par
“Your attentions charm me, my Son.”\par
\par
While Rosario dispersed the contents of his Basket in small Vases\par
placed for that purpose in various parts of the room, the Abbot thus\par
continued the conversation.\par
\par
“I saw you not in the Church this evening, Rosario.”\par
\par
“Yet I was present, Father. I am too grateful for your protection to\par
lose an opportunity of witnessing your Triumph.”\par
\par
“Alas! Rosario, I have but little cause to triumph: The Saint spoke by\par
my mouth; To him belongs all the merit. It seems then you were\par
contented with my discourse?”\par
\par
“Contented, say you? Oh! you surpassed yourself! Never did I hear such\par
eloquence ... save once!”\par
\par
Here the Novice heaved an involuntary sigh.\par
\par
“When was that once?” demanded the Abbot.\par
\par
“When you preached upon the sudden indisposition of our late Superior.”\par
\par
“I remember it: That is more than two years ago. And were you present?\par
I knew you not at that time, Rosario.”\par
\par
“’Tis true, Father; and would to God! I had expired, ere I beheld that\par
day! What sufferings, what sorrows should I have escaped!”\par
\par
“Sufferings at your age, Rosario?”\par
\par
“Aye, Father; Sufferings, which if known to you, would equally raise\par
your anger and compassion! Sufferings, which form at once the torment\par
and pleasure of my existence! Yet in this retreat my bosom would feel\par
tranquil, were it not for the tortures of apprehension. Oh God! Oh God!\par
how cruel is a life of fear!—Father! I have given up all; I have\par
abandoned the world and its delights for ever: Nothing now remains,\par
Nothing now has charms for me, but your friendship, but your affection.\par
If I lose that, Father! Oh! if I lose that, tremble at the effects of\par
my despair!”\par
\par
“You apprehend the loss of my friendship? How has my conduct justified\par
this fear? Know me better, Rosario, and think me worthy of your\par
confidence. What are your sufferings? Reveal them to me, and believe\par
that if ’tis in my power to relieve them....”\par
\par
“Ah! ’tis in no one’s power but yours. Yet I must not let you know\par
them. You would hate me for my avowal! You would drive me from your\par
presence with scorn and ignominy!”\par
\par
“My Son, I conjure you! I entreat you!”\par
\par
“For pity’s sake, enquire no further! I must not ... I dare not...\par
Hark! The Bell rings for Vespers! Father, your benediction, and I leave\par
you!”\par
\par
As He said this, He threw himself upon his knees and received the\par
blessing which He demanded. Then pressing the Abbot’s hand to his lips,\par
He started from the ground and hastily quitted the apartment. Soon\par
after Ambrosio descended to Vespers (which were celebrated in a small\par
chapel belonging to the Abbey), filled with surprise at the singularity\par
of the Youth’s behaviour.\par
\par
Vespers being over, the Monks retired to their respective Cells. The\par
Abbot alone remained in the Chapel to receive the Nuns of St. Clare. He\par
had not been long seated in the confessional chair before the Prioress\par
made her appearance. Each of the Nuns was heard in her turn, while the\par
Others waited with the Domina in the adjoining Vestry. Ambrosio\par
listened to the confessions with attention, made many exhortations,\par
enjoined penance proportioned to each offence, and for some time every\par
thing went on as usual: till at last one of the Nuns, conspicuous from\par
the nobleness of her air and elegance of her figure, carelessly\par
permitted a letter to fall from her bosom. She was retiring,\par
unconscious of her loss. Ambrosio supposed it to have been written by\par
some one of her Relations, and picked it up intending to restore it to\par
her.\par
\par
“Stay, Daughter,” said He; “You have let fall....”\par
\par
At this moment, the paper being already open, his eye involuntarily\par
read the first words. He started back with surprise! The Nun had turned\par
round on hearing his voice: She perceived her letter in his hand, and\par
uttering a shriek of terror, flew hastily to regain it.\par
\par
“Hold!” said the Friar in a tone of severity; “Daughter, I must read\par
this letter.”\par
\par
“Then I am lost!” She exclaimed clasping her hands together wildly.\par
\par
All colour instantly faded from her face; she trembled with agitation,\par
and was obliged to fold her arms round a Pillar of the Chapel to save\par
herself from sinking upon the floor. In the meanwhile the Abbot read\par
the following lines:\par
\par
“All is ready for your escape, my dearest Agnes. At twelve tomorrow\par
night I shall expect to find you at the Garden door: I have obtained\par
the Key, and a few hours will suffice to place you in a secure asylum.\par
Let no mistaken scruples induce you to reject the certain means of\par
preserving yourself and the innocent Creature whom you nourish in your\par
bosom. Remember that you had promised to be mine, long ere you engaged\par
yourself to the church; that your situation will soon be evident to the\par
prying eyes of your Companions; and that flight is the only means of\par
avoiding the effects of their malevolent resentment. Farewell, my\par
Agnes! my dear and destined Wife! Fail not to be at the Garden door at\par
twelve!”\par
\par
\par
As soon as He had finished, Ambrosio bent an eye stern and angry upon\par
the imprudent Nun.\par
\par
“This letter must to the Prioress!” said He, and passed her.\par
\par
His words sounded like thunder to her ears: She awoke from her\par
torpidity only to be sensible of the dangers of her situation. She\par
followed him hastily, and detained him by his garment.\par
\par
“Stay! Oh! stay!” She cried in the accents of despair, while She threw\par
herself at the Friar’s feet, and bathed them with her tears. “Father,\par
compassionate my youth! Look with indulgence on a Woman’s weakness, and\par
deign to conceal my frailty! The remainder of my life shall be employed\par
in expiating this single fault, and your lenity will bring back a soul\par
to heaven!”\par
\par
“Amazing confidence! What! Shall St. Clare’s Convent become the retreat\par
of Prostitutes? Shall I suffer the Church of Christ to cherish in its\par
bosom debauchery and shame? Unworthy Wretch! such lenity would make me\par
your accomplice. Mercy would here be criminal. You have abandoned\par
yourself to a Seducer’s lust; You have defiled the sacred habit by your\par
impurity; and still dare you think yourself deserving my compassion?\par
Hence, nor detain me longer! Where is the Lady Prioress?” He added,\par
raising his voice.\par
\par
“Hold! Father, Hold! Hear me but for one moment! Tax me not with\par
impurity, nor think that I have erred from the warmth of temperament.\par
Long before I took the veil, Raymond was Master of my heart: He\par
inspired me with the purest, the most irreproachable passion, and was\par
on the point of becoming my lawful husband. An horrible adventure, and\par
the treachery of a Relation, separated us from each other: I believed\par
him for ever lost to me, and threw myself into a Convent from motives\par
of despair. Accident again united us; I could not refuse myself the\par
melancholy pleasure of mingling my tears with his: We met nightly in\par
the Gardens of St. Clare, and in an unguarded moment I violated my vows\par
of Chastity. I shall soon become a Mother: Reverend Ambrosio, take\par
compassion on me; take compassion on the innocent Being whose existence\par
is attached to mine. If you discover my imprudence to the Domina, both\par
of us are lost: The punishment which the laws of St. Clare assign to\par
Unfortunates like myself is most severe and cruel. Worthy, worthy\par
Father! Let not your own untainted conscience render you unfeeling\par
towards those less able to withstand temptation! Let not mercy be the\par
only virtue of which your heart is unsusceptible! Pity me, most\par
reverend! Restore my letter, nor doom me to inevitable destruction!”\par
\par
“Your boldness confounds me! Shall _I_ conceal your crime, _I_ whom you\par
have deceived by your feigned confession? No, Daughter, no! I will\par
render you a more essential service. I will rescue you from perdition\par
in spite of yourself; Penance and mortification shall expiate your\par
offence, and Severity force you back to the paths of holiness. What;\par
Ho! Mother St. Agatha!”\par
\par
“Father! By all that is sacred, by all that is most dear to you, I\par
supplicate, I entreat....”\par
\par
“Release me! I will not hear you. Where is the Domina? Mother St.\par
Agatha, where are you?”\par
\par
The door of the Vestry opened, and the Prioress entered the Chapel,\par
followed by her Nuns.\par
\par
“Cruel! Cruel!” exclaimed Agnes, relinquishing her hold.\par
\par
Wild and desperate, She threw herself upon the ground, beating her\par
bosom and rending her veil in all the delirium of despair. The Nuns\par
gazed with astonishment upon the scene before them. The Friar now\par
presented the fatal paper to the Prioress, informed her of the manner\par
in which he had found it, and added, that it was her business to\par
decide, what penance the delinquent merited.\par
\par
While She perused the letter, the Domina’s countenance grew inflamed\par
with passion. What! Such a crime committed in her Convent, and made\par
known to Ambrosio, to the Idol of Madrid, to the Man whom She was most\par
anxious to impress with the opinion of the strictness and regularity of\par
her House! Words were inadequate to express her fury. She was silent,\par
and darted upon the prostrate Nun looks of menace and malignity.\par
\par
“Away with her to the Convent!” said She at length to some of her\par
Attendants.\par
\par
Two of the oldest Nuns now approaching Agnes, raised her forcibly from\par
the ground, and prepared to conduct her from the Chapel.\par
\par
“What!” She exclaimed suddenly shaking off their hold with distracted\par
gestures; “Is all hope then lost? Already do you drag me to punishment?\par
Where are you, Raymond? Oh! save me! save me!”\par
\par
Then casting upon the Abbot a frantic look, “Hear me!” She continued;\par
“Man of an hard heart! Hear me, Proud, Stern, and Cruel! You could have\par
saved me; you could have restored me to happiness and virtue, but would\par
not! You are the destroyer of my Soul; You are my Murderer, and on you\par
fall the curse of my death and my unborn Infant’s! Insolent in your\par
yet-unshaken virtue, you disdained the prayers of a Penitent; But God\par
will show mercy, though you show none. And where is the merit of your\par
boasted virtue? What temptations have you vanquished? Coward! you have\par
fled from it, not opposed seduction. But the day of Trial will arrive!\par
Oh! then when you yield to impetuous passions! when you feel that Man\par
is weak, and born to err; When shuddering you look back upon your\par
crimes, and solicit with terror the mercy of your God, Oh! in that\par
fearful moment think upon me! Think upon your Cruelty! Think upon\par
Agnes, and despair of pardon!”\par
\par
As She uttered these last words, her strength was exhausted, and She\par
sank inanimate upon the bosom of a Nun who stood near her. She was\par
immediately conveyed from the Chapel, and her Companions followed her.\par
\par
Ambrosio had not listened to her reproaches without emotion. A secret\par
pang at his heart made him feel, that He had treated this Unfortunate\par
with too great severity. He therefore detained the Prioress and\par
ventured to pronounce some words in favour of the Delinquent.\par
\par
“The violence of her despair,” said He, “proves, that at least Vice is\par
not become familiar to her. Perhaps by treating her with somewhat less\par
rigour than is generally practised, and mitigating in some degree the\par
accustomed penance....”\par
\par
“Mitigate it, Father?” interrupted the Lady Prioress; “Not I, believe\par
me. The laws of our order are strict and severe; they have fallen into\par
disuse of late, But the crime of Agnes shows me the necessity of their\par
revival. I go to signify my intention to the Convent, and Agnes shall\par
be the first to feel the rigour of those laws, which shall be obeyed to\par
the very letter. Father, Farewell.”\par
\par
Thus saying, She hastened out of the Chapel.\par
\par
“I have done my duty,” said Ambrosio to himself.\par
\par
Still did He not feel perfectly satisfied by this reflection. To\par
dissipate the unpleasant ideas which this scene had excited in him,\par
upon quitting the Chapel He descended into the Abbey Garden.\par
\par
In all Madrid there was no spot more beautiful or better regulated. It\par
was laid out with the most exquisite taste. The choicest flowers\par
adorned it in the height of luxuriance, and though artfully arranged,\par
seemed only planted by the hand of Nature: Fountains, springing from\par
basons of white Marble, cooled the air with perpetual showers; and the\par
Walls were entirely covered by Jessamine, vines, and Honeysuckles. The\par
hour now added to the beauty of the scene. The full Moon, ranging\par
through a blue and cloudless sky, shed upon the trees a trembling\par
lustre, and the waters of the fountains sparkled in the silver beam: A\par
gentle breeze breathed the fragrance of Orange-blossoms along the\par
Alleys; and the Nightingale poured forth her melodious murmur from the\par
shelter of an artificial wilderness. Thither the Abbot bent his steps.\par
\par
In the bosom of this little Grove stood a rustic Grotto, formed in\par
imitation of an Hermitage. The walls were constructed of roots of\par
trees, and the interstices filled up with Moss and Ivy. Seats of Turf\par
were placed on either side, and a natural Cascade fell from the Rock\par
above. Buried in himself the Monk approached the spot. The universal\par
calm had communicated itself to his bosom, and a voluptuous\par
tranquillity spread languor through his soul.\par
\par
He reached the Hermitage, and was entering to repose himself, when He\par
stopped on perceiving it to be already occupied. Extended upon one of\par
the Banks lay a man in a melancholy posture.\par
\par
His head was supported upon his arm, and He seemed lost in mediation.\par
The Monk drew nearer, and recognised Rosario: He watched him in\par
silence, and entered not the Hermitage. After some minutes the Youth\par
raised his eyes, and fixed them mournfully upon the opposite Wall.\par
\par
“Yes!” said He with a deep and plaintive sigh; “I feel all the\par
happiness of thy situation, all the misery of my own! Happy were I,\par
could I think like Thee! Could I look like Thee with disgust upon\par
Mankind, could bury myself for ever in some impenetrable solitude, and\par
forget that the world holds Beings deserving to be loved! Oh God! What\par
a blessing would Misanthropy be to me!”\par
\par
“That is a singular thought, Rosario,” said the Abbot, entering the\par
Grotto.\par
\par
“You here, reverend Father?” cried the Novice.\par
\par
At the same time starting from his place in confusion, He drew his Cowl\par
hastily over his face. Ambrosio seated himself upon the Bank, and\par
obliged the Youth to place himself by him.\par
\par
“You must not indulge this disposition to melancholy,” said He; “What\par
can possibly have made you view in so desirable a light, Misanthropy,\par
of all sentiments the most hateful?”\par
\par
“The perusal of these Verses, Father, which till now had escaped my\par
observation. The Brightness of the Moonbeams permitted my reading them;\par
and Oh! how I envy the feelings of the Writer!”\par
\par
As He said this, He pointed to a marble Tablet fixed against the\par
opposite Wall: On it were engraved the following lines.\par
\par
INSCRIPTION IN AN HERMITAGE\par
\par
\par
Whoe’er Thou art these lines now reading,\par
Think not, though from the world receding\par
I joy my lonely days to lead in\par
        This Desart drear,\par
That with remorse a conscience bleeding\par
        Hath led me here.\par
\par
No thought of guilt my bosom sowrs:\par
Free-willed I fled from courtly bowers;\par
For well I saw in Halls and Towers\par
        That Lust and Pride,\par
The Arch-Fiend’s dearest darkest Powers,\par
        In state preside.\par
\par
I saw Mankind with vice incrusted;\par
I saw that Honour’s sword was rusted;\par
That few for aught but folly lusted;\par
That He was still deceiv’d, who trusted\par
        In Love or Friend;\par
And hither came with Men disgusted\par
        My life to end.\par
\par
In this lone Cave, in garments lowly,\par
Alike a Foe to noisy folly,\par
And brow-bent gloomy melancholy\par
        I wear away\par
My life, and in my office holy\par
        Consume the day.\par
\par
Content and comfort bless me more in\par
This Grot, than e’er I felt before in\par
A Palace, and with thoughts still soaring\par
        To God on high,\par
Each night and morn with voice imploring\par
        This wish I sigh.\par
\par
“Let me, Oh! Lord! from life retire,\par
Unknown each guilty worldly fire,\par
Remorseful throb, or loose desire;\par
        And when I die,\par
Let me in this belief expire,\par
        ‘To God I fly’!”\par
\par
Stranger, if full of youth and riot\par
As yet no grief has marred thy quiet,\par
Thou haply throw’st a scornful eye at\par
        The Hermit’s prayer:\par
But if Thou hast a cause to sigh at\par
        Thy fault, or care;\par
\par
If Thou hast known false Love’s vexation,\par
Or hast been exil’d from thy Nation,\par
Or guilt affrights thy contemplation,\par
        And makes thee pine,\par
Oh! how must Thou lament thy station,\par
        And envy mine!\par
\par
\par
“Were it possible” said the Friar, “for Man to be so totally wrapped up\par
in himself as to live in absolute seclusion from human nature, and\par
could yet feel the contented tranquillity which these lines express, I\par
allow that the situation would be more desirable, than to live in a\par
world so pregnant with every vice and every folly. But this never can\par
be the case. This inscription was merely placed here for the ornament\par
of the Grotto, and the sentiments and the Hermit are equally imaginary.\par
Man was born for society. However little He may be attached to the\par
World, He never can wholly forget it, or bear to be wholly forgotten by\par
it. Disgusted at the guilt or absurdity of Mankind, the Misanthrope\par
flies from it: He resolves to become an Hermit, and buries himself in\par
the Cavern of some gloomy Rock. While Hate inflames his bosom, possibly\par
He may feel contented with his situation: But when his passions begin\par
to cool; when Time has mellowed his sorrows, and healed those wounds\par
which He bore with him to his solitude, think you that Content becomes\par
his Companion? Ah! no, Rosario. No longer sustained by the violence of\par
his passions, He feels all the monotony of his way of living, and his\par
heart becomes the prey of Ennui and weariness. He looks round, and\par
finds himself alone in the Universe: The love of society revives in his\par
bosom, and He pants to return to that world which He has abandoned.\par
Nature loses all her charms in his eyes: No one is near him to point\par
out her beauties, or share in his admiration of her excellence and\par
variety. Propped upon the fragment of some Rock, He gazes upon the\par
tumbling waterfall with a vacant eye, He views without emotion the\par
glory of the setting Sun. Slowly He returns to his Cell at Evening, for\par
no one there is anxious for his arrival; He has no comfort in his\par
solitary unsavoury meal: He throws himself upon his couch of Moss\par
despondent and dissatisfied, and wakes only to pass a day as joyless,\par
as monotonous as the former.”\par
\par
“You amaze me, Father! Suppose that circumstances condemned you to\par
solitude; Would not the duties of Religion and the consciousness of a\par
life well spent communicate to your heart that calm which....”\par
\par
“I should deceive myself, did I fancy that they could. I am convinced\par
of the contrary, and that all my fortitude would not prevent me from\par
yielding to melancholy and disgust. After consuming the day in study,\par
if you knew my pleasure at meeting my Brethren in the Evening! After\par
passing many a long hour in solitude, if I could express to you the joy\par
which I feel at once more beholding a fellow-Creature! ’Tis in this\par
particular that I place the principal merit of a Monastic Institution.\par
It secludes Man from the temptations of Vice; It procures that leisure\par
necessary for the proper service of the Supreme; It spares him the\par
mortification of witnessing the crimes of the worldly, and yet permits\par
him to enjoy the blessings of society. And do you, Rosario, do _you_\par
envy an Hermit’s life? Can you be thus blind to the happiness of your\par
situation? Reflect upon it for a moment. This Abbey is become your\par
Asylum: Your regularity, your gentleness, your talents have rendered\par
you the object of universal esteem: You are secluded from the world\par
which you profess to hate; yet you remain in possession of the benefits\par
of society, and that a society composed of the most estimable of\par
Mankind.”\par
\par
“Father! Father! ’tis that which causes my Torment! Happy had it been\par
for me, had my life been passed among the vicious and abandoned! Had I\par
never heard pronounced the name of Virtue! ’Tis my unbounded adoration\par
of religion; ’Tis my soul’s exquisite sensibility of the beauty of fair\par
and good, that loads me with shame! that hurries me to perdition! Oh!\par
that I had never seen these Abbey walls!”\par
\par
“How, Rosario? When we last conversed, you spoke in a different tone.\par
Is my friendship then become of such little consequence? Had you never\par
seen these Abbey walls, you never had seen me: Can that really be your\par
wish?”\par
\par
“Had never seen you?” repeated the Novice, starting from the Bank, and\par
grasping the Friar’s hand with a frantic air; “You? You? Would to God,\par
that lightning had blasted them, before you ever met my eyes! Would to\par
God! that I were never to see you more, and could forget that I had\par
ever seen you!”\par
\par
With these words He flew hastily from the Grotto. Ambrosio remained in\par
his former attitude, reflecting on the Youth’s unaccountable behaviour.\par
He was inclined to suspect the derangement of his senses: yet the\par
general tenor of his conduct, the connexion of his ideas, and calmness\par
of his demeanour till the moment of his quitting the Grotto, seemed to\par
discountenance this conjecture. After a few minutes Rosario returned.\par
He again seated himself upon the Bank: He reclined his cheek upon one\par
hand, and with the other wiped away the tears which trickled from his\par
eyes at intervals.\par
\par
The Monk looked upon him with compassion, and forbore to interrupt his\par
meditations. Both observed for some time a profound silence. The\par
Nightingale had now taken her station upon an Orange Tree fronting the\par
Hermitage, and poured forth a strain the most melancholy and melodious.\par
Rosario raised his head, and listened to her with attention.\par
\par
“It was thus,” said He, with a deep-drawn sigh; “It was thus, that\par
during the last month of her unhappy life, my Sister used to sit\par
listening to the Nightingale. Poor Matilda! She sleeps in the Grave,\par
and her broken heart throbs no more with passion.”\par
\par
“You had a Sister?”\par
\par
“You say right, that I HAD; Alas! I have one no longer. She sunk\par
beneath the weight of her sorrows in the very spring of life.”\par
\par
“What were those sorrows?”\par
\par
“They will not excite _your_ pity: _you_ know not the power of those\par
irresistible, those fatal sentiments, to which her Heart was a prey.\par
Father, She loved unfortunately. A passion for One endowed with every\par
virtue, for a Man, Oh! rather let me say, for a divinity, proved the\par
bane of her existence. His noble form, his spotless character, his\par
various talents, his wisdom solid, wonderful, and glorious, might have\par
warmed the bosom of the most insensible. My Sister saw him, and dared\par
to love though She never dared to hope.”\par
\par
“If her love was so well bestowed, what forbad her to hope the\par
obtaining of its object?”\par
\par
“Father, before He knew her, Julian had already plighted his vows to a\par
Bride most fair, most heavenly! Yet still my Sister loved, and for the\par
Husband’s sake She doted upon the Wife. One morning She found means to\par
escape from our Father’s House: Arrayed in humble weeds She offered\par
herself as a Domestic to the Consort of her Beloved, and was accepted.\par
She was now continually in his presence: She strove to ingratiate\par
herself into his favour: She succeeded. Her attentions attracted\par
Julian’s notice; The virtuous are ever grateful, and He distinguished\par
Matilda above the rest of her Companions.”\par
\par
“And did not your Parents seek for her? Did they submit tamely to their\par
loss, nor attempt to recover their wandering Daughter?”\par
\par
“Ere they could find her, She discovered herself. Her love grew too\par
violent for concealment; Yet She wished not for Julian’s person, She\par
ambitioned but a share of his heart. In an unguarded moment She\par
confessed her affection. What was the return? Doating upon his Wife,\par
and believing that a look of pity bestowed upon another was a theft\par
from what He owed to her, He drove Matilda from his presence. He forbad\par
her ever again appearing before him. His severity broke her heart: She\par
returned to her Father’s, and in a few Months after was carried to her\par
Grave.”\par
\par
“Unhappy Girl! Surely her fate was too severe, and Julian was too\par
cruel.”\par
\par
“Do you think so, Father?” cried the Novice with vivacity; “Do you\par
think that He was cruel?”\par
\par
“Doubtless I do, and pity her most sincerely.”\par
\par
“You pity her? You pity her? Oh! Father! Father! Then pity me!”\par
\par
The Friar started; when after a moment’s pause Rosario added with a\par
faltering voice,—“for my sufferings are still greater. My Sister had a\par
Friend, a real Friend, who pitied the acuteness of her feelings, nor\par
reproached her with her inability to repress them. I ...! I have no\par
Friend! The whole wide world cannot furnish an heart that is willing to\par
participate in the sorrows of mine!”\par
\par
As He uttered these words, He sobbed audibly. The Friar was affected.\par
He took Rosario’s hand, and pressed it with tenderness.\par
\par
“You have no Friend, say you? What then am I? Why will you not confide\par
in me, and what can you fear? My severity? Have I ever used it with\par
you? The dignity of my habit? Rosario, I lay aside the Monk, and bid\par
you consider me as no other than your Friend, your Father. Well may I\par
assume that title, for never did Parent watch over a Child more fondly\par
than I have watched over you. From the moment in which I first beheld\par
you, I perceived sensations in my bosom till then unknown to me; I\par
found a delight in your society which no one’s else could afford; and\par
when I witnessed the extent of your genius and information, I rejoiced\par
as does a Father in the perfections of his Son. Then lay aside your\par
fears; Speak to me with openness: Speak to me, Rosario, and say that\par
you will confide in me. If my aid or my pity can alleviate your\par
distress....”\par
\par
“Yours can! Yours only can! Ah! Father, how willingly would I unveil to\par
you my heart! How willingly would I declare the secret which bows me\par
down with its weight! But Oh! I fear! I fear!”\par
\par
“What, my Son?”\par
\par
“That you should abhor me for my weakness; That the reward of my\par
confidence should be the loss of your esteem.”\par
\par
“How shall I reassure you? Reflect upon the whole of my past conduct,\par
upon the paternal tenderness which I have ever shown you. Abhor you,\par
Rosario? It is no longer in my power. To give up your society would be\par
to deprive myself of the greatest pleasure of my life. Then reveal to\par
me what afflicts you, and believe me while I solemnly swear....”\par
\par
“Hold!” interrupted the Novice; “Swear, that whatever be my secret, you\par
will not oblige me to quit the Monastery till my Noviciate shall\par
expire.”\par
\par
“I promise it faithfully, and as I keep my vows to you, may Christ keep\par
his to Mankind. Now then explain this mystery, and rely upon my\par
indulgence.”\par
\par
“I obey you. Know then.... Oh! how I tremble to name the word! Listen\par
to me with pity, revered Ambrosio! Call up every latent spark of human\par
weakness that may teach you compassion for mine! Father!” continued He\par
throwing himself at the Friar’s feet, and pressing his hand to his lips\par
with eagerness, while agitation for a moment choaked his voice;\par
“Father!” continued He in faltering accents, “I am a Woman!”\par
\par
The Abbot started at this unexpected avowal. Prostrate on the ground\par
lay the feigned Rosario, as if waiting in silence the decision of his\par
Judge. Astonishment on the one part, apprehension on the other, for\par
some minutes chained them in the same attitudes, as had they been\par
touched by the Rod of some Magician. At length recovering from his\par
confusion, the Monk quitted the Grotto, and sped with precipitation\par
towards the Abbey. His action did not escape the Suppliant. She sprang\par
from the ground; She hastened to follow him, overtook him, threw\par
herself in his passage, and embraced his knees. Ambrosio strove in vain\par
to disengage himself from her grasp.\par
\par
“Do not fly me!” She cried; “Leave me not abandoned to the impulse of\par
despair! Listen, while I excuse my imprudence; while I acknowledge my\par
Sister’s story to be my own! I am Matilda; You are her Beloved.”\par
\par
If Ambrosio’s surprise was great at her first avowal, upon hearing her\par
second it exceeded all bounds. Amazed, embarrassed, and irresolute He\par
found himself incapable of pronouncing a syllable, and remained in\par
silence gazing upon Matilda: This gave her opportunity to continue her\par
explanation as follows.\par
\par
“Think not, Ambrosio, that I come to rob your Bride of your affections.\par
No, believe me: Religion alone deserves you; and far is it from\par
Matilda’s wish to draw you from the paths of virtue. What I feel for\par
you is love, not licentiousness; I sigh to be possessor of your heart,\par
not lust for the enjoyment of your person. Deign to listen to my\par
vindication: A few moments will convince you that this holy retreat is\par
not polluted by my presence, and that you may grant me your compassion\par
without trespassing against your vows.”—She seated herself: Ambrosio,\par
scarcely conscious of what He did, followed her example, and She\par
proceeded in her discourse.\par
\par
“I spring from a distinguished family: My Father was Chief of the noble\par
House of Villanegas. He died while I was still an Infant, and left me\par
sole Heiress of his immense possessions. Young and wealthy, I was\par
sought in marriage by the noblest Youths of Madrid; But no one\par
succeeded in gaining my affections. I had been brought up under the\par
care of an Uncle possessed of the most solid judgment and extensive\par
erudition. He took pleasure in communicating to me some portion of his\par
knowledge. Under his instructions my understanding acquired more\par
strength and justness than generally falls to the lot of my sex: The\par
ability of my Preceptor being aided by natural curiosity, I not only\par
made a considerable progress in sciences universally studied, but in\par
others, revealed but to few, and lying under censure from the blindness\par
of superstition. But while my Guardian laboured to enlarge the sphere\par
of my knowledge, He carefully inculcated every moral precept: He\par
relieved me from the shackles of vulgar prejudice; He pointed out the\par
beauty of Religion; He taught me to look with adoration upon the pure\par
and virtuous, and, woe is me! I have obeyed him but too well!\par
\par
“With such dispositions, Judge whether I could observe with any other\par
sentiment than disgust the vice, dissipation, and ignorance, which\par
disgrace our Spanish Youth. I rejected every offer with disdain. My\par
heart remained without a Master till chance conducted me to the\par
Cathedral of the Capuchins. Oh! surely on that day my Guardian Angel\par
slumbered neglectful of his charge! Then was it that I first beheld\par
you: You supplied the Superior’s place, absent from illness. You cannot\par
but remember the lively enthusiasm which your discourse created. Oh!\par
how I drank your words! How your eloquence seemed to steal me from\par
myself! I scarcely dared to breathe, fearing to lose a syllable; and\par
while you spoke, Methought a radiant glory beamed round your head, and\par
your countenance shone with the majesty of a God. I retired from the\par
Church, glowing with admiration. From that moment you became the idol\par
of my heart, the never-changing object of my Meditations. I enquired\par
respecting you. The reports which were made me of your mode of life, of\par
your knowledge, piety, and self-denial riveted the chains imposed on me\par
by your eloquence. I was conscious that there was no longer a void in\par
my heart; That I had found the Man whom I had sought till then in vain.\par
In expectation of hearing you again, every day I visited your\par
Cathedral: You remained secluded within the Abbey walls, and I always\par
withdrew, wretched and disappointed. The Night was more propitious to\par
me, for then you stood before me in my dreams; You vowed to me eternal\par
friendship; You led me through the paths of virtue, and assisted me to\par
support the vexations of life. The Morning dispelled these pleasing\par
visions; I woke, and found myself separated from you by Barriers which\par
appeared insurmountable. Time seemed only to increase the strength of\par
my passion: I grew melancholy and despondent; I fled from society, and\par
my health declined daily. At length no longer able to exist in this\par
state of torture, I resolved to assume the disguise in which you see\par
me. My artifice was fortunate: I was received into the Monastery, and\par
succeeded in gaining your esteem.\par
\par
“Now then I should have felt compleatly happy, had not my quiet been\par
disturbed by the fear of detection. The pleasure which I received from\par
your society, was embittered by the idea that perhaps I should soon be\par
deprived of it: and my heart throbbed so rapturously at obtaining the\par
marks of your friendship, as to convince me that I never should survive\par
its loss. I resolved, therefore, not to leave the discovery of my sex\par
to chance, to confess the whole to you, and throw myself entirely on\par
your mercy and indulgence. Ah! Ambrosio, can I have been deceived? Can\par
you be less generous than I thought you? I will not suspect it. You\par
will not drive a Wretch to despair; I shall still be permitted to see\par
you, to converse with you, to adore you! Your virtues shall be my\par
example through life; and when we expire, our bodies shall rest in the\par
same Grave.”\par
\par
She ceased. While She spoke, a thousand opposing sentiments combated in\par
Ambrosio’s bosom. Surprise at the singularity of this adventure,\par
Confusion at her abrupt declaration, Resentment at her boldness in\par
entering the Monastery, and Consciousness of the austerity with which\par
it behoved him to reply, such were the sentiments of which He was\par
aware; But there were others also which did not obtain his notice. He\par
perceived not, that his vanity was flattered by the praises bestowed\par
upon his eloquence and virtue; that He felt a secret pleasure in\par
reflecting that a young and seemingly lovely Woman had for his sake\par
abandoned the world, and sacrificed every other passion to that which\par
He had inspired: Still less did He perceive that his heart throbbed\par
with desire, while his hand was pressed gently by Matilda’s ivory\par
fingers.\par
\par
By degrees He recovered from his confusion. His ideas became less\par
bewildered: He was immediately sensible of the extreme impropriety,\par
should Matilda be permitted to remain in the Abbey after this avowal of\par
her sex. He assumed an air of severity, and drew away his hand.\par
\par
“How, Lady!” said He; “Can you really hope for my permission to remain\par
amongst us? Even were I to grant your request, what good could you\par
derive from it? Think you that I ever can reply to an affection,\par
which...”\par
\par
“No, Father, No! I expect not to inspire you with a love like mine. I\par
only wish for the liberty to be near you, to pass some hours of the day\par
in your society; to obtain your compassion, your friendship and esteem.\par
Surely my request is not unreasonable.”\par
\par
“But reflect, Lady! Reflect only for a moment on the impropriety of my\par
harbouring a Woman in the Abbey; and that too a Woman, who confesses\par
that She loves me. It must not be. The risque of your being discovered\par
is too great, and I will not expose myself to so dangerous a\par
temptation.”\par
\par
“Temptation, say you? Forget that I am a Woman, and it no longer\par
exists: Consider me only as a Friend, as an Unfortunate, whose\par
happiness, whose life depends upon your protection. Fear not lest I\par
should ever call to your remembrance that love the most impetuous, the\par
most unbounded, has induced me to disguise my sex; or that instigated\par
by desires, offensive to _your_ vows and my own honour, I should\par
endeavour to seduce you from the path of rectitude. No, Ambrosio, learn\par
to know me better. I love you for your virtues: Lose them, and with\par
them you lose my affections. I look upon you as a Saint; Prove to me\par
that you are no more than Man, and I quit you with disgust. Is it then\par
from me that you fear temptation? From me, in whom the world’s dazzling\par
pleasures created no other sentiment than contempt? From me, whose\par
attachment is grounded on your exemption from human frailty? Oh!\par
dismiss such injurious apprehensions! Think nobler of me, think nobler\par
of yourself. I am incapable of seducing you to error; and surely your\par
Virtue is established on a basis too firm to be shaken by unwarranted\par
desires. Ambrosio, dearest Ambrosio! drive me not from your presence;\par
Remember your promise, and authorize my stay!”\par
\par
“Impossible, Matilda; _your_ interest commands me to refuse your\par
prayer, since I tremble for you, not for myself. After vanquishing the\par
impetuous ebullitions of Youth; After passing thirty years in\par
mortification and penance, I might safely permit your stay, nor fear\par
your inspiring me with warmer sentiments than pity. But to yourself,\par
remaining in the Abbey can produce none but fatal consequences. You\par
will misconstrue my every word and action; You will seize every\par
circumstance with avidity, which encourages you to hope the return of\par
your affection; Insensibly your passions will gain a superiority over\par
your reason; and far from these being repressed by my presence, every\par
moment which we pass together, will only serve to irritate and excite\par
them. Believe me, unhappy Woman! you possess my sincere compassion. I\par
am convinced that you have hitherto acted upon the purest motives; But\par
though you are blind to the imprudence of your conduct, in me it would\par
be culpable not to open your eyes. I feel that Duty obliges my treating\par
you with harshness: I must reject your prayer, and remove every shadow\par
of hope which may aid to nourish sentiments so pernicious to your\par
repose. Matilda, you must from hence tomorrow.”\par
\par
“Tomorrow, Ambrosio? Tomorrow? Oh! surely you cannot mean it!\par
\par
You cannot resolve on driving me to despair! You cannot have the\par
cruelty....”\par
\par
“You have heard my decision, and it must be obeyed. The Laws of our\par
Order forbid your stay: It would be perjury to conceal that a Woman is\par
within these Walls, and my vows will oblige me to declare your story to\par
the Community. You must from hence!—I pity you, but can do no more!”\par
\par
He pronounced these words in a faint and trembling voice: Then rising\par
from his seat, He would have hastened towards the Monastery. Uttering a\par
loud shriek, Matilda followed, and detained him.\par
\par
“Stay yet one moment, Ambrosio! Hear me yet speak one word!”\par
\par
“I dare not listen! Release me! You know my resolution!”\par
\par
“But one word! But one last word, and I have done!”\par
\par
“Leave me! Your entreaties are in vain! You must from hence tomorrow!”\par
\par
“Go then, Barbarian! But this resource is still left me.”\par
\par
As She said this, She suddenly drew a poignard: She rent open her\par
garment, and placed the weapon’s point against her bosom.\par
\par
“Father, I will never quit these Walls alive!”\par
\par
“Hold! Hold, Matilda! What would you do?”\par
\par
“You are determined, so am I: The Moment that you leave me, I plunge\par
this Steel in my heart.”\par
\par
“Holy St. Francis! Matilda, have you your senses? Do you know the\par
consequences of your action? That Suicide is the greatest of crimes?\par
That you destroy your Soul? That you lose your claim to salvation? That\par
you prepare for yourself everlasting torments?”\par
\par
“I care not! I care not!” She replied passionately; “Either your hand\par
guides me to Paradise, or my own dooms me to perdition! Speak to me,\par
Ambrosio! Tell me that you will conceal my story, that I shall remain\par
your Friend and your Companion, or this poignard drinks my blood!”\par
\par
As She uttered these last words, She lifted her arm, and made a motion\par
as if to stab herself. The Friar’s eyes followed with dread the course\par
of the dagger. She had torn open her habit, and her bosom was half\par
exposed. The weapon’s point rested upon her left breast: And Oh! that\par
was such a breast! The Moonbeams darting full upon it enabled the Monk\par
to observe its dazzling whiteness. His eye dwelt with insatiable\par
avidity upon the beauteous Orb. A sensation till then unknown filled\par
his heart with a mixture of anxiety and delight: A raging fire shot\par
through every limb; The blood boiled in his veins, and a thousand wild\par
wishes bewildered his imagination.\par
\par
“Hold!” He cried in an hurried faultering voice; “I can resist no\par
longer! Stay, then, Enchantress; Stay for my destruction!”\par
\par
He said, and rushing from the place, hastened towards the Monastery: He\par
regained his Cell and threw himself upon his Couch, distracted\par
irresolute and confused.\par
\par
He found it impossible for some time to arrange his ideas. The scene in\par
which He had been engaged had excited such a variety of sentiments in\par
his bosom, that He was incapable of deciding which was predominant. He\par
was irresolute what conduct He ought to hold with the disturber of his\par
repose. He was conscious that prudence, religion, and propriety\par
necessitated his obliging her to quit the Abbey: But on the other hand\par
such powerful reasons authorized her stay that He was but too much\par
inclined to consent to her remaining. He could not avoid being\par
flattered by Matilda’s declaration, and at reflecting that He had\par
unconsciously vanquished an heart which had resisted the attacks of\par
Spain’s noblest Cavaliers: The manner in which He had gained her\par
affections was also the most satisfactory to his vanity: He remembered\par
the many happy hours which He had passed in Rosario’s society, and\par
dreaded that void in his heart which parting with him would occasion.\par
Besides all this, He considered, that as Matilda was wealthy, her\par
favour might be of essential benefit to the Abbey.\par
\par
“And what do I risque,” said He to himself, “by authorizing her stay?\par
May I not safely credit her assertions? Will it not be easy for me to\par
forget her sex, and still consider her as my Friend and my disciple?\par
Surely her love is as pure as She describes. Had it been the offspring\par
of mere licentiousness, would She so long have concealed it in her own\par
bosom? Would She not have employed some means to procure its\par
gratification? She has done quite the contrary: She strove to keep me\par
in ignorance of her sex; and nothing but the fear of detection, and my\par
instances, would have compelled her to reveal the secret. She has\par
observed the duties of religion not less strictly than myself. She has\par
made no attempts to rouze my slumbering passions, nor has She ever\par
conversed with me till this night on the subject of Love. Had She been\par
desirous to gain my affections, not my esteem, She would not have\par
concealed from me her charms so carefully: At this very moment I have\par
never seen her face: Yet certainly that face must be lovely, and her\par
person beautiful, to judge by her ... by what I have seen.”\par
\par
As this last idea passed through his imagination, a blush spread itself\par
over his cheek. Alarmed at the sentiments which He was indulging, He\par
betook himself to prayer; He started from his Couch, knelt before the\par
beautiful Madona, and entreated her assistance in stifling such\par
culpable emotions. He then returned to his Bed, and resigned himself to\par
slumber.\par
\par
He awoke, heated and unrefreshed. During his sleep his inflamed\par
imagination had presented him with none but the most voluptuous\par
objects. Matilda stood before him in his dreams, and his eyes again\par
dwelt upon her naked breast. She repeated her protestations of eternal\par
love, threw her arms round his neck, and loaded him with kisses: He\par
returned them; He clasped her passionately to his bosom, and ... the\par
vision was dissolved. Sometimes his dreams presented the image of his\par
favourite Madona, and He fancied that He was kneeling before her: As He\par
offered up his vows to her, the eyes of the Figure seemed to beam on\par
him with inexpressible sweetness. He pressed his lips to hers, and\par
found them warm: The animated form started from the Canvas, embraced\par
him affectionately, and his senses were unable to support delight so\par
exquisite. Such were the scenes, on which his thoughts were employed\par
while sleeping: His unsatisfied Desires placed before him the most\par
lustful and provoking Images, and he rioted in joys till then unknown\par
to him.\par
\par
He started from his Couch, filled with confusion at the remembrance of\par
his dreams. Scarcely was He less ashamed, when He reflected on his\par
reasons of the former night which induced him to authorize Matilda’s\par
stay. The cloud was now dissipated which had obscured his judgment: He\par
shuddered when He beheld his arguments blazoned in their proper\par
colours, and found that He had been a slave to flattery, to avarice,\par
and self-love. If in one hour’s conversation Matilda had produced a\par
change so remarkable in his sentiments, what had He not to dread from\par
her remaining in the Abbey? Become sensible of his danger, awakened\par
from his dream of confidence, He resolved to insist on her departing\par
without delay. He began to feel that He was not proof against\par
temptation; and that however Matilda might restrain herself within the\par
bounds of modesty, He was unable to contend with those passions, from\par
which He falsely thought himself exempted.\par
\par
“Agnes! Agnes!” He exclaimed, while reflecting on his embarrassments,\par
“I already feel thy curse!”\par
\par
He quitted his Cell, determined upon dismissing the feigned Rosario. He\par
appeared at Matins; But his thoughts were absent, and He paid them but\par
little attention. His heart and brain were both of them filled with\par
worldly objects, and He prayed without devotion. The service over, He\par
descended into the Garden. He bent his steps towards the same spot\par
where, on the preceding night, He had made this embarrassing discovery.\par
He doubted not but that Matilda would seek him there: He was not\par
deceived. She soon entered the Hermitage, and approached the Monk with\par
a timid air. After a few minutes during which both were silent, She\par
appeared as if on the point of speaking; But the Abbot, who during this\par
time had been summoning up all his resolution, hastily interrupted her.\par
Though still unconscious how extensive was its influence, He dreaded\par
the melodious seduction of her voice.\par
\par
“Seat yourself by my side, Matilda,” said He, assuming a look of\par
firmness, though carefully avoiding the least mixture of severity;\par
“Listen to me patiently, and believe, that in what I shall say, I am\par
not more influenced by my own interest than by yours: Believe, that I\par
feel for you the warmest friendship, the truest compassion, and that\par
you cannot feel more grieved than I do, when I declare to you that we\par
must never meet again.”\par
\par
“Ambrosio!” She cried, in a voice at once expressive of surprise and\par
sorrow.\par
\par
“Be calm, my Friend! My Rosario! Still let me call you by that name so\par
dear to me! Our separation is unavoidable; I blush to own, how sensibly\par
it affects me.— But yet it must be so. I feel myself incapable of\par
treating you with indifference, and that very conviction obliges me to\par
insist upon your departure. Matilda, you must stay here no longer.”\par
\par
“Oh! where shall I now seek for probity? Disgusted with a perfidious\par
world, in what happy region does Truth conceal herself? Father, I hoped\par
that She resided here; I thought that your bosom had been her favourite\par
shrine. And you too prove false? Oh God! And you too can betray me?”\par
\par
“Matilda!”\par
\par
“Yes, Father, Yes! ’Tis with justice that I reproach you. Oh! where are\par
your promises? My Noviciate is not expired, and yet will you compell me\par
to quit the Monastery? Can you have the heart to drive me from you? And\par
have I not received your solemn oath to the contrary?”\par
\par
“I will not compell you to quit the Monastery: You have received my\par
solemn oath to the contrary. But yet when I throw myself upon your\par
generosity, when I declare to you the embarrassments in which your\par
presence involves me, will you not release me from that oath? Reflect\par
upon the danger of a discovery, upon the opprobrium in which such an\par
event would plunge me: Reflect that my honour and reputation are at\par
stake, and that my peace of mind depends on your compliance. As yet my\par
heart is free; I shall separate from you with regret, but not with\par
despair. Stay here, and a few weeks will sacrifice my happiness on the\par
altar of your charms. You are but too interesting, too amiable! I\par
should love you, I should doat on you! My bosom would become the prey\par
of desires which Honour and my profession forbid me to gratify. If I\par
resisted them, the impetuosity of my wishes unsatisfied would drive me\par
to madness: If I yielded to the temptation, I should sacrifice to one\par
moment of guilty pleasure my reputation in this world, my salvation in\par
the next. To you then I fly for defence against myself. Preserve me\par
from losing the reward of thirty years of sufferings! Preserve me from\par
becoming the Victim of Remorse! _your_ heart has already felt the\par
anguish of hopeless love; Oh! then if you really value me, spare mine\par
that anguish! Give me back my promise; Fly from these walls. Go, and\par
you bear with you my warmest prayers for your happiness, my friendship,\par
my esteem and admiration: Stay, and you become to me the source of\par
danger, of sufferings, of despair! Answer me, Matilda; What is your\par
resolve?”—She was silent—“Will you not speak, Matilda? Will you not\par
name your choice?”\par
\par
“Cruel! Cruel!” She exclaimed, wringing her hands in agony; “You know\par
too well that you offer me no choice! You know too well that I can have\par
no will but yours!”\par
\par
“I was not then deceived! Matilda’s generosity equals my expectations.”\par
\par
“Yes; I will prove the truth of my affection by submitting to a decree\par
which cuts me to the very heart. Take back your promise. I will quit\par
the Monastery this very day. I have a Relation, Abbess of a Covent in\par
Estramadura: To her will I bend my steps, and shut myself from the\par
world for ever. Yet tell me, Father, shall I bear your good wishes with\par
me to my solitude? Will you sometimes abstract your attention from\par
heavenly objects to bestow a thought upon me?”\par
\par
“Ah! Matilda, I fear that I shall think on you but too often for my\par
repose!”\par
\par
“Then I have nothing more to wish for, save that we may meet in heaven.\par
Farewell, my Friend! my Ambrosio!— And yet methinks, I would fain bear\par
with me some token of your regard!”\par
\par
“What shall I give you?”\par
\par
“Something.—Any thing.—One of those flowers will be sufficient.” (Here\par
She pointed to a bush of Roses, planted at the door of the Grotto.) “I\par
will hide it in my bosom, and when I am dead, the Nuns shall find it\par
withered upon my heart.”\par
\par
The Friar was unable to reply: With slow steps, and a soul heavy with\par
affliction, He quitted the Hermitage. He approached the Bush, and\par
stooped to pluck one of the Roses. Suddenly He uttered a piercing cry,\par
started back hastily, and let the flower, which He already held, fall\par
from his hand. Matilda heard the shriek, and flew anxiously towards\par
him.\par
\par
“What is the matter?” She cried; “Answer me, for God’s sake! What has\par
happened?”\par
\par
“I have received my death!” He replied in a faint voice; “Concealed\par
among the Roses ... A Serpent....”\par
\par
Here the pain of his wound became so exquisite, that Nature was unable\par
to bear it: His senses abandoned him, and He sank inanimate into\par
Matilda’s arms.\par
\par
Her distress was beyond the power of description. She rent her hair,\par
beat her bosom, and not daring to quit Ambrosio, endeavoured by loud\par
cries to summon the Monks to her assistance. She at length succeeded.\par
Alarmed by her shrieks, Several of the Brothers hastened to the spot,\par
and the Superior was conveyed back to the Abbey. He was immediately put\par
to bed, and the Monk who officiated as Surgeon to the Fraternity\par
prepared to examine the wound. By this time Ambrosio’s hand had swelled\par
to an extraordinary size; The remedies which had been administered to\par
him, ’tis true, restored him to life, but not to his senses; He raved\par
in all the horrors of delirium, foamed at the mouth, and four of the\par
strongest Monks were scarcely able to hold him in his bed.\par
\par
Father Pablos, such was the Surgeon’s name, hastened to examine the\par
wounded hand. The Monks surrounded the Bed, anxiously waiting for the\par
decision: Among these the feigned Rosario appeared not the most\par
insensible to the Friar’s calamity. He gazed upon the Sufferer with\par
inexpressible anguish; and the groans which every moment escaped from\par
his bosom sufficiently betrayed the violence of his affliction.\par
\par
Father Pablos probed the wound. As He drew out his Lancet, its point\par
was tinged with a greenish hue. He shook his head mournfully, and\par
quitted the bedside.\par
\par
“’Tis as I feared!” said He; “There is no hope.”\par
\par
“No hope?” exclaimed the Monks with one voice; “Say you, no hope?”\par
\par
“From the sudden effects, I suspected that the Abbot was stung by a\par
cientipedoro:[1] The venom which you see upon my Lancet confirms my\par
idea: He cannot live three days.”\par
\par
    [1] The cientipedoro is supposed to be a native of Cuba, and to\par
    have been brought into Spain from that island in the vessel of\par
    Columbus.\par
\par
“And can no possible remedy be found?” enquired Rosario.\par
\par
“Without extracting the poison, He cannot recover; and how to extract\par
it is to me still a secret. All that I can do is to apply such herbs to\par
the wound as will relieve the anguish: The Patient will be restored to\par
his senses; But the venom will corrupt the whole mass of his blood, and\par
in three days He will exist no longer.”\par
\par
Excessive was the universal grief at hearing this decision. Pablos, as\par
He had promised, dressed the wound, and then retired, followed by his\par
Companions: Rosario alone remained in the Cell, the Abbot at his urgent\par
entreaty having been committed to his care. Ambrosio’s strength worn\par
out by the violence of his exertions, He had by this time fallen into a\par
profound sleep. So totally was He overcome by weariness, that He\par
scarcely gave any signs of life; He was still in this situation, when\par
the Monks returned to enquire whether any change had taken place.\par
Pablos loosened the bandage which concealed the wound, more from a\par
principle of curiosity than from indulging the hope of discovering any\par
favourable symptoms. What was his astonishment at finding, that the\par
inflammation had totally subsided! He probed the hand; His Lancet came\par
out pure and unsullied; No traces of the venom were perceptible; and\par
had not the orifice still been visible, Pablos might have doubted that\par
there had ever been a wound.\par
\par
He communicated this intelligence to his Brethren; their delight was\par
only equalled by their surprize. From the latter sentiment, however,\par
they were soon released by explaining the circumstance according to\par
their own ideas: They were perfectly convinced that their Superior was\par
a Saint, and thought, that nothing could be more natural than for St.\par
Francis to have operated a miracle in his favour. This opinion was\par
adopted unanimously: They declared it so loudly, and vociferated,—“A\par
miracle! a miracle!”—with such fervour, that they soon interrupted\par
Ambrosio’s slumbers.\par
\par
The Monks immediately crowded round his Bed, and expressed their\par
satisfaction at his wonderful recovery. He was perfectly in his senses,\par
and free from every complaint except feeling weak and languid. Pablos\par
gave him a strengthening medicine, and advised his keeping his bed for\par
the two succeeding days: He then retired, having desired his Patient\par
not to exhaust himself by conversation, but rather to endeavour at\par
taking some repose. The other Monks followed his example, and the Abbot\par
and Rosario were left without Observers.\par
\par
For some minutes Ambrosio regarded his Attendant with a look of mingled\par
pleasure and apprehension. She was seated upon the side of the Bed, her\par
head bending down, and as usual enveloped in the Cowl of her Habit.\par
\par
“And you are still here, Matilda?” said the Friar at length. “Are you\par
not satisfied with having so nearly effected my destruction, that\par
nothing but a miracle could have saved me from the Grave? Ah! surely\par
Heaven sent that Serpent to punish....”\par
\par
Matilda interrupted him by putting her hand before his lips with an air\par
of gaiety.\par
\par
“Hush! Father, Hush! You must not talk!”\par
\par
“He who imposed that order, knew not how interesting are the subjects\par
on which I wish to speak.”\par
\par
“But I know it, and yet issue the same positive command. I am appointed\par
your Nurse, and you must not disobey my orders.”\par
\par
“You are in spirits, Matilda!”\par
\par
“Well may I be so: I have just received a pleasure unexampled through\par
my whole life.”\par
\par
“What was that pleasure?”\par
\par
“What I must conceal from all, but most from you.”\par
\par
“But most from me? Nay then, I entreat you, Matilda....”\par
\par
“Hush, Father! Hush! You must not talk. But as you do not seem inclined\par
to sleep, shall I endeavour to amuse you with my Harp?”\par
\par
“How? I knew not that you understood Music.”\par
\par
“Oh! I am a sorry Performer! Yet as silence is prescribed you for eight\par
and forty hours, I may possibly entertain you, when wearied of your own\par
reflections. I go to fetch my Harp.”\par
\par
She soon returned with it.\par
\par
“Now, Father; What shall I sing? Will you hear the Ballad which treats\par
of the gallant Durandarte, who died in the famous battle of\par
Roncevalles?”\par
\par
“What you please, Matilda.”\par
\par
“Oh! call me not Matilda! Call me Rosario, call me your Friend! Those\par
are the names, which I love to hear from your lips. Now listen!”\par
\par
She then tuned her harp, and afterwards preluded for some moments with\par
such exquisite taste as to prove her a perfect Mistress of the\par
Instrument. The air which She played was soft and plaintive:\par
\par
Ambrosio, while He listened, felt his uneasiness subside, and a\par
pleasing melancholy spread itself into his bosom. Suddenly Matilda\par
changed the strain: With an hand bold and rapid She struck a few loud\par
martial chords, and then chaunted the following Ballad to an air at\par
once simple and melodious.\par
\par
DURANDARTE AND BELERMA\par
\par
\par
Sad and fearful is the story\par
Of the Roncevalles fight;\par
On those fatal plains of glory\par
Perished many a gallant Knight.\par
\par
There fell Durandarte; Never\par
Verse a nobler Chieftain named:\par
He, before his lips for ever\par
Closed in silence thus exclaimed.\par
\par
“Oh! Belerma! Oh! my dear-one!\par
For my pain and pleasure born!\par
Seven long years I served thee, fair-one,\par
Seven long years my fee was scorn:\par
\par
“And when now thy heart replying\par
To my wishes, burns like mine,\par
Cruel Fate my bliss denying\par
Bids me every hope resign.\par
\par
“Ah! Though young I fall, believe me,\par
Death would never claim a sigh;\par
’Tis to lose thee, ’tis to leave thee,\par
Makes me think it hard to die!\par
\par
“Oh! my Cousin Montesinos,\par
By that friendship firm and dear\par
Which from Youth has lived between us,\par
Now my last petition hear!\par
\par
“When my Soul these limbs forsaking\par
Eager seeks a purer air,\par
From my breast the cold heart taking,\par
Give it to Belerma’s care.\par
\par
Say, I of my lands Possessor\par
Named her with my dying breath:\par
Say, my lips I op’d to bless her,\par
Ere they closed for aye in death:\par
\par
“Twice a week too how sincerely\par
I adored her, Cousin, say;\par
Twice a week for one who dearly\par
Loved her, Cousin, bid her pray.\par
\par
“Montesinos, now the hour\par
Marked by fate is near at hand:\par
Lo! my arm has lost its power!\par
Lo! I drop my trusty brand!\par
\par
“Eyes, which forth beheld me going,\par
Homewards ne’er shall see me hie!\par
Cousin, stop those tears o’er-flowing,\par
Let me on thy bosom die!\par
\par
“Thy kind hand my eyelids closing,\par
Yet one favour I implore:\par
Pray Thou for my Soul’s reposing,\par
When my heart shall throb no more;\par
\par
“So shall Jesus, still attending\par
Gracious to a Christian’s vow,\par
Pleased accept my Ghost ascending,\par
And a seat in heaven allow.”\par
\par
Thus spoke gallant Durandarte;\par
Soon his brave heart broke in twain.\par
Greatly joyed the Moorish party,\par
That the gallant Knight was slain.\par
\par
Bitter weeping Montesinos\par
Took from him his helm and glaive;\par
Bitter weeping Montesinos\par
Dug his gallant Cousin’s grave.\par
\par
To perform his promise made, He\par
Cut the heart from out the breast,\par
That Belerma, wretched Lady!\par
Might receive the last bequest.\par
\par
Sad was Montesinos’ heart, He\par
Felt distress his bosom rend.\par
“Oh! my Cousin Durandarte,\par
Woe is me to view thy end!\par
\par
“Sweet in manners, fair in favour,\par
Mild in temper, fierce in fight,\par
Warrior, nobler, gentler, braver,\par
Never shall behold the light!\par
\par
“Cousin, Lo! my tears bedew thee!\par
How shall I thy loss survive!\par
Durandarte, He who slew thee,\par
Wherefore left He me alive!”\par
\par
\par
While She sung, Ambrosio listened with delight: Never had He heard a\par
voice more harmonious; and He wondered how such heavenly sounds could\par
be produced by any but Angels. But though He indulged the sense of\par
hearing, a single look convinced him that He must not trust to that of\par
sight. The Songstress sat at a little distance from his Bed. The\par
attitude in which She bent over her harp, was easy and graceful: Her\par
Cowl had fallen backwarder than usual: Two coral lips were visible,\par
ripe, fresh, and melting, and a Chin in whose dimples seemed to lurk a\par
thousand Cupids. Her Habit’s long sleeve would have swept along the\par
Chords of the Instrument: To prevent this inconvenience She had drawn\par
it above her elbow, and by this means an arm was discovered formed in\par
the most perfect symmetry, the delicacy of whose skin might have\par
contended with snow in whiteness. Ambrosio dared to look on her but\par
once: That glance sufficed to convince him, how dangerous was the\par
presence of this seducing Object. He closed his eyes, but strove in\par
vain to banish her from his thoughts. There She still moved before him,\par
adorned with all those charms which his heated imagination could\par
supply: Every beauty which He had seen, appeared embellished, and those\par
still concealed Fancy represented to him in glowing colours. Still,\par
however, his vows and the necessity of keeping to them were present to\par
his memory. He struggled with desire, and shuddered when He beheld how\par
deep was the precipice before him.\par
\par
Matilda ceased to sing. Dreading the influence of her charms, Ambrosio\par
remained with his eyes closed, and offered up his prayers to St.\par
Francis to assist him in this dangerous trial! Matilda believed that He\par
was sleeping. She rose from her seat, approached the Bed softly, and\par
for some minutes gazed upon him attentively.\par
\par
“He sleeps!” said She at length in a low voice, but whose accents the\par
Abbot distinguished perfectly; “Now then I may gaze upon him without\par
offence! I may mix my breath with his; I may doat upon his features,\par
and He cannot suspect me of impurity and deceit!—He fears my seducing\par
him to the violation of his vows! Oh! the Unjust! Were it my wish to\par
excite desire, should I conceal my features from him so carefully?\par
Those features, of which I daily hear him....”\par
\par
She stopped, and was lost in her reflections.\par
\par
“It was but yesterday!” She continued; “But a few short hours have\par
past, since I was dear to him! He esteemed me, and my heart was\par
satisfied! Now!... Oh! now how cruelly is my situation changed! He\par
looks on me with suspicion! He bids me leave him, leave him for ever!\par
Oh! You, my Saint! my Idol! You, holding the next place to God in my\par
breast! Yet two days, and my heart will be unveiled to you.—Could you\par
know my feelings, when I beheld your agony! Could you know, how much\par
your sufferings have endeared you to me! But the time will come, when\par
you will be convinced that my passion is pure and disinterested. Then\par
you will pity me, and feel the whole weight of these sorrows!”\par
\par
As She said this, her voice was choaked by weeping. While She bent over\par
Ambrosio, a tear fell upon his cheek.\par
\par
“Ah! I have disturbed him!” cried Matilda, and retreated hastily.\par
\par
Her alarm was ungrounded. None sleep so profoundly, as those who are\par
determined not to wake. The Friar was in this predicament: He still\par
seemed buried in a repose, which every succeeding minute rendered him\par
less capable of enjoying. The burning tear had communicated its warmth\par
to his heart.\par
\par
“What affection! What purity!” said He internally; “Ah! since my bosom\par
is thus sensible of pity, what would it be if agitated by love?”\par
\par
Matilda again quitted her seat, and retired to some distance from the\par
Bed. Ambrosio ventured to open his eyes, and to cast them upon her\par
fearfully. Her face was turned from him. She rested her head in a\par
melancholy posture upon her Harp, and gazed on the picture which hung\par
opposite to the Bed.\par
\par
“Happy, happy Image!” Thus did She address the beautiful Madona; “’Tis\par
to you that He offers his prayers! ’Tis on you that He gazes with\par
admiration! I thought you would have lightened my sorrows; You have\par
only served to increase their weight: You have made me feel that had I\par
known him ere his vows were pronounced, Ambrosio and happiness might\par
have been mine. With what pleasure He views this picture! With what\par
fervour He addresses his prayers to the insensible Image! Ah! may not\par
his sentiments be inspired by some kind and secret Genius, Friend to my\par
affection? May it not be Man’s natural instinct which informs him... Be\par
silent, idle hopes! Let me not encourage an idea which takes from the\par
brilliance of Ambrosio’s virtue. ’Tis Religion, not Beauty which\par
attracts his admiration; ’Tis not to the Woman, but the Divinity that\par
He kneels. Would He but address to me the least tender expression which\par
He pours forth to this Madona! Would He but say that were He not\par
already affianced to the Church, He would not have despised Matilda!\par
Oh! let me nourish that fond idea! Perhaps He may yet acknowledge that\par
He feels for me more than pity, and that affection like mine might well\par
have deserved a return; Perhaps, He may own thus much when I lye on my\par
deathbed! He then need not fear to infringe his vows, and the\par
confession of his regard will soften the pangs of dying. Would I were\par
sure of this! Oh! how earnestly should I sigh for the moment of\par
dissolution!”\par
\par
Of this discourse the Abbot lost not a syllable; and the tone in which\par
She pronounced these last words pierced to his heart. Involuntarily He\par
raised himself from his pillow.\par
\par
“Matilda!” He said in a troubled voice; “Oh! my Matilda!”\par
\par
She started at the sound, and turned towards him hastily. The\par
suddenness of her movement made her Cowl fall back from her head; Her\par
features became visible to the Monk’s enquiring eye. What was his\par
amazement at beholding the exact resemblance of his admired Madona? The\par
same exquisite proportion of features, the same profusion of golden\par
hair, the same rosy lips, heavenly eyes, and majesty of countenance\par
adorned Matilda! Uttering an exclamation of surprize, Ambrosio sank\par
back upon his pillow, and doubted whether the Object before him was\par
mortal or divine.\par
\par
Matilda seemed penetrated with confusion. She remained motionless in\par
her place, and supported herself upon her Instrument. Her eyes were\par
bent upon the earth, and her fair cheeks overspread with blushes. On\par
recovering herself, her first action was to conceal her features. She\par
then in an unsteady and troubled voice ventured to address these words\par
to the Friar.\par
\par
“Accident has made you Master of a secret, which I never would have\par
revealed but on the Bed of death. Yes, Ambrosio; In Matilda de\par
Villanegas you see the original of your beloved Madona. Soon after I\par
conceived my unfortunate passion, I formed the project of conveying to\par
you my Picture: Crowds of Admirers had persuaded me that I possessed\par
some beauty, and I was anxious to know what effect it would produce\par
upon you. I caused my Portrait to be drawn by Martin Galuppi, a\par
celebrated Venetian at that time resident in Madrid. The resemblance\par
was striking: I sent it to the Capuchin Abbey as if for sale, and the\par
Jew from whom you bought it was one of my Emissaries. You purchased it.\par
Judge of my rapture, when informed that you had gazed upon it with\par
delight, or rather with adoration; that you had suspended it in your\par
Cell, and that you addressed your supplications to no other Saint. Will\par
this discovery make me still more regarded as an object of suspicion?\par
Rather should it convince you how pure is my affection, and engage you\par
to suffer me in your society and esteem. I heard you daily extol the\par
praises of my Portrait: I was an eyewitness of the transports, which\par
its beauty excited in you: Yet I forbore to use against your virtue\par
those arms, with which yourself had furnished me. I concealed those\par
features from your sight, which you loved unconsciously. I strove not\par
to excite desire by displaying my charms, or to make myself Mistress of\par
your heart through the medium of your senses. To attract your notice by\par
studiously attending to religious duties, to endear myself to you by\par
convincing you that my mind was virtuous and my attachment sincere,\par
such was my only aim. I succeeded; I became your companion and your\par
Friend. I concealed my sex from your knowledge; and had you not pressed\par
me to reveal my secret, had I not been tormented by the fear of a\par
discovery, never had you known me for any other than Rosario. And still\par
are you resolved to drive me from you? The few hours of life which yet\par
remain for me, may I not pass them in your presence? Oh! speak,\par
Ambrosio, and tell me that I may stay!”\par
\par
This speech gave the Abbot an opportunity of recollecting himself. He\par
was conscious that in the present disposition of his mind, avoiding her\par
society was his only refuge from the power of this enchanting Woman.\par
\par
“You declaration has so much astonished me,” said He, “that I am at\par
present incapable of answering you. Do not insist upon a reply,\par
Matilda; Leave me to myself; I have need to be alone.”\par
\par
“I obey you—But before I go, promise not to insist upon my quitting the\par
Abbey immediately.”\par
\par
“Matilda, reflect upon your situation; Reflect upon the consequences of\par
your stay. Our separation is indispensable, and we must part.”\par
\par
“But not to-day, Father! Oh! in pity not today!”\par
\par
“You press me too hard, but I cannot resist that tone of supplication.\par
Since you insist upon it, I yield to your prayer: I consent to your\par
remaining here a sufficient time to prepare in some measure the\par
Brethren for your departure. Stay yet two days; But on the third,” ...\par
(He sighed involuntarily)—“Remember, that on the third we must part for\par
ever!”\par
\par
She caught his hand eagerly, and pressed it to her lips.\par
\par
“On the third?” She exclaimed with an air of wild solemnity; “You are\par
right, Father! You are right! On the third we must part for ever!”\par
\par
There was a dreadful expression in her eye as She uttered these words,\par
which penetrated the Friar’s soul with horror: Again She kissed his\par
hand, and then fled with rapidity from the chamber.\par
\par
Anxious to authorise the presence of his dangerous Guest, yet conscious\par
that her stay was infringing the laws of his order, Ambrosio’s bosom\par
became the Theatre of a thousand contending passions. At length his\par
attachment to the feigned Rosario, aided by the natural warmth of his\par
temperament, seemed likely to obtain the victory: The success was\par
assured, when that presumption which formed the groundwork of his\par
character came to Matilda’s assistance. The Monk reflected that to\par
vanquish temptation was an infinitely greater merit than to avoid it:\par
He thought that He ought rather to rejoice in the opportunity given him\par
of proving the firmness of his virtue. St. Anthony had withstood all\par
seductions to lust; Then why should not He? Besides, St. Anthony was\par
tempted by the Devil, who put every art into practice to excite his\par
passions: Whereas, Ambrosio’s danger proceeded from a mere mortal\par
Woman, fearful and modest, whose apprehensions of his yielding were not\par
less violent than his own.\par
\par
“Yes,” said He; “The Unfortunate shall stay; I have nothing to fear\par
from her presence. Even should my own prove too weak to resist the\par
temptation, I am secured from danger by the innocence of Matilda.”\par
\par
Ambrosio was yet to learn, that to an heart unacquainted with her, Vice\par
is ever most dangerous when lurking behind the Mask of Virtue.\par
\par
He found himself so perfectly recovered, that when Father Pablos\par
visited him again at night, He entreated permission to quit his chamber\par
on the day following. His request was granted. Matilda appeared no more\par
that evening, except in company with the Monks when they came in a body\par
to enquire after the Abbot’s health. She seemed fearful of conversing\par
with him in private, and stayed but a few minutes in his room. The\par
Friar slept well; But the dreams of the former night were repeated, and\par
his sensations of voluptuousness were yet more keen and exquisite. The\par
same lust-exciting visions floated before his eyes: Matilda, in all the\par
pomp of beauty, warm, tender, and luxurious, clasped him to her bosom,\par
and lavished upon him the most ardent caresses. He returned them as\par
eagerly, and already was on the point of satisfying his desires, when\par
the faithless form disappeared, and left him to all the horrors of\par
shame and disappointment.\par
\par
The Morning dawned. Fatigued, harassed, and exhausted by his provoking\par
dreams, He was not disposed to quit his Bed. He excused himself from\par
appearing at Matins: It was the first morning in his life that He had\par
ever missed them. He rose late. During the whole of the day He had no\par
opportunity of speaking to Matilda without witnesses. His Cell was\par
thronged by the Monks, anxious to express their concern at his illness;\par
And He was still occupied in receiving their compliments on his\par
recovery, when the Bell summoned them to the Refectory.\par
\par
After dinner the Monks separated, and dispersed themselves in various\par
parts of the Garden, where the shade of trees or retirement of some\par
Grotto presented the most agreeable means of enjoying the Siesta. The\par
Abbot bent his steps towards the Hermitage: A glance of his eye invited\par
Matilda to accompany him.\par
\par
She obeyed, and followed him thither in silence. They entered the\par
Grotto, and seated themselves. Both seemed unwilling to begin the\par
conversation, and to labour under the influence of mutual\par
embarrassment. At length the Abbot spoke: He conversed only on\par
indifferent topics, and Matilda answered him in the same tone. She\par
seemed anxious to make him forget that the Person who sat by him was\par
any other than Rosario. Neither of them dared, or indeed wished to make\par
an allusion, to the subject which was most at the hearts of both.\par
\par
Matilda’s efforts to appear gay were evidently forced: Her spirits were\par
oppressed by the weight of anxiety, and when She spoke her voice was\par
low and feeble. She seemed desirous of finishing a conversation which\par
embarrassed her; and complaining that She was unwell, She requested\par
Ambrosio’s permission to return to the Abbey. He accompanied her to the\par
door of her cell; and when arrived there, He stopped her to declare his\par
consent to her continuing the Partner of his solitude so long as should\par
be agreeable to herself.\par
\par
She discovered no marks of pleasure at receiving this intelligence,\par
though on the preceding day She had been so anxious to obtain the\par
permission.\par
\par
“Alas! Father,” She said, waving her head mournfully; “Your kindness\par
comes too late! My doom is fixed. We must separate for ever. Yet\par
believe, that I am grateful for your generosity, for your compassion of\par
an Unfortunate who is but too little deserving of it!”\par
\par
She put her handkerchief to her eyes. Her Cowl was only half drawn over\par
her face. Ambrosio observed that She was pale, and her eyes sunk and\par
heavy.\par
\par
“Good God!” He cried; “You are very ill, Matilda! I shall send Father\par
Pablos to you instantly.”\par
\par
“No; Do not. I am ill, ’tis true; But He cannot cure my malady.\par
Farewell, Father! Remember me in your prayers tomorrow, while I shall\par
remember you in heaven!”\par
\par
She entered her cell, and closed the door.\par
\par
The Abbot dispatched to her the Physician without losing a moment, and\par
waited his report impatiently. But Father Pablos soon returned, and\par
declared that his errand had been fruitless. Rosario refused to admit\par
him, and had positively rejected his offers of assistance. The\par
uneasiness which this account gave Ambrosio was not trifling: Yet He\par
determined that Matilda should have her own way for that night: But\par
that if her situation did not mend by the morning, he would insist upon\par
her taking the advice of Father Pablos.\par
\par
He did not find himself inclined to sleep. He opened his casement, and\par
gazed upon the moonbeams as they played upon the small stream whose\par
waters bathed the walls of the Monastery. The coolness of the night\par
breeze and tranquillity of the hour inspired the Friar’s mind with\par
sadness. He thought upon Matilda’s beauty and affection; Upon the\par
pleasures which He might have shared with her, had He not been\par
restrained by monastic fetters. He reflected, that unsustained by hope\par
her love for him could not long exist; That doubtless She would succeed\par
in extinguishing her passion, and seek for happiness in the arms of One\par
more fortunate. He shuddered at the void which her absence would leave\par
in his bosom. He looked with disgust on the monotony of a Convent, and\par
breathed a sigh towards that world from which He was for ever\par
separated. Such were the reflections which a loud knocking at his door\par
interrupted. The Bell of the Church had already struck Two. The Abbot\par
hastened to enquire the cause of this disturbance. He opened the door\par
of his Cell, and a Lay-Brother entered, whose looks declared his hurry\par
and confusion.\par
\par
“Hasten, reverend Father!” said He; “Hasten to the young Rosario.\par
\par
He earnestly requests to see you; He lies at the point of death.”\par
\par
“Gracious God! Where is Father Pablos? Why is He not with him? Oh! I\par
fear! I fear!”\par
\par
“Father Pablos has seen him, but his art can do nothing. He says that\par
He suspects the Youth to be poisoned.”\par
\par
“Poisoned? Oh! The Unfortunate! It is then as I suspected! But let me\par
not lose a moment; Perhaps it may yet be time to save her!”\par
\par
He said, and flew towards the Cell of the Novice. Several Monks were\par
already in the chamber. Father Pablos was one of them, and held a\par
medicine in his hand which He was endeavouring to persuade Rosario to\par
swallow. The Others were employed in admiring the Patient’s divine\par
countenance, which They now saw for the first time. She looked lovelier\par
than ever. She was no longer pale or languid; A bright glow had spread\par
itself over her cheeks; her eyes sparkled with a serene delight, and\par
her countenance was expressive of confidence and resignation.\par
\par
“Oh! torment me no more!” was She saying to Pablos, when the terrified\par
Abbot rushed hastily into the Cell; “My disease is far beyond the reach\par
of your skill, and I wish not to be cured of it”—Then perceiving\par
Ambrosio,— “Ah! ’tis He!” She cried; “I see him once again, before we\par
part for ever! Leave me, my Brethren; Much have I to tell this holy Man\par
in private.”\par
\par
The Monks retired immediately, and Matilda and the Abbot remained\par
together.\par
\par
“What have you done, imprudent Woman!” exclaimed the Latter, as soon as\par
they were left alone; “Tell me; Are my suspicions just? Am I indeed to\par
lose you? Has your own hand been the instrument of your destruction?”\par
\par
She smiled, and grasped his hand.\par
\par
“In what have I been imprudent, Father? I have sacrificed a pebble, and\par
saved a diamond: My death preserves a life valuable to the world, and\par
more dear to me than my own. Yes, Father; I am poisoned; But know that\par
the poison once circulated in your veins.”\par
\par
“Matilda!”\par
\par
“What I tell you I resolved never to discover to you but on the bed of\par
death: That moment is now arrived. You cannot have forgotten the day\par
already, when your life was endangered by the bite of a Cientipedoro.\par
The Physician gave you over, declaring himself ignorant how to extract\par
the venom: I knew but of one means, and hesitated not a moment to\par
employ it. I was left alone with you: You slept; I loosened the bandage\par
from your hand; I kissed the wound, and drew out the poison with my\par
lips. The effect has been more sudden than I expected. I feel death at\par
my heart; Yet an hour, and I shall be in a better world.”\par
\par
“Almighty God!” exclaimed the Abbot, and sank almost lifeless upon the\par
Bed.\par
\par
After a few minutes He again raised himself up suddenly, and gazed upon\par
Matilda with all the wildness of despair.\par
\par
“And you have sacrificed yourself for me! You die, and die to preserve\par
Ambrosio! And is there indeed no remedy, Matilda? And is there indeed\par
no hope? Speak to me, Oh! speak to me! Tell me, that you have still the\par
means of life!”\par
\par
“Be comforted, my only Friend! Yes, I have still the means of life in\par
my power: But ’tis a means which I dare not employ. It is dangerous! It\par
is dreadful! Life would be purchased at too dear a rate, ... unless it\par
were permitted me to live for you.”\par
\par
“Then live for me, Matilda, for me and gratitude!”— (He caught her\par
hand, and pressed it rapturously to his lips.)—“Remember our late\par
conversations; I now consent to every thing: Remember in what lively\par
colours you described the union of souls; Be it ours to realize those\par
ideas. Let us forget the distinctions of sex, despise the world’s\par
prejudices, and only consider each other as Brother and Friend. Live\par
then, Matilda! Oh! live for me!”\par
\par
“Ambrosio, it must not be. When I thought thus, I deceived both you and\par
myself. Either I must die at present, or expire by the lingering\par
torments of unsatisfied desire. Oh! since we last conversed together, a\par
dreadful veil has been rent from before my eyes. I love you no longer\par
with the devotion which is paid to a Saint: I prize you no more for the\par
virtues of your soul; I lust for the enjoyment of your person. The\par
Woman reigns in my bosom, and I am become a prey to the wildest of\par
passions. Away with friendship! ’tis a cold unfeeling word. My bosom\par
burns with love, with unutterable love, and love must be its return.\par
Tremble then, Ambrosio, tremble to succeed in your prayers. If I live,\par
your truth, your reputation, your reward of a life past in sufferings,\par
all that you value is irretrievably lost. I shall no longer be able to\par
combat my passions, shall seize every opportunity to excite your\par
desires, and labour to effect your dishonour and my own. No, no,\par
Ambrosio; I must not live! I am convinced with every moment, that I\par
have but one alternative; I feel with every heart-throb, that I must\par
enjoy you, or die.”\par
\par
“Amazement!—Matilda! Can it be you who speak to me?”\par
\par
He made a movement as if to quit his seat. She uttered a loud shriek,\par
and raising herself half out of the Bed, threw her arms round the Friar\par
to detain him.\par
\par
“Oh! do not leave me! Listen to my errors with compassion! In a few\par
hours I shall be no more; Yet a little, and I am free from this\par
disgraceful passion.”\par
\par
“Wretched Woman, what can I say to you! I cannot ... I must not ... But\par
live, Matilda! Oh! live!”\par
\par
“You do not reflect on what you ask. What? Live to plunge myself in\par
infamy? To become the Agent of Hell? To work the destruction both of\par
you and of Myself? Feel this heart, Father!”\par
\par
She took his hand: Confused, embarrassed, and fascinated, He withdrew\par
it not, and felt her heart throb under it.\par
\par
“Feel this heart, Father! It is yet the seat of honour, truth, and\par
chastity: If it beats tomorrow, it must fall a prey to the blackest\par
crimes. Oh! let me then die today! Let me die, while I yet deserve the\par
tears of the virtuous! Thus will expire!”—(She reclined her head upon\par
his shoulder; Her golden Hair poured itself over his Chest.)— “Folded\par
in your arms, I shall sink to sleep; Your hand shall close my eyes for\par
ever, and your lips receive my dying breath. And will you not sometimes\par
think of me? Will you not sometimes shed a tear upon my Tomb? Oh! Yes!\par
Yes! Yes! That kiss is my assurance!”\par
\par
The hour was night. All was silence around. The faint beams of a\par
solitary Lamp darted upon Matilda’s figure, and shed through the\par
chamber a dim mysterious light. No prying eye, or curious ear was near\par
the Lovers: Nothing was heard but Matilda’s melodious accents. Ambrosio\par
was in the full vigour of Manhood. He saw before him a young and\par
beautiful Woman, the preserver of his life, the Adorer of his person,\par
and whom affection for him had reduced to the brink of the Grave. He\par
sat upon her Bed; His hand rested upon her bosom; Her head reclined\par
voluptuously upon his breast. Who then can wonder, if He yielded to the\par
temptation? Drunk with desire, He pressed his lips to those which\par
sought them: His kisses vied with Matilda’s in warmth and passion. He\par
clasped her rapturously in his arms; He forgot his vows, his sanctity,\par
and his fame: He remembered nothing but the pleasure and opportunity.\par
\par
“Ambrosio! Oh! my Ambrosio!” sighed Matilda.\par
\par
“Thine, ever thine!” murmured the Friar, and sank upon her bosom.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER III\par
\par
\par
——These are the Villains\par
Whom all the Travellers do fear so much.\par
————Some of them are Gentlemen\par
Such as the fury of ungoverned Youth\par
Thrust from the company of awful Men.\par
\par
TWO GENTLEMEN OF VERONA.\par
\par
The Marquis and Lorenzo proceeded to the Hotel in silence. The Former\par
employed himself in calling every circumstance to his mind, which\par
related might give Lorenzo’s the most favourable idea of his connexion\par
with Agnes. The Latter, justly alarmed for the honour of his family,\par
felt embarrassed by the presence of the Marquis: The adventure which He\par
had just witnessed forbad his treating him as a Friend; and Antonia’s\par
interests being entrusted to his mediation, He saw the impolicy of\par
treating him as a Foe. He concluded from these reflections, that\par
profound silence would be the wisest plan, and waited with impatience\par
for Don Raymond’s explanation.\par
\par
They arrived at the Hotel de las Cisternas. The Marquis immediately\par
conducted him to his apartment, and began to express his satisfaction\par
at finding him at Madrid. Lorenzo interrupted him.\par
\par
“Excuse me, my Lord,” said He with a distant air, “if I reply somewhat\par
coldly to your expressions of regard. A Sister’s honour is involved in\par
this affair: Till that is established, and the purport of your\par
correspondence with Agnes cleared up, I cannot consider you as my\par
Friend. I am anxious to hear the meaning of your conduct, and hope that\par
you will not delay the promised explanation.”\par
\par
“First give me your word, that you will listen with patience and\par
indulgence.”\par
\par
“I love my Sister too well to judge her harshly; and till this moment I\par
possessed no Friend so dear to me as yourself. I will also confess,\par
that your having it in your power to oblige me in a business which I\par
have much at heart, makes me very anxious to find you still deserving\par
my esteem.”\par
\par
“Lorenzo, you transport me! No greater pleasure can be given me, than\par
an opportunity of serving the Brother of Agnes.”\par
\par
“Convince me that I can accept your favours without dishonour, and\par
there is no Man in the world to whom I am more willing to be obliged.”\par
\par
“Probably, you have already heard your Sister mention the name of\par
Alphonso d’Alvarada?”\par
\par
“Never. Though I feel for Agnes an affection truly fraternal,\par
circumstances have prevented us from being much together. While yet a\par
Child She was consigned to the care of her Aunt, who had married a\par
German Nobleman. At his Castle She remained till two years since, when\par
She returned to Spain, determined upon secluding herself from the\par
world.”\par
\par
“Good God! Lorenzo, you knew of her intention, and yet strove not to\par
make her change it?”\par
\par
“Marquis, you wrong me. The intelligence, which I received at Naples,\par
shocked me extremely, and I hastened my return to Madrid for the\par
express purpose of preventing the sacrifice. The moment that I arrived,\par
I flew to the Convent of St. Clare, in which Agnes had chosen to\par
perform her Noviciate. I requested to see my Sister. Conceive my\par
surprise when She sent me a refusal; She declared positively, that\par
apprehending my influence over her mind, She would not trust herself in\par
my society till the day before that on which She was to receive the\par
Veil. I supplicated the Nuns; I insisted upon seeing Agnes, and\par
hesitated not to avow my suspicions that her being kept from me was\par
against her own inclinations. To free herself from the imputation of\par
violence, the Prioress brought me a few lines written in my Sister’s\par
well-known hand, repeating the message already delivered. All future\par
attempts to obtain a moment’s conversation with her were as fruitless\par
as the first. She was inflexible, and I was not permitted to see her\par
till the day preceding that on which She entered the Cloister never to\par
quit it more. This interview took place in the presence of our\par
principal Relations. It was for the first time since her childhood that\par
I saw her, and the scene was most affecting. She threw herself upon my\par
bosom, kissed me, and wept bitterly. By every possible argument, by\par
tears, by prayers, by kneeling, I strove to make her abandon her\par
intention. I represented to her all the hardships of a religious life;\par
I painted to her imagination all the pleasures which She was going to\par
quit, and besought her to disclose to me, what occasioned her disgust\par
to the world. At this last question She turned pale, and her tears\par
flowed yet faster. She entreated me not to press her on that subject;\par
That it sufficed me to know that her resolution was taken, and that a\par
Convent was the only place where She could now hope for tranquillity.\par
She persevered in her design, and made her profession. I visited her\par
frequently at the Grate, and every moment that I passed with her, made\par
me feel more affliction at her loss. I was shortly after obliged to\par
quit Madrid; I returned but yesterday evening, and since then have not\par
had time to call at St. Clare’s Convent.”\par
\par
“Then till I mentioned it, you never heard the name of Alphonso\par
d’Alvarada?”\par
\par
“Pardon me: my Aunt wrote me word that an Adventurer so called had\par
found means to get introduced into the Castle of Lindenberg; That He\par
had insinuated himself into my Sister’s good graces, and that She had\par
even consented to elope with him. However, before the plan could be\par
executed, the Cavalier discovered that the estates which He believed\par
Agnes to possess in Hispaniola, in reality belonged to me. This\par
intelligence made him change his intention; He disappeared on the day\par
that the elopement was to have taken place, and Agnes, in despair at\par
his perfidy and meanness, had resolved upon seclusion in a Convent. She\par
added, that as this adventurer had given himself out to be a Friend of\par
mine, She wished to know whether I had any knowledge of him. I replied\par
in the negative. I had then very little idea, that Alphonso d’Alvarada\par
and the Marquis de las Cisternas were one and the same person: The\par
description given me of the first by no means tallied with what I knew\par
of the latter.”\par
\par
“In this I easily recognize Donna Rodolpha’s perfidious character.\par
Every word of this account is stamped with marks of her malice, of her\par
falsehood, of her talents for misrepresenting those whom She wishes to\par
injure. Forgive me, Medina, for speaking so freely of your Relation.\par
The mischief which She has done me authorises my resentment, and when\par
you have heard my story, you will be convinced that my expressions have\par
not been too severe.”\par
\par
He then began his narrative in the following manner:—\par
\par
HISTORY OF DON RAYMOND,\par
MARQUIS DE LAS CISTERNAS\par
\par
Long experience, my dear Lorenzo, has convinced me how generous is your\par
nature: I waited not for your declaration of ignorance respecting your\par
Sister’s adventures to suppose that they had been purposely concealed\par
from you. Had they reached your knowledge, from what misfortunes should\par
both Agnes and myself have escaped! Fate had ordained it otherwise! You\par
were on your Travels when I first became acquainted with your Sister;\par
and as our Enemies took care to conceal from her your direction, it was\par
impossible for her to implore by letter your protection and advice.\par
\par
On leaving Salamanca, at which University as I have since heard, you\par
remained a year after I quitted it, I immediately set out upon my\par
Travels. My Father supplied me liberally with money; But He insisted\par
upon my concealing my rank, and presenting myself as no more than a\par
private Gentleman. This command was issued by the counsels of his\par
Friend, the Duke of Villa Hermosa, a Nobleman for whose abilities and\par
knowledge of the world I have ever entertained the most profound\par
veneration.\par
\par
“Believe me,” said He, “my dear Raymond, you will hereafter feel the\par
benefits of this temporary degradation. ’Tis true, that as the Condé de\par
las Cisternas you would have been received with open arms; and your\par
youthful vanity might have felt gratified by the attentions showered\par
upon you from all sides. At present, much will depend upon yourself:\par
You have excellent recommendations, but it must be your own business to\par
make them of use to you. You must lay yourself out to please; You must\par
labour to gain the approbation of those, to whom you are presented:\par
They who would have courted the friendship of the Condé de las\par
Cisternas will have no interest in finding out the merits, or bearing\par
patiently with the faults, of Alphonso d’Alvarada. Consequently, when\par
you find yourself really liked, you may safely ascribe it to your good\par
qualities, not your rank, and the distinction shown you will be\par
infinitely more flattering. Besides, your exalted birth would not\par
permit your mixing with the lower classes of society, which will now be\par
in your power, and from which, in my opinion, you will derive\par
considerable benefit. Do not confine yourself to the Illustrious of\par
those Countries through which you pass. Examine the manners and customs\par
of the multitude: Enter into the Cottages; and by observing how the\par
Vassals of Foreigners are treated, learn to diminish the burthens and\par
augment the comforts of your own. According to my ideas, of those\par
advantages which a Youth destined to the possession of power and wealth\par
may reap from travel, He should not consider as the least essential,\par
the opportunity of mixing with the classes below him, and becoming an\par
eyewitness of the sufferings of the People.”\par
\par
Forgive me, Lorenzo, if I seem tedious in my narration. The close\par
connexion which now exists between us, makes me anxious that you should\par
know every particular respecting me; and in my fear of omitting the\par
least circumstance which may induce you to think favourably of your\par
Sister and myself, I may possibly relate many which you may think\par
uninteresting.\par
\par
I followed the Duke’s advice; I was soon convinced of its wisdom.\par
\par
I quitted Spain, calling myself by the assumed title of Don Alphonso\par
d’Alvarada, and attended by a single Domestic of approved fidelity.\par
Paris was my first station. For some time I was enchanted with it, as\par
indeed must be every Man who is young, rich, and fond of pleasure. Yet\par
among all its gaieties, I felt that something was wanting to my heart.\par
I grew sick of dissipation: I discovered, that the People among whom I\par
lived, and whose exterior was so polished and seducing, were at bottom\par
frivolous, unfeeling and insincere. I turned from the Inhabitants of\par
Paris with disgust, and quitted that Theatre of Luxury without heaving\par
one sigh of regret.\par
\par
I now bent my course towards Germany, intending to visit most of the\par
principal courts: Prior to this expedition, I meant to make some little\par
stay at Strasbourg. On quitting my Chaise at Luneville to take some\par
refreshment, I observed a splendid Equipage, attended by four Domestics\par
in rich liveries, waiting at the door of the Silver Lion. Soon after as\par
I looked out of the window, I saw a Lady of noble presence, followed by\par
two female Attendants, step into the Carriage, which drove off\par
immediately.\par
\par
I enquired of the Host, who the Lady was, that had just departed.\par
\par
“A German Baroness, Monsieur, of great rank and fortune. She has been\par
upon a visit to the Duchess of Longueville, as her Servants informed\par
me; She is going to Strasbourg, where She will find her Husband, and\par
then both return to their Castle in Germany.”\par
\par
I resumed my journey, intending to reach Strasbourg that night. My\par
hopes, however were frustrated by the breaking down of my Chaise. The\par
accident happened in the middle of a thick Forest, and I was not a\par
little embarrassed as to the means of proceeding.\par
\par
It was the depth of winter: The night was already closing round us; and\par
Strasbourg, which was the nearest Town, was still distant from us\par
several leagues. It seemed to me that my only alternative to passing\par
the night in the Forest, was to take my Servant’s Horse and ride on to\par
Strasbourg, an undertaking at that season very far from agreeable.\par
However, seeing no other resource, I was obliged to make up my mind to\par
it. Accordingly I communicated my design to the Postillion, telling him\par
that I would send People to assist him as soon as I reached Strasbourg.\par
I had not much confidence in his honesty; But Stephano being\par
well-armed, and the Driver to all appearance considerably advanced in\par
years, I believed I ran no danger of losing my Baggage.\par
\par
Luckily, as I then thought, an opportunity presented itself of passing\par
the night more agreeably than I expected. On mentioning my design of\par
proceeding by myself to Strasbourg, the Postillion shook his head in\par
disapprobation.\par
\par
“It is a long way,” said He; “You will find it a difficult matter to\par
arrive there without a Guide. Besides, Monsieur seems unaccustomed to\par
the season’s severity, and ’tis possible that unable to sustain the\par
excessive cold....”\par
\par
“What use is there to present me with all these objections?” said I,\par
impatiently interrupting him; “I have no other resource: I run still\par
greater risque of perishing with cold by passing the night in the\par
Forest.”\par
\par
“Passing the night in the Forest?” He replied; “Oh! by St. Denis! We\par
are not in quite so bad a plight as that comes to yet. If I am not\par
mistaken, we are scarcely five minutes walk from the Cottage of my old\par
Friend, Baptiste. He is a Wood-cutter, and a very honest Fellow. I\par
doubt not but He will shelter you for the night with pleasure. In the\par
meantime I can take the saddle-Horse, ride to Strasbourg, and be back\par
with proper people to mend your Carriage by break of day.”\par
\par
“And in the name of God,” said I, “How could you leave me so long in\par
suspense? Why did you not tell me of this Cottage sooner? What\par
excessive stupidity!”\par
\par
“I thought that perhaps Monsieur would not deign to accept....”\par
\par
“Absurd! Come, come! Say no more, but conduct us without delay to the\par
Wood-man’s Cottage.”\par
\par
He obeyed, and we moved onwards: The Horses contrived with some\par
difficulty to drag the shattered vehicle after us. My Servant was\par
become almost speechless, and I began to feel the effects of the cold\par
myself, before we reached the wished-for Cottage. It was a small but\par
neat Building: As we drew near it, I rejoiced at observing through the\par
window the blaze of a comfortable fire. Our Conductor knocked at the\par
door: It was some time before any one answered; The People within\par
seemed in doubt whether we should be admitted.\par
\par
“Come! Come, Friend Baptiste!” cried the Driver with impatience; “What\par
are you about? Are you asleep? Or will you refuse a night’s lodging to\par
a Gentleman, whose Chaise has just broken down in the Forest?”\par
\par
“Ah! is it you, honest Claude?” replied a Man’s voice from within;\par
“Wait a moment, and the door shall be opened.”\par
\par
Soon after the bolts were drawn back. The door was unclosed, and a Man\par
presented himself to us with a Lamp in his hand. He gave the Guide an\par
hearty reception, and then addressed himself to me.\par
\par
“Walk in, Monsieur; Walk in, and welcome! Excuse me for not admitting\par
you at first: But there are so many Rogues about this place, that\par
saving your presence, I suspected you to be one.”\par
\par
Thus saying, He ushered me into the room, where I had observed the\par
fire: I was immediately placed in an Easy Chair, which stood close to\par
the Hearth. A Female, whom I supposed to be the Wife of my Host, rose\par
from her seat upon my entrance, and received me with a slight and\par
distant reverence. She made no answer to my compliment, but immediately\par
re-seating herself, continued the work on which She had been employed.\par
Her Husband’s manners were as friendly as hers were harsh and\par
repulsive.\par
\par
“I wish, I could lodge you more conveniently, Monsieur,” said He; “But\par
we cannot boast of much spare room in this hovel. However, a chamber\par
for yourself, and another for your Servant, I think, we can make shift\par
to supply. You must content yourself with sorry fare; But to what we\par
have, believe me, you are heartily welcome.” ——Then turning to his\par
wife—“Why, how you sit there, Marguerite, with as much tranquillity as\par
if you had nothing better to do! Stir about, Dame! Stir about! Get some\par
supper; Look out some sheets; Here, here; throw some logs upon the\par
fire, for the Gentleman seems perished with cold.”\par
\par
The wife threw her work hastily upon the Table, and proceeded to\par
execute his commands with every mark of unwillingness. Her countenance\par
had displeased me on the first moment of my examining it. Yet upon the\par
whole her features were handsome unquestionably; But her skin was\par
sallow, and her person thin and meagre; A louring gloom over-spread her\par
countenance; and it bore such visible marks of rancour and ill-will, as\par
could not escape being noticed by the most inattentive Observer. Her\par
every look and action expressed discontent and impatience, and the\par
answers which She gave Baptiste, when He reproached her good-humouredly\par
for her dissatisfied air, were tart, short, and cutting. In fine, I\par
conceived at first sight equal disgust for her, and prepossession in\par
favour of her Husband, whose appearance was calculated to inspire\par
esteem and confidence. His countenance was open, sincere, and friendly;\par
his manners had all the Peasant’s honesty unaccompanied by his\par
rudeness; His cheeks were broad, full, and ruddy; and in the solidity\par
of his person He seemed to offer an ample apology for the leanness of\par
his Wife’s. From the wrinkles on his brow I judged him to be turned of\par
sixty; But He bore his years well, and seemed still hearty and strong:\par
The Wife could not be more than thirty, but in spirits and vivacity She\par
was infinitely older than the Husband.\par
\par
However, in spite of her unwillingness, Marguerite began to prepare the\par
supper, while the Wood-man conversed gaily on different subjects. The\par
Postillion, who had been furnished with a bottle of spirits, was now\par
ready to set out for Strasbourg, and enquired, whether I had any\par
further commands.\par
\par
“For Strasbourg?” interrupted Baptiste; “You are not going thither\par
tonight?”\par
\par
“I beg your pardon: If I do not fetch Workmen to mend the Chaise, How\par
is Monsieur to proceed tomorrow?”\par
\par
“That is true, as you say; I had forgotten the Chaise. Well, but\par
Claude; You may at least eat your supper here? That can make you lose\par
very little time, and Monsieur looks too kind-hearted to send you out\par
with an empty stomach on such a bitter cold night as this is.”\par
\par
To this I readily assented, telling the Postillion that my reaching\par
Strasbourg the next day an hour or two later would be perfectly\par
immaterial. He thanked me, and then leaving the Cottage with Stephano,\par
put up his Horses in the Wood-man’s Stable. Baptiste followed them to\par
the door, and looked out with anxiety.\par
\par
“’Tis a sharp biting wind!” said He; “I wonder, what detains my Boys so\par
long! Monsieur, I shall show you two of the finest Lads, that ever\par
stept in shoe of leather. The eldest is three and twenty, the second a\par
year younger: Their Equals for sense, courage, and activity, are not to\par
be found within fifty miles of Strasbourg. Would They were back again!\par
I begin to feel uneasy about them.”\par
\par
Marguerite was at this time employed in laying the cloth.\par
\par
“And are you equally anxious for the return of your Sons?” said I to\par
her.\par
\par
“Not I!” She replied peevishly; “They are no children of mine.”\par
\par
“Come! Come, Marguerite!” said the Husband; “Do not be out of humour\par
with the Gentleman for asking a simple question. Had you not looked so\par
cross, He would never have thought you old enough to have a Son of\par
three and twenty: But you see how many years ill-temper adds to\par
you!—Excuse my Wife’s rudeness, Monsieur. A little thing puts her out,\par
and She is somewhat displeased at your not thinking her to be under\par
thirty. That is the truth, is it not, Marguerite? You know, Monsieur,\par
that Age is always a ticklish subject with a Woman. Come! come!\par
Marguerite, clear up a little. If you have not Sons as old, you will\par
some twenty years hence, and I hope, that we shall live to see them\par
just such Lads as Jacques and Robert.”\par
\par
Marguerite clasped her hands together passionately.\par
\par
“God forbid!” said She; “God forbid! If I thought it, I would strangle\par
them with my own hands!”\par
\par
She quitted the room hastily, and went up stairs.\par
\par
I could not help expressing to the Wood-man how much I pitied him for\par
being chained for life to a Partner of such ill-humour.\par
\par
“Ah! Lord! Monsieur, Every one has his share of grievances, and\par
Marguerite has fallen to mine. Besides, after all She is only cross,\par
and not malicious. The worst is, that her affection for two children by\par
a former Husband makes her play the Step-mother with my two Sons. She\par
cannot bear the sight of them, and by her good-will they would never\par
set a foot within my door. But on this point I always stand firm, and\par
never will consent to abandon the poor Lads to the world’s mercy, as\par
She has often solicited me to do. In every thing else I let her have\par
her own way; and truly She manages a family rarely, that I must say for\par
her.”\par
\par
We were conversing in this manner, when our discourse was interrupted\par
by a loud halloo, which rang through the Forest.\par
\par
“My Sons, I hope!” exclaimed the Wood-man, and ran to open the door.\par
\par
The halloo was repeated: We now distinguished the trampling of Horses,\par
and soon after a Carriage, attended by several Cavaliers stopped at the\par
Cottage door. One of the Horsemen enquired how far they were still from\par
Strasbourg. As He addressed himself to me, I answered in the number of\par
miles which Claude had told me; Upon which a volley of curses was\par
vented against the Drivers for having lost their way. The Persons in\par
the Coach were now informed of the distance of Strasbourg, and also\par
that the Horses were so fatigued as to be incapable of proceeding\par
further. A Lady, who appeared to be the principal, expressed much\par
chagrin at this intelligence; But as there was no remedy, one of the\par
Attendants asked the Wood-man, whether He could furnish them with\par
lodging for the night.\par
\par
He seemed much embarrassed, and replied in the negative; Adding that a\par
Spanish Gentleman and his Servant were already in possession of the\par
only spare apartments in his House. On hearing this, the gallantry of\par
my nation would not permit me to retain those accommodations, of which\par
a Female was in want. I instantly signified to the Wood-man, that I\par
transferred my right to the Lady; He made some objections; But I\par
overruled them, and hastening to the Carriage, opened the door, and\par
assisted the Lady to descend. I immediately recognized her for the same\par
person whom I had seen at the Inn at Luneville. I took an opportunity\par
of asking one of her Attendants, what was her name?\par
\par
“The Baroness Lindenberg,” was the answer.\par
\par
I could not but remark how different a reception our Host had given\par
these newcomers and myself. His reluctance to admit them was visibly\par
expressed on his countenance, and He prevailed on himself with\par
difficulty to tell the Lady that She was welcome. I conducted her into\par
the House, and placed her in the armed-chair, which I had just quitted.\par
She thanked me very graciously; and made a thousand apologies for\par
putting me to an inconvenience. Suddenly the Wood-man’s countenance\par
cleared up.\par
\par
“At last I have arranged it!” said He, interrupting her excuses; “I can\par
lodge you and your suite, Madam, and you will not be under the\par
necessity of making this Gentleman suffer for his politeness.\par
\par
We have two spare chambers, one for the Lady, the other, Monsieur, for\par
you: My Wife shall give up hers to the two Waiting-women; As for the\par
Men-servants, they must content themselves with passing the night in a\par
large Barn, which stands at a few yards distance from the House. There\par
they shall have a blazing fire, and as good a supper as we can make\par
shift to give them.”\par
\par
After several expressions of gratitude on the Lady’s part, and\par
opposition on mine to Marguerite’s giving up her bed, this arrangement\par
was agreed to. As the Room was small, the Baroness immediately\par
dismissed her Male Domestics: Baptiste was on the point of conducting\par
them to the Barn which He had mentioned when two young Men appeared at\par
the door of the Cottage.\par
\par
“Hell and Furies!” exclaimed the first starting back; “Robert, the\par
House is filled with Strangers!”\par
\par
“Ha! There are my Sons!” cried our Host. “Why, Jacques! Robert! whither\par
are you running, Boys? There is room enough still for you.”\par
\par
Upon this assurance the Youths returned. The Father presented them to\par
the Baroness and myself: After which He withdrew with our Domestics,\par
while at the request of the two Waiting-women, Marguerite conducted\par
them to the room designed for their Mistress.\par
\par
The two new-comers were tall, stout, well-made young Men,\par
hard-featured, and very much sun-burnt. They paid their compliments to\par
us in few words, and acknowledged Claude, who now entered the room, as\par
an old acquaintance. They then threw aside their cloaks in which they\par
were wrapped up, took off a leathern belt to which a large Cutlass was\par
suspended, and each drawing a brace of pistols from his girdle laid\par
them upon a shelf.\par
\par
“You travel well-armed,” said I.\par
\par
“True, Monsieur;” replied Robert. “We left Strasbourg late this\par
Evening, and ’tis necessary to take precautions at passing through this\par
Forest after dark. It does not bear a good repute, I promise you.”\par
\par
“How?” said the Baroness; “Are there Robbers hereabout?”\par
\par
“So it is said, Madame; For my own part, I have travelled through the\par
wood at all hours, and never met with one of them.”\par
\par
Here Marguerite returned. Her Stepsons drew her to the other end of the\par
room, and whispered her for some minutes. By the looks which they cast\par
towards us at intervals, I conjectured them to be enquiring our\par
business in the Cottage.\par
\par
In the meanwhile the Baroness expressed her apprehensions, that her\par
Husband would be suffering much anxiety upon her account. She had\par
intended to send on one of her Servants to inform the Baron of her\par
delay; But the account which the young Men gave of the Forest rendered\par
this plan impracticable. Claude relieved her from her embarrassment. He\par
informed her that He was under the necessity of reaching Strasbourg\par
that night, and that would She trust him with a letter, She might\par
depend upon its being safely delivered.\par
\par
“And how comes it,” said I, “that you are under no apprehension of\par
meeting these Robbers?”\par
\par
“Alas! Monsieur, a poor Man with a large family must not lose certain\par
profit because ’tis attended with a little danger, and perhaps my Lord\par
the Baron may give me a trifle for my pains. Besides, I have nothing to\par
lose except my life, and that will not be worth the Robbers taking.”\par
\par
I thought his arguments bad, and advised his waiting till the Morning;\par
But as the Baroness did not second me, I was obliged to give up the\par
point. The Baroness Lindenberg, as I found afterwards, had long been\par
accustomed to sacrifice the interests of others to her own, and her\par
wish to send Claude to Strasbourg blinded her to the danger of the\par
undertaking. Accordingly, it was resolved that He should set out\par
without delay. The Baroness wrote her letter to her Husband, and I sent\par
a few lines to my Banker, apprising him that I should not be at\par
Strasbourg till the next day. Claude took our letters, and left the\par
Cottage.\par
\par
The Lady declared herself much fatigued by her journey: Besides having\par
come from some distance, the Drivers had contrived to lose their way in\par
the Forest. She now addressed herself to Marguerite, desiring to be\par
shown to her chamber, and permitted to take half an hour’s repose. One\par
of the Waiting-women was immediately summoned; She appeared with a\par
light, and the Baroness followed her up stairs. The cloth was spreading\par
in the chamber where I was, and Marguerite soon gave me to understand\par
that I was in her way. Her hints were too broad to be easily mistaken;\par
I therefore desired one of the young Men to conduct me to the chamber\par
where I was to sleep, and where I could remain till supper was ready.\par
\par
“Which chamber is it, Mother?” said Robert.\par
\par
“The One with green hangings,” She replied; “I have just been at the\par
trouble of getting it ready, and have put fresh sheets upon the Bed; If\par
the Gentleman chooses to lollop and lounge upon it, He may make it\par
again himself for me.”\par
\par
“You are out of humour, Mother, but that is no novelty. Have the\par
goodness to follow me, Monsieur.”\par
\par
He opened the door, and advanced towards a narrow staircase.\par
\par
“You have got no light!” said Marguerite; “Is it your own neck or the\par
Gentleman’s that you have a mind to break?”\par
\par
She crossed by me, and put a candle into Robert’s hand, having received\par
which, He began to ascend the staircase. Jacques was employed in laying\par
the cloth, and his back was turned towards me.\par
\par
Marguerite seized the moment, when we were unobserved. She caught my\par
hand, and pressed it strongly.\par
\par
“Look at the Sheets!” said She as She passed me, and immediately\par
resumed her former occupation.\par
\par
Startled by the abruptness of her action, I remained as if petrified.\par
Robert’s voice, desiring me to follow him, recalled me to myself. I\par
ascended the staircase. My conductor ushered me into a chamber, where\par
an excellent wood-fire was blazing upon the hearth. He placed the light\par
upon the Table, enquired whether I had any further commands, and on my\par
replying in the negative, He left me to myself. You may be certain that\par
the moment when I found myself alone was that on which I complied with\par
Marguerite’s injunction. I took the candle, hastily approached the Bed,\par
and turned down the Coverture. What was my astonishment, my horror, at\par
finding the sheets crimsoned with blood!\par
\par
At that moment a thousand confused ideas passed before my imagination.\par
The Robbers who infested the Wood, Marguerite’s exclamation respecting\par
her Children, the arms and appearance of the two young Men, and the\par
various Anecdotes which I had heard related, respecting the secret\par
correspondence which frequently exists between Banditti and\par
Postillions, all these circumstances flashed upon my mind, and inspired\par
me with doubt and apprehension. I ruminated on the most probable means\par
of ascertaining the truth of my conjectures. Suddenly I was aware of\par
Someone below pacing hastily backwards and forwards. Every thing now\par
appeared to me an object of suspicion. With precaution I drew near the\par
window, which, as the room had been long shut up, was left open in\par
spite of the cold. I ventured to look out. The beams of the Moon\par
permitted me to distinguish a Man, whom I had no difficulty to\par
recognize for my Host. I watched his movements.\par
\par
He walked swiftly, then stopped, and seemed to listen: He stamped upon\par
the ground, and beat his stomach with his arms as if to guard himself\par
from the inclemency of the season. At the least noise, if a voice was\par
heard in the lower part of the House, if a Bat flitted past him, or the\par
wind rattled amidst the leafless boughs, He started, and looked round\par
with anxiety.\par
\par
“Plague take him!” said He at length with impatience; “What can He be\par
about!”\par
\par
He spoke in a low voice; but as He was just below my window, I had no\par
difficulty to distinguish his words.\par
\par
I now heard the steps of one approaching. Baptiste went towards the\par
sound; He joined a man, whom his low stature and the Horn suspended\par
from his neck, declared to be no other than my faithful Claude, whom I\par
had supposed to be already on his way to Strasbourg. Expecting their\par
discourse to throw some light upon my situation, I hastened to put\par
myself in a condition to hear it with safety. For this purpose I\par
extinguished the candle, which stood upon a table near the Bed: The\par
flame of the fire was not strong enough to betray me, and I immediately\par
resumed my place at the window.\par
\par
The objects of my curiosity had stationed themselves directly under it.\par
I suppose that during my momentary absence the Wood-man had been\par
blaming Claude for tardiness, since when I returned to the window, the\par
latter was endeavouring to excuse his fault.\par
\par
“However,” added He, “my diligence at present shall make up for my past\par
delay.”\par
\par
“On that condition,” answered Baptiste, “I shall readily forgive you.\par
But in truth as you share equally with us in our prizes, your own\par
interest will make you use all possible diligence. ’Twould be a shame\par
to let such a noble booty escape us! You say, that this Spaniard is\par
rich?”\par
\par
“His Servant boasted at the Inn, that the effects in his Chaise were\par
worth above two thousand Pistoles.”\par
\par
Oh! how I cursed Stephano’s imprudent vanity!\par
\par
“And I have been told,” continued the Postillion, “that this Baroness\par
carries about her a casket of jewels of immense value.”\par
\par
“May be so, but I had rather She had stayed away. The Spaniard was a\par
secure prey. The Boys and myself could easily have mastered him and his\par
Servant, and then the two thousand Pistoles would have been shared\par
between us four. Now we must let in the Band for a share, and perhaps\par
the whole Covey may escape us. Should our Friends have betaken\par
themselves to their different posts before you reach the Cavern, all\par
will be lost. The Lady’s Attendants are too numerous for us to\par
overpower them: Unless our Associates arrive in time, we must needs let\par
these Travellers set out tomorrow without damage or hurt.”\par
\par
“’Tis plaguy unlucky that my Comrades who drove the Coach should be\par
those unacquainted with our Confederacy! But never fear, Friend\par
Baptiste. An hour will bring me to the Cavern; It is now but ten\par
o’clock, and by twelve you may expect the arrival of the Band. By the\par
bye, take care of your Wife: You know how strong is her repugnance to\par
our mode of life, and She may find means to give information to the\par
Lady’s Servants of our design.”\par
\par
“Oh! I am secure of her silence; She is too much afraid of me, and fond\par
of her children, to dare to betray my secret. Besides, Jacques and\par
Robert keep a strict eye over her, and She is not permitted to set a\par
foot out of the Cottage. The Servants are safely lodged in the Barn; I\par
shall endeavour to keep all quiet till the arrival of our Friends. Were\par
I assured of your finding them, the Strangers should be dispatched this\par
instant; But as it is possible for you to miss the Banditti, I am\par
fearful of being summoned to produce them by their Domestics in the\par
Morning.”\par
\par
“And suppose either of the Travellers should discover your design?”\par
\par
“Then we must poignard those in our power, and take our chance about\par
mastering the rest. However, to avoid running such a risque, hasten to\par
the Cavern: The Banditti never leave it before eleven, and if you use\par
diligence, you may reach it in time to stop them.”\par
\par
“Tell Robert that I have taken his Horse: My own has broken his bridle,\par
and escaped into the Wood. What is the watch-word?”\par
\par
“The reward of Courage.”\par
\par
“’Tis sufficient. I hasten to the Cavern.”\par
\par
“And I to rejoin my Guests, lest my absence should create suspicion.\par
Farewell, and be diligent.”\par
\par
These worthy Associates now separated: The One bent his course towards\par
the Stable, while the Other returned to the House.\par
\par
You may judge, what must have been my feelings during this\par
conversation, of which I lost not a single syllable. I dared not trust\par
myself to my reflections, nor did any means present itself to escape\par
the dangers which threatened me. Resistance, I knew to be vain; I was\par
unarmed, and a single Man against Three: However, I resolved at least\par
to sell my life as dearly as I could. Dreading lest Baptiste should\par
perceive my absence, and suspect me to have overheard the message with\par
which Claude was dispatched, I hastily relighted my candle and quitted\par
the chamber. On descending, I found the Table spread for six Persons.\par
The Baroness sat by the fireside: Marguerite was employed in dressing a\par
sallad, and her Step-sons were whispering together at the further end\par
of the room. Baptiste having the round of the Garden to make, ere He\par
could reach the Cottage door, was not yet arrived. I seated myself\par
quietly opposite to the Baroness.\par
\par
A glance upon Marguerite told her that her hint had not been thrown\par
away upon me. How different did She now appear to me! What before\par
seemed gloom and sullenness, I now found to be disgust at her\par
Associates, and compassion for my danger. I looked up to her as to my\par
only resource; Yet knowing her to be watched by her Husband with a\par
suspicious eye, I could place but little reliance on the exertions of\par
her good-will.\par
\par
In spite of all my endeavours to conceal it, my agitation was but too\par
visibly expressed upon my countenance. I was pale, and both my words\par
and actions were disordered and embarrassed. The young Men observed\par
this, and enquired the cause. I attributed it to excess of fatigue, and\par
the violent effect produced on me by the severity of the season.\par
Whether they believed me or not, I will not pretend to say: They at\par
least ceased to embarrass me with their questions. I strove to divert\par
my attention from the perils which surrounded me, by conversing on\par
different subjects with the Baroness. I talked of Germany, declaring my\par
intention of visiting it immediately: God knows, that I little thought\par
at that moment of ever seeing it! She replied to me with great ease and\par
politeness, professed that the pleasure of making my acquaintance amply\par
compensated for the delay in her journey, and gave me a pressing\par
invitation to make some stay at the Castle of Lindenberg. As She spoke\par
thus, the Youths exchanged a malicious smile, which declared that She\par
would be fortunate if She ever reached that Castle herself. This action\par
did not escape me; But I concealed the emotion which it excited in my\par
breast. I continued to converse with the Lady; But my discourse was so\par
frequently incoherent, that as She has since informed me, She began to\par
doubt whether I was in my right senses. The fact was, that while my\par
conversation turned upon one subject, my thoughts were entirely\par
occupied by another. I meditated upon the means of quitting the\par
Cottage, finding my way to the Barn, and giving the Domestics\par
information of our Host’s designs. I was soon convinced, how\par
impracticable was the attempt. Jacques and Robert watched my every\par
movement with an attentive eye, and I was obliged to abandon the idea.\par
All my hopes now rested upon Claude’s not finding the Banditti: In that\par
case, according to what I had overheard, we should be permitted to\par
depart unhurt.\par
\par
I shuddered involuntarily as Baptiste entered the room. He made many\par
apologies for his long absence, but “He had been detained by affairs\par
impossible to be delayed.” He then entreated permission for his family\par
to sup at the same table with us, without which, respect would not\par
authorize his taking such a liberty. Oh! how in my heart I cursed the\par
Hypocrite! How I loathed his presence, who was on the point of\par
depriving me of an existence, at that time infinitely dear! I had every\par
reason to be satisfied with life; I had youth, wealth, rank, and\par
education; and the fairest prospects presented themselves before me. I\par
saw those prospects on the point of closing in the most horrible\par
manner: Yet was I obliged to dissimulate, and to receive with a\par
semblance of gratitude the false civilities of him who held the dagger\par
to my bosom.\par
\par
The permission which our Host demanded, was easily obtained. We seated\par
ourselves at the Table. The Baroness and myself occupied one side: The\par
Sons were opposite to us with their backs to the door. Baptiste took\par
his seat by the Baroness at the upper end, and the place next to him\par
was left for his Wife. She soon entered the room, and placed before us\par
a plain but comfortable Peasant’s repast. Our Host thought it necessary\par
to apologize for the poorness of the supper: “He had not been apprized\par
of our coming; He could only offer us such fare as had been intended\par
for his own family:”\par
\par
“But,” added He, “should any accident detain my noble Guests longer\par
than they at present intend, I hope to give them a better treatment.”\par
\par
The Villain! I well knew the accident to which He alluded; I shuddered\par
at the treatment which He taught us to expect!\par
\par
My Companion in danger seemed entirely to have got rid of her chagrin\par
at being delayed. She laughed, and conversed with the family with\par
infinite gaiety. I strove but in vain to follow her example. My spirits\par
were evidently forced, and the constraint which I put upon myself\par
escaped not Baptiste’s observation.\par
\par
“Come, come, Monsieur, cheer up!” said He; “You seem not quite\par
recovered from your fatigue. To raise your spirits, what say you to a\par
glass of excellent old wine which was left me by my Father? God rest\par
his soul, He is in a better world! I seldom produce this wine; But as I\par
am not honoured with such Guests every day, this is an occasion which\par
deserves a Bottle.”\par
\par
He then gave his Wife a Key, and instructed her where to find the wine\par
of which He spoke. She seemed by no means pleased with the commission;\par
She took the Key with an embarrassed air, and hesitated to quit the\par
Table.\par
\par
“Did you hear me?” said Baptiste in an angry tone.\par
\par
Marguerite darted upon him a look of mingled anger and fear, and left\par
the chamber. His eyes followed her suspiciously, till She had closed\par
the door.\par
\par
She soon returned with a bottle sealed with yellow wax. She placed it\par
upon the table, and gave the Key back to her Husband. I suspected that\par
this liquor was not presented to us without design, and I watched\par
Marguerite’s movements with inquietude. She was employed in rinsing\par
some small horn Goblets. As She placed them before Baptiste, She saw\par
that my eye was fixed upon her; and at the moment when She thought\par
herself unobserved by the Banditti, She motioned to me with her head\par
not to taste the liquor, She then resumed her place.\par
\par
In the mean while our Host had drawn the Cork, and filling two of the\par
Goblets, offered them to the Lady and myself. She at first made some\par
objections, but the instances of Baptiste were so urgent, that She was\par
obliged to comply. Fearing to excite suspicion, I hesitated not to take\par
the Goblet presented to me. By its smell and colour I guessed it to be\par
Champagne; But some grains of powder floating upon the top convinced me\par
that it was not unadulterated. However, I dared not to express my\par
repugnance to drinking it; I lifted it to my lips, and seemed to be\par
swallowing it: Suddenly starting from my chair, I made the best of my\par
way towards a Vase of water at some distance, in which Marguerite had\par
been rinsing the Goblets. I pretended to spit out the wine with\par
disgust, and took an opportunity unperceived of emptying the liquor\par
into the Vase.\par
\par
The Banditti seemed alarmed at my action. Jacques half rose from his\par
chair, put his hand into his bosom, and I discovered the haft of a\par
dagger. I returned to my seat with tranquillity, and affected not to\par
have observed their confusion.\par
\par
“You have not suited my taste, honest Friend,” said I, addressing\par
myself to Baptiste. “I never can drink Champagne without its producing\par
a violent illness. I swallowed a few mouthfuls ere I was aware of its\par
quality, and fear that I shall suffer for my imprudence.”\par
\par
Baptiste and Jacques exchanged looks of distrust.\par
\par
“Perhaps,” said Robert, “the smell may be disagreeable to you.”\par
\par
He quitted his chair, and removed the Goblet. I observed, that He\par
examined, whether it was nearly empty.\par
\par
“He must have drank sufficient,” said He to his Brother in a low voice,\par
while He reseated himself.\par
\par
Marguerite looked apprehensive, that I had tasted the liquor: A glance\par
from my eye reassured her.\par
\par
I waited with anxiety for the effects which the Beverage would produce\par
upon the Lady. I doubted not but the grains which I had observed were\par
poisonous, and lamented that it had been impossible for me to warn her\par
of the danger. But a few minutes had elapsed before I perceived her\par
eyes grow heavy; Her head sank upon her shoulder, and She fell into a\par
deep sleep. I affected not to attend to this circumstance, and\par
continued my conversation with Baptiste, with all the outward gaiety in\par
my power to assume. But He no longer answered me without constraint. He\par
eyed me with distrust and astonishment, and I saw that the Banditti\par
were frequently whispering among themselves. My situation became every\par
moment more painful; I sustained the character of confidence with a\par
worse grace than ever. Equally afraid of the arrival of their\par
Accomplices and of their suspecting my knowledge of their designs, I\par
knew not how to dissipate the distrust which the Banditti evidently\par
entertained for me. In this new dilemma the friendly Marguerite again\par
assisted me. She passed behind the Chairs of her Stepsons, stopped for\par
a moment opposite to me, closed her eyes, and reclined her head upon\par
her shoulder. This hint immediately dispelled my incertitude. It told\par
me, that I ought to imitate the Baroness, and pretend that the liquor\par
had taken its full effect upon me. I did so, and in a few minutes\par
seemed perfectly overcome with slumber.\par
\par
“So!” cried Baptiste, as I fell back in my chair; “At last He sleeps! I\par
began to think that He had scented our design, and that we should have\par
been forced to dispatch him at all events.”\par
\par
“And why not dispatch him at all events?” enquired the ferocious\par
Jacques. “Why leave him the possibility of betraying our secret?\par
Marguerite, give me one of my Pistols: A single touch of the trigger\par
will finish him at once.”\par
\par
“And supposing,” rejoined the Father, “Supposing that our Friends\par
should not arrive tonight, a pretty figure we should make when the\par
Servants enquire for him in the Morning! No, no, Jacques; We must wait\par
for our Associates. If they join us, we are strong enough to dispatch\par
the Domestics as well as their Masters, and the booty is our own; If\par
Claude does not find the Troop, we must take patience, and suffer the\par
prey to slip through our fingers. Ah! Boys, Boys, had you arrived but\par
five minutes sooner, the Spaniard would have been done for, and two\par
thousand Pistoles our own. But you are always out of the way when you\par
are most wanted.\par
\par
You are the most unlucky Rogues!”\par
\par
“Well, well, Father!” answered Jacques; “Had you been of my mind, all\par
would have been over by this time. You, Robert, Claude, and myself, why\par
the Strangers were but double the number, and I warrant you we might\par
have mastered them. However, Claude is gone; ’Tis too late to think of\par
it now. We must wait patiently for the arrival of the Gang; and if the\par
Travellers escape us tonight, we must take care to waylay them\par
tomorrow.”\par
\par
“True! True!” said Baptiste; “Marguerite, have you given the\par
sleeping-draught to the Waiting-women?”\par
\par
She replied in the affirmative.\par
\par
“All then is safe. Come, come, Boys; Whatever falls out, we have no\par
reason to complain of this adventure. We run no danger, may gain much,\par
and can lose nothing.”\par
\par
At this moment I heard a trampling of Horses. Oh! how dreadful was the\par
sound to my ears. A cold sweat flowed down my forehead, and I felt all\par
the terrors of impending death. I was by no means reassured by hearing\par
the compassionate Marguerite exclaim in the accents of despair,\par
\par
“Almighty God! They are lost!”\par
\par
Luckily the Wood-man and his Sons were too much occupied by the arrival\par
of their Associates to attend to me, or the violence of my agitation\par
would have convinced them that my sleep was feigned.\par
\par
“Open! Open!” exclaimed several voices on the outside of the Cottage.\par
\par
“Yes! Yes!” cried Baptiste joyfully; “They are our Friends sure enough!\par
Now then our booty is certain. Away! Lads, Away! Lead them to the Barn;\par
You know what is to be done there.”\par
\par
Robert hastened to open the door of the Cottage.\par
\par
“But first,” said Jacques, taking up his arms; “first let me dispatch\par
these Sleepers.”\par
\par
“No, no, no!” replied his Father; “Go you to the Barn, where your\par
presence is wanted. Leave me to take care of these and the Women\par
above.”\par
\par
Jacques obeyed, and followed his Brother. They seemed to converse with\par
the New-Comers for a few minutes: After which I heard the Robbers\par
dismount, and as I conjectured, bend their course towards the Barn.\par
\par
“So! That is wisely done!” muttered Baptiste; “They have quitted their\par
Horses, that They may fall upon the Strangers by surprise. Good! Good!\par
and now to business.”\par
\par
I heard him approach a small Cupboard which was fixed up in a distant\par
part of the room, and unlock it. At this moment I felt myself shaken\par
gently.\par
\par
“Now! Now!” whispered Marguerite.\par
\par
I opened my eyes. Baptiste stood with his back towards me. No one else\par
was in the room save Marguerite and the sleeping Lady. The Villain had\par
taken a dagger from the Cupboard and seemed examining whether it was\par
sufficiently sharp. I had neglected to furnish myself with arms; But I\par
perceived this to be my only chance of escaping, and resolved not to\par
lose the opportunity. I sprang from my seat, darted suddenly upon\par
Baptiste, and clasping my hands round his throat, pressed it so\par
forcibly as to prevent his uttering a single cry. You may remember that\par
I was remarkable at Salamanca for the power of my arm: It now rendered\par
me an essential service. Surprised, terrified, and breathless, the\par
Villain was by no means an equal Antagonist. I threw him upon the\par
ground; I grasped him still tighter; and while I fixed him without\par
motion upon the floor, Marguerite, wresting the dagger from his hand,\par
plunged it repeatedly in his heart till He expired.\par
\par
No sooner was this horrible but necessary act perpetrated than\par
Marguerite called on me to follow her.\par
\par
“Flight is our only refuge!” said She; “Quick! Quick! Away!”\par
\par
I hesitated not to obey her: but unwilling to leave the Baroness a\par
victim to the vengeance of the Robbers, I raised her in my arms still\par
sleeping, and hastened after Marguerite. The Horses of the Banditti\par
were fastened near the door: My Conductress sprang upon one of them. I\par
followed her example, placed the Baroness before me, and spurred on my\par
Horse. Our only hope was to reach Strasbourg, which was much nearer\par
than the perfidious Claude had assured me. Marguerite was well\par
acquainted with the road, and galloped on before me. We were obliged to\par
pass by the Barn, where the Robbers were slaughtering our Domestics.\par
The door was open: We distinguished the shrieks of the dying and\par
imprecations of the Murderers! What I felt at that moment language is\par
unable to describe!\par
\par
Jacques heard the trampling of our Horses as we rushed by the Barn. He\par
flew to the Door with a burning Torch in his hand, and easily\par
recognised the Fugitives.\par
\par
“Betrayed! Betrayed!” He shouted to his Companions.\par
\par
Instantly they left their bloody work, and hastened to regain their\par
Horses. We heard no more. I buried my spurs in the sides of my Courser,\par
and Marguerite goaded on hers with the poignard, which had already\par
rendered us such good service. We flew like lightning, and gained the\par
open plains. Already was Strasbourg’s Steeple in sight, when we heard\par
the Robbers pursuing us. Marguerite looked back, and distinguished our\par
followers descending a small Hill at no great distance. It was in vain\par
that we urged on our Horses; The noise approached nearer with every\par
moment.\par
\par
“We are lost!” She exclaimed; “The Villains gain upon us!”\par
\par
“On! On!” replied I; “I hear the trampling of Horses coming from the\par
Town.”\par
\par
We redoubled our exertions, and were soon aware of a numerous band of\par
Cavaliers, who came towards us at full speed. They were on the point of\par
passing us.\par
\par
“Stay! Stay!” shrieked Marguerite; “Save us! For God’s sake, save us!”\par
\par
The Foremost, who seemed to act as Guide, immediately reined in his\par
Steed.\par
\par
“’Tis She! ’Tis She!” exclaimed He, springing upon the ground; “Stop,\par
my Lord, stop! They are safe! ’Tis my Mother!”\par
\par
At the same moment Marguerite threw herself from her Horse, clasped him\par
in her arms, and covered him with Kisses. The other Cavaliers stopped\par
at the exclamation.\par
\par
“The Baroness Lindenberg?” cried another of the Strangers eagerly;\par
“Where is She? Is She not with you?”\par
\par
He stopped on beholding her lying senseless in my arms. Hastily He\par
caught her from me. The profound sleep in which She was plunged made\par
him at first tremble for her life; but the beating of her heart soon\par
reassured him.\par
\par
“God be thanked!” said He; “She has escaped unhurt.”\par
\par
I interrupted his joy by pointing out the Brigands, who continued to\par
approach. No sooner had I mentioned them than the greatest part of the\par
Company, which appeared to be chiefly composed of soldiers, hastened\par
forward to meet them. The Villains stayed not to receive their attack:\par
Perceiving their danger they turned the heads of their Horses, and fled\par
into the wood, whither they were followed by our Preservers. In the\par
mean while the Stranger, whom I guessed to be the Baron Lindenberg,\par
after thanking me for my care of his Lady, proposed our returning with\par
all speed to the Town. The Baroness, on whom the effects of the opiate\par
had not ceased to operate, was placed before him; Marguerite and her\par
Son remounted their Horses; the Baron’s Domestics followed, and we soon\par
arrived at the Inn, where He had taken his apartments.\par
\par
This was at the Austrian Eagle, where my Banker, whom before my\par
quitting Paris I had apprised of my intention to visit Strasbourg, had\par
prepared Lodgings for me. I rejoiced at this circumstance. It gave me\par
an opportunity of cultivating the Baron’s acquaintance, which I foresaw\par
would be of use to me in Germany. Immediately upon our arrival the Lady\par
was conveyed to bed; A Physician was sent for, who prescribed a\par
medicine likely to counteract the effects of the sleepy potion, and\par
after it had been poured down her throat, She was committed to the care\par
of the Hostess. The Baron then addressed himself to me, and entreated\par
me to recount the particulars of this adventure. I complied with his\par
request instantaneously; for in pain respecting Stephano’s fate, whom I\par
had been compelled to abandon to the cruelty of the Banditti, I found\par
it impossible for me to repose, till I had some news of him. I received\par
but too soon the intelligence, that my trusty Servant had perished. The\par
Soldiers who had pursued the Brigands returned while I was employed in\par
relating my adventure to the Baron. By their account I found that the\par
Robbers had been overtaken: Guilt and true courage are incompatible;\par
They had thrown themselves at the feet of their Pursuers, had\par
surrendered themselves without striking a blow, had discovered their\par
secret retreat, made known their signals by which the rest of the Gang\par
might be seized, and in short had betrayed ever mark of cowardice and\par
baseness. By this means the whole of the Band, consisting of near sixty\par
persons, had been made Prisoners, bound, and conducted to Strasbourg.\par
Some of the Soldiers hastened to the Cottage, One of the Banditti\par
serving them as Guide. Their first visit was to the fatal Barn, where\par
they were fortunate enough to find two of the Baron’s Servants still\par
alive, though desperately wounded. The rest had expired beneath the\par
swords of the Robbers, and of these my unhappy Stephano was one.\par
\par
Alarmed at our escape, the Robbers in their haste to overtake us, had\par
neglected to visit the Cottage. In consequence, the Soldiers found the\par
two Waiting-women unhurt, and buried in the same death-like slumber\par
which had overpowered their Mistress. There was nobody else found in\par
the Cottage, except a child not above four years old, which the\par
Soldiers brought away with them. We were busying ourselves with\par
conjectures respecting the birth of this little unfortunate, when\par
Marguerite rushed into the room with the Baby in her arms. She fell at\par
the feet of the Officer who was making us this report, and blessed him\par
a thousand times for the preservation of her Child.\par
\par
When the first burst of maternal tenderness was over, I besought her to\par
declare, by what means She had been united to a Man whose principles\par
seemed so totally discordant with her own. She bent her eyes downwards,\par
and wiped a few tears from her cheek.\par
\par
“Gentlemen,” said She after a silence of some minutes, “I would request\par
a favour of you: You have a right to know on whom you confer an\par
obligation. I will not therefore stifle a confession which covers me\par
with shame; But permit me to comprise it in as few words as possible.\par
\par
“I was born in Strasbourg of respectable Parents; Their names I must at\par
present conceal: My Father still lives, and deserves not to be involved\par
in my infamy; If you grant my request, you shall be informed of my\par
family name. A Villain made himself Master of my affections, and to\par
follow him I quitted my Father’s House. Yet though my passions\par
overpowered my virtue, I sank not into that degeneracy of vice, but too\par
commonly the lot of Women who make the first false step. I loved my\par
Seducer; dearly loved him! I was true to his Bed; this Baby, and the\par
Youth who warned you, my Lord Baron, of your Lady’s danger, are the\par
pledges of our affection. Even at this moment I lament his loss, though\par
’tis to him that I owe all the miseries of my existence.\par
\par
“He was of noble birth, but He had squandered away his paternal\par
inheritance. His Relations considered him as a disgrace to their name,\par
and utterly discarded him. His excesses drew upon him the indignation\par
of the Police. He was obliged to fly from Strasbourg, and saw no other\par
resource from beggary than an union with the Banditti who infested the\par
neighbouring Forest, and whose Troop was chiefly composed of Young Men\par
of family in the same predicament with himself. I was determined not to\par
forsake him. I followed him to the Cavern of the Brigands, and shared\par
with him the misery inseparable from a life of pillage. But though I\par
was aware that our existence was supported by plunder, I knew not all\par
the horrible circumstances attached to my Lover’s profession. These He\par
concealed from me with the utmost care; He was conscious that my\par
sentiments were not sufficiently depraved to look without horror upon\par
assassination: He supposed, and with justice, that I should fly with\par
detestation from the embraces of a Murderer. Eight years of possession\par
had not abated his love for me; and He cautiously removed from my\par
knowledge every circumstance, which might lead me to suspect the crimes\par
in which He but too often participated. He succeeded perfectly: It was\par
not till after my Seducer’s death, that I discovered his hands to have\par
been stained with the blood of innocence.\par
\par
“One fatal night He was brought back to the Cavern covered with wounds:\par
He received them in attacking an English Traveller, whom his Companions\par
immediately sacrificed to their resentment. He had only time to entreat\par
my pardon for all the sorrows which He had caused me: He pressed my\par
hand to his lips, and expired. My grief was inexpressible. As soon as\par
its violence abated, I resolved to return to Strasbourg, to throw\par
myself with my two Children at my Father’s feet, and implore his\par
forgiveness, though I little hoped to obtain it. What was my\par
consternation when informed that no one entrusted with the secret of\par
their retreat was ever permitted to quit the troop of the Banditti;\par
That I must give up all hopes of ever rejoining society, and consent\par
instantly to accepting one of their Band for my Husband! My prayers and\par
remonstrances were vain. They cast lots to decide to whose possession I\par
should fall; I became the property of the infamous Baptiste. A Robber,\par
who had once been a Monk, pronounced over us a burlesque rather than a\par
religious Ceremony: I and my Children were delivered into the hands of\par
my new Husband, and He conveyed us immediately to his home.\par
\par
“He assured me that He had long entertained for me the most ardent\par
regard; But that Friendship for my deceased Lover had obliged him to\par
stifle his desires. He endeavoured to reconcile me to my fate, and for\par
some time treated me with respect and gentleness: At length finding\par
that my aversion rather increased than diminished, He obtained those\par
favours by violence, which I persisted to refuse him. No resource\par
remained for me but to bear my sorrows with patience; I was conscious\par
that I deserved them but too well. Flight was forbidden: My Children\par
were in the power of Baptiste, and He had sworn that if I attempted to\par
escape, their lives should pay for it. I had had too many opportunities\par
of witnessing the barbarity of his nature to doubt his fulfilling his\par
oath to the very letter. Sad experience had convinced me of the horrors\par
of my situation: My first Lover had carefully concealed them from me;\par
Baptiste rather rejoiced in opening my eyes to the cruelties of his\par
profession, and strove to familiarise me with blood and slaughter.\par
\par
“My nature was licentious and warm, but not cruel: My conduct had been\par
imprudent, but my heart was not unprincipled. Judge then what I must\par
have felt at being a continual witness of crimes the most horrible and\par
revolting! Judge how I must have grieved at being united to a Man who\par
received the unsuspecting Guest with an air of openness and\par
hospitality, at the very moment that He meditated his destruction.\par
Chagrin and discontent preyed upon my constitution: The few charms\par
bestowed on me by nature withered away, and the dejection of my\par
countenance denoted the sufferings of my heart. I was tempted a\par
thousand times to put an end to my existence; But the remembrance of my\par
Children held my hand. I trembled to leave my dear Boys in my Tyrant’s\par
power, and trembled yet more for their virtue than their lives. The\par
Second was still too young to benefit by my instructions; But in the\par
heart of my Eldest I laboured unceasingly to plant those principles,\par
which might enable him to avoid the crimes of his Parents. He listened\par
to me with docility, or rather with eagerness. Even at his early age,\par
He showed that He was not calculated for the society of Villains; and\par
the only comfort which I enjoyed among my sorrows, was to witness the\par
dawning virtues of my Theodore.\par
\par
“Such was my situation, when the perfidy of Don Alphonso’s postillion\par
conducted him to the Cottage. His youth, air, and manners interested me\par
most forcibly in his behalf. The absence of my Husband’s Sons gave me\par
an opportunity which I had long wished to find, and I resolved to\par
risque every thing to preserve the Stranger. The vigilance of Baptiste\par
prevented me from warning Don Alphonso of his danger: I knew that my\par
betraying the secret would be immediately punished with death; and\par
however embittered was my life by calamities, I wanted courage to\par
sacrifice it for the sake of preserving that of another Person. My only\par
hope rested upon procuring succour from Strasbourg: At this I resolved\par
to try; and should an opportunity offer of warning Don Alphonso of his\par
danger unobserved, I was determined to seize it with avidity. By\par
Baptiste’s orders I went upstairs to make the Stranger’s Bed: I spread\par
upon it Sheets in which a Traveller had been murdered but a few nights\par
before, and which still were stained with blood. I hoped that these\par
marks would not escape the vigilance of our Guest, and that He would\par
collect from them the designs of my perfidious Husband. Neither was\par
this the only step which I took to preserve the Stranger. Theodore was\par
confined to his bed by illness. I stole into his room unobserved by my\par
Tyrant, communicated to him my project, and He entered into it with\par
eagerness. He rose in spite of his malady, and dressed himself with all\par
speed. I fastened one of the Sheets round his arms, and lowered him\par
from the Window. He flew to the Stable, took Claude’s Horse, and\par
hastened to Strasbourg. Had He been accosted by the Banditti, He was to\par
have declared himself sent upon a message by Baptiste, but fortunately\par
He reached the Town without meeting any obstacle. Immediately upon his\par
arrival at Strasbourg, He entreated assistance from the Magistrature:\par
His Story passed from mouth to mouth, and at length came to the\par
knowledge of my Lord the Baron. Anxious for the safety of his Lady,\par
whom He knew would be upon the road that Evening, it struck him that\par
She might have fallen into the power of the Robbers. He accompanied\par
Theodore who guided the Soldiers towards the Cottage, and arrived just\par
in time to save us from falling once more into the hands of our\par
Enemies.”\par
\par
Here I interrupted Marguerite to enquire why the sleepy potion had been\par
presented to me. She said that Baptiste supposed me to have arms about\par
me, and wished to incapacitate me from making resistance: It was a\par
precaution which He always took, since as the Travellers had no hopes\par
of escaping, Despair would have incited them to sell their lives\par
dearly.\par
\par
The Baron then desired Marguerite to inform him, what were her present\par
plans. I joined him in declaring my readiness to show my gratitude to\par
her for the preservation of my life.\par
\par
“Disgusted with a world,” She replied, “in which I have met with\par
nothing but misfortunes, my only wish is to retire into a Convent. But\par
first I must provide for my Children. I find that my Mother is no more,\par
probably driven to an untimely grave by my desertion! My Father is\par
still living; He is not an hard Man; Perhaps, Gentlemen, in spite of my\par
ingratitude and imprudence, your intercessions may induce him to\par
forgive me, and to take charge of his unfortunate Grand-sons. If you\par
obtain this boon for me, you will repay my services a thousand-fold!”\par
\par
Both the Baron and myself assured Marguerite, that we would spare no\par
pains to obtain her pardon: and that even should her Father be\par
inflexible, She need be under no apprehensions respecting the fate of\par
her Children. I engaged myself to provide for Theodore, and the Baron\par
promised to take the youngest under his protection.\par
\par
The grateful Mother thanked us with tears for what She called\par
generosity, but which in fact was no more than a proper sense of our\par
obligations to her. She then left the room to put her little Boy to\par
bed, whom fatigue and sleep had compleatly overpowered.\par
\par
The Baroness, on recovering and being informed from what dangers I had\par
rescued her, set no bounds to the expressions of her gratitude. She was\par
joined so warmly by her Husband in pressing me to accompany them to\par
their Castle in Bavaria, that I found it impossible to resist their\par
entreaties. During a week which we passed at Strasbourg, the interests\par
of Marguerite were not forgotten: In our application to her Father we\par
succeeded as amply as we could wish. The good old Man had lost his\par
Wife: He had no Children but this unfortunate Daughter, of whom He had\par
received no news for almost fourteen years. He was surrounded by\par
distant Relations, who waited with impatience for his decease in order\par
to get possession of his money. When therefore Marguerite appeared\par
again so unexpectedly, He considered her as a gift from heaven: He\par
received her and her Children with open arms, and insisted upon their\par
establishing themselves in his House without delay. The disappointed\par
Cousins were obliged to give place. The old Man would not hear of his\par
Daughter’s retiring into a Convent: He said that She was too necessary\par
to his happiness, and She was easily persuaded to relinquish her\par
design. But no persuasions could induce Theodore to give up the plan\par
which I had at first marked out for him. He had attached himself to me\par
most sincerely during my stay at Strasbourg; and when I was on the\par
point of leaving it, He besought me with tears to take him into my\par
service: He set forth all his little talents in the most favourable\par
colours, and tried to convince me that I should find him of infinite\par
use to me upon the road. I was unwilling to charge myself with a Lad\par
but scarcely turned of thirteen, whom I knew could only be a burthen to\par
me: However, I could not resist the entreaties of this affectionate\par
Youth, who in fact possessed a thousand estimable qualities. With some\par
difficulty He persuaded his relations to let him follow me, and that\par
permission once obtained, He was dubbed with the title of my Page.\par
Having passed a week at Strasbourg, Theodore and myself set out for\par
Bavaria in company with the Baron and his Lady. These Latter as well as\par
myself had forced Marguerite to accept several presents of value, both\par
for herself, and her youngest Son: On leaving her, I promised his\par
Mother faithfully that I would restore Theodore to her within the year.\par
\par
I have related this adventure at length, Lorenzo, that you might\par
understand the means by which “The Adventurer, Alphonso d’Alvarada got\par
introduced into the Castle of Lindenberg.” Judge from this specimen how\par
much faith should be given to your Aunt’s assertions!\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER IV.\par
\par
\par
Avaunt! and quit my sight! Let the Earth hide thee!\par
Thy bones are marrowless, thy blood is cold!\par
Thou hast no speculation in those eyes\par
Which Thou dost glare with! Hence, horrible shadow!\par
Unreal mockery hence!\par
\par
MACBETH.\par
\par
Continuation of the History of Don Raymond.\par
\par
My journey was uncommonly agreeable: I found the Baron a Man of some\par
sense, but little knowledge of the world. He had past a great part of\par
his life without stirring beyond the precincts of his own domains, and\par
consequently his manners were far from being the most polished: But He\par
was hearty, good-humoured, and friendly. His attention to me was all\par
that I could wish, and I had every reason to be satisfied with his\par
behaviour. His ruling passion was Hunting, which He had brought himself\par
to consider as a serious occupation; and when talking over some\par
remarkable chace, He treated the subject with as much gravity as it had\par
been a Battle on which the fate of two kingdoms was depending. I\par
happened to be a tolerable Sportsman: Soon after my arrival at\par
Lindenberg I gave some proofs of my dexterity. The Baron immediately\par
marked me down for a Man of Genius, and vowed to me an eternal\par
friendship.\par
\par
That friendship was become to me by no means indifferent. At the Castle\par
of Lindenberg I beheld for the first time your Sister, the lovely\par
Agnes. For me whose heart was unoccupied, and who grieved at the void,\par
to see her and to love her were the same. I found in Agnes all that was\par
requisite to secure my affection. She was then scarcely sixteen; Her\par
person light and elegant was already formed; She possessed several\par
talents in perfection, particularly those of Music and drawing: Her\par
character was gay, open, and good-humoured; and the graceful simplicity\par
of her dress and manners formed an advantageous contrast to the art and\par
studied Coquetry of the Parisian Dames, whom I had just quitted. From\par
the moment that I beheld her, I felt the most lively interest in her\par
fate. I made many enquiries respecting her of the Baroness.\par
\par
“She is my Niece,” replied that Lady; “You are still ignorant, Don\par
Alphonso, that I am your Countrywoman. I am Sister to the Duke of\par
Medina Celi: Agnes is the Daughter of my second Brother, Don Gaston:\par
She has been destined to the Convent from her cradle, and will soon\par
make her profession at Madrid.”\par
\par
(Here Lorenzo interrupted the Marquis by an exclamation of surprise.\par
\par
“Intended for the Convent from her cradle?” said He; “By heaven, this\par
is the first word that I ever heard of such a design!”\par
\par
“I believe it, my dear Lorenzo,” answered Don Raymond; “But you must\par
listen to me with patience. You will not be less surprised, when I\par
relate some particulars of your family still unknown to you, and which\par
I have learnt from the mouth of Agnes herself.”\par
\par
He then resumed his narrative as follows.)\par
\par
You cannot but be aware that your Parents were unfortunately Slaves to\par
the grossest superstition: When this foible was called into play, their\par
every other sentiment, their every other passion yielded to its\par
irresistible strength. While She was big with Agnes, your Mother was\par
seized by a dangerous illness, and given over by her Physicians. In\par
this situation, Donna Inesilla vowed, that if She recovered from her\par
malady, the Child then living in her bosom if a Girl should be\par
dedicated to St. Clare, if a Boy to St. Benedict. Her prayers were\par
heard; She got rid of her complaint; Agnes entered the world alive, and\par
was immediately destined to the service of St. Clare.\par
\par
Don Gaston readily chimed in with his Lady’s wishes: But knowing the\par
sentiments of the Duke, his Brother, respecting a Monastic life, it was\par
determined that your Sister’s destination should be carefully concealed\par
from him. The better to guard the secret, it was resolved that Agnes\par
should accompany her Aunt, Donna Rodolpha into Germany, whither that\par
Lady was on the point of following her new-married Husband, Baron\par
Lindenberg. On her arrival at that Estate, the young Agnes was put into\par
a Convent, situated but a few miles from the Castle. The Nuns to whom\par
her education was confided performed their charge with exactitude: They\par
made her a perfect Mistress of many talents, and strove to infuse into\par
her mind a taste for the retirement and tranquil pleasures of a\par
Convent. But a secret instinct made the young Recluse sensible that She\par
was not born for solitude: In all the freedom of youth and gaiety, She\par
scrupled not to treat as ridiculous many ceremonies which the Nuns\par
regarded with awe; and She was never more happy than when her lively\par
imagination inspired her with some scheme to plague the stiff Lady\par
Abbess, or the ugly ill-tempered old Porteress. She looked with disgust\par
upon the prospect before her: However no alternative was offered to\par
her, and She submitted to the decree of her Parents, though not without\par
secret repining.\par
\par
That repugnance She had not art enough to conceal long: Don Gaston was\par
informed of it. Alarmed, Lorenzo, lest your affection for her should\par
oppose itself to his projects, and lest you should positively object to\par
your Sister’s misery, He resolved to keep the whole affair from _your_\par
knowledge as well as the Duke’s, till the sacrifice should be\par
consummated. The season of her taking the veil was fixed for the time\par
when you should be upon your travels: In the meanwhile no hint was\par
dropped of Donna Inesilla’s fatal vow. Your Sister was never permitted\par
to know your direction. All your letters were read before She received\par
them, and those parts effaced, which were likely to nourish her\par
inclination for the world: Her answers were dictated either by her\par
Aunt, or by Dame Cunegonda, her Governess. These particulars I learnt\par
partly from Agnes, partly from the Baroness herself.\par
\par
I immediately determined upon rescuing this lovely Girl from a fate so\par
contrary to her inclinations, and ill-suited to her merit. I\par
endeavoured to ingratiate myself into her favour: I boasted of my\par
friendship and intimacy with you. She listened to me with avidity; She\par
seemed to devour my words while I spoke in your praise, and her eyes\par
thanked me for my affection to her Brother. My constant and unremitted\par
attention at length gained me her heart, and with difficulty I obliged\par
her to confess that She loved me. When however, I proposed her quitting\par
the Castle of Lindenberg, She rejected the idea in positive terms.\par
\par
“Be generous, Alphonso,” She said; “You possess my heart, but use not\par
the gift ignobly. Employ not your ascendancy over me in persuading me\par
to take a step, at which I should hereafter have to blush. I am young\par
and deserted: My Brother, my only Friend, is separated from me, and my\par
other Relations act with me as my Enemies. Take pity on my unprotected\par
situation. Instead of seducing me to an action which would cover me\par
with shame, strive rather to gain the affections of those who govern\par
me. The Baron esteems you. My Aunt, to others ever harsh proud and\par
contemptuous, remembers that you rescued her from the hands of\par
Murderers, and wears with you alone the appearance of kindness and\par
benignity. Try then your influence over my Guardians. If they consent\par
to our union my hand is yours: From your account of my Brother, I\par
cannot doubt your obtaining his approbation: And when they find the\par
impossibility of executing their design, I trust that my Parents will\par
excuse my disobedience, and expiate by some other sacrifice my Mother’s\par
fatal vow.”\par
\par
From the first moment that I beheld Agnes, I had endeavoured to\par
conciliate the favour of her Relations. Authorised by the confession of\par
her regard, I redoubled my exertions. My principal Battery was directed\par
against the Baroness; It was easy to discover that her word was law in\par
the Castle: Her Husband paid her the most absolute submission, and\par
considered her as a superior Being. She was about forty: In her youth\par
She had been a Beauty; But her charms had been upon that large scale\par
which can but ill sustain the shock of years: However She still\par
possessed some remains of them. Her understanding was strong and\par
excellent when not obscured by prejudice, which unluckily was but\par
seldom the case. Her passions were violent: She spared no pains to\par
gratify them, and pursued with unremitting vengeance those who opposed\par
themselves to her wishes. The warmest of Friends, the most inveterate\par
of Enemies, such was the Baroness Lindenberg.\par
\par
I laboured incessantly to please her: Unluckily I succeeded but too\par
well. She seemed gratified by my attention, and treated me with a\par
distinction accorded by her to no one else. One of my daily occupations\par
was reading to her for several hours: Those hours I should much rather\par
have past with Agnes; But as I was conscious that complaisance for her\par
Aunt would advance our union, I submitted with a good grace to the\par
penance imposed upon me. Donna Rodolpha’s Library was principally\par
composed of old Spanish Romances: These were her favourite studies, and\par
once a day one of these unmerciful Volumes was put regularly into my\par
hands. I read the wearisome adventures of “_Perceforest_,” “_Tirante\par
the White_,” “_Palmerin of England_,” and “_the Knight of the Sun_,”\par
till the Book was on the point of falling from my hands through Ennui.\par
However, the increasing pleasure which the Baroness seemed to take in\par
my society, encouraged me to persevere; and latterly She showed for me\par
a partiality so marked, that Agnes advised me to seize the first\par
opportunity of declaring our mutual passion to her Aunt.\par
\par
One Evening, I was alone with Donna Rodolpha in her own apartment. As\par
our readings generally treated of love, Agnes was never permitted to\par
assist at them. I was just congratulating myself on having finished\par
“_The Loves of Tristan and the Queen Iseult_——”\par
\par
“Ah! The Unfortunates!” cried the Baroness; “How say you, Segnor? Do\par
you think it possible for Man to feel an attachment so disinterested\par
and sincere?”\par
\par
“I cannot doubt it,” replied I; “My own heart furnishes me with the\par
certainty. Ah! Donna Rodolpha, might I but hope for your approbation of\par
my love! Might I but confess the name of my Mistress without incurring\par
your resentment!”\par
\par
She interrupted me.\par
\par
“Suppose, I were to spare you that confession? Suppose I were to\par
acknowledge that the object of your desires is not unknown to me?\par
Suppose I were to say that She returns your affection, and laments not\par
less sincerely than yourself the unhappy vows which separate her from\par
you?”\par
\par
“Ah! Donna Rodolpha!” I exclaimed, throwing myself upon my knees before\par
her, and pressing her hand to my lips, “You have discovered my secret!\par
What is your decision? Must I despair, or may I reckon upon your\par
favour?”\par
\par
She withdrew not the hand which I held; But She turned from me, and\par
covered her face with the other.\par
\par
“How can I refuse it you?” She replied; “Ah! Don Alphonso, I have long\par
perceived to whom your attentions were directed, but till now I\par
perceived not the impression which they made upon my heart.\par
\par
At length I can no longer hide my weakness either from myself or from\par
you. I yield to the violence of my passion, and own that I adore you!\par
For three long months I stifled my desires; But grown stronger by\par
resistance, I submit to their impetuosity. Pride, fear, and honour,\par
respect for myself, and my engagements to the Baron, all are\par
vanquished. I sacrifice them to my love for you, and it still seems to\par
me that I pay too mean a price for your possession.”\par
\par
She paused for an answer.—Judge, my Lorenzo, what must have been my\par
confusion at this discovery. I at once saw all the magnitude of this\par
obstacle, which I had raised myself to my happiness. The Baroness had\par
placed those attentions to her own account, which I had merely paid her\par
for the sake of Agnes: And the strength of her expressions, the looks\par
which accompanied them, and my knowledge of her revengeful disposition\par
made me tremble for myself and my Beloved. I was silent for some\par
minutes. I knew not how to reply to her declaration: I could only\par
resolve to clear up the mistake without delay, and for the present to\par
conceal from her knowledge the name of my Mistress. No sooner had She\par
avowed her passion than the transports which before were evident in my\par
features gave place to consternation and constraint. I dropped her\par
hand, and rose from my knees. The change in my countenance did not\par
escape her observation.\par
\par
“What means this silence?” said She in a trembling voice; “Where is\par
that joy which you led me to expect?”\par
\par
“Forgive me, Segnora,” I answered, “if what necessity forces from me\par
should seem harsh and ungrateful: To encourage you in an error, which,\par
however it may flatter myself, must prove to you the source of\par
disappointment, would make me appear criminal in every eye. Honour\par
obliges me to inform you that you have mistaken for the solicitude of\par
Love what was only the attention of Friendship. The latter sentiment is\par
that which I wished to excite in your bosom: To entertain a warmer,\par
respect for you forbids me, and gratitude for the Baron’s generous\par
treatment. Perhaps these reasons would not be sufficient to shield me\par
from your attractions, were it not that my affections are already\par
bestowed upon another. You have charms, Segnora, which might captivate\par
the most insensible; No heart unoccupied could resist them. Happy is it\par
for me that mine is no longer in my possession; or I should have to\par
reproach myself for ever with having violated the Laws of Hospitality.\par
Recollect yourself, noble Lady; Recollect what is owed by you to\par
honour, by me to the Baron, and replace by esteem and friendship those\par
sentiments which I never can return.”\par
\par
The Baroness turned pale at this unexpected and positive declaration:\par
She doubted whether She slept or woke. At length recovering from her\par
surprise, consternation gave place to rage, and the blood rushed back\par
into her cheeks with violence.\par
\par
“Villain!” She cried; “Monster of deceit! Thus is the avowal of my love\par
received? Is it thus that.... But no, no! It cannot, it shall not be!\par
Alphonso, behold me at your feet! Be witness of my despair! Look with\par
pity on a Woman who loves you with sincere affection! She who possesses\par
your heart, how has She merited such a treasure? What sacrifice has She\par
made to you?\par
\par
What raises her above Rodolpha?”\par
\par
I endeavoured to lift her from her Knees.\par
\par
“For God’s sake, Segnora, restrain these transports: They disgrace\par
yourself and me. Your exclamations may be heard, and your secret\par
divulged to your Attendants. I see that my presence only irritates you:\par
permit me to retire.”\par
\par
I prepared to quit the apartment: The Baroness caught me suddenly by\par
the arm.\par
\par
“And who is this happy Rival?” said She in a menacing tone; “I will\par
know her name, and _when_ I know it.... ! She is someone in my power;\par
You entreated my favour, my protection! Let me but find her, let me but\par
know who dares to rob me of your heart, and She shall suffer every\par
torment which jealousy and disappointment can inflict! Who is She?\par
Answer me this moment. Hope not to conceal her from my vengeance! Spies\par
shall be set over you; every step, every look shall be watched; Your\par
eyes will discover my Rival; I shall know her, and when She is found,\par
tremble, Alphonso for her and for yourself!”\par
\par
As She uttered these last words her fury mounted to such a pitch as to\par
stop her powers of respiration. She panted, groaned, and at length\par
fainted away. As She was falling I caught her in my arms, and placed\par
her upon a Sopha. Then hastening to the door, I summoned her Women to\par
her assistance; I committed her to their care, and seized the\par
opportunity of escaping.\par
\par
Agitated and confused beyond expression I bent my steps towards the\par
Garden. The benignity with which the Baroness had listened to me at\par
first raised my hopes to the highest pitch: I imagined her to have\par
perceived my attachment for her Niece, and to approve of it. Extreme\par
was my disappointment at understanding the true purport of her\par
discourse. I knew not what course to take: The superstition of the\par
Parents of Agnes, aided by her Aunt’s unfortunate passion, seemed to\par
oppose such obstacles to our union as were almost insurmountable.\par
\par
As I past by a low parlour, whose windows looked into the Garden,\par
through the door which stood half open I observed Agnes seated at a\par
Table. She was occupied in drawing, and several unfinished sketches\par
were scattered round her. I entered, still undetermined whether I\par
should acquaint her with the declaration of the Baroness.\par
\par
“Oh! is it only you?” said She, raising her head; “You are no Stranger,\par
and I shall continue my occupation without ceremony. Take a Chair, and\par
seat yourself by me.”\par
\par
I obeyed, and placed myself near the Table. Unconscious what I was\par
doing, and totally occupied by the scene which had just passed, I took\par
up some of the drawings, and cast my eye over them. One of the subjects\par
struck me from its singularity. It represented the great Hall of the\par
Castle of Lindenberg. A door conducting to a narrow staircase stood\par
half open. In the foreground appeared a Groupe of figures, placed in\par
the most grotesque attitudes; Terror was expressed upon every\par
countenance.\par
\par
Here was One upon his knees with his eyes cast up to heaven, and\par
praying most devoutly; There Another was creeping away upon all fours.\par
Some hid their faces in their cloaks or the laps of their Companions;\par
Some had concealed themselves beneath a Table, on which the remnants of\par
a feast were visible; While Others with gaping mouths and eyes\par
wide-stretched pointed to a Figure, supposed to have created this\par
disturbance. It represented a Female of more than human stature,\par
clothed in the habit of some religious order. Her face was veiled; On\par
her arm hung a chaplet of beads; Her dress was in several places\par
stained with the blood which trickled from a wound upon her bosom. In\par
one hand She held a Lamp, in the other a large Knife, and She seemed\par
advancing towards the iron gates of the Hall.\par
\par
“What does this mean, Agnes?” said I; “Is this some invention of your\par
own?”\par
\par
She cast her eye upon the drawing.\par
\par
“Oh! no,” She replied; “’Tis the invention of much wiser heads than\par
mine. But can you possibly have lived at Lindenberg for three whole\par
Months without hearing of the Bleeding Nun?”\par
\par
“You are the first, who ever mentioned the name to me. Pray, who may\par
the Lady be?”\par
\par
“That is more than I can pretend to tell you. All my knowledge of her\par
History comes from an old tradition in this family, which has been\par
handed down from Father to Son, and is firmly credited throughout the\par
Baron’s domains. Nay, the Baron believes it himself; and as for my Aunt\par
who has a natural turn for the marvellous, She would sooner doubt the\par
veracity of the Bible, than of the Bleeding Nun. Shall I tell you this\par
History?”\par
\par
I answered that She would oblige me much by relating it: She resumed\par
her drawing, and then proceeded as follows in a tone of burlesqued\par
gravity.\par
\par
“It is surprising that in all the Chronicles of past times, this\par
remarkable Personage is never once mentioned. Fain would I recount to\par
you her life; But unluckily till after her death She was never known to\par
have existed. Then first did She think it necessary to make some noise\par
in the world, and with that intention She made bold to seize upon the\par
Castle of Lindenberg. Having a good taste, She took up her abode in the\par
best room of the House: and once established there, She began to amuse\par
herself by knocking about the tables and chairs in the middle of the\par
night. Perhaps She was a bad Sleeper, but this I have never been able\par
to ascertain. According to the tradition, this entertainment commenced\par
about a Century ago. It was accompanied with shrieking, howling,\par
groaning, swearing, and many other agreeable noises of the same kind.\par
But though one particular room was more especially honoured with her\par
visits, She did not entirely confine herself to it. She occasionally\par
ventured into the old Galleries, paced up and down the spacious Halls,\par
or sometimes stopping at the doors of the Chambers, She wept and wailed\par
there to the universal terror of the Inhabitants. In these nocturnal\par
excursions She was seen by different People, who all describe her\par
appearance as you behold it here, traced by the hand of her unworthy\par
Historian.”\par
\par
The singularity of this account insensibly engaged my attention.\par
\par
“Did She never speak to those who met her?” said I.\par
\par
“Not She. The specimens indeed, which She gave nightly of her talents\par
for conversation, were by no means inviting. Sometimes the Castle rung\par
with oaths and execrations: A Moment after She repeated her\par
Paternoster: Now She howled out the most horrible blasphemies, and then\par
chaunted De Profundis, as orderly as if still in the Choir. In short\par
She seemed a mighty capricious Being: But whether She prayed or cursed,\par
whether She was impious or devout, She always contrived to terrify her\par
Auditors out of their senses. The Castle became scarcely habitable; and\par
its Lord was so frightened by these midnight Revels, that one fine\par
morning He was found dead in his bed. This success seemed to please the\par
Nun mightily, for now She made more noise than ever. But the next Baron\par
proved too cunning for her. He made his appearance with a celebrated\par
Exorciser in his hand, who feared not to shut himself up for a night in\par
the haunted Chamber. There it seems that He had an hard battle with the\par
Ghost, before She would promise to be quiet. She was obstinate, but He\par
was more so, and at length She consented to let the Inhabitants of the\par
Castle take a good night’s rest. For some time after no news was heard\par
of her. But at the end of five years the Exorciser died, and then the\par
Nun ventured to peep abroad again. However, She was now grown much more\par
tractable and well-behaved. She walked about in silence, and never made\par
her appearance above once in five years. This custom, if you will\par
believe the Baron, She still continues. He is fully persuaded, that on\par
the fifth of May of every fifth year, as soon as the Clock strikes One,\par
the Door of the haunted Chamber opens. (Observe, that this room has\par
been shut up for near a Century.) Then out walks the Ghostly Nun with\par
her Lamp and dagger: She descends the staircase of the Eastern Tower;\par
and crosses the great Hall! On that night the Porter always leaves the\par
Gates of the Castle open, out of respect to the Apparition: Not that\par
this is thought by any means necessary, since She could easily whip\par
through the Keyhole if She chose it; But merely out of politeness, and\par
to prevent her from making her exit in a way so derogatory to the\par
dignity of her Ghost-ship.”\par
\par
“And whither does She go on quitting the Castle?”\par
\par
“To Heaven, I hope; But if She does, the place certainly is not to her\par
taste, for She always returns after an hour’s absence. The Lady then\par
retires to her chamber, and is quiet for another five years.”\par
\par
“And you believe this, Agnes?”\par
\par
“How can you ask such a question? No, no, Alphonso! I have too much\par
reason to lament superstition’s influence to be its Victim myself.\par
However I must not avow my incredulity to the Baroness: She entertains\par
not a doubt of the truth of this History. As to Dame Cunegonda, my\par
Governess, She protests that fifteen years ago She saw the Spectre with\par
her own eyes. She related to me one evening how She and several other\par
Domestics had been terrified while at Supper by the appearance of the\par
Bleeding Nun, as the Ghost is called in the Castle: ’Tis from her\par
account that I drew this sketch, and you may be certain that Cunegonda\par
was not omitted. There She is! I shall never forget what a passion She\par
was in, and how ugly She looked while She scolded me for having made\par
her picture so like herself!”\par
\par
Here She pointed to a burlesque figure of an old Woman in an attitude\par
of terror.\par
\par
In spite of the melancholy which oppressed me, I could not help smiling\par
at the playful imagination of Agnes: She had perfectly preserved Dame\par
Cunegonda’s resemblance, but had so much exaggerated every fault, and\par
rendered every feature so irresistibly laughable, that I could easily\par
conceive the Duenna’s anger.\par
\par
“The figure is admirable, my dear Agnes! I knew not that you possessed\par
such talents for the ridiculous.”\par
\par
“Stay a moment,” She replied; “I will show you a figure still more\par
ridiculous than Dame Cunegonda’s. If it pleases you, you may dispose of\par
it as seems best to yourself.”\par
\par
She rose, and went to a Cabinet at some little distance. Unlocking a\par
drawer, She took out a small case, which She opened, and presented to\par
me.\par
\par
“Do you know the resemblance?” said She smiling.\par
\par
It was her own.\par
\par
Transported at the gift, I pressed the portrait to my lips with\par
passion: I threw myself at her feet, and declared my gratitude in the\par
warmest and most affectionate terms. She listened to me with\par
complaisance, and assured me that She shared my sentiments: When\par
suddenly She uttered a loud shriek, disengaged the hand which I held,\par
and flew from the room by a door which opened to the Garden. Amazed at\par
this abrupt departure, I rose hastily from my knees. I beheld with\par
confusion the Baroness standing near me glowing with jealousy, and\par
almost choaked with rage. On recovering from her swoon, She had\par
tortured her imagination to discover her concealed Rival. No one\par
appeared to deserve her suspicions more than Agnes. She immediately\par
hastened to find her Niece, tax her with encouraging my addresses, and\par
assure herself whether her conjectures were well-grounded.\par
Unfortunately She had already seen enough to need no other\par
confirmation. She arrived at the door of the room at the precise\par
moment, when Agnes gave me her Portrait. She heard me profess an\par
everlasting attachment to her Rival, and saw me kneeling at her feet.\par
She advanced to separate us; We were too much occupied by each other to\par
perceive her approach, and were not aware of it, till Agnes beheld her\par
standing by my side.\par
\par
Rage on the part of Donna Rodolpha, embarrassment on mine, for some\par
time kept us both silent. The Lady recovered herself first.\par
\par
“My suspicions then were just,” said She; “The Coquetry of my Niece has\par
triumphed, and ’tis to her that I am sacrificed. In one respect however\par
I am fortunate: I shall not be the only one who laments a disappointed\par
passion. You too shall know, what it is to love without hope! I daily\par
expect orders for restoring Agnes to her Parents. Immediately upon her\par
arrival in Spain, She will take the veil, and place an insuperable\par
barrier to your union. You may spare your supplications.” She\par
continued, perceiving me on the point of speaking; “My resolution is\par
fixed and immoveable. Your Mistress shall remain a close Prisoner in\par
her chamber till She exchanges this Castle for the Cloister. Solitude\par
will perhaps recall her to a sense of her duty: But to prevent your\par
opposing that wished event, I must inform you, Don Alphonso, that your\par
presence here is no longer agreeable either to the Baron or Myself. It\par
was not to talk nonsense to my Niece that your Relations sent you to\par
Germany: Your business was to travel, and I should be sorry to impede\par
any longer so excellent a design. Farewell, Segnor; Remember, that\par
tomorrow morning we meet for the last time.”\par
\par
Having said this, She darted upon me a look of pride, contempt, and\par
malice, and quitted the apartment. I also retired to mine, and consumed\par
the night in planning the means of rescuing Agnes from the power of her\par
tyrannical Aunt.\par
\par
After the positive declaration of its Mistress, it was impossible for\par
me to make a longer stay at the Castle of Lindenberg. Accordingly I the\par
next day announced my immediate departure. The Baron declared that it\par
gave him sincere pain; and He expressed himself in my favour so warmly,\par
that I endeavoured to win him over to my interest. Scarcely had I\par
mentioned the name of Agnes when He stopped me short, and said, that it\par
was totally out of his power to interfere in the business. I saw that\par
it was in vain to argue; The Baroness governed her Husband with\par
despotic sway, and I easily perceived that She had prejudiced him\par
against the match. Agnes did not appear: I entreated permission to take\par
leave of her, but my prayer was rejected. I was obliged to depart\par
without seeing her.\par
\par
At quitting him the Baron shook my hand affectionately, and assured me\par
that as soon as his Niece was gone, I might consider his House as my\par
own.\par
\par
“Farewell, Don Alphonso!” said the Baroness, and stretched out her hand\par
to me.\par
\par
I took it, and offered to carry it to my lips. She prevented me.\par
\par
Her Husband was at the other end of the room, and out of hearing.\par
\par
“Take care of yourself,” She continued; “My love is become hatred, and\par
my wounded pride shall not be unatoned. Go where you will, my vengeance\par
shall follow you!”\par
\par
She accompanied these words with a look sufficient to make me tremble.\par
I answered not, but hastened to quit the Castle.\par
\par
As my Chaise drove out of the Court, I looked up to the windows of your\par
Sister’s chamber. Nobody was to be seen there: I threw myself back\par
despondent in my Carriage. I was attended by no other servants than a\par
Frenchman whom I had hired at Strasbourg in Stephano’s room, and my\par
little Page whom I before mentioned to you. The fidelity, intelligence,\par
and good temper of Theodore had already made him dear to me; But He now\par
prepared to lay an obligation on me, which made me look upon him as a\par
Guardian Genius. Scarcely had we proceeded half a mile from the Castle,\par
when He rode up to the Chaise-door.\par
\par
“Take courage, Segnor!” said He in Spanish, which He had already learnt\par
to speak with fluency and correctness. “While you were with the Baron,\par
I watched the moment when Dame Cunegonda was below stairs, and mounted\par
into the chamber over that of Donna Agnes. I sang as loud as I could a\par
little German air well-known to her, hoping that She would recollect my\par
voice. I was not disappointed, for I soon heard her window open. I\par
hastened to let down a string with which I had provided myself: Upon\par
hearing the casement closed again, I drew up the string, and fastened\par
to it I found this scrap of paper.”\par
\par
He then presented me with a small note addressed to me. I opened it\par
with impatience: It contained the following words written in pencil:\par
\par
“Conceal yourself for the next fortnight in some neighbouring Village.\par
My Aunt will believe you to have quitted Lindenberg, and I shall be\par
restored to liberty. I will be in the West Pavilion at twelve on the\par
night of the thirtieth. Fail not to be there, and we shall have an\par
opportunity of concerting our future plans. Adieu.\par
\par
\par
“AGNES.”\par
\par
\par
At perusing these lines my transports exceeded all bounds; Neither did\par
I set any to the expressions of gratitude which I heaped upon Theodore.\par
In fact his address and attention merited my warmest praise. You will\par
readily believe that I had not entrusted him with my passion for Agnes;\par
But the arch Youth had too much discernment not to discover my secret,\par
and too much discretion not to conceal his knowledge of it. He observed\par
in silence what was going on, nor strove to make himself an Agent in\par
the business till my interests required his interference. I equally\par
admired his judgment, his penetration, his address, and his fidelity.\par
This was not the first occasion in which I had found him of infinite\par
use, and I was every day more convinced of his quickness and capacity.\par
During my short stay at Strasbourg, He had applied himself diligently\par
to learning the rudiments of Spanish: He continued to study it, and\par
with so much success that He spoke it with the same facility as his\par
native language. He past the greatest part of his time in reading; He\par
had acquired much information for his Age; and united the advantages of\par
a lively countenance and prepossessing figure to an excellent\par
understanding and the very best of hearts. He is now fifteen; He is\par
still in my service, and when you see him, I am sure that He will\par
please you. But excuse this digression: I return to the subject which I\par
quitted.\par
\par
I obeyed the instructions of Agnes. I proceeded to Munich. There I left\par
my Chaise under the care of Lucas, my French Servant, and then returned\par
on Horseback to a small Village about four miles distant from the\par
Castle of Lindenberg. Upon arriving there a story was related to the\par
Host at whose Inn I descended, which prevented his wondering at my\par
making so long a stay in his House. The old Man fortunately was\par
credulous and incurious: He believed all I said, and sought to know no\par
more than what I thought proper to tell him. Nobody was with me but\par
Theodore; Both were disguised, and as we kept ourselves close, we were\par
not suspected to be other than what we seemed. In this manner the\par
fortnight passed away. During that time I had the pleasing conviction\par
that Agnes was once more at liberty. She past through the Village with\par
Dame Cunegonda: She seemed in health and spirits, and talked to her\par
Companion without any appearance of constraint.\par
\par
“Who are those Ladies?” said I to my Host, as the Carriage past.\par
\par
“Baron Lindenberg’s Niece with her Governess,” He replied; “She goes\par
regularly every Friday to the Convent of St. Catharine, in which She\par
was brought up, and which is situated about a mile from hence.”\par
\par
You may be certain that I waited with impatience for the ensuing\par
Friday. I again beheld my lovely Mistress. She cast her eyes upon me,\par
as She passed the Inn-door. A blush which overspread her cheek told me\par
that in spite of my disguise I had been recognised. I bowed profoundly.\par
She returned the compliment by a slight inclination of the head as if\par
made to one inferior, and looked another way till the Carriage was out\par
of sight.\par
\par
The long-expected, long-wished for night arrived. It was calm, and the\par
Moon was at the full. As soon as the Clock struck eleven I hastened to\par
my appointment, determined not to be too late. Theodore had provided a\par
Ladder; I ascended the Garden wall without difficulty; The Page\par
followed me, and drew the Ladder after us. I posted myself in the West\par
Pavilion, and waited impatiently for the approach of Agnes. Every\par
breeze that whispered, every leaf that fell, I believed to be her\par
footstep, and hastened to meet her. Thus was I obliged to pass a full\par
hour, every minute of which appeared to me an age. The Castle Bell at\par
length tolled twelve, and scarcely could I believe the night to be no\par
further advanced. Another quarter of an hour elapsed, and I heard the\par
light foot of my Mistress approaching the Pavilion with precaution. I\par
flew to receive her, and conducted her to a seat. I threw myself at her\par
feet, and was expressing my joy at seeing her, when She thus\par
interrupted me.\par
\par
“We have no time to lose, Alphonso: The moments are precious, for\par
though no more a Prisoner, Cunegonda watches my every step. An express\par
is arrived from my Father; I must depart immediately for Madrid, and\par
’tis with difficulty that I have obtained a week’s delay. The\par
superstition of my Parents, supported by the representations of my\par
cruel Aunt, leaves me no hope of softening them to compassion. In this\par
dilemma I have resolved to commit myself to your honour: God grant that\par
you may never give me cause to repent my resolution! Flight is my only\par
resource from the horrors of a Convent, and my imprudence must be\par
excused by the urgency of the danger. Now listen to the plan by which I\par
hope to effect my escape.\par
\par
“We are now at the thirtieth of April. On the fifth day from this the\par
Visionary Nun is expected to appear. In my last visit to the Convent I\par
provided myself with a dress proper for the character: A Friend, whom I\par
have left there and to whom I made no scruple to confide my secret,\par
readily consented to supply me with a religious habit. Provide a\par
carriage, and be with it at a little distance from the great Gate of\par
the Castle. As soon as the Clock strikes “one,” I shall quit my\par
chamber, drest in the same apparel as the Ghost is supposed to wear.\par
Whoever meets me will be too much terrified to oppose my escape. I\par
shall easily reach the door, and throw myself under your protection.\par
Thus far success is certain: But Oh! Alphonso, should you deceive me!\par
Should you despise my imprudence and reward it with ingratitude, the\par
World will not hold a Being more wretched than myself! I feel all the\par
dangers to which I shall be exposed. I feel that I am giving you a\par
right to treat me with levity: But I rely upon your love, upon your\par
honour! The step which I am on the point of taking, will incense my\par
Relations against me: Should you desert me, should you betray the trust\par
reposed in you, I shall have no friend to punish your insult, or\par
support my cause. On yourself alone rests all my hope, and if your own\par
heart does not plead in my behalf, I am undone for ever!”\par
\par
The tone in which She pronounced these words was so touching, that in\par
spite of my joy at receiving her promise to follow me, I could not help\par
being affected. I also repined in secret at not having taken the\par
precaution to provide a Carriage at the Village, in which case I might\par
have carried off Agnes that very night. Such an attempt was now\par
impracticable: Neither Carriage or Horses were to be procured nearer\par
than Munich, which was distant from Lindenberg two good days journey. I\par
was therefore obliged to chime in with her plan, which in truth seemed\par
well arranged: Her disguise would secure her from being stopped in\par
quitting the Castle, and would enable her to step into the Carriage at\par
the very Gate without difficulty or losing time.\par
\par
Agnes reclined her head mournfully upon my shoulder, and by the light\par
of the Moon I saw tears flowing down her cheek. I strove to dissipate\par
her melancholy, and encouraged her to look forward to the prospect of\par
happiness. I protested in the most solemn terms that her virtue and\par
innocence would be safe in my keeping, and that till the church had\par
made her my lawful Wife, her honour should be held by me as sacred as a\par
Sister’s. I told her that my first care should be to find you out,\par
Lorenzo, and reconcile you to our union; and I was continuing to speak\par
in the same strain, when a noise without alarmed me. Suddenly the door\par
of the Pavilion was thrown open, and Cunegonda stood before us. She had\par
heard Agnes steal out of her chamber, followed her into the Garden, and\par
perceived her entering the Pavilion. Favoured by the Trees which shaded\par
it, and unperceived by Theodore who waited at a little distance, She\par
had approached in silence, and overheard our whole conversation.\par
\par
“Admirable!” cried Cunegonda in a voice shrill with passion, while\par
Agnes uttered a loud shriek; “By St. Barbara, young Lady, you have an\par
excellent invention! You must personate the Bleeding Nun, truly? What\par
impiety! What incredulity! Marry, I have a good mind to let you pursue\par
your plan: When the real Ghost met you, I warrant, you would be in a\par
pretty condition! Don Alphonso, you ought to be ashamed of yourself for\par
seducing a young ignorant Creature to leave her family and Friends:\par
However, for this time at least I shall mar your wicked designs. The\par
noble Lady shall be informed of the whole affair, and Agnes must defer\par
playing the Spectre till a better opportunity. Farewell, Segnor— Donna\par
Agnes, let me have the honour of conducting your Ghost-ship back to\par
your apartment.”\par
\par
She approached the Sopha on which her trembling Pupil was seated, took\par
her by the hand, and prepared to lead her from the Pavilion.\par
\par
I detained her, and strove by entreaties, soothing, promises, and\par
flattery to win her to my party: But finding all that I could say of no\par
avail, I abandoned the vain attempt.\par
\par
“Your obstinacy must be its own punishment,” said I; “But one resource\par
remains to save Agnes and myself, and I shall not hesitate to employ\par
it.”\par
\par
Terrified at this menace, She again endeavoured to quit the Pavilion;\par
But I seized her by the wrist, and detained her forcibly. At the same\par
moment Theodore, who had followed her into the room, closed the door,\par
and prevented her escape. I took the veil of Agnes: I threw it round\par
the Duenna’s head, who uttered such piercing shrieks that in spite of\par
our distance from the Castle, I dreaded their being heard. At length I\par
succeeded in gagging her so compleatly that She could not produce a\par
single sound. Theodore and myself with some difficulty next contrived\par
to bind her hands and feet with our handkerchiefs; And I advised Agnes\par
to regain her chamber with all diligence. I promised that no harm\par
should happen to Cunegonda, bad her remember that on the fifth of May I\par
should be in waiting at the Great Gate of the Castle, and took of her\par
an affectionate farewell. Trembling and uneasy She had scarce power\par
enough to signify her consent to my plans, and fled back to her\par
apartment in disorder and confusion.\par
\par
In the meanwhile Theodore assisted me in carrying off my antiquated\par
Prize. She was hoisted over the wall, placed before me upon my Horse\par
like a Portmanteau, and I galloped away with her from the Castle of\par
Lindenberg. The unlucky Duenna never had made a more disagreeable\par
journey in her life: She was jolted and shaken till She was become\par
little more than an animated Mummy; not to mention her fright when we\par
waded through a small River through which it was necessary to pass in\par
order to regain the Village. Before we reached the Inn, I had already\par
determined how to dispose of the troublesome Cunegonda. We entered the\par
Street in which the Inn stood, and while the page knocked, I waited at\par
a little distance. The Landlord opened the door with a Lamp in his\par
hand.\par
\par
“Give me the light!” said Theodore; “My Master is coming.”\par
\par
He snatched the Lamp hastily, and purposely let it fall upon the\par
ground: The Landlord returned to the Kitchen to re-light the Lamp,\par
leaving the door open. I profited by the obscurity, sprang from my\par
Horse with Cunegonda in my arms, darted up stairs, reached my chamber\par
unperceived, and unlocking the door of a spacious Closet, stowed her\par
within it, and then turned the Key. The Landlord and Theodore soon\par
after appeared with lights: The Former expressed himself a little\par
surprised at my returning so late, but asked no impertinent questions.\par
He soon quitted the room, and left me to exult in the success of my\par
undertaking.\par
\par
I immediately paid a visit to my Prisoner. I strove to persuade her\par
submitting with patience to her temporary confinement. My attempt was\par
unsuccessful. Unable to speak or move, She expressed her fury by her\par
looks, and except at meals I never dared to unbind her, or release her\par
from the Gag. At such times I stood over her with a drawn sword, and\par
protested, that if She uttered a single cry, I would plunge it in her\par
bosom. As soon as She had done eating, the Gag was replaced. I was\par
conscious that this proceeding was cruel, and could only be justified\par
by the urgency of circumstances: As to Theodore, He had no scruples\par
upon the subject. Cunegonda’s captivity entertained him beyond measure.\par
During his abode in the Castle, a continual warfare had been carried on\par
between him and the Duenna; and now that He found his Enemy so\par
absolutely in his power, He triumphed without mercy. He seemed to think\par
of nothing but how to find out new means of plaguing her: Sometimes He\par
affected to pity her misfortune, then laughed at, abused, and mimicked\par
her; He played her a thousand tricks, each more provoking than the\par
other, and amused himself by telling her that her elopement must have\par
occasioned much surprise at the Baron’s. This was in fact the case. No\par
one except Agnes could imagine what was become of Dame Cunegonda: Every\par
hole and corner was searched for her; The Ponds were dragged, and the\par
Woods underwent a thorough examination. Still no Dame Cunegonda made\par
her appearance. Agnes kept the secret, and I kept the Duenna: The\par
Baroness, therefore, remained in total ignorance respecting the old\par
Woman’s fate, but suspected her to have perished by suicide. Thus past\par
away five days, during which I had prepared every thing necessary for\par
my enterprise. On quitting Agnes, I had made it my first business to\par
dispatch a Peasant with a letter to Lucas at Munich, ordering him to\par
take care that a Coach and four should arrive about ten o’clock on the\par
fifth of May at the Village of Rosenwald. He obeyed my instructions\par
punctually: The Equipage arrived at the time appointed. As the period\par
of her Lady’s elopement drew nearer, Cunegonda’s rage increased. I\par
verily believe that spight and passion would have killed her, had I not\par
luckily discovered her prepossession in favour of Cherry Brandy. With\par
this favourite liquor She was plentifully supplied, and Theodore always\par
remaining to guard her, the Gag was occasionally removed. The liquor\par
seemed to have a wonderful effect in softening the acrimony of her\par
nature; and her confinement not admitting of any other amusement, She\par
got drunk regularly once a day just by way of passing the time.\par
\par
The fifth of May arrived, a period by me never to be forgotten! Before\par
the Clock struck twelve, I betook myself to the scene of action.\par
Theodore followed me on horseback. I concealed the Carriage in a\par
spacious Cavern of the Hill, on whose brow the Castle was situated:\par
This Cavern was of considerable depth, and among the peasants was known\par
by the name of Lindenberg Hole. The night was calm and beautiful: The\par
Moonbeams fell upon the antient Towers of the Castle, and shed upon\par
their summits a silver light. All was still around me: Nothing was to\par
be heard except the night breeze sighing among the leaves, the distant\par
barking of Village Dogs, or the Owl who had established herself in a\par
nook of the deserted Eastern Turret. I heard her melancholy shriek, and\par
looked upwards. She sat upon the ride of a window, which I recognized\par
to be that of the haunted Room. This brought to my remembrance the\par
story of the Bleeding Nun, and I sighed while I reflected on the\par
influence of superstition and weakness of human reason. Suddenly I\par
heard a faint chorus steal upon the silence of the night.\par
\par
“What can occasion that noise, Theodore?”\par
\par
“A Stranger of distinction,” replied He, “passed through the Village\par
today in his way to the Castle: He is reported to be the Father of\par
Donna Agnes. Doubtless, the Baron has given an entertainment to\par
celebrate his arrival.”\par
\par
The Castle Bell announced the hour of midnight: This was the usual\par
signal for the family to retire to Bed. Soon after I perceived lights\par
in the Castle moving backwards and forwards in different directions. I\par
conjectured the company to be separating. I could hear the heavy doors\par
grate as they opened with difficulty, and as they closed again the\par
rotten Casements rattled in their frames. The chamber of Agnes was on\par
the other side of the Castle. I trembled lest She should have failed in\par
obtaining the Key of the haunted Room: Through this it was necessary\par
for her to pass in order to reach the narrow Staircase by which the\par
Ghost was supposed to descend into the great Hall. Agitated by this\par
apprehension, I kept my eyes constantly fixed upon the window, where I\par
hoped to perceive the friendly glare of a Lamp borne by Agnes. I now\par
heard the massy Gates unbarred. By the candle in his hand I\par
distinguished old Conrad, the Porter. He set the Portal doors wide\par
open, and retired. The lights in the Castle gradually disappeared, and\par
at length the whole Building was wrapt in darkness.\par
\par
While I sat upon a broken ridge of the hill, the stillness of the scene\par
inspired me with melancholy ideas not altogether unpleasing. The Castle\par
which stood full in my sight, formed an object equally awful and\par
picturesque. Its ponderous Walls tinged by the moon with solemn\par
brightness, its old and partly-ruined Towers lifting themselves into\par
the clouds and seeming to frown on the plains around them, its lofty\par
battlements overgrown with ivy, and folding Gates expanding in honour\par
of the Visionary Inhabitant, made me sensible of a sad and reverential\par
horror. Yet did not these sensations occupy me so fully, as to prevent\par
me from witnessing with impatience the slow progress of time. I\par
approached the Castle, and ventured to walk round it. A few rays of\par
light still glimmered in the chamber of Agnes. I observed them with\par
joy. I was still gazing upon them, when I perceived a figure draw near\par
the window, and the Curtain was carefully closed to conceal the Lamp\par
which burned there. Convinced by this observation that Agnes had not\par
abandoned our plan, I returned with a light heart to my former station.\par
\par
The half-hour struck! The three-quarters struck! My bosom beat high\par
with hope and expectation. At length the wished-for sound was heard.\par
The Bell tolled “One,” and the Mansion echoed with the noise loud and\par
solemn. I looked up to the Casement of the haunted Chamber. Scarcely\par
had five minutes elapsed, when the expected light appeared. I was now\par
close to the Tower. The window was not so far from the Ground but that\par
I fancied I perceived a female figure with a Lamp in her hand moving\par
slowly along the Apartment. The light soon faded away, and all was\par
again dark and gloomy.\par
\par
Occasional gleams of brightness darted from the Staircase windows as\par
the lovely Ghost past by them. I traced the light through the Hall: It\par
reached the Portal, and at length I beheld Agnes pass through the\par
folding gates. She was habited exactly as She had described the\par
Spectre. A chaplet of Beads hung upon her arm; her head was enveloped\par
in a long white veil; Her Nun’s dress was stained with blood, and She\par
had taken care to provide herself with a Lamp and dagger. She advanced\par
towards the spot where I stood. I flew to meet her, and clasped her in\par
my arms.\par
\par
“Agnes!” said I while I pressed her to my bosom,\par
\par
Agnes! Agnes! Thou art mine!\par
Agnes! Agnes! I am thine!\par
In my veins while blood shall roll,\par
Thou art mine!\par
I am thine!\par
Thine my body! Thine my soul!\par
\par
\par
Terrified and breathless She was unable to speak: She dropt her Lamp\par
and dagger, and sank upon my bosom in silence. I raised her in my arms,\par
and conveyed her to the Carriage. Theodore remained behind in order to\par
release Dame Cunegonda. I also charged him with a letter to the\par
Baroness explaining the whole affair, and entreating her good offices\par
in reconciling Don Gaston to my union with his Daughter. I discovered\par
to her my real name: I proved to her that my birth and expectations\par
justified my pretending to her Niece, and assured her, though it was\par
out of my power to return her love, that I would strive unceasingly to\par
obtain her esteem and friendship.\par
\par
I stepped into the Carriage, where Agnes was already seated. Theodore\par
closed the door, and the Postillions drove away. At first I was\par
delighted with the rapidity of our progress; But as soon as we were in\par
no danger of pursuit, I called to the Drivers, and bad them moderate\par
their pace. They strove in vain to obey me. The Horses refused to\par
answer the rein, and continued to rush on with astonishing swiftness.\par
The Postillions redoubled their efforts to stop them, but by kicking\par
and plunging the Beasts soon released themselves from this restraint.\par
Uttering a loud shriek, the Drivers were hurled upon the ground.\par
Immediately thick clouds obscured the sky: The winds howled around us,\par
the lightning flashed, and the Thunder roared tremendously. Never did I\par
behold so frightful a Tempest! Terrified by the jar of contending\par
elements, the Horses seemed every moment to increase their speed.\par
Nothing could interrupt their career; They dragged the Carriage through\par
Hedges and Ditches, dashed down the most dangerous precipices, and\par
seemed to vye in swiftness with the rapidity of the winds.\par
\par
All this while my Companion lay motionless in my arms. Truly alarmed by\par
the magnitude of the danger, I was in vain attempting to recall her to\par
her senses; when a loud crash announced, that a stop was put to our\par
progress in the most disagreeable manner. The Carriage was shattered to\par
pieces. In falling I struck my temple against a flint. The pain of the\par
wound, the violence of the shock, and apprehension for the safety of\par
Agnes combined to overpower me so compleatly, that my senses forsook\par
me, and I lay without animation on the ground.\par
\par
I probably remained for some time in this situation, since when I\par
opened my eyes, it was broad daylight. Several Peasants were standing\par
round me, and seemed disputing whether my recovery was possible. I\par
spoke German tolerably well. As soon as I could utter an articulate\par
sound, I enquired after Agnes. What was my surprise and distress, when\par
assured by the Peasants, that nobody had been seen answering the\par
description which I gave of her! They told me that in going to their\par
daily labour they had been alarmed by observing the fragments of my\par
Carriage, and by hearing the groans of an Horse, the only one of the\par
four which remained alive: The other Three lay dead by my side. Nobody\par
was near me when they came up, and much time had been lost, before they\par
succeeded in recovering me. Uneasy beyond expression respecting the\par
fate of my Companion, I besought the Peasants to disperse themselves in\par
search of her: I described her dress, and promised immense rewards to\par
whoever brought me any intelligence. As for myself, it was impossible\par
for me to join in the pursuit: I had broken two of my ribs in the fall:\par
My arm being dislocated hung useless by my side; and my left leg was\par
shattered so terribly, that I never expected to recover its use.\par
\par
The Peasants complied with my request: All left me except Four, who\par
made a litter of boughs and prepared to convey me to the neighbouring\par
Town. I enquired its name. It proved to be Ratisbon, and I could\par
scarcely persuade myself that I had travelled to such a distance in a\par
single night. I told the Countrymen that at one o’clock that morning I\par
had past through the Village of Rosenwald. They shook their heads\par
wistfully, and made signs to each other that I must certainly be\par
delirious. I was conveyed to a decent Inn and immediately put to bed. A\par
Physician was sent for, who set my arm with success. He then examined\par
my other hurts, and told me that I need be under no apprehension of the\par
consequences of any of them; But ordered me to keep myself quiet, and\par
be prepared for a tedious and painful cure. I answered him that if He\par
hoped to keep me quiet, He must first endeavour to procure me some news\par
of a Lady who had quitted Rosenwald in my company the night before, and\par
had been with me at the moment when the Coach broke down. He smiled,\par
and only replied by advising me to make myself easy, for that all\par
proper care should be taken of me. As He quitted me, the Hostess met\par
him at the door of the room.\par
\par
“The Gentleman is not quite in his right senses;” I heard him say to\par
her in a low voice; “’Tis the natural consequence of his fall, but that\par
will soon be over.”\par
\par
One after another the Peasants returned to the Inn, and informed me\par
that no traces had been discovered of my unfortunate Mistress.\par
\par
Uneasiness now became despair. I entreated them to renew their search\par
in the most urgent terms, doubling the promises which I had already\par
made them. My wild and frantic manner confirmed the bye-standers in the\par
idea of my being delirious. No signs of the Lady having appeared, they\par
believed her to be a creature fabricated by my over-heated brain, and\par
paid no attention to my entreaties. However, the Hostess assured me\par
that a fresh enquiry should be made, but I found afterwards that her\par
promise was only given to quiet me. No further steps were taken in the\par
business.\par
\par
Though my Baggage was left at Munich under the care of my French\par
Servant, having prepared myself for a long journey, my purse was amply\par
furnished: Besides my equipage proved me to be of distinction, and in\par
consequence all possible attention was paid me at the Inn. The day\par
passed away: Still no news arrived of Agnes. The anxiety of fear now\par
gave place to despondency. I ceased to rave about her and was plunged\par
in the depth of melancholy reflections. Perceiving me to be silent and\par
tranquil, my Attendants believed my delirium to have abated, and that\par
my malady had taken a favourable turn. According to the Physician’s\par
order I swallowed a composing medicine; and as soon as the night shut\par
in, my attendants withdrew and left me to repose.\par
\par
That repose I wooed in vain. The agitation of my bosom chased away\par
sleep. Restless in my mind, in spite of the fatigue of my body, I\par
continued to toss about from side to side, till the Clock in a\par
neighbouring Steeple struck “One.” As I listened to the mournful hollow\par
sound, and heard it die away in the wind, I felt a sudden chillness\par
spread itself over my body. I shuddered without knowing wherefore; Cold\par
dews poured down my forehead, and my hair stood bristling with alarm.\par
Suddenly I heard slow and heavy steps ascending the staircase. By an\par
involuntary movement I started up in my bed, and drew back the curtain.\par
A single rush-light which glimmered upon the hearth shed a faint gleam\par
through the apartment, which was hung with tapestry. The door was\par
thrown open with violence. A figure entered, and drew near my Bed with\par
solemn measured steps. With trembling apprehension I examined this\par
midnight Visitor. God Almighty! It was the Bleeding Nun! It was my lost\par
Companion! Her face was still veiled, but She no longer held her Lamp\par
and dagger. She lifted up her veil slowly. What a sight presented\par
itself to my startled eyes! I beheld before me an animated Corse. Her\par
countenance was long and haggard; Her cheeks and lips were bloodless;\par
The paleness of death was spread over her features, and her eyeballs\par
fixed stedfastly upon me were lustreless and hollow.\par
\par
I gazed upon the Spectre with horror too great to be described. My\par
blood was frozen in my veins. I would have called for aid, but the\par
sound expired ere it could pass my lips. My nerves were bound up in\par
impotence, and I remained in the same attitude inanimate as a Statue.\par
\par
The visionary Nun looked upon me for some minutes in silence: There was\par
something petrifying in her regard. At length in a low sepulchral voice\par
She pronounced the following words:\par
\par
‘Raymond! Raymond! Thou art mine!\par
Raymond! Raymond! I am thine!\par
In thy veins while blood shall roll,\par
I am thine!\par
Thou art mine!\par
Mine thy body! Mine thy soul!——’\par
\par
\par
Breathless with fear, I listened while She repeated my own expressions.\par
The Apparition seated herself opposite to me at the foot of the Bed,\par
and was silent. Her eyes were fixed earnestly upon mine: They seemed\par
endowed with the property of the Rattlesnake’s, for I strove in vain to\par
look off her. My eyes were fascinated, and I had not the power of\par
withdrawing them from the Spectre’s.\par
\par
In this attitude She remained for a whole long hour without speaking or\par
moving; nor was I able to do either. At length the Clock struck two.\par
The Apparition rose from her seat, and approached the side of the bed.\par
She grasped with her icy fingers my hand which hung lifeless upon the\par
Coverture, and pressing her cold lips to mine, again repeated,\par
\par
‘Raymond! Raymond! Thou art mine!\par
Raymond! Raymond! I am thine! &c.—’\par
\par
\par
She then dropped my hand, quitted the chamber with slow steps, and the\par
Door closed after her. Till that moment the faculties of my body had\par
been all suspended; Those of my mind had alone been waking. The charm\par
now ceased to operate: The blood which had been frozen in my veins\par
rushed back to my heart with violence: I uttered a deep groan, and sank\par
lifeless upon my pillow.\par
\par
The adjoining room was only separated from mine by a thin partition: It\par
was occupied by the Host and his Wife: The Former was rouzed by my\par
groan, and immediately hastened to my chamber: The Hostess soon\par
followed him. With some difficulty they succeeded in restoring me to my\par
senses, and immediately sent for the Physician, who arrived in all\par
diligence. He declared my fever to be very much increased, and that if\par
I continued to suffer such violent agitation, He would not take upon\par
him to ensure my life. Some medicines which He gave me in some degree\par
tranquillized my spirits. I fell into a sort of slumber towards\par
daybreak; But fearful dreams prevented me from deriving any benefit\par
from my repose. Agnes and the Bleeding Nun presented themselves by\par
turns to my fancy, and combined to harass and torment me. I awoke\par
fatigued and unrefreshed. My fever seemed rather augmented than\par
diminished; The agitation of my mind impeded my fractured bones from\par
knitting: I had frequent fainting fits, and during the whole day the\par
Physician judged it expedient not to quit me for two hours together.\par
\par
The singularity of my adventure made me determine to conceal it from\par
every one, since I could not expect that a circumstance so strange\par
should gain credit. I was very uneasy about Agnes. I knew not what She\par
would think at not finding me at the rendezvous, and dreaded her\par
entertaining suspicions of my fidelity. However, I depended upon\par
Theodore’s discretion, and trusted that my letter to the Baroness would\par
convince her of the rectitude of my intentions. These considerations\par
somewhat lightened my inquietude upon her account: But the impression\par
left upon my mind by my nocturnal Visitor grew stronger with every\par
succeeding moment. The night drew near; I dreaded its arrival. Yet I\par
strove to persuade myself that the Ghost would appear no more, and at\par
all events I desired that a Servant might sit up in my chamber.\par
\par
The fatigue of my body from not having slept on the former night,\par
co-operating with the strong opiates administered to me in profusion,\par
at length procured me that repose of which I was so much in need. I\par
sank into a profound and tranquil slumber, and had already slept for\par
some hours, when the neighbouring Clock rouzed me by striking “One”.\par
Its sound brought with it to my memory all the horrors of the night\par
before. The same cold shivering seized me. I started up in my bed, and\par
perceived the Servant fast asleep in an armed-Chair near me. I called\par
him by his name: He made no answer. I shook him forcibly by the arm,\par
and strove in vain to wake him. He was perfectly insensible to my\par
efforts. I now heard the heavy steps ascending the staircase; The Door\par
was thrown open, and again the Bleeding Nun stood before me. Once more\par
my limbs were chained in second infancy. Once more I heard those fatal\par
words repeated,\par
\par
‘Raymond! Raymond! Thou art mine!\par
Raymond! Raymond! I am thine! &c.——’\par
\par
\par
The scene which had shocked me so sensibly on the former night, was\par
again presented. The Spectre again pressed her lips to mine, again\par
touched me with her rotting fingers, and as on her first appearance,\par
quitted the chamber as soon as the Clock told “Two.”\par
\par
Even night was this repeated. Far from growing accustomed to the Ghost,\par
every succeeding visit inspired me with greater horror. Her idea\par
pursued me continually, and I became the prey of habitual melancholy.\par
The constant agitation of my mind naturally retarded the\par
re-establishment of my health. Several months elapsed before I was able\par
to quit my bed; and when at length I was moved to a Sopha, I was so\par
faint, spiritless, and emaciated, that I could not cross the room\par
without assistance. The looks of my Attendants sufficiently denoted the\par
little hope, which they entertained of my recovery. The profound\par
sadness, which oppressed me without remission made the Physician\par
consider me to be an Hypochondriac. The cause of my distress I\par
carefully concealed in my own bosom, for I knew that no one could give\par
me relief: The Ghost was not even visible to any eye but mine. I had\par
frequently caused Attendants to sit up in my room: But the moment that\par
the Clock struck “One,” irresistible slumber seized them, nor left them\par
till the departure of the Ghost.\par
\par
You may be surprized that during this time I made no enquiries after\par
your Sister. Theodore, who with difficulty had discovered my abode, had\par
quieted my apprehensions for her safety: At the same time He convinced\par
me that all attempts to release her from captivity must be fruitless\par
till I should be in a condition to return to Spain. The particulars of\par
her adventure which I shall now relate to you, were partly communicated\par
to me by Theodore, and partly by Agnes herself.\par
\par
On the fatal night when her elopement was to have taken place, accident\par
had not permitted her to quit her chamber at the appointed time. At\par
length She ventured into the haunted room, descended the staircase\par
leading into the Hall, found the Gates open as She expected, and left\par
the Castle unobserved. What was her surprize at not finding me ready to\par
receive her! She examined the Cavern, ranged through every Alley of the\par
neighbouring wood, and passed two full hours in this fruitless enquiry.\par
She could discover no traces either of me or of the Carriage. Alarmed\par
and disappointed, her only resource was to return to the Castle before\par
the Baroness missed her: But here She found herself in a fresh\par
embarrassment. The Bell had already tolled “Two:” The Ghostly hour was\par
past, and the careful Porter had locked the folding gates. After much\par
irresolution She ventured to knock softly. Luckily for her, Conrad was\par
still awake: He heard the noise and rose, murmuring at being called up\par
a second time. No sooner had He opened one of the Doors, and beheld the\par
supposed Apparition waiting there for admittance, than He uttered a\par
loud cry, and sank upon his knees. Agnes profited by his terror. She\par
glided by him, flew to her own apartment, and having thrown off her\par
Spectre’s trappings, retired to bed endeavouring in vain to account for\par
my disappearing.\par
\par
In the mean while Theodore having seen my Carriage drive off with the\par
false Agnes, returned joyfully to the Village. The next morning He\par
released Cunegonda from her confinement, and accompanied her to the\par
Castle. There He found the Baron, his Lady, and Don Gaston, disputing\par
together upon the Porter’s relation. All of them agreed in believing\par
the existence of Spectres: But the Latter contended, that for a Ghost\par
to knock for admittance was a proceeding till then unwitnessed, and\par
totally incompatible with the immaterial nature of a Spirit. They were\par
still discussing this subject when the Page appeared with Cunegonda and\par
cleared up the mystery. On hearing his deposition, it was agreed\par
unanimously that the Agnes whom Theodore had seen step into my Carriage\par
must have been the Bleeding Nun, and that the Ghost who had terrified\par
Conrad was no other than Don Gaston’s Daughter.\par
\par
The first surprize which this discovery occasioned being over, the\par
Baroness resolved to make it of use in persuading her Niece to take the\par
veil. Fearing lest so advantageous an establishment for his Daughter\par
should induce Don Gaston to renounce his resolution, She suppressed my\par
letter, and continued to represent me as a needy unknown Adventurer. A\par
childish vanity had led me to conceal my real name even from my\par
Mistress; I wished to be loved for myself, not for being the Son and\par
Heir of the Marquis de las Cisternas. The consequence was that my rank\par
was known to no one in the Castle except the Baroness, and She took\par
good care to confine the knowledge to her own breast. Don Gaston having\par
approved his Sister’s design, Agnes was summoned to appear before them.\par
She was taxed with having meditated an elopement, obliged to make a\par
full confession, and was amazed at the gentleness with which it was\par
received: But what was her affliction, when informed that the failure\par
of her project must be attributed to me! Cunegonda, tutored by the\par
Baroness, told her that when I released her, I had desired her to\par
inform her Lady that our connexion was at an end, that the whole affair\par
was occasioned by a false report, and that it by no means suited my\par
circumstances to marry a Woman without fortune or expectations.\par
\par
To this account my sudden disappearing gave but too great an air of\par
probability. Theodore, who could have contradicted the story, by Donna\par
Rodolpha’s order was kept out of her sight: What proved a still greater\par
confirmation of my being an Impostor, was the arrival of a letter from\par
yourself declaring that you had no sort of acquaintance with Alphonso\par
d’Alvarada. These seeming proofs of my perfidy, aided by the artful\par
insinuations of her Aunt, by Cunegonda’s flattery, and her Father’s\par
threats and anger, entirely conquered your Sister’s repugnance to a\par
Convent. Incensed at my behaviour, and disgusted with the world in\par
general, She consented to receive the veil. She past another Month at\par
the Castle of Lindenberg, during which my non-appearance confirmed her\par
in her resolution, and then accompanied Don Gaston into Spain. Theodore\par
was now set at liberty. He hastened to Munich, where I had promised to\par
let him hear from me; But finding from Lucas that I had never arrived\par
there, He pursued his search with indefatigable perseverance, and at\par
length succeeded in rejoining me at Ratisbon.\par
\par
So much was I altered, that scarcely could He recollect my features:\par
The distress visible upon his sufficiently testified how lively was the\par
interest which He felt for me. The society of this amiable Boy, whom I\par
had always considered rather as a Companion than a Servant, was now my\par
only comfort. His conversation was gay yet sensible, and his\par
observations shrewd and entertaining: He had picked up much more\par
knowledge than is usual at his Age: But what rendered him most\par
agreeable to me, was his having a delightful voice, and some skill in\par
Music. He had also acquired some taste in poetry, and even ventured\par
sometimes to write verses himself. He occasionally composed little\par
Ballads in Spanish, his compositions were but indifferent, I must\par
confess; yet they were pleasing to me from their novelty, and hearing\par
him sing them to his guitar was the only amusement, which I was capable\par
of receiving. Theodore perceived well enough that something preyed upon\par
my mind; But as I concealed the cause of my grief even from him,\par
Respect would not permit him to pry into my secrets.\par
\par
One Evening I was lying upon my Sopha, plunged in reflections very far\par
from agreeable: Theodore amused himself by observing from the window a\par
Battle between two Postillions, who were quarrelling in the Inn-yard.\par
\par
“Ha! Ha!” cried He suddenly; “Yonder is the Great Mogul.”\par
\par
“Who?” said I.\par
\par
“Only a Man who made me a strange speech at Munich.”\par
\par
“What was the purport of it?”\par
\par
“Now you put me in mind of it, Segnor, it was a kind of message to you;\par
but truly it was not worth delivering. I believe the Fellow to be mad,\par
for my part. When I came to Munich in search of you, I found him living\par
at “The King of the Romans,” and the Host gave me an odd account of\par
him. By his accent He is supposed to be a Foreigner, but of what\par
Country nobody can tell. He seemed to have no acquaintance in the Town,\par
spoke very seldom, and never was seen to smile. He had neither Servants\par
or Baggage; But his Purse seemed well-furnished, and He did much good\par
in the Town. Some supposed him to be an Arabian Astrologer, Others to\par
be a Travelling Mountebank, and many declared that He was Doctor\par
Faustus, whom the Devil had sent back to Germany. The Landlord, however\par
told me, that He had the best reasons to believe him to be the Great\par
Mogul incognito.”\par
\par
“But the strange speech, Theodore.”\par
\par
“True, I had almost forgotten the speech: Indeed for that matter, it\par
would not have been a great loss if I had forgotten it altogether. You\par
are to know, Segnor, that while I was enquiring about you of the\par
Landlord, this Stranger passed by. He stopped, and looked at me\par
earnestly. “Youth!” said He in a solemn voice, “He whom you seek, has\par
found that which He would fain lose. My hand alone can dry up the\par
blood: Bid your Master wish for me when the Clock strikes, “One.”\par
\par
“How?” cried I, starting from my Sopha. (The words which Theodore had\par
repeated, seemed to imply the Stranger’s knowledge of my secret) “Fly\par
to him, my Boy! Entreat him to grant me one moment’s conversation!”\par
\par
Theodore was surprised at the vivacity of my manner: However, He asked\par
no questions, but hastened to obey me. I waited his return impatiently.\par
But a short space of time had elapsed when He again appeared and\par
ushered the expected Guest into my chamber. He was a Man of majestic\par
presence: His countenance was strongly marked, and his eyes were large,\par
black, and sparkling: Yet there was a something in his look which, the\par
moment that I saw him, inspired me with a secret awe, not to say\par
horror. He was drest plainly, his hair was unpowdered, and a band of\par
black velvet which encircled his forehead spread over his features an\par
additional gloom. His countenance wore the marks of profound\par
melancholy; his step was slow, and his manner grave, stately, and\par
solemn.\par
\par
He saluted me with politeness; and having replied to the usual\par
compliments of introduction, He motioned to Theodore to quit the\par
chamber. The Page instantly withdrew.\par
\par
“I know your business,” said He, without giving me time to speak.\par
\par
“I have the power of releasing you from your nightly Visitor; But this\par
cannot be done before Sunday. On the hour when the Sabbath Morning\par
breaks, Spirits of darkness have least influence over Mortals. After\par
Saturday the Nun shall visit you no more.”\par
\par
“May I not enquire,” said I, “by what means you are in possession of a\par
secret which I have carefully concealed from the knowledge of\par
everyone?”\par
\par
“How can I be ignorant of your distress, when their cause at this\par
moment stands beside you?”\par
\par
I started. The Stranger continued.\par
\par
“Though to you only visible for one hour in the twenty-four, neither\par
day or night does She ever quit you; Nor will She ever quit you till\par
you have granted her request.”\par
\par
“And what is that request?”\par
\par
“That She must herself explain: It lies not in my knowledge. Wait with\par
patience for the night of Saturday: All shall be then cleared up.”\par
\par
I dared not press him further. He soon after changed the conversation\par
and talked of various matters. He named People who had ceased to exist\par
for many Centuries, and yet with whom He appeared to have been\par
personally acquainted. I could not mention a Country however distant\par
which He had not visited, nor could I sufficiently admire the extent\par
and variety of his information. I remarked to him that having\par
travelled, seen, and known so much, must have given him infinite\par
pleasure. He shook his head mournfully.\par
\par
“No one,” He replied, “is adequate to comprehending the misery of my\par
lot! Fate obliges me to be constantly in movement: I am not permitted\par
to pass more than a fortnight in the same place. I have no Friend in\par
the world, and from the restlessness of my destiny I never can acquire\par
one. Fain would I lay down my miserable life, for I envy those who\par
enjoy the quiet of the Grave: But Death eludes me, and flies from my\par
embrace. In vain do I throw myself in the way of danger. I plunge into\par
the Ocean; The Waves throw me back with abhorrence upon the shore: I\par
rush into fire; The flames recoil at my approach: I oppose myself to\par
the fury of Banditti; Their swords become blunted, and break against my\par
breast: The hungry Tiger shudders at my approach, and the Alligator\par
flies from a Monster more horrible than itself. God has set his seal\par
upon me, and all his Creatures respect this fatal mark!”\par
\par
He put his hand to the velvet, which was bound round his forehead.\par
There was in his eyes an expression of fury, despair, and malevolence,\par
that struck horror to my very soul. An involuntary convulsion made me\par
shudder. The Stranger perceived it.\par
\par
“Such is the curse imposed on me,” he continued: “I am doomed to\par
inspire all who look on me with terror and detestation. You already\par
feel the influence of the charm, and with every succeeding moment will\par
feel it more. I will not add to your sufferings by my presence.\par
Farewell till Saturday. As soon as the Clock strikes twelve, expect me\par
at your chamber door.”\par
\par
Having said this He departed, leaving me in astonishment at the\par
mysterious turn of his manner and conversation.\par
\par
His assurances that I should soon be relieved from the Apparition’s\par
visits produced a good effect upon my constitution. Theodore, whom I\par
rather treated as an adopted Child than a Domestic, was surprized at\par
his return to observe the amendment in my looks. He congratulated me on\par
this symptom of returning health, and declared himself delighted at my\par
having received so much benefit from my conference with the Great\par
Mogul. Upon enquiry I found that the Stranger had already past eight\par
days in Ratisbon: According to his own account, therefore, He was only\par
to remain there six days longer. Saturday was still at the distance of\par
Three. Oh! with what impatience did I expect its arrival! In the\par
interim, the Bleeding Nun continued her nocturnal visits; But hoping\par
soon to be released from them altogether, the effects which they\par
produced on me became less violent than before.\par
\par
The wished-for night arrived. To avoid creating suspicion I retired to\par
bed at my usual hour: But as soon as my Attendants had left me, I\par
dressed myself again, and prepared for the Stranger’s reception. He\par
entered my room upon the turn of midnight. A small Chest was in his\par
hand, which He placed near the Stove. He saluted me without speaking; I\par
returned the compliment, observing an equal silence. He then opened his\par
Chest. The first thing which He produced was a small wooden Crucifix:\par
He sank upon his knees, gazed upon it mournfully, and cast his eyes\par
towards heaven. He seemed to be praying devoutly. At length He bowed\par
his head respectfully, kissed the Crucifix thrice, and quitted his\par
kneeling posture. He next drew from the Chest a covered Goblet: With\par
the liquor which it contained, and which appeared to be blood, He\par
sprinkled the floor, and then dipping in it one end of the Crucifix, He\par
described a circle in the middle of the room. Round about this He\par
placed various reliques, sculls, thigh-bones &c; I observed, that He\par
disposed them all in the forms of Crosses. Lastly He took out a large\par
Bible, and beckoned me to follow him into the Circle. I obeyed.\par
\par
“Be cautious not to utter a syllable!” whispered the Stranger; “Step\par
not out of the circle, and as you love yourself, dare not to look upon\par
my face!”\par
\par
Holding the Crucifix in one hand, the Bible in the other, He seemed to\par
read with profound attention. The Clock struck “One”! As usual I heard\par
the Spectre’s steps upon the Staircase: But I was not seized with the\par
accustomed shivering. I waited her approach with confidence. She\par
entered the room, drew near the Circle, and stopped. The Stranger\par
muttered some words, to me unintelligible. Then raising his head from\par
the Book, and extending the Crucifix towards the Ghost, He pronounced\par
in a voice distinct and solemn,\par
\par
“Beatrice! Beatrice! Beatrice!”\par
\par
“What wouldst Thou?” replied the Apparition in a hollow faltering tone.\par
\par
“What disturbs thy sleep? Why dost thou afflict and torture this Youth?\par
How can rest be restored to thy unquiet Spirit?”\par
\par
“I dare not tell!—I must not tell!—Fain would I repose in my Grave, but\par
stern commands force me to prolong my punishment!”\par
\par
“Knowest Thou this blood? Knowest Thou in whose veins it flowed?\par
\par
Beatrice! Beatrice! In his name I charge thee to answer me!”\par
\par
“I dare not disobey my taskers.”\par
\par
“Darest Thou disobey Me?”\par
\par
He spoke in a commanding tone, and drew the sable band from his\par
forehead. In spite of his injunctions to the contrary, Curiosity would\par
not suffer me to keep my eyes off his face: I raised them, and beheld a\par
burning Cross impressed upon his brow. For the horror with which this\par
object inspired me I cannot account, but I never felt its equal! My\par
senses left me for some moments; A mysterious dread overcame my\par
courage, and had not the Exorciser caught my hand, I should have fallen\par
out of the Circle.\par
\par
When I recovered myself, I perceived that the burning Cross had\par
produced an effect no less violent upon the Spectre. Her countenance\par
expressed reverence, and horror, and her visionary limbs were shaken by\par
fear.\par
\par
“Yes!” She said at length; “I tremble at that mark!—respect it!—I obey\par
you! Know then, that my bones lie still unburied: They rot in the\par
obscurity of Lindenberg Hole. None but this Youth has the right of\par
consigning them to the Grave. His own lips have made over to me his\par
body and his soul: Never will I give back his promise, never shall He\par
know a night devoid of terror, unless He engages to collect my\par
mouldering bones, and deposit them in the family vault of his\par
Andalusian Castle. Then let thirty Masses be said for the repose of my\par
Spirit, and I trouble this world no more. Now let me depart! Those\par
flames are scorching!”\par
\par
He let the hand drop slowly which held the Crucifix, and which till\par
then He had pointed towards her. The apparition bowed her head, and her\par
form melted into air. The Exorciser led me out of the Circle. He\par
replaced the Bible &c. in the Chest, and then addressed himself to me,\par
who stood near him speechless from astonishment.\par
\par
“Don Raymond, you have heard the conditions on which repose is promised\par
you. Be it your business to fulfil them to the letter. For me nothing\par
more remains than to clear up the darkness still spread over the\par
Spectre’s History, and inform you that when living, Beatrice bore the\par
name of las Cisternas. She was the great Aunt of your Grandfather: In\par
quality of your relation, her ashes demand respect from you, though the\par
enormity of her crimes must excite your abhorrence. The nature of those\par
crimes no one is more capable of explaining to you than myself: I was\par
personally acquainted with the holy Man who proscribed her nocturnal\par
riots in the Castle of Lindenberg, and I hold this narrative from his\par
own lips.\par
\par
“Beatrice de las Cisternas took the veil at an early age, not by her\par
own choice, but at the express command of her Parents. She was then too\par
young to regret the pleasures of which her profession deprived her: But\par
no sooner did her warm and voluptuous character begin to be developed\par
than She abandoned herself freely to the impulse of her passions, and\par
seized the first opportunity to procure their gratification. This\par
opportunity was at length presented, after many obstacles which only\par
added new force to her desires. She contrived to elope from the\par
Convent, and fled to Germany with the Baron Lindenberg. She lived at\par
his Castle several months as his avowed Concubine: All Bavaria was\par
scandalized by her impudent and abandoned conduct. Her feasts vied in\par
luxury with Cleopatra’s, and Lindenberg became the Theatre of the most\par
unbridled debauchery. Not satisfied with displaying the incontinence of\par
a Prostitute, She professed herself an Atheist: She took every\par
opportunity to scoff at her monastic vows, and loaded with ridicule the\par
most sacred ceremonies of Religion.\par
\par
“Possessed of a character so depraved, She did not long confine her\par
affections to one object. Soon after her arrival at the Castle, the\par
Baron’s younger Brother attracted her notice by his strong-marked\par
features, gigantic Stature, and Herculean limbs. She was not of an\par
humour to keep her inclinations long unknown; But She found in Otto von\par
Lindenberg her equal in depravity. He returned her passion just\par
sufficiently to increase it; and when He had worked it up to the\par
desired pitch, He fixed the price of his love at his Brother’s murder.\par
The Wretch consented to this horrible agreement. A night was pitched\par
upon for perpetrating the deed. Otto, who resided on a small Estate a\par
few miles distant from the Castle, promised that at One in the morning\par
He would be waiting for her at Lindenberg Hole; that He would bring\par
with him a party of chosen Friends, by whose aid He doubted not being\par
able to make himself Master of the Castle; and that his next step\par
should be the uniting her hand to his. It was this last promise, which\par
overruled every scruple of Beatrice, since in spite of his affection\par
for her, the Baron had declared positively that He never would make her\par
his Wife.\par
\par
“The fatal night arrived. The Baron slept in the arms of his perfidious\par
Mistress, when the Castle-Bell struck “One.” Immediately Beatrice drew\par
a dagger from underneath the pillow, and plunged it in her Paramour’s\par
heart. The Baron uttered a single dreadful groan, and expired. The\par
Murderess quitted her bed hastily, took a Lamp in one hand, in the\par
other the bloody dagger, and bent her course towards the cavern. The\par
Porter dared not to refuse opening the Gates to one more dreaded in the\par
Castle than its Master. Beatrice reached Lindenberg Hole unopposed,\par
where according to promise She found Otto waiting for her. He received\par
and listened to her narrative with transport: But ere She had time to\par
ask why He came unaccompanied, He convinced her that He wished for no\par
witnesses to their interview. Anxious to conceal his share in the\par
murder, and to free himself from a Woman, whose violent and atrocious\par
character made him tremble with reason for his own safety, He had\par
resolved on the destruction of his wretched Agent. Rushing upon her\par
suddenly, He wrested the dagger from her hand: He plunged it still\par
reeking with his Brother’s blood in her bosom, and put an end to her\par
existence by repeated blows.\par
\par
“Otto now succeeded to the Barony of Lindenberg. The murder was\par
attributed solely to the fugitive Nun, and no one suspected him to have\par
persuaded her to the action. But though his crime was unpunished by\par
Man, God’s justice permitted him not to enjoy in peace his\par
blood-stained honours. Her bones lying still unburied in the Cave, the\par
restless soul of Beatrice continued to inhabit the Castle. Drest in her\par
religious habit in memory of her vows broken to heaven, furnished with\par
the dagger which had drank the blood of her Paramour, and holding the\par
Lamp which had guided her flying steps, every night did She stand\par
before the Bed of Otto. The most dreadful confusion reigned through the\par
Castle; The vaulted chambers resounded with shrieks and groans; And the\par
Spectre, as She ranged along the antique Galleries, uttered an\par
incoherent mixture of prayers and blasphemies. Otto was unable to\par
withstand the shock which He felt at this fearful Vision: Its horror\par
increased with every succeeding appearance: His alarm at length became\par
so insupportable that his heart burst, and one morning He was found in\par
his bed totally deprived of warmth and animation. His death did not put\par
an end to the nocturnal riots. The bones of Beatrice continued to lie\par
unburied, and her Ghost continued to haunt the Castle.\par
\par
“The domains of Lindenberg now fell to a distant Relation. But\par
terrified by the accounts given him of the Bleeding Nun (So was the\par
Spectre called by the multitude), the new Baron called to his\par
assistance a celebrated Exorciser. This holy Man succeeded in obliging\par
her to temporary repose; But though She discovered to him her history,\par
He was not permitted to reveal it to others, or cause her skeleton to\par
be removed to hallowed ground. That Office was reserved for you, and\par
till your coming, her Ghost was doomed to wander about the Castle and\par
lament the crime which She had there committed. However, the Exorciser\par
obliged her to silence during his lifetime. So long as He existed, the\par
haunted chamber was shut up, and the Spectre was invisible. At his\par
death which happened in five years after, She again appeared, but only\par
once on every fifth year, on the same day and at the same hour when She\par
plunged her Knife in the heart of her sleeping Lover: She then visited\par
the Cavern which held her mouldering skeleton, returned to the Castle\par
as soon as the Clock struck “Two,” and was seen no more till the next\par
five years had elapsed.\par
\par
“She was doomed to suffer during the space of a Century. That period is\par
past. Nothing now remains but to consign to the Grave the ashes of\par
Beatrice. I have been the means of releasing you from your visionary\par
Tormentor; and amidst all the sorrows which oppress me, to think that I\par
have been of use to you, is some consolation. Youth, farewell! May the\par
Ghost of your Relation enjoy that rest in the Tomb, which the\par
Almighty’s vengeance has denied to me for ever!”\par
\par
Here the Stranger prepared to quit the apartment.\par
\par
“Stay yet one moment!” said I; “You have satisfied my curiosity with\par
regard to the Spectre, but you leave me in prey to yet greater\par
respecting yourself. Deign to inform me, to whom I am under such real\par
obligations. You mention circumstances long past, and persons long\par
dead: You were personally acquainted with the Exorciser, who by your\par
own account has been deceased near a Century. How am I to account for\par
this? What means that burning Cross upon your forehead, and why did the\par
sight of it strike such horror to my soul?”\par
\par
On these points He for some time refused to satisfy me. At length\par
overcome by my entreaties, He consented to clear up the whole, on\par
condition that I would defer his explanation till the next day. With\par
this request I was obliged to comply, and He left me. In the Morning my\par
first care was to enquire after the mysterious Stranger. Conceive my\par
disappointment when informed that He had already quitted Ratisbon. I\par
dispatched messengers in pursuit of him but in vain. No traces of the\par
Fugitive were discovered. Since that moment I never have heard any more\par
of him, and ’tis most probable that I never shall.”\par
\par
(Lorenzo here interrupted his Friend’s narrative.\par
\par
“How?” said He; “You have never discovered who He was, or even formed a\par
guess?”\par
\par
“Pardon me,” replied the Marquis; “When I related this adventure to my\par
Uncle, the Cardinal-Duke, He told me that He had no doubt of this\par
singular Man’s being the celebrated Character known universally by the\par
name of “the wandering Jew.” His not being permitted to pass more than\par
fourteen days on the same spot, the burning Cross impressed upon his\par
forehead, the effect which it produced upon the Beholders, and many\par
other circumstances give this supposition the colour of truth. The\par
Cardinal is fully persuaded of it; and for my own part I am inclined to\par
adopt the only solution which offers itself to this riddle. I return to\par
the narrative from which I have digressed.”)\par
\par
From this period I recovered my health so rapidly as to astonish my\par
Physicians. The Bleeding Nun appeared no more, and I was soon able to\par
set out for Lindenberg. The Baron received me with open arms. I\par
confided to him the sequel of my adventure; and He was not a little\par
pleased to find that his Mansion would be no longer troubled with the\par
Phantom’s quiennial visits. I was sorry to perceive that absence had\par
not weakened Donna Rodolpha’s imprudent passion. In a private\par
conversation which I had with her during my short stay at the Castle,\par
She renewed her attempts to persuade me to return her affection.\par
Regarding her as the primary cause of all my sufferings, I entertained\par
for her no other sentiment than disgust. The Skeleton of Beatrice was\par
found in the place which She had mentioned. This being all that I\par
sought at Lindenberg, I hastened to quit the Baron’s domains, equally\par
anxious to perform the obsequies of the murdered Nun, and escape the\par
importunity of a Woman whom I detested. I departed, followed by Donna\par
Rodolpha’s menaces that my contempt should not be long unpunished.\par
\par
I now bent my course towards Spain with all diligence. Lucas with my\par
Baggage had joined me during my abode at Lindenberg. I arrived in my\par
native Country without any accident, and immediately proceeded to my\par
Father’s Castle in Andalusia. The remains of Beatrice were deposited in\par
the family vault, all due ceremonies performed, and the number of\par
Masses said which She had required. Nothing now hindered me from\par
employing all my endeavours to discover the retreat of Agnes. The\par
Baroness had assured me that her Niece had already taken the veil: This\par
intelligence I suspected to have been forged by jealousy, and hoped to\par
find my Mistress still at liberty to accept my hand. I enquired after\par
her family; I found that before her Daughter could reach Madrid, Donna\par
Inesilla was no more: You, my dear Lorenzo, were said to be abroad, but\par
where I could not discover: Your Father was in a distant Province on a\par
visit to the Duke de Medina, and as to Agnes, no one could or would\par
inform me what was become of her. Theodore, according to promise, had\par
returned to Strasbourg, where He found his Grandfather dead, and\par
Marguerite in possession of his fortune. All her persuasions to remain\par
with her were fruitless: He quitted her a second time, and followed me\par
to Madrid. He exerted himself to the utmost in forwarding my search:\par
But our united endeavours were unattended by success. The retreat which\par
concealed Agnes remained an impenetrable mystery, and I began to\par
abandon all hopes of recovering her.\par
\par
About eight months ago I was returning to my Hotel in a melancholy\par
humour, having past the evening at the Play-House. The Night was dark,\par
and I was unaccompanied. Plunged in reflections which were far from\par
being agreeable, I perceived not that three Men had followed me from\par
the Theatre; till, on turning into an unfrequented Street, they all\par
attacked me at the same time with the utmost fury. I sprang back a few\par
paces, drew my sword, and threw my cloak over my left arm. The\par
obscurity of the night was in my favour. For the most part the blows of\par
the Assassins, being aimed at random, failed to touch me. I at length\par
was fortunate enough to lay one of my Adversaries at my feet; But\par
before this I had already received so many wounds, and was so warmly\par
pressed, that my destruction would have been inevitable, had not the\par
clashing of swords called a Cavalier to my assistance. He ran towards\par
me with his sword drawn: Several Domestics followed him with torches.\par
His arrival made the combat equal: Yet would not the Bravoes abandon\par
their design till the Servants were on the point of joining us. They\par
then fled away, and we lost them in the obscurity.\par
\par
The Stranger now addressed himself to me with politeness, and enquired\par
whether I was wounded. Faint with the loss of blood, I could scarcely\par
thank him for his seasonable aid, and entreat him to let some of his\par
Servants convey me to the Hotel de las Cisternas. I no sooner mentioned\par
the name than He profest himself an acquaintance of my Father’s, and\par
declared that He would not permit my being transported to such a\par
distance before my wounds had been examined. He added that his House\par
was hard by, and begged me to accompany him thither. His manner was so\par
earnest, that I could not reject his offer, and leaning upon his arm, a\par
few minutes brought me to the Porch of a magnificent Hotel.\par
\par
On entering the House, an old grey-headed Domestic came to welcome my\par
Conductor: He enquired when the Duke, his Master, meant to quit the\par
Country, and was answered that He would remain there yet some months.\par
My Deliverer then desired the family Surgeon to be summoned without\par
delay. His orders were obeyed. I was seated upon a Sopha in a noble\par
apartment; and my wounds being examined, they were declared to be very\par
slight. The Surgeon, however, advised me not to expose myself to the\par
night air; and the Stranger pressed me so earnestly to take a bed in\par
his House, that I consented to remain where I was for the present.\par
\par
Being now left alone with my Deliverer, I took the opportunity of\par
thanking him in more express terms, than I had done hitherto: But He\par
begged me to be silent upon the subject.\par
\par
“I esteem myself happy,” said He, “in having had it in my power to\par
render you this little service; and I shall think myself eternally\par
obliged to my Daughter for detaining me so late at the Convent of St.\par
Clare. The high esteem in which I have ever held the Marquis de las\par
Cisternas, though accident has not permitted our being so intimate as I\par
could wish, makes me rejoice in the opportunity of making his Son’s\par
acquaintance. I am certain that my Brother in whose House you now are,\par
will lament his not being at Madrid to receive you himself: But in the\par
Duke’s absence I am Master of the family, and may assure you in his\par
name, that every thing in the Hotel de Medina is perfectly at your\par
disposal.”\par
\par
Conceive my surprize, Lorenzo, at discovering in the person of my\par
Preserver Don Gaston de Medina: It was only to be equalled by my secret\par
satisfaction at the assurance that Agnes inhabited the Convent of St.\par
Clare. This latter sensation was not a little weakened, when in answer\par
to my seemingly indifferent questions He told me that his Daughter had\par
really taken the veil. I suffered not my grief at this circumstance to\par
take root in my mind: I flattered myself with the idea that my Uncle’s\par
credit at the Court of Rome would remove this obstacle, and that\par
without difficulty I should obtain for my Mistress a dispensation from\par
her vows. Buoyed up with this hope I calmed the uneasiness of my bosom;\par
and I redoubled my endeavours to appear grateful for the attention and\par
pleased with the society of Don Gaston.\par
\par
A Domestic now entered the room, and informed me that the Bravo whom I\par
had wounded discovered some signs of life. I desired that He might be\par
carried to my Father’s Hotel, and that as soon as He recovered his\par
voice, I would examine him respecting his reasons for attempting my\par
life. I was answered that He was already able to speak, though with\par
difficulty: Don Gaston’s curiosity made him press me to interrogate the\par
Assassin in his presence, but this curiosity I was by no means inclined\par
to gratify. One reason was, that doubting from whence the blow came, I\par
was unwilling to place before Don Gaston’s eyes the guilt of a Sister:\par
Another was, that I feared to be recognized for Alphonso d’Alvarada,\par
and precautions taken in consequence to keep me from the sight of\par
Agnes. To avow my passion for his Daughter, and endeavour to make him\par
enter into my schemes, what I knew of Don Gaston’s character convinced\par
me would be an imprudent step: and considering it to be essential that\par
He should know me for no other than the Condé de las Cisternas, I was\par
determined not to let him hear the Bravo’s confession. I insinuated to\par
him, that as I suspected a Lady to be concerned in the Business, whose\par
name might accidentally escape from the Assassin, it was necessary for\par
me to examine the Man in private. Don Gaston’s delicacy would not\par
permit his urging the point any longer, and in consequence the Bravo\par
was conveyed to my Hotel.\par
\par
The next Morning I took leave of my Host, who was to return to the Duke\par
on the same day. My wounds had been so trifling that, except being\par
obliged to wear my arm in a sling for a short time, I felt no\par
inconvenience from the night’s adventure. The Surgeon who examined the\par
Bravo’s wound declared it to be mortal: He had just time to confess\par
that He had been instigated to murder me by the revengeful Donna\par
Rodolpha, and expired in a few minutes after.\par
\par
All my thoughts were now bent upon getting to the speech of my lovely\par
Nun. Theodore set himself to work, and for this time with better\par
success. He attacked the Gardener of St. Clare so forcibly with bribes\par
and promises that the Old Man was entirely gained over to my interests;\par
and it was settled that I should be introduced into the Convent in the\par
character of his Assistant. The plan was put into execution without\par
delay. Disguised in a common habit, and a black patch covering one of\par
my eyes, I was presented to the Lady Prioress, who condescended to\par
approve of the Gardener’s choice. I immediately entered upon my\par
employment. Botany having been a favourite study with me, I was by no\par
means at a loss in my new station. For some days I continued to work in\par
the Convent Garden without meeting the Object of my disguise: On the\par
fourth Morning I was more successful. I heard the voice of Agnes, and\par
was speeding towards the sound, when the sight of the Domina stopped\par
me. I drew back with caution, and concealed myself behind a thick clump\par
of Trees.\par
\par
The Prioress advanced and seated herself with Agnes on a Bench at no\par
great distance. I heard her in an angry tone blame her Companion’s\par
continual melancholy: She told her that to weep the loss of any Lover\par
in her situation was a crime; But that to weep the loss of a faithless\par
one was folly and absurdity in the extreme. Agnes replied in so low a\par
voice that I could not distinguish her words, but I perceived that She\par
used terms of gentleness and submission. The conversation was\par
interrupted by the arrival of a young Pensioner who informed the Domina\par
that She was waited for in the Parlour. The old Lady rose, kissed the\par
cheek of Agnes, and retired. The newcomer remained. Agnes spoke much to\par
her in praise of somebody whom I could not make out, but her Auditor\par
seemed highly delighted, and interested by the conversation. The Nun\par
showed her several letters; the Other perused them with evident\par
pleasure, obtained permission to copy them, and withdrew for that\par
purpose to my great satisfaction.\par
\par
No sooner was She out of sight, than I quitted my concealment. Fearing\par
to alarm my lovely Mistress, I drew near her gently, intending to\par
discover myself by degrees. But who for a moment can deceive the eyes\par
of love? She raised her head at my approach, and recognised me in spite\par
of my disguise at a single glance. She rose hastily from her seat with\par
an exclamation of surprize, and attempted to retire; But I followed\par
her, detained her, and entreated to be heard. Persuaded of my falsehood\par
She refused to listen to me, and ordered me positively to quit the\par
Garden. It was now my turn to refuse. I protested that however\par
dangerous might be the consequences, I would not leave her till She had\par
heard my justification. I assured her that She had been deceived by the\par
artifices of her Relations; that I could convince her beyond the power\par
of doubt that my passion had been pure and disinterested; and I asked\par
her what should induce me to seek her in the Convent, were I influenced\par
by the selfish motives which my Enemies had ascribed to me.\par
\par
My prayers, my arguments, and vows not to quit her, till She had\par
promised to listen to me, united to her fears lest the Nuns should see\par
me with her, to her natural curiosity, and to the affection which She\par
still felt for me in spite of my supposed desertion, at length\par
prevailed. She told me that to grant my request at that moment was\par
impossible; But She engaged to be in the same spot at eleven that\par
night, and to converse with me for the last time. Having obtained this\par
promise I released her hand, and She fled back with rapidity towards\par
the Convent.\par
\par
I communicated my success to my Ally, the old Gardener: He pointed out\par
an hiding place where I might shelter myself till night without fear of\par
a discovery. Thither I betook myself at the hour when I ought to have\par
retired with my supposed Master, and waited impatiently for the\par
appointed time. The chillness of the night was in my favour, since it\par
kept the other Nuns confined to their Cells. Agnes alone was insensible\par
of the inclemency of the Air, and before eleven joined me at the spot\par
which had witnessed our former interview. Secure from interruption, I\par
related to her the true cause of my disappearing on the fatal fifth of\par
May. She was evidently much affected by my narrative: When it was\par
concluded, She confessed the injustice of her suspicions, and blamed\par
herself for having taken the veil through despair at my ingratitude.\par
\par
“But now it is too late to repine!” She added; “The die is thrown: I\par
have pronounced my vows, and dedicated myself to the service of heaven.\par
I am sensible, how ill I am calculated for a Convent. My disgust at a\par
monastic life increases daily: Ennui and discontent are my constant\par
Companions; and I will not conceal from you that the passion which I\par
formerly felt for one so near being my Husband is not yet extinguished\par
in my bosom. But we must part! Insuperable Barriers divide us from each\par
other, and on this side the Grave we must never meet again!”\par
\par
I now exerted myself to prove that our union was not so impossible as\par
She seemed to think it. I vaunted to her the Cardinal-Duke of Lerma’s\par
influence at the Court of Rome: I assured her that I should easily\par
obtain a dispensation from her vows; and I doubted not but Don Gaston\par
would coincide with my views, when informed of my real name and long\par
attachment. Agnes replied that since I encouraged such an hope, I could\par
know but little of her Father. Liberal and kind in every other respect,\par
Superstition formed the only stain upon his character. Upon this head\par
He was inflexible; He sacrificed his dearest interests to his scruples,\par
and would consider it an insult to suppose him capable of authorising\par
his daughter to break her vows to heaven.\par
\par
“But suppose,” said I interrupting her; “Suppose that He should\par
disapprove of our union; Let him remain ignorant of my proceedings,\par
till I have rescued you from the prison in which you are now confined.\par
Once my Wife, you are free from his authority: I need from him no\par
pecuniary assistance; and when He sees his resentment to be unavailing,\par
He will doubtless restore you to his favour. But let the worst happen;\par
Should Don Gaston be irreconcileable, my Relations will vie with each\par
other in making you forget his loss: and you will find in my Father a\par
substitute for the Parent of whom I shall deprive you.”\par
\par
“Don Raymond,” replied Agnes in a firm and resolute voice, “I love my\par
Father: He has treated me harshly in this one instance; but I have\par
received from him in every other so many proofs of love that his\par
affection is become necessary to my existence. Were I to quit the\par
Convent, He never would forgive me; nor can I think that on his\par
deathbed He would leave me his curse, without shuddering at the very\par
idea. Besides, I am conscious myself, that my vows are binding:\par
Wilfully did I contract my engagement with heaven; I cannot break it\par
without a crime. Then banish from your mind the idea of our being ever\par
united. I am devoted to religion; and however I may grieve at our\par
separation, I would oppose obstacles myself, to what I feel would\par
render me guilty.”\par
\par
I strove to overrule these ill-grounded scruples: We were still\par
disputing upon the subject, when the Convent Bell summoned the Nuns to\par
Matins. Agnes was obliged to attend them; But She left me not till I\par
had compelled her to promise that on the following night She would be\par
at the same place at the same hour. These meetings continued for\par
several Weeks uninterrupted; and ’tis now, Lorenzo, that I must implore\par
your indulgence. Reflect upon our situation, our youth, our long\par
attachment: Weigh all the circumstances which attended our\par
assignations, and you will confess the temptation to have been\par
irresistible; you will even pardon me when I acknowledge, that in an\par
unguarded moment, the honour of Agnes was sacrificed to my passion.”\par
\par
(Lorenzo’s eyes sparkled with fury: A deep crimson spread itself over\par
his face. He started from his seat, and attempted to draw his sword.\par
The Marquis was aware of his movement, and caught his hand: He pressed\par
it affectionately.\par
\par
“My Friend! My Brother! Hear me to the conclusion! Till then restrain\par
your passion, and be at least convinced, that if what I have related is\par
criminal, the blame must fall upon me, and not upon your Sister.”\par
\par
Lorenzo suffered himself to be prevailed upon by Don Raymond’s\par
entreaties. He resumed his place, and listened to the rest of the\par
narrative with a gloomy and impatient countenance. The Marquis thus\par
continued.)\par
\par
“Scarcely was the first burst of passion past when Agnes, recovering\par
herself, started from my arms with horror. She called me infamous\par
Seducer, loaded me with the bitterest reproaches, and beat her bosom in\par
all the wildness of delirium. Ashamed of my imprudence, I with\par
difficulty found words to excuse myself. I endeavoured to console her;\par
I threw myself at her feet, and entreated her forgiveness. She forced\par
her hand from me, which I had taken, and would have prest to my lips.\par
\par
“Touch me not!” She cried with a violence which terrified me; “Monster\par
of perfidy and ingratitude, how have I been deceived in you! I looked\par
upon you as my Friend, my Protector: I trusted myself in your hands\par
with confidence, and relying upon your honour, thought that mine ran no\par
risque. And ’tis by you, whom I adored, that I am covered with infamy!\par
’Tis by you that I have been seduced into breaking my vows to God, that\par
I am reduced to a level with the basest of my sex! Shame upon you,\par
Villain, you shall never see me more!”\par
\par
She started from the Bank on which She was seated. I endeavoured to\par
detain her; But She disengaged herself from me with violence, and took\par
refuge in the Convent.\par
\par
I retired, filled with confusion and inquietude. The next morning I\par
failed not as usual to appear in the Garden; but Agnes was no where to\par
be seen. At night I waited for her at the place where we generally met;\par
I found no better success. Several days and nights passed away in the\par
same manner. At length I saw my offended Mistress cross the walk on\par
whose borders I was working: She was accompanied by the same young\par
Pensioner, on whose arm She seemed from weakness obliged to support\par
herself. She looked upon me for a moment, but instantly turned her head\par
away. I waited her return; But She passed on to the Convent without\par
paying any attention to me, or the penitent looks with which I implored\par
her forgiveness.\par
\par
As soon as the Nuns were retired, the old Gardener joined me with a\par
sorrowful air.\par
\par
“Segnor,” said He, “it grieves me to say, that I can be no longer of\par
use to you. The Lady whom you used to meet has just assured me that if\par
I admitted you again into the Garden, She would discover the whole\par
business to the Lady Prioress. She bade me tell you also, that your\par
presence was an insult, and that if you still possess the least respect\par
for her, you will never attempt to see her more. Excuse me then for\par
informing you that I can favour your disguise no longer. Should the\par
Prioress be acquainted with my conduct, She might not be contented with\par
dismissing me her service: Out of revenge She might accuse me of having\par
profaned the Convent, and cause me to be thrown into the Prisons of the\par
Inquisition.”\par
\par
Fruitless were my attempts to conquer his resolution. He denied me all\par
future entrance into the Garden, and Agnes persevered in neither\par
letting me see or hear from her. In about a fortnight after, a violent\par
illness which had seized my Father obliged me to set out for Andalusia.\par
I hastened thither, and as I imagined, found the Marquis at the point\par
of death. Though on its first appearance his complaint was declared\par
mortal, He lingered out several Months; during which my attendance upon\par
him during his malady, and the occupation of settling his affairs after\par
his decease, permitted not my quitting Andalusia. Within these four\par
days I returned to Madrid, and on arriving at my Hotel, I there found\par
this letter waiting for me.\par
\par
(Here the Marquis unlocked the drawer of a Cabinet: He took out a\par
folded paper, which He presented to his Auditor. Lorenzo opened it, and\par
recognised his Sister’s hand. The contents were as follows:\par
\par
“Into what an abyss of misery have you plunged me! Raymond, you force\par
me to become as criminal as yourself. I had resolved never to see you\par
more; if possible, to forget you; If not, only to remember you with\par
hate. A Being for whom I already feel a Mother’s tenderness, solicits\par
me to pardon my Seducer, and apply to his love for the means of\par
preservation. Raymond, your child lives in my bosom. I tremble at the\par
vengeance of the Prioress; I tremble much for myself, yet more for the\par
innocent Creature whose existence depends upon mine. Both of us are\par
lost, should my situation be discovered. Advise me then what steps to\par
take, but seek not to see me. The Gardener, who undertakes to deliver\par
this, is dismissed, and we have nothing to hope from that quarter: The\par
Man engaged in his place is of incorruptible fidelity. The best means\par
of conveying to me your answer, is by concealing it under the great\par
Statue of St. Francis, which stands in the Capuchin Cathedral. Thither\par
I go every Thursday to confession, and shall easily have an opportunity\par
of securing your letter. I hear that you are now absent from Madrid;\par
Need I entreat you to write the very moment of your return? I will not\par
think it. Ah! Raymond! Mine is a cruel situation! Deceived by my\par
nearest Relations, compelled to embrace a profession the duties of\par
which I am ill-calculated to perform, conscious of the sanctity of\par
those duties, and seduced into violating them by One whom I least\par
suspected of perfidy, I am now obliged by circumstances to chuse\par
between death and perjury. Woman’s timidity, and maternal affection,\par
permit me not to balance in the choice. I feel all the guilt into which\par
I plunge myself, when I yield to the plan which you before proposed to\par
me. My poor Father’s death which has taken place since we met, has\par
removed one obstacle. He sleeps in his grave, and I no longer dread his\par
anger. But from the anger of God, Oh! Raymond! who shall shield me? Who\par
can protect me against my conscience, against myself? I dare not dwell\par
upon these thoughts; They will drive me mad. I have taken my\par
resolution: Procure a dispensation from my vows; I am ready to fly with\par
you. Write to me, my Husband! Tell me, that absence has not abated your\par
love, tell me that you will rescue from death your unborn Child, and\par
its unhappy Mother. I live in all the agonies of terror: Every eye\par
which is fixed upon me seems to read my secret and my shame. And you\par
are the cause of those agonies! Oh! When my heart first loved you, how\par
little did it suspect you of making it feel such pangs!\par
\par
\par
“AGNES.”\par
\par
\par
Having perused the letter, Lorenzo restored it in silence. The Marquis\par
replaced it in the Cabinet, and then proceeded.)\par
\par
“Excessive was my joy at reading this intelligence so\par
earnestly-desired, so little expected. My plan was soon arranged. When\par
Don Gaston discovered to me his Daughter’s retreat, I entertained no\par
doubt of her readiness to quit the Convent: I had, therefore, entrusted\par
the Cardinal-Duke of Lerma with the whole affair, who immediately\par
busied himself in obtaining the necessary Bull. Fortunately I had\par
afterwards neglected to stop his proceedings. Not long since I received\par
a letter from him, stating that He expected daily to receive the order\par
from the Court of Rome. Upon this I would willingly have relyed: But\par
the Cardinal wrote me word, that I must find some means of conveying\par
Agnes out of the Convent, unknown to the Prioress. He doubted not but\par
this Latter would be much incensed by losing a Person of such high rank\par
from her society, and consider the renunciation of Agnes as an insult\par
to her House. He represented her as a Woman of a violent and revengeful\par
character, capable of proceeding to the greatest extremities. It was\par
therefore to be feared, lest by confining Agnes in the Convent She\par
should frustrate my hopes, and render the Pope’s mandate unavailing.\par
Influenced by this consideration, I resolved to carry off my Mistress,\par
and conceal her till the arrival of the expected Bull in the\par
Cardinal-Duke’s Estate. He approved of my design, and profest himself\par
ready to give a shelter to the Fugitive. I next caused the new Gardener\par
of St. Clare to be seized privately, and confined in my Hotel. By this\par
means I became Master of the Key to the Garden door, and I had now\par
nothing more to do than prepare Agnes for the elopement. This was done\par
by the letter, which you saw me deliver this Evening. I told her in it,\par
that I should be ready to receive her at twelve tomorrow night, that I\par
had secured the Key of the Garden, and that She might depend upon a\par
speedy release.\par
\par
You have now, Lorenzo, heard the whole of my long narrative. I have\par
nothing to say in my excuse, save that my intentions towards your\par
Sister have been ever the most honourable: That it has always been, and\par
still is my design to make her my Wife: And that I trust, when you\par
consider these circumstances, our youth, and our attachment, you will\par
not only forgive our momentary lapse from virtue, but will aid me in\par
repairing my faults to Agnes, and securing a lawful title to her person\par
and her heart.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER V.\par
\par
\par
O You! whom Vanity’s light bark conveys\par
On Fame’s mad voyage by the wind of praise,\par
With what a shifting gale your course you ply,\par
For ever sunk too low, or borne too high!\par
Who pants for glory finds but short repose,\par
A breath revives him, and a breath o’er-throws.\par
\par
POPE.\par
\par
Here the Marquis concluded his adventures. Lorenzo, before He could\par
determine on his reply, past some moments in reflection. At length He\par
broke silence.\par
\par
“Raymond,” said He taking his hand, “strict honour would oblige me to\par
wash off in your blood the stain thrown upon my family; But the\par
circumstances of your case forbid me to consider you as an Enemy. The\par
temptation was too great to be resisted. ’Tis the superstition of my\par
Relations which has occasioned these misfortunes, and they are more the\par
Offenders than yourself and Agnes. What has past between you cannot be\par
recalled, but may yet be repaired by uniting you to my Sister. You have\par
ever been, you still continue to be, my dearest and indeed my only\par
Friend. I feel for Agnes the truest affection, and there is no one on\par
whom I would bestow her more willingly than on yourself. Pursue then\par
your design. I will accompany you tomorrow night, and conduct her\par
myself to the House of the Cardinal. My presence will be a sanction for\par
her conduct, and prevent her incurring blame by her flight from the\par
Convent.”\par
\par
The Marquis thanked him in terms by no means deficient in gratitude.\par
Lorenzo then informed him that He had nothing more to apprehend from\par
Donna Rodolpha’s enmity. Five Months had already elapsed since, in an\par
excess of passion, She broke a blood-vessel and expired in the course\par
of a few hours. He then proceeded to mention the interests of Antonia.\par
The Marquis was much surprized at hearing of this new Relation: His\par
Father had carried his hatred of Elvira to the Grave, and had never\par
given the least hint that He knew what was become of his eldest Son’s\par
Widow. Don Raymond assured his friend that He was not mistaken in\par
supposing him ready to acknowledge his Sister-in-law and her amiable\par
Daughter. The preparations for the elopement would not permit his\par
visiting them the next day; But in the meanwhile He desired Lorenzo to\par
assure them of his friendship, and to supply Elvira upon his account\par
with any sums which She might want. This the Youth promised to do, as\par
soon as her abode should be known to him: He then took leave of his\par
future Brother, and returned to the Palace de Medina.\par
\par
The day was already on the point of breaking when the Marquis retired\par
to his chamber. Conscious that his narrative would take up some hours,\par
and wishing to secure himself from interruption on returning to the\par
Hotel, He ordered his Attendants not to sit up for him. Consequently,\par
He was somewhat surprised on entering his Antiroom, to find Theodore\par
established there. The Page sat near a Table with a pen in his hand,\par
and was so totally occupied by his employment that He perceived not his\par
Lord’s approach. The Marquis stopped to observe him. Theodore wrote a\par
few lines, then paused, and scratched out a part of the writing: Then\par
wrote again, smiled, and seemed highly pleased with what He had been\par
about. At last He threw down his pen, sprang from his chair, and\par
clapped his hands together joyfully.\par
\par
“There it is!” cried He aloud: “Now they are charming!”\par
\par
His transports were interrupted by a laugh from the Marquis, who\par
suspected the nature of his employment.\par
\par
“What is so charming, Theodore?”\par
\par
The Youth started, and looked round. He blushed, ran to the Table,\par
seized the paper on which He had been writing, and concealed it in\par
confusion.\par
\par
“Oh! my Lord, I knew not that you were so near me. Can I be of use to\par
you? Lucas is already gone to bed.”\par
\par
“I shall follow his example when I have given my opinion of your\par
verses.”\par
\par
“My verses, my Lord?”\par
\par
“Nay, I am sure that you have been writing some, for nothing else could\par
have kept you awake till this time of the morning. Where are they,\par
Theodore? I shall like to see your composition.”\par
\par
Theodore’s cheeks glowed with still deeper crimson: He longed to show\par
his poetry, but first chose to be pressed for it.\par
\par
“Indeed, my Lord, they are not worthy your attention.”\par
\par
“Not these verses, which you just now declared to be so charming?\par
\par
Come, come, let me see whether our opinions are the same. I promise\par
that you shall find in me an indulgent Critic.”\par
\par
The Boy produced his paper with seeming reluctance; but the\par
satisfaction which sparkled in his dark expressive eyes betrayed the\par
vanity of his little bosom. The Marquis smiled while He observed the\par
emotions of an heart as yet but little skilled in veiling its\par
sentiments. He seated himself upon a Sopha: Theodore, while Hope and\par
fear contended on his anxious countenance, waited with inquietude for\par
his Master’s decision, while the Marquis read the following lines.\par
\par
LOVE AND AGE\par
\par
\par
    The night was dark; The wind blew cold;\par
    Anacreon, grown morose and old,\par
Sat by his fire, and fed the chearful flame:\par
    Suddenly the Cottage-door expands,\par
    And lo! before him Cupid stands,\par
Casts round a friendly glance, and greets him by his name.\par
\par
    “What is it Thou?” the startled Sire\par
    In sullen tone exclaimed, while ire\par
With crimson flushed his pale and wrinkled cheek:\par
    “Wouldst Thou again with amorous rage\par
    Inflame my bosom? Steeled by age,\par
Vain Boy, to pierce my breast thine arrows are too weak.\par
\par
    “What seek You in this desart drear?\par
    No smiles or sports inhabit here;\par
Ne’er did these vallies witness dalliance sweet:\par
    Eternal winter binds the plains;\par
    Age in my house despotic reigns,\par
My Garden boasts no flower, my bosom boasts no heat.\par
\par
    “Begone, and seek the blooming bower,\par
    Where some ripe Virgin courts thy power,\par
Or bid provoking dreams flit round her bed;\par
    On Damon’s amorous breast repose;\par
    Wanton—on Chloe’s lip of rose,\par
Or make her blushing cheek a pillow for thy head.\par
\par
    “Be such thy haunts; These regions cold\par
    Avoid! Nor think grown wise and old\par
This hoary head again thy yoke shall bear:\par
    Remembering that my fairest years\par
    By Thee were marked with sighs and tears,\par
I think thy friendship false, and shun the guileful snare.\par
\par
    “I have not yet forgot the pains\par
    I felt, while bound in Julia’s chains;\par
The ardent flames with which my bosom burned;\par
    The nights I passed deprived of rest;\par
    The jealous pangs which racked my breast;\par
My disappointed hopes, and passion unreturned.\par
\par
    “Then fly, and curse mine eyes no more!\par
    Fly from my peaceful Cottage-door!\par
No day, no hour, no moment shalt Thou stay.\par
    I know thy falsehood, scorn thy arts,\par
    Distrust thy smiles, and fear thy darts;\par
Traitor, begone, and seek some other to betray!”\par
\par
    “Does Age, old Man, your wits confound?”\par
    Replied the offended God, and frowned;\par
(His frown was sweet as is the Virgin’s smile!)\par
    “Do You to Me these words address?\par
    To Me, who do not love you less,\par
Though You my friendship scorn, and pleasures past revile!\par
\par
    “If one proud Fair you chanced to find,\par
    An hundred other Nymphs were kind,\par
Whose smiles might well for Julia’s frowns atone:\par
    But such is Man! His partial hand\par
    Unnumbered favours writes on sand,\par
But stamps one little fault on solid lasting stone.\par
\par
    “Ingrate! Who led Thee to the wave,\par
    At noon where Lesbia loved to lave?\par
Who named the bower alone where Daphne lay?\par
    And who, when Caelia shrieked for aid,\par
    Bad you with kisses hush the Maid?\par
What other was’t than Love, Oh! false Anacreon, say!\par
\par
    “Then You could call me—‘Gentle Boy!\par
    ‘My only bliss! my source of joy!’—\par
Then You could prize me dearer than your soul!\par
    Could kiss, and dance me on your knees;\par
    And swear, not wine itself would please,\par
Had not the lip of Love first touched the flowing bowl!\par
\par
    “Must those sweet days return no more?\par
    Must I for aye your loss deplore,\par
Banished your heart, and from your favour driven?\par
    Ah! no; My fears that smile denies;\par
    That heaving breast, those sparkling eyes\par
Declare me ever dear and all my faults forgiven.\par
\par
    “Again beloved, esteemed, carest,\par
    Cupid shall in thine arms be prest,\par
Sport on thy knees, or on thy bosom sleep:\par
    My Torch thine age-struck heart shall warm;\par
    My Hand pale Winter’s rage disarm,\par
And Youth and Spring shall here once more their revels keep.”—\par
\par
    A feather now of golden hue\par
    He smiling from his pinion drew;\par
This to the Poet’s hand the Boy commits;\par
    And straight before Anacreon’s eyes\par
    The fairest dreams of fancy rise,\par
And round his favoured head wild inspiration flits.\par
\par
    His bosom glows with amorous fire\par
    Eager He grasps the magic lyre;\par
Swift o’er the tuneful chords his fingers move:\par
    The Feather plucked from Cupid’s wing\par
    Sweeps the too-long-neglected string,\par
While soft Anacreon sings the power and praise of Love.\par
\par
    Soon as that name was heard, the Woods\par
    Shook off their snows; The melting floods\par
Broke their cold chains, and Winter fled away.\par
    Once more the earth was deckt with flowers;\par
    Mild Zephyrs breathed through blooming bowers;\par
High towered the glorious Sun, and poured the blaze of day.\par
\par
    Attracted by the harmonious sound,\par
    Sylvans and Fauns the Cot surround,\par
And curious crowd the Minstrel to behold:\par
    The Wood-nymphs haste the spell to prove;\par
    Eager They run; They list, they love,\par
And while They hear the strain, forget the Man is old.\par
\par
    Cupid, to nothing constant long,\par
    Perched on the Harp attends the song,\par
Or stifles with a kiss the dulcet notes:\par
    Now on the Poet’s breast reposes,\par
    Now twines his hoary locks with roses,\par
Or borne on wings of gold in wanton circle floats.\par
\par
    Then thus Anacreon—“I no more\par
    At other shrine my vows will pour,\par
Since Cupid deigns my numbers to inspire:\par
    From Phœbus or the blue-eyed Maid\par
    Now shall my verse request no aid,\par
For Love alone shall be the Patron of my Lyre.\par
\par
    “In lofty strain, of earlier days,\par
    I spread the King’s or Hero’s praise,\par
And struck the martial Chords with epic fire:\par
    But farewell, Hero! farewell, King!\par
    Your deeds my lips no more shall sing,\par
For Love alone shall be the subject of my Lyre.\par
\par
\par
The Marquis returned the paper with a smile of encouragement.\par
\par
“Your little poem pleases me much,” said He; “However, you must not\par
count my opinion for anything. I am no judge of verses, and for my own\par
part, never composed more than six lines in my life: Those six produced\par
so unlucky an effect that I am fully resolved never to compose another.\par
But I wander from my subject. I was going to say that you cannot employ\par
your time worse than in making verses. An Author, whether good or bad,\par
or between both, is an Animal whom everybody is privileged to attack;\par
For though All are not able to write books, all conceive themselves\par
able to judge them. A bad composition carries with it its own\par
punishment, contempt and ridicule. A good one excites envy, and entails\par
upon its Author a thousand mortifications. He finds himself assailed by\par
partial and ill-humoured Criticism: One Man finds fault with the plan,\par
Another with the style, a Third with the precept, which it strives to\par
inculcate; and they who cannot succeed in finding fault with the Book,\par
employ themselves in stigmatizing its Author. They maliciously rake out\par
from obscurity every little circumstance which may throw ridicule upon\par
his private character or conduct, and aim at wounding the Man, since\par
They cannot hurt the Writer. In short, to enter the lists of literature\par
is wilfully to expose yourself to the arrows of neglect, ridicule,\par
envy, and disappointment. Whether you write well or ill, be assured\par
that you will not escape from blame; Indeed this circumstance contains\par
a young Author’s chief consolation: He remembers that Lope de Vega and\par
Calderona had unjust and envious Critics, and He modestly conceives\par
himself to be exactly in their predicament. But I am conscious that all\par
these sage observations are thrown away upon you. Authorship is a mania\par
to conquer which no reasons are sufficiently strong; and you might as\par
easily persuade me not to love, as I persuade you not to write.\par
However, if you cannot help being occasionally seized with a poetical\par
paroxysm, take at least the precaution of communicating your verses to\par
none but those whose partiality for you secures their approbation.”\par
\par
“Then, my Lord, you do not think these lines tolerable?” said Theodore\par
with an humble and dejected air.\par
\par
“You mistake my meaning. As I said before, they have pleased me much;\par
But my regard for you makes me partial, and Others might judge them\par
less favourably. I must still remark that even my prejudice in your\par
favour does not blind me so much as to prevent my observing several\par
faults. For instance, you make a terrible confusion of metaphors; You\par
are too apt to make the strength of your lines consist more in the\par
words than sense; Some of the verses only seem introduced in order to\par
rhyme with others; and most of the best ideas are borrowed from other\par
Poets, though possibly you are unconscious of the theft yourself. These\par
faults may occasionally be excused in a work of length; But a short\par
Poem must be correct and perfect.”\par
\par
“All this is true, Segnor; But you should consider that I only write\par
for pleasure.”\par
\par
“Your defects are the less excusable. Their incorrectness may be\par
forgiven in those who work for money, who are obliged to compleat a\par
given task in a given time, and are paid according to the bulk, not\par
value of their productions. But in those whom no necessity forces to\par
turn Author, who merely write for fame, and have full leisure to polish\par
their compositions, faults are impardonable, and merit the sharpest\par
arrows of criticism.”\par
\par
The Marquis rose from the Sopha; the Page looked discouraged and\par
melancholy, and this did not escape his Master’s observation.\par
\par
“However” added He smiling, “I think that these lines do you no\par
discredit. Your versification is tolerably easy, and your ear seems to\par
be just. The perusal of your little poem upon the whole gave me much\par
pleasure; and if it is not asking too great a favour, I shall be highly\par
obliged to you for a Copy.”\par
\par
The Youth’s countenance immediately cleared up. He perceived not the\par
smile, half approving, half ironical, which accompanied the request,\par
and He promised the Copy with great readiness. The Marquis withdrew to\par
his chamber, much amused by the instantaneous effect produced upon\par
Theodore’s vanity by the conclusion of his Criticism. He threw himself\par
upon his Couch; Sleep soon stole over him, and his dreams presented him\par
with the most flattering pictures of happiness with Agnes.\par
\par
On reaching the Hotel de Medina, Lorenzo’s first care was to enquire\par
for Letters. He found several waiting for him; but that which He sought\par
was not amongst them. Leonella had found it impossible to write that\par
evening. However, her impatience to secure Don Christoval’s heart, on\par
which She flattered herself with having made no slight impression,\par
permitted her not to pass another day without informing him where She\par
was to be found. On her return from the Capuchin Church, She had\par
related to her Sister with exultation how attentive an handsome\par
Cavalier had been to her; as also how his Companion had undertaken to\par
plead Antonia’s cause with the Marquis de las Cisternas. Elvira\par
received this intelligence with sensations very different from those\par
with which it was communicated. She blamed her Sister’s imprudence in\par
confiding her history to an absolute Stranger, and expressed her fears\par
lest this inconsiderate step should prejudice the Marquis against her.\par
The greatest of her apprehensions She concealed in her own breast. She\par
had observed with inquietude that at the mention of Lorenzo, a deep\par
blush spread itself over her Daughter’s cheek. The timid Antonia dared\par
not to pronounce his name: Without knowing wherefore, She felt\par
embarrassed when He was made the subject of discourse, and endeavoured\par
to change the conversation to Ambrosio. Elvira perceived the emotions\par
of this young bosom: In consequence, She insisted upon Leonella’s\par
breaking her promise to the Cavaliers. A sigh, which on hearing this\par
order escaped from Antonia, confirmed the wary Mother in her\par
resolution.\par
\par
Through this resolution Leonella was determined to break: She conceived\par
it to be inspired by envy, and that her Sister dreaded her being\par
elevated above her. Without imparting her design to anyone, She took an\par
opportunity of dispatching the following note to Lorenzo; It was\par
delivered to him as soon as he woke:\par
\par
“Doubtless, Segnor Don Lorenzo, you have frequently accused me of\par
ingratitude and forgetfulness: But on the word of a Virgin, it was out\par
of my power to perform my promise yesterday. I know not in what words\par
to inform you how strange a reception my Sister gave your kind wish to\par
visit her. She is an odd Woman, with many good points about her; But\par
her jealousy of me frequently makes her conceive notions quite\par
unaccountable. On hearing that your Friend had paid some little\par
attention to me, She immediately took the alarm: She blamed my conduct,\par
and has absolutely forbidden me to let you know our abode. My strong\par
sense of gratitude for your kind offers of service, and ... Shall I\par
confess it? my desire to behold once more the too amiable Don\par
Christoval, will not permit my obeying her injunctions. I have\par
therefore stolen a moment to inform you, that we lodge in the Strada di\par
San Iago, four doors from the Palace d’Albornos, and nearly opposite to\par
the Barber’s Miguel Coello. Enquire for Donna Elvira Dalfa, since in\par
compliance with her Father-in-law’s order, my Sister continues to be\par
called by her maiden name. At eight this evening you will be sure of\par
finding us: But let not a word drop which may raise a suspicion of my\par
having written this letter. Should you see the Condé d’Ossorio, tell\par
him ... I blush while I declare it ... Tell him that his presence will\par
be but too acceptable to the sympathetic\par
\par
\par
LEONELLA.\par
\par
\par
The latter sentences were written in red ink, to express the blushes of\par
her cheek, while She committed an outrage upon her virgin modesty.\par
\par
Lorenzo had no sooner perused this note than He set out in search of\par
Don Christoval. Not being able to find him in the course of the day, He\par
proceeded to Donna Elvira’s alone, to Leonella’s infinite\par
disappointment. The Domestic by whom He sent up his name, having\par
already declared his Lady to be at home, She had no excuse for refusing\par
his visit: Yet She consented to receive it with much reluctance. That\par
reluctance was increased by the changes which his approach produced in\par
Antonia’s countenance; nor was it by any means abated when the Youth\par
himself appeared. The symmetry of his person, animation of his\par
features, and natural elegance of his manners and address, convinced\par
Elvira that such a Guest must be dangerous for her Daughter. She\par
resolved to treat him with distant politeness, to decline his services\par
with gratitude for the tender of them, and to make him feel, without\par
offence, that his future visits would be far from acceptable.\par
\par
On his entrance He found Elvira, who was indisposed, reclining upon a\par
Sopha: Antonia sat by her embroidery frame, and Leonella, in a pastoral\par
dress, held “_Montemayor’s Diana_.” In spite of her being the Mother of\par
Antonia, Lorenzo could not help expecting to find in Elvira Leonella’s\par
true Sister, and the Daughter of “as honest a painstaking Shoe-maker,\par
as any in Cordova.” A single glance was sufficient to undeceive him. He\par
beheld a Woman whose features, though impaired by time and sorrow,\par
still bore the marks of distinguished beauty: A serious dignity reigned\par
upon her countenance, but was tempered by a grace and sweetness which\par
rendered her truly enchanting. Lorenzo fancied that She must have\par
resembled her Daughter in her youth, and readily excused the imprudence\par
of the late Condé de las Cisternas. She desired him to be seated, and\par
immediately resumed her place upon the Sopha.\par
\par
Antonia received him with a simple reverence, and continued her work:\par
Her cheeks were suffused with crimson, and She strove to conceal her\par
emotion by leaning over her embroidery frame. Her Aunt also chose to\par
play off her airs of modesty; She affected to blush and tremble, and\par
waited with her eyes cast down to receive, as She expected, the\par
compliments of Don Christoval. Finding after some time that no sign of\par
his approach was given, She ventured to look round the room, and\par
perceived with vexation that Medina was unaccompanied. Impatience would\par
not permit her waiting for an explanation: Interrupting Lorenzo, who\par
was delivering Raymond’s message, She desired to know what was become\par
of his Friend.\par
\par
He, who thought it necessary to maintain himself in her good graces,\par
strove to console her under her disappointment by committing a little\par
violence upon truth.\par
\par
“Ah! Segnora,” He replied in a melancholy voice “How grieved will He be\par
at losing this opportunity of paying you his respects! A Relation’s\par
illness has obliged him to quit Madrid in haste: But on his return, He\par
will doubtless seize the first moment with transport to throw himself\par
at your feet!”\par
\par
As He said this, his eyes met those of Elvira: She punished his\par
falsehood sufficiently by darting at him a look expressive of\par
displeasure and reproach. Neither did the deceit answer his intention.\par
Vexed and disappointed Leonella rose from her seat, and retired in\par
dudgeon to her own apartment.\par
\par
Lorenzo hastened to repair the fault, which had injured him in Elvira’s\par
opinion. He related his conversation with the Marquis respecting her:\par
He assured her that Raymond was prepared to acknowledge her for his\par
Brother’s Widow; and that till it was in his power to pay his\par
compliments to her in person, Lorenzo was commissioned to supply his\par
place. This intelligence relieved Elvira from an heavy weight of\par
uneasiness: She had now found a Protector for the fatherless Antonia,\par
for whose future fortunes She had suffered the greatest apprehensions.\par
She was not sparing of her thanks to him who had interfered so\par
generously in her behalf; But still She gave him no invitation to\par
repeat his visit.\par
\par
However, when upon rising to depart He requested permission to enquire\par
after her health occasionally, the polite earnestness of his manner,\par
gratitude for his services, and respect for his Friend the Marquis,\par
would not admit of a refusal. She consented reluctantly to receive him:\par
He promised not to abuse her goodness, and quitted the House.\par
\par
Antonia was now left alone with her Mother: A temporary silence ensued.\par
Both wished to speak upon the same subject, but Neither knew how to\par
introduce it. The one felt a bashfulness which sealed up her lips, and\par
for which She could not account: The other feared to find her\par
apprehensions true, or to inspire her Daughter with notions to which\par
She might be still a Stranger. At length Elvira began the conversation.\par
\par
“That is a charming young Man, Antonia; I am much pleased with him. Was\par
He long near you yesterday in the Cathedral?”\par
\par
“He quitted me not for a moment while I staid in the Church: He gave me\par
his seat, and was very obliging and attentive.”\par
\par
“Indeed? Why then have you never mentioned his name to me? Your Aunt\par
lanched out in praise of his Friend, and you vaunted Ambrosio’s\par
eloquence: But Neither said a word of Don Lorenzo’s person and\par
accomplishments. Had not Leonella spoken of his readiness to undertake\par
our cause, I should not have known him to be in existence.”\par
\par
She paused. Antonia coloured, but was silent.\par
\par
“Perhaps you judge him less favourably than I do. In my opinion his\par
figure is pleasing, his conversation sensible, and manners engaging.\par
Still He may have struck you differently: You may think him\par
disagreeable, and ...”.\par
\par
“Disagreeable? Oh! dear Mother, how should I possibly think him so? I\par
should be very ungrateful were I not sensible of his kindness\par
yesterday, and very blind if his merits had escaped me. His figure is\par
so graceful, so noble! His manners so gentle, yet so manly! I never yet\par
saw so many accomplishments united in one person, and I doubt whether\par
Madrid can produce his equal.”\par
\par
“Why then were you so silent in praise of this Phoenix of Madrid?\par
\par
Why was it concealed from me that his society had afforded you\par
pleasure?”\par
\par
“In truth, I know not: You ask me a question which I cannot resolve\par
myself. I was on the point of mentioning him a thousand times: His name\par
was constantly upon my lips, but when I would have pronounced it, I\par
wanted courage to execute my design. However, if I did not speak of\par
him, it was not that I thought of him the less.”\par
\par
“That I believe; But shall I tell you why you wanted courage? It was\par
because, accustomed to confide to me your most secret thoughts, you\par
knew not how to conceal, yet feared to acknowledge, that your heart\par
nourished a sentiment which you were conscious I should disapprove.\par
Come hither to me, my Child.”\par
\par
Antonia quitted her embroidery frame, threw herself upon her knees by\par
the Sopha, and hid her face in her Mother’s lap.\par
\par
“Fear not, my sweet Girl! Consider me equally as your Friend and\par
Parent, and apprehend no reproof from me. I have read the emotions of\par
your bosom; you are yet ill-skilled in concealing them, and they could\par
not escape my attentive eye. This Lorenzo is dangerous to your repose;\par
He has already made an impression upon your heart. ’Tis true that I\par
perceive easily that your affection is returned; But what can be the\par
consequences of this attachment? You are poor and friendless, my\par
Antonia; Lorenzo is the Heir of the Duke of Medina Celi. Even should\par
Himself mean honourably, his Uncle never will consent to your union;\par
Nor without that Uncle’s consent, will I. By sad experience I know what\par
sorrows She must endure, who marries into a family unwilling to receive\par
her. Then struggle with your affection: Whatever pains it may cost you,\par
strive to conquer it. Your heart is tender and susceptible: It has\par
already received a strong impression; But when once convinced that you\par
should not encourage such sentiments, I trust, that you have sufficient\par
fortitude to drive them from your bosom.”\par
\par
Antonia kissed her hand, and promised implicit obedience. Elvira then\par
continued.\par
\par
“To prevent your passion from growing stronger, it will be needful to\par
prohibit Lorenzo’s visits. The service which He has rendered me permits\par
not my forbidding them positively; But unless I judge too favourably of\par
his character, He will discontinue them without taking offence, if I\par
confess to him my reasons, and throw myself entirely on his generosity.\par
The next time that I see him, I will honestly avow to him the\par
embarrassment which his presence occasions. How say you, my Child? Is\par
not this measure necessary?”\par
\par
Antonia subscribed to every thing without hesitation, though not\par
without regret. Her Mother kissed her affectionately, and retired to\par
bed. Antonia followed her example, and vowed so frequently never more\par
to think of Lorenzo, that till Sleep closed her eyes She thought of\par
nothing else.\par
\par
While this was passing at Elvira’s, Lorenzo hastened to rejoin the\par
Marquis. Every thing was ready for the second elopement of Agnes; and\par
at twelve the two Friends with a Coach and four were at the Garden wall\par
of the Convent. Don Raymond drew out his Key, and unlocked the door.\par
They entered, and waited for some time in expectation of being joined\par
by Agnes. At length the Marquis grew impatient: Beginning to fear that\par
his second attempt would succeed no better than the first, He proposed\par
to reconnoitre the Convent. The Friends advanced towards it. Every\par
thing was still and dark. The Prioress was anxious to keep the story a\par
secret, fearing lest the crime of one of its members should bring\par
disgrace upon the whole community, or that the interposition of\par
powerful Relations should deprive her vengeance of its intended victim.\par
She took care therefore to give the Lover of Agnes no cause to suppose\par
that his design was discovered, and his Mistress on the point of\par
suffering the punishment of her fault. The same reason made her reject\par
the idea of arresting the unknown Seducer in the Garden; Such a\par
proceeding would have created much disturbance, and the disgrace of her\par
Convent would have been noised about Madrid. She contented herself with\par
confining Agnes closely; As to the Lover, She left him at liberty to\par
pursue his designs. What She had expected was the result. The Marquis\par
and Lorenzo waited in vain till the break of day: They then retired\par
without noise, alarmed at the failure of their plan, and ignorant of\par
the cause of its ill-success.\par
\par
The next morning Lorenzo went to the Convent, and requested to see his\par
Sister. The Prioress appeared at the Grate with a melancholy\par
countenance: She informed him that for several days Agnes had appeared\par
much agitated; That She had been prest by the Nuns in vain to reveal\par
the cause, and apply to their tenderness for advice and consolation;\par
That She had obstinately persisted in concealing the cause of her\par
distress; But that on Thursday Evening it had produced so violent an\par
effect upon her constitution, that She had fallen ill, and was actually\par
confined to her bed. Lorenzo did not credit a syllable of this account:\par
He insisted upon seeing his Sister; If She was unable to come to the\par
Grate, He desired to be admitted to her Cell. The Prioress crossed\par
herself! She was shocked at the very idea of a Man’s profane eye\par
pervading the interior of her holy Mansion, and professed herself\par
astonished that Lorenzo could think of such a thing. She told him that\par
his request could not be granted; But that if He returned the next day,\par
She hoped that her beloved Daughter would then be sufficiently\par
recovered to join him at the Parlour grate.\par
\par
With this answer Lorenzo was obliged to retire, unsatisfied and\par
trembling for his Sister’s safety.\par
\par
He returned the next morning at an early hour. “Agnes was worse; The\par
Physician had pronounced her to be in imminent danger; She was ordered\par
to remain quiet, and it was utterly impossible for her to receive her\par
Brother’s visit.” Lorenzo stormed at this answer, but there was no\par
resource. He raved, He entreated, He threatened: No means were left\par
untried to obtain a sight of Agnes. His endeavours were as fruitless as\par
those of the day before, and He returned in despair to the Marquis. On\par
his side, the Latter had spared no pains to discover what had\par
occasioned his plot to fail: Don Christoval, to whom the affair was now\par
entrusted, endeavoured to worm out the secret from the Old Porteress of\par
St. Clare, with whom He had formed an acquaintance; But She was too\par
much upon her guard, and He gained from her no intelligence. The\par
Marquis was almost distracted, and Lorenzo felt scarcely less\par
inquietude. Both were convinced that the purposed elopement must have\par
been discovered: They doubted not but the malady of Agnes was a\par
pretence, But they knew not by what means to rescue her from the hands\par
of the Prioress.\par
\par
Regularly every day did Lorenzo visit the Convent: As regularly was He\par
informed that his Sister rather grew worse than better. Certain that\par
her indisposition was feigned, these accounts did not alarm him: But\par
his ignorance of her fate, and of the motives which induced the\par
Prioress to keep her from him, excited the most serious uneasiness. He\par
was still uncertain what steps He ought to take, when the Marquis\par
received a letter from the Cardinal-Duke of Lerma. It inclosed the\par
Pope’s expected Bull, ordering that Agnes should be released from her\par
vows, and restored to her Relations. This essential paper decided at\par
once the proceedings of her Friends: They resolved that Lorenzo should\par
carry it to the Domina without delay, and demand that his Sister should\par
be instantly given up to him. Against this mandate illness could not be\par
pleaded: It gave her Brother the power of removing her instantly to the\par
Palace de Medina, and He determined to use that power on the following\par
day.\par
\par
His mind relieved from inquietude respecting his Sister, and his\par
Spirits raised by the hope of soon restoring her to freedom, He now had\par
time to give a few moments to love and to Antonia. At the same hour as\par
on his former visit He repaired to Donna Elvira’s: She had given orders\par
for his admission. As soon as He was announced, her Daughter retired\par
with Leonella, and when He entered the chamber, He found the Lady of\par
the House alone. She received him with less distance than before, and\par
desired him to place himself near her upon the Sopha. She then without\par
losing time opened her business, as had been agreed between herself and\par
Antonia.\par
\par
“You must not think me ungrateful, Don Lorenzo, or forgetful how\par
essential are the services which you have rendered me with the Marquis.\par
I feel the weight of my obligations; Nothing under the Sun should\par
induce my taking the step to which I am now compelled but the interest\par
of my Child, of my beloved Antonia. My health is declining; God only\par
knows how soon I may be summoned before his Throne. My Daughter will be\par
left without Parents, and should She lose the protection of the\par
Cisternas family, without Friends.\par
\par
She is young and artless, uninstructed in the world’s perfidy, and with\par
charms sufficient to render her an object of seduction. Judge then, how\par
I must tremble at the prospect before her! Judge how anxious I must be\par
to keep her from their society who may excite the yet dormant passions\par
of her bosom. You are amiable, Don Lorenzo: Antonia has a susceptible,\par
a loving heart, and is grateful for the favours conferred upon us by\par
your interference with the Marquis. Your presence makes me tremble: I\par
fear lest it should inspire her with sentiments which may embitter the\par
remainder of her life, or encourage her to cherish hopes in her\par
situation unjustifiable and futile. Pardon me when I avow my terrors,\par
and let my frankness plead in my excuse. I cannot forbid you my House,\par
for gratitude restrains me; I can only throw myself upon your\par
generosity, and entreat you to spare the feelings of an anxious, of a\par
doting Mother. Believe me when I assure you that I lament the necessity\par
of rejecting your acquaintance; But there is no remedy, and Antonia’s\par
interest obliges me to beg you to forbear your visits. By complying\par
with my request, you will increase the esteem which I already feel for\par
you, and of which everything convinces me that you are truly\par
deserving.”\par
\par
“Your frankness charms me,” replied Lorenzo; “You shall find that in\par
your favourable opinion of me you were not deceived. Yet I hope that\par
the reasons, now in my power to allege, will persuade you to withdraw a\par
request which I cannot obey without infinite reluctance. I love your\par
Daughter, love her most sincerely: I wish for no greater happiness than\par
to inspire her with the same sentiments, and receive her hand at the\par
Altar as her Husband. ’Tis true, I am not rich myself; My Father’s\par
death has left me but little in my own possession; But my expectations\par
justify my pretending to the Condé de las Cisternas’ Daughter.”\par
\par
He was proceeding, but Elvira interrupted him.\par
\par
“Ah! Don Lorenzo, you forget in that pompous title the meanness of my\par
origin. You forget that I have now past fourteen years in Spain,\par
disavowed by my Husband’s family, and existing upon a stipend barely\par
sufficient for the support and education of my Daughter. Nay, I have\par
even been neglected by most of my own Relations, who out of envy affect\par
to doubt the reality of my marriage. My allowance being discontinued at\par
my Father-in-law’s death, I was reduced to the very brink of want. In\par
this situation I was found by my Sister, who amongst all her foibles\par
possesses a warm, generous, and affectionate heart. She aided me with\par
the little fortune which my Father left her, persuaded me to visit\par
Madrid, and has supported my Child and myself since our quitting\par
Murcia. Then consider not Antonia as descended from the Condé de la\par
Cisternas: Consider her as a poor and unprotected Orphan, as the\par
Grand-child of the Tradesman Torribio Dalfa, as the needy Pensioner of\par
that Tradesman’s Daughter. Reflect upon the difference between such a\par
situation, and that of the Nephew and Heir of the potent Duke of\par
Medina. I believe your intentions to be honourable; But as there are no\par
hopes that your Uncle will approve of the union, I foresee that the\par
consequences of your attachment must be fatal to my Child’s repose.”\par
\par
“Pardon me, Segnora; You are misinformed if you suppose the Duke of\par
Medina to resemble the generality of Men. His sentiments are liberal\par
and disinterested: He loves me well; and I have no reason to dread his\par
forbidding the marriage when He perceives that my happiness depends\par
upon Antonia. But supposing him to refuse his sanction, what have I\par
still to fear? My Parents are no more; My little fortune is in my own\par
possession: It will be sufficient to support Antonia, and I shall\par
exchange for her hand Medina’s Dukedom without one sigh of regret.”\par
\par
“You are young and eager; It is natural for you to entertain such\par
ideas. But Experience has taught me to my cost that curses accompany an\par
unequal alliance. I married the Condé de las Cisternas in opposition to\par
the will of his Relations; Many an heart-pang has punished me for the\par
imprudent step. Whereever we bent our course, a Father’s execration\par
pursued Gonzalvo. Poverty overtook us, and no Friend was near to\par
relieve our wants. Still our mutual affection existed, but alas! not\par
without interruption.\par
\par
Accustomed to wealth and ease, ill could my Husband support the\par
transition to distress and indigence. He looked back with repining to\par
the comforts which He once enjoyed. He regretted the situation which\par
for my sake He had quitted; and in moments when Despair possessed his\par
mind, has reproached me with having made him the Companion of want and\par
wretchedness! He has called me his bane! The source of his sorrows, the\par
cause of his destruction! Ah God! He little knew how much keener were\par
my own heart’s reproaches! He was ignorant that I suffered trebly, for\par
myself, for my Children, and for him! ’Tis true that his anger seldom\par
lasted long: His sincere affection for me soon revived in his heart;\par
and then his repentance for the tears which He had made me shed\par
tortured me even more than his reproaches. He would throw himself on\par
the ground, implore my forgiveness in the most frantic terms, and load\par
himself with curses for being the Murderer of my repose. Taught by\par
experience that an union contracted against the inclinations of\par
families on either side must be unfortunate, I will save my Daughter\par
from those miseries which I have suffered. Without your Uncle’s\par
consent, while I live, She never shall be yours. Undoubtedly He will\par
disapprove of the union; His power is immense, and Antonia shall not be\par
exposed to his anger and persecution.”\par
\par
“His persecution? How easily may that be avoided! Let the worst happen,\par
it is but quitting Spain. My wealth may easily be realised; The Indian\par
Islands will offer us a secure retreat; I have an estate, though not of\par
value, in Hispaniola: Thither will we fly, and I shall consider it to\par
be my native Country, if it gives me Antonia’s undisturbed possession.”\par
\par
“Ah! Youth, this is a fond romantic vision. Gonzalvo thought the same.\par
He fancied that He could leave Spain without regret; But the moment of\par
parting undeceived him. You know not yet what it is to quit your native\par
land; to quit it, never to behold it more!\par
\par
You know not, what it is to exchange the scenes where you have passed\par
your infancy, for unknown realms and barbarous climates! To be\par
forgotten, utterly eternally forgotten, by the Companions of your\par
Youth! To see your dearest Friends, the fondest objects of your\par
affection, perishing with diseases incidental to Indian atmospheres,\par
and find yourself unable to procure for them necessary assistance! I\par
have felt all this! My Husband and two sweet Babes found their Graves\par
in Cuba: Nothing would have saved my young Antonia but my sudden return\par
to Spain. Ah! Don Lorenzo, could you conceive what I suffered during my\par
absence! Could you know how sorely I regretted all that I left behind,\par
and how dear to me was the very name of Spain! I envied the winds which\par
blew towards it: And when the Spanish Sailor chaunted some well-known\par
air as He past my window, tears filled my eyes while I thought upon my\par
native land. Gonzalvo too ... My Husband ...”.\par
\par
Elvira paused. Her voice faltered, and She concealed her face with her\par
handkerchief. After a short silence She rose from the Sopha, and\par
proceeded.\par
\par
“Excuse my quitting you for a few moments: The remembrance of what I\par
have suffered has much agitated me, and I need to be alone. Till I\par
return peruse these lines. After my Husband’s death I found them among\par
his papers; Had I known sooner that He entertained such sentiments,\par
Grief would have killed me. He wrote these verses on his voyage to\par
Cuba, when his mind was clouded by sorrow, and He forgot that He had a\par
Wife and Children.\par
\par
What we are losing, ever seems to us the most precious: Gonzalvo was\par
quitting Spain for ever, and therefore was Spain dearer to his eyes\par
than all else which the World contained. Read them, Don Lorenzo; They\par
will give you some idea of the feelings of a banished Man!”\par
\par
Elvira put a paper into Lorenzo’s hand, and retired from the chamber.\par
The Youth examined the contents, and found them to be as follows.\par
\par
THE EXILE\par
\par
\par
Farewell, Oh! native Spain! Farewell for ever!\par
    These banished eyes shall view thy coasts no more;\par
A mournful presage tells my heart, that never\par
    Gonzalvo’s steps again shall press thy shore.\par
\par
Hushed are the winds; While soft the Vessel sailing\par
    With gentle motion plows the unruffled Main,\par
I feel my bosom’s boasted courage failing,\par
    And curse the waves which bear me far from Spain.\par
\par
I see it yet! Beneath yon blue clear Heaven\par
    Still do the Spires, so well beloved, appear;\par
From yonder craggy point the gale of Even\par
    Still wafts my native accents to mine ear:\par
\par
Propped on some moss-crowned Rock, and gaily singing,\par
    There in the Sun his nets the Fisher dries;\par
Oft have I heard the plaintive Ballad, bringing\par
    Scenes of past joys before my sorrowing eyes.\par
\par
Ah! Happy Swain! He waits the accustomed hour,\par
    When twilight-gloom obscures the closing sky;\par
Then gladly seeks his loved paternal bower,\par
    And shares the feast his native fields supply:\par
\par
Friendship and Love, his Cottage Guests, receive him\par
    With honest welcome and with smile sincere;\par
No threatening woes of present joys bereave him,\par
    No sigh his bosom owns, his cheek no tear.\par
\par
Ah! Happy Swain! Such bliss to me denying,\par
    Fortune thy lot with envy bids me view;\par
Me, who from home and Spain an Exile flying,\par
    Bid all I value, all I love, adieu.\par
\par
No more mine ear shall list the well-known ditty\par
    Sung by some Mountain-Girl, who tends her Goats,\par
Some Village-Swain imploring amorous pity,\par
    Or Shepherd chaunting wild his rustic notes:\par
\par
No more my arms a Parent’s fond embraces,\par
    No more my heart domestic calm, must know;\par
Far from these joys, with sighs which Memory traces,\par
    To sultry skies, and distant climes I go.\par
\par
Where Indian Suns engender new diseases,\par
    Where snakes and tigers breed, I bend my way\par
To brave the feverish thirst no art appeases,\par
    The yellow plague, and madding blaze of day:\par
\par
But not to feel slow pangs consume my liver,\par
    To die by piece-meal in the bloom of age,\par
My boiling blood drank by insatiate fever,\par
    And brain delirious with the day-star’s rage,\par
\par
Can make me know such grief, as thus to sever\par
    With many a bitter sigh, Dear Land, from Thee;\par
To feel this heart must doat on thee for ever,\par
    And feel, that all thy joys are torn from me!\par
\par
Ah me! How oft will Fancy’s spells in slumber\par
    Recall my native Country to my mind!\par
How oft regret will bid me sadly number\par
    Each lost delight and dear Friend left behind!\par
\par
Wild Murcia’s Vales, and loved romantic bowers,\par
    The River on whose banks a Child I played,\par
My Castle’s antient Halls, its frowning Towers,\par
    Each much-regretted wood, and well-known Glade,\par
\par
Dreams of the land where all my wishes centre,\par
    Thy scenes, which I am doomed no more to know,\par
Full oft shall Memory trace, my soul’s Tormentor,\par
    And turn each pleasure past to present woe.\par
\par
But Lo! The Sun beneath the waves retires;\par
    Night speeds apace her empire to restore:\par
Clouds from my sight obscure the village-spires,\par
    Now seen but faintly, and now seen no more.\par
\par
Oh! breathe not, Winds! Still be the Water’s motion!\par
    Sleep, sleep, my Bark, in silence on the Main!\par
So when to-morrow’s light shall gild the Ocean,\par
    Once more mine eyes shall see the coast of Spain.\par
\par
Vain is the wish! My last petition scorning,\par
    Fresh blows the Gale, and high the Billows swell:\par
Far shall we be before the break of Morning;\par
    Oh! then for ever, native Spain, farewell!\par
\par
\par
Lorenzo had scarcely time to read these lines, when Elvira returned to\par
him: The giving a free course to her tears had relieved her, and her\par
spirits had regained their usual composure.\par
\par
“I have nothing more to say, my Lord,” said She; “You have heard my\par
apprehensions, and my reasons for begging you not to repeat your\par
visits. I have thrown myself in full confidence upon your honour: I am\par
certain that you will not prove my opinion of you to have been too\par
favourable.”\par
\par
“But one question more, Segnora, and I leave you. Should the Duke of\par
Medina approve my love, would my addresses be unacceptable to yourself\par
and the fair Antonia?”\par
\par
“I will be open with you, Don Lorenzo: There being little probability\par
of such an union taking place, I fear that it is desired but too\par
ardently by my Daughter. You have made an impression upon her young\par
heart, which gives me the most serious alarm: To prevent that\par
impression from growing stronger, I am obliged to decline your\par
acquaintance. For me, you may be sure that I should rejoice at\par
establishing my Child so advantageously. Conscious that my\par
constitution, impaired by grief and illness, forbids me to expect a\par
long continuance in this world, I tremble at the thought of leaving her\par
under the protection of a perfect Stranger. The Marquis de las\par
Cisternas is totally unknown to me:\par
\par
He will marry; His Lady may look upon Antonia with an eye of\par
displeasure, and deprive her of her only Friend. Should the Duke, your\par
Uncle, give his consent, you need not doubt obtaining mine, and my\par
Daughter’s: But without his, hope not for ours. At all events, what\par
ever steps you may take, what ever may be the Duke’s decision, till you\par
know it let me beg your forbearing to strengthen by your presence\par
Antonia’s prepossession. If the sanction of your Relations authorises\par
your addressing her as your Wife, my Doors fly open to you: If that\par
sanction is refused, be satisfied to possess my esteem and gratitude,\par
but remember, that we must meet no more.”\par
\par
Lorenzo promised reluctantly to conform to this decree: But He added\par
that He hoped soon to obtain that consent which would give him a claim\par
to the renewal of their acquaintance. He then explained to her why the\par
Marquis had not called in person, and made no scruple of confiding to\par
her his Sister’s History. He concluded by saying that He hoped to set\par
Agnes at liberty the next day; and that as soon as Don Raymond’s fears\par
were quieted upon this subject, He would lose no time in assuring Donna\par
Elvira of his friendship and protection.\par
\par
The Lady shook her head.\par
\par
“I tremble for your Sister,” said She; “I have heard many traits of the\par
Domina of St. Clare’s character, from a Friend who was educated in the\par
same Convent with her. She reported her to be haughty, inflexible,\par
superstitious, and revengeful. I have since heard that She is\par
infatuated with the idea of rendering her Convent the most regular in\par
Madrid, and never forgave those whose imprudence threw upon it the\par
slightest stain. Though naturally violent and severe, when her\par
interests require it, She well knows how to assume an appearance of\par
benignity. She leaves no means untried to persuade young Women of rank\par
to become Members of her Community: She is implacable when once\par
incensed, and has too much intrepidity to shrink at taking the most\par
rigorous measures for punishing the Offender. Doubtless, She will\par
consider your Sister’s quitting the Convent as a disgrace thrown upon\par
it: She will use every artifice to avoid obeying the mandate of his\par
Holiness, and I shudder to think that Donna Agnes is in the hands of\par
this dangerous Woman.”\par
\par
Lorenzo now rose to take leave. Elvira gave him her hand at parting,\par
which He kissed respectfully; and telling her that He soon hoped for\par
the permission to salute that of Antonia, He returned to his Hotel. The\par
Lady was perfectly satisfied with the conversation which had past\par
between them. She looked forward with satisfaction to the prospect of\par
his becoming her Son-in-law; But Prudence bad her conceal from her\par
Daughter’s knowledge the flattering hopes which Herself now ventured to\par
entertain.\par
\par
Scarcely was it day, and already Lorenzo was at the Convent of St.\par
Clare, furnished with the necessary mandate. The Nuns were at Matins.\par
He waited impatiently for the conclusion of the service, and at length\par
the Prioress appeared at the Parlour Grate. Agnes was demanded. The old\par
Lady replied, with a melancholy air, that the dear Child’s situation\par
grew hourly more dangerous; That the Physicians despaired of her life;\par
But that they had declared the only chance for her recovery to consist\par
in keeping her quiet, and not to permit those to approach her whose\par
presence was likely to agitate her. Not a word of all this was believed\par
by Lorenzo, any more than He credited the expressions of grief and\par
affection for Agnes, with which this account was interlarded. To end\par
the business, He put the Pope’s Bull into the hands of the Domina, and\par
insisted that, ill or in health, his Sister should be delivered to him\par
without delay.\par
\par
The Prioress received the paper with an air of humility: But no sooner\par
had her eye glanced over the contents, than her resentment baffled all\par
the efforts of Hypocrisy. A deep crimson spread itself over her face,\par
and She darted upon Lorenzo looks of rage and menace.\par
\par
“This order is positive,” said She in a voice of anger, which She in\par
vain strove to disguise; “Willingly would I obey it; But unfortunately\par
it is out of my power.”\par
\par
Lorenzo interrupted her by an exclamation of surprize.\par
\par
“I repeat it, Segnor; to obey this order is totally out of my power.\par
From tenderness to a Brother’s feelings, I would have communicated the\par
sad event to you by degrees, and have prepared you to hear it with\par
fortitude. My measures are broken through: This order commands me to\par
deliver up to you the Sister Agnes without delay; I am therefore\par
obliged to inform you without circumlocution, that on Friday last, She\par
expired.”\par
\par
Lorenzo started back with horror, and turned pale. A moment’s\par
recollection convinced him that this assertion must be false, and it\par
restored him to himself.\par
\par
“You deceive me!” said He passionately; “But five minutes past since\par
you assured me that though ill She was still alive. Produce her this\par
instant! See her I must and will, and every attempt to keep her from me\par
will be unavailing.”\par
\par
“You forget yourself, Segnor; You owe respect to my age as well as my\par
profession. Your Sister is no more. If I at first concealed her death,\par
it was from dreading lest an event so unexpected should produce on you\par
too violent an effect. In truth, I am but ill repaid for my attention.\par
And what interest, I pray you, should I have in detaining her? To know\par
her wish of quitting our society is a sufficient reason for me to wish\par
her absence, and think her a disgrace to the Sisterhood of St. Clare:\par
But She has forfeited my affection in a manner yet more culpable. Her\par
crimes were great, and when you know the cause of her death, you will\par
doubtless rejoice, Don Lorenzo, that such a Wretch is no longer in\par
existence. She was taken ill on Thursday last on returning from\par
confession in the Capuchin Chapel. Her malady seemed attended with\par
strange circumstances; But She persisted in concealing its cause:\par
Thanks to the Virgin, we were too ignorant to suspect it! Judge then\par
what must have been our consternation, our horror, when She was\par
delivered the next day of a stillborn Child, whom She immediately\par
followed to the Grave. How, Segnor? Is it possible that your\par
countenance expresses no surprize, no indignation? Is it possible that\par
your Sister’s infamy was known to you, and that still She possessed\par
your affection? In that case, you have no need of my compassion. I can\par
say nothing more, except repeat my inability of obeying the orders of\par
his Holiness. Agnes is no more, and to convince you that what I say is\par
true, I swear by our blessed Saviour, that three days have past since\par
She was buried.”\par
\par
Here She kissed a small crucifix which hung at her girdle. She then\par
rose from her chair, and quitted the Parlour. As She withdrew, She cast\par
upon Lorenzo a scornful smile.\par
\par
“Farewell, Segnor,” said She; “I know no remedy for this accident: I\par
fear that even a second Bull from the Pope will not procure your\par
Sister’s resurrection.”\par
\par
Lorenzo also retired, penetrated with affliction: But Don Raymond’s at\par
the news of this event amounted to Madness. He would not be convinced\par
that Agnes was really dead, and continued to insist that the Walls of\par
St. Clare still confined her. No arguments could make him abandon his\par
hopes of regaining her: Every day some fresh scheme was invented for\par
procuring intelligence of her, and all of them were attended with the\par
same success.\par
\par
On his part, Medina gave up the idea of ever seeing his Sister more:\par
Yet He believed that She had been taken off by unfair means. Under this\par
persuasion, He encouraged Don Raymond’s researches, determined, should\par
He discover the least warrant for his suspicions, to take a severe\par
vengeance upon the unfeeling Prioress. The loss of his Sister affected\par
him sincerely; Nor was it the least cause of his distress that\par
propriety obliged him for some time to defer mentioning Antonia to the\par
Duke. In the meanwhile his emissaries constantly surrounded Elvira’s\par
Door. He had intelligence of all the movements of his Mistress: As She\par
never failed every Thursday to attend the Sermon in the Capuchin\par
Cathedral, He was secure of seeing her once a week, though in\par
compliance with his promise, He carefully shunned her observation. Thus\par
two long Months passed away. Still no information was procured of\par
Agnes: All but the Marquis credited her death; and now Lorenzo\par
determined to disclose his sentiments to his Uncle. He had already\par
dropt some hints of his intention to marry; They had been as favourably\par
received as He could expect, and He harboured no doubt of the success\par
of his application.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER VI.\par
\par
\par
While in each other’s arms entranced They lay,\par
They blessed the night, and curst the coming day.\par
\par
LEE.\par
\par
The burst of transport was past: Ambrosio’s lust was satisfied;\par
Pleasure fled, and Shame usurped her seat in his bosom. Confused and\par
terrified at his weakness, He drew himself from Matilda’s arms. His\par
perjury presented itself before him: He reflected on the scene which\par
had just been acted, and trembled at the consequences of a discovery.\par
He looked forward with horror; His heart was despondent, and became the\par
abode of satiety and disgust. He avoided the eyes of his Partner in\par
frailty; A melancholy silence prevailed, during which Both seemed\par
busied with disagreeable reflections.\par
\par
Matilda was the first to break it. She took his hand gently, and\par
pressed it to her burning lips.\par
\par
“Ambrosio!” She murmured in a soft and trembling voice.\par
\par
The Abbot started at the sound. He turned his eyes upon Matilda’s: They\par
were filled with tears; Her cheeks were covered with blushes, and her\par
supplicating looks seemed to solicit his compassion.\par
\par
“Dangerous Woman!” said He; “Into what an abyss of misery have you\par
plunged me! Should your sex be discovered, my honour, nay my life, must\par
pay for the pleasure of a few moments. Fool that I was, to trust myself\par
to your seductions! What can now be done? How can my offence be\par
expiated? What atonement can purchase the pardon of my crime? Wretched\par
Matilda, you have destroyed my quiet for ever!”\par
\par
“To me these reproaches, Ambrosio? To me, who have sacrificed for you\par
the world’s pleasures, the luxury of wealth, the delicacy of sex, my\par
Friends, my fortune, and my fame? What have you lost, which I\par
preserved? Have _I_ not shared in _your_ guilt? Have _you_ not shared\par
in _my_ pleasure? Guilt, did I say? In what consists ours, unless in\par
the opinion of an ill-judging World? Let that World be ignorant of\par
them, and our joys become divine and blameless! Unnatural were your\par
vows of Celibacy; Man was not created for such a state; And were Love a\par
crime, God never would have made it so sweet, so irresistible! Then\par
banish those clouds from your brow, my Ambrosio! Indulge in those\par
pleasures freely, without which life is a worthless gift: Cease to\par
reproach me with having taught you what is bliss, and feel equal\par
transports with the Woman who adores you!”\par
\par
As She spoke, her eyes were filled with a delicious languor. Her bosom\par
panted: She twined her arms voluptuously round him, drew him towards\par
her, and glewed her lips to his. Ambrosio again raged with desire: The\par
die was thrown: His vows were already broken; He had already committed\par
the crime, and why should He refrain from enjoying its reward? He\par
clasped her to his breast with redoubled ardour. No longer repressed by\par
the sense of shame, He gave a loose to his intemperate appetites. While\par
the fair Wanton put every invention of lust in practice, every\par
refinement in the art of pleasure which might heighten the bliss of her\par
possession, and render her Lover’s transports still more exquisite,\par
Ambrosio rioted in delights till then unknown to him: Swift fled the\par
night, and the Morning blushed to behold him still clasped in the\par
embraces of Matilda.\par
\par
Intoxicated with pleasure, the Monk rose from the Syren’s luxurious\par
Couch. He no longer reflected with shame upon his incontinence, or\par
dreaded the vengeance of offended heaven. His only fear was lest Death\par
should rob him of enjoyments, for which his long Fast had only given a\par
keener edge to his appetite. Matilda was still under the influence of\par
poison, and the voluptuous Monk trembled less for his Preserver’s life\par
than his Concubine’s. Deprived of her, He would not easily find another\par
Mistress with whom He could indulge his passions so fully, and so\par
safely. He therefore pressed her with earnestness to use the means of\par
preservation which She had declared to be in her possession.\par
\par
“Yes!” replied Matilda; “Since you have made me feel that Life is\par
valuable, I will rescue mine at any rate. No dangers shall appall me: I\par
will look upon the consequences of my action boldly, nor shudder at the\par
horrors which they present. I will think my sacrifice scarcely worthy\par
to purchase your possession, and remember that a moment past in your\par
arms in this world o’er-pays an age of punishment in the next. But\par
before I take this step, Ambrosio, give me your solemn oath never to\par
enquire by what means I shall preserve myself.”\par
\par
He did so in a manner the most binding.\par
\par
“I thank you, my Beloved. This precaution is necessary, for though you\par
know it not, you are under the command of vulgar prejudices: The\par
Business on which I must be employed this night, might startle you from\par
its singularity, and lower me in your opinion. Tell me; Are you\par
possessed of the Key of the low door on the western side of the\par
Garden?”\par
\par
“The Door which opens into the burying-ground common to us and the\par
Sisterhood of St. Clare? I have not the Key, but can easily procure\par
it.”\par
\par
“You have only this to do. Admit me into the burying-ground at\par
midnight; Watch while I descend into the vaults of St. Clare, lest some\par
prying eye should observe my actions; Leave me there alone for an hour,\par
and that life is safe which I dedicate to your pleasures. To prevent\par
creating suspicion, do not visit me during the day. Remember the Key,\par
and that I expect you before twelve. Hark! I hear steps approaching!\par
Leave me; I will pretend to sleep.”\par
\par
The Friar obeyed, and left the Cell. As He opened the door, Father\par
Pablos made his appearance.\par
\par
“I come,” said the Latter, “to enquire after the health of my young\par
Patient.”\par
\par
“Hush!” replied Ambrosio, laying his finger upon his lip; “Speak\par
softly; I am just come from him. He has fallen into a profound slumber,\par
which doubtless will be of service to him. Do not disturb him at\par
present, for He wishes to repose.”\par
\par
Father Pablos obeyed, and hearing the Bell ring, accompanied the Abbot\par
to Matins. Ambrosio felt embarrassed as He entered the Chapel. Guilt\par
was new to him, and He fancied that every eye could read the\par
transactions of the night upon his countenance. He strove to pray; His\par
bosom no longer glowed with devotion; His thoughts insensibly wandered\par
to Matilda’s secret charms. But what He wanted in purity of heart, He\par
supplied by exterior sanctity. The better to cloak his transgression,\par
He redoubled his pretensions to the semblance of virtue, and never\par
appeared more devoted to Heaven as since He had broken through his\par
engagements. Thus did He unconsciously add Hypocrisy to perjury and\par
incontinence; He had fallen into the latter errors from yielding to\par
seduction almost irresistible; But he was now guilty of a voluntary\par
fault by endeavouring to conceal those into which Another had betrayed\par
him.\par
\par
The Matins concluded, Ambrosio retired to his Cell. The pleasures which\par
He had just tasted for the first time were still impressed upon his\par
mind. His brain was bewildered, and presented a confused Chaos of\par
remorse, voluptuousness, inquietude, and fear. He looked back with\par
regret to that peace of soul, that security of virtue, which till then\par
had been his portion. He had indulged in excesses whose very idea but\par
four and twenty hours before He had recoiled at with horror. He\par
shuddered at reflecting that a trifling indiscretion on his part, or on\par
Matilda’s, would overturn that fabric of reputation which it had cost\par
him thirty years to erect, and render him the abhorrence of that People\par
of whom He was then the Idol. Conscience painted to him in glaring\par
colours his perjury and weakness; Apprehension magnified to him the\par
horrors of punishment, and He already fancied himself in the prisons of\par
the Inquisition. To these tormenting ideas succeeded Matilda’s beauty,\par
and those delicious lessons which, once learnt, can never be forgotten.\par
A single glance thrown upon these reconciled him with himself. He\par
considered the pleasures of the former night to have been purchased at\par
an easy price by the sacrifice of innocence and honour. Their very\par
remembrance filled his soul with ecstacy; He cursed his foolish vanity,\par
which had induced him to waste in obscurity the bloom of life, ignorant\par
of the blessings of Love and Woman. He determined at all events to\par
continue his commerce with Matilda, and called every argument to his\par
aid which might confirm his resolution. He asked himself, provided his\par
irregularity was unknown, in what would his fault consist, and what\par
consequences He had to apprehend? By adhering strictly to every rule of\par
his order save Chastity, He doubted not to retain the esteem of Men,\par
and even the protection of heaven. He trusted easily to be forgiven so\par
slight and natural a deviation from his vows: But He forgot that having\par
pronounced those vows, Incontinence, in Laymen the most venial of\par
errors, became in his person the most heinous of crimes.\par
\par
Once decided upon his future conduct, his mind became more easy. He\par
threw himself upon his bed, and strove by sleeping to recruit his\par
strength exhausted by his nocturnal excesses. He awoke refreshed, and\par
eager for a repetition of his pleasures. Obedient to Matilda’s order,\par
He visited not her Cell during the day. Father Pablos mentioned in the\par
Refectory that Rosario had at length been prevailed upon to follow his\par
prescription; But that the medicine had not produced the slightest\par
effect, and that He believed no mortal skill could rescue him from the\par
Grave. With this opinion the Abbot agreed, and affected to lament the\par
untimely fate of a Youth, whose talents had appeared so promising.\par
\par
The night arrived. Ambrosio had taken care to procure from the Porter\par
the Key of the low door opening into the Cemetery. Furnished with this,\par
when all was silent in the Monastery, He quitted his Cell, and hastened\par
to Matilda’s. She had left her bed, and was drest before his arrival.\par
\par
“I have been expecting you with impatience,” said She; “My life depends\par
upon these moments. Have you the Key?”\par
\par
“I have.”\par
\par
“Away then to the garden. We have no time to lose. Follow me!”\par
\par
She took a small covered Basket from the Table. Bearing this in one\par
hand, and the Lamp, which was flaming upon the Hearth, in the other,\par
She hastened from the Cell. Ambrosio followed her. Both maintained a\par
profound silence. She moved on with quick but cautious steps, passed\par
through the Cloisters, and reached the Western side of the Garden. Her\par
eyes flashed with a fire and wildness which impressed the Monk at once\par
with awe and horror. A determined desperate courage reigned upon her\par
brow. She gave the Lamp to Ambrosio; Then taking from him the Key, She\par
unlocked the low Door, and entered the Cemetery. It was a vast and\par
spacious Square planted with yew trees: Half of it belonged to the\par
Abbey; The other half was the property of the Sisterhood of St. Clare,\par
and was protected by a roof of Stone. The Division was marked by an\par
iron railing, the wicket of which was generally left unlocked.\par
\par
Thither Matilda bent her course. She opened the wicket and sought for\par
the door leading to the subterraneous Vaults, where reposed the\par
mouldering Bodies of the Votaries of St. Clare. The night was perfectly\par
dark; Neither Moon or Stars were visible. Luckily there was not a\par
breath of Wind, and the Friar bore his Lamp in full security: By the\par
assistance of its beams, the door of the Sepulchre was soon discovered.\par
It was sunk within the hollow of a wall, and almost concealed by thick\par
festoons of ivy hanging over it. Three steps of rough-hewn Stone\par
conducted to it, and Matilda was on the point of descending them when\par
She suddenly started back.\par
\par
“There are People in the Vaults!” She whispered to the Monk; “Conceal\par
yourself till they are past.\par
\par
She took refuge behind a lofty and magnificent Tomb, erected in honour\par
of the Convent’s Foundress. Ambrosio followed her example, carefully\par
hiding his Lamp lest its beams should betray them. But a few moments\par
had elapsed when the Door was pushed open leading to the subterraneous\par
Caverns. Rays of light proceeded up the Staircase: They enabled the\par
concealed Spectators to observe two Females drest in religious habits,\par
who seemed engaged in earnest conversation. The Abbot had no difficulty\par
to recognize the Prioress of St. Clare in the first, and one of the\par
elder Nuns in her Companion.\par
\par
“Every thing is prepared,” said the Prioress; “Her fate shall be\par
decided tomorrow. All her tears and sighs will be unavailing. No! In\par
five and twenty years that I have been Superior of this Convent, never\par
did I witness a transaction more infamous!”\par
\par
“You must expect much opposition to your will;” the Other replied in a\par
milder voice; “Agnes has many Friends in the Convent, and in particular\par
the Mother St. Ursula will espouse her cause most warmly. In truth, She\par
merits to have Friends; and I wish I could prevail upon you to consider\par
her youth, and her peculiar situation. She seems sensible of her fault;\par
The excess of her grief proves her penitence, and I am convinced that\par
her tears flow more from contrition than fear of punishment. Reverend\par
Mother, would you be persuaded to mitigate the severity of your\par
sentence, would you but deign to overlook this first transgression, I\par
offer myself as the pledge of her future conduct.”\par
\par
“Overlook it, say you? Mother Camilla, you amaze me! What? After\par
disgracing me in the presence of Madrid’s Idol, of the very Man on whom\par
I most wished to impress an idea of the strictness of my discipline?\par
How despicable must I have appeared to the reverend Abbot! No, Mother,\par
No! I never can forgive the insult. I cannot better convince Ambrosio\par
that I abhor such crimes, than by punishing that of Agnes with all the\par
rigour of which our severe laws admit. Cease then your supplications;\par
They will all be unavailing. My resolution is taken: Tomorrow Agnes\par
shall be made a terrible example of my justice and resentment.”\par
\par
The Mother Camilla seemed not to give up the point, but by this time\par
the Nuns were out of hearing. The Prioress unlocked the door which\par
communicated with St. Clare’s Chapel, and having entered with her\par
Companion, closed it again after them.\par
\par
Matilda now asked, who was this Agnes with whom the Prioress was thus\par
incensed, and what connexion She could have with Ambrosio. He related\par
her adventure; and He added, that since that time his ideas having\par
undergone a thorough revolution, He now felt much compassion for the\par
unfortunate Nun.\par
\par
“I design,” said He, “to request an audience of the Domina tomorrow,\par
and use every means of obtaining a mitigation of her sentence.”\par
\par
“Beware of what you do!” interrupted Matilda; “Your sudden change of\par
sentiment may naturally create surprize, and may give birth to\par
suspicions which it is most our interest to avoid. Rather, redouble\par
your outward austerity, and thunder out menaces against the errors of\par
others, the better to conceal your own. Abandon the Nun to her fate.\par
Your interfering might be dangerous, and her imprudence merits to be\par
punished: She is unworthy to enjoy Love’s pleasures, who has not wit\par
enough to conceal them. But in discussing this trifling subject I waste\par
moments which are precious. The night flies apace, and much must be\par
done before morning. The Nuns are retired; All is safe. Give me the\par
Lamp, Ambrosio. I must descend alone into these Caverns: Wait here, and\par
if any one approaches, warn me by your voice; But as you value your\par
existence, presume not to follow me. Your life would fall a victim to\par
your imprudent curiosity.”\par
\par
Thus saying She advanced towards the Sepulchre, still holding her Lamp\par
in one hand, and her little Basket in the other. She touched the door:\par
It turned slowly upon its grating hinges, and a narrow winding\par
staircase of black marble presented itself to her eyes. She descended\par
it. Ambrosio remained above, watching the faint beams of the Lamp as\par
they still proceeded up the stairs. They disappeared, and He found\par
himself in total darkness.\par
\par
Left to himself He could not reflect without surprize on the sudden\par
change in Matilda’s character and sentiments. But a few days had past\par
since She appeared the mildest and softest of her sex, devoted to his\par
will, and looking up to him as to a superior Being. Now She assumed a\par
sort of courage and manliness in her manners and discourse but\par
ill-calculated to please him. She spoke no longer to insinuate, but\par
command: He found himself unable to cope with her in argument, and was\par
unwillingly obliged to confess the superiority of her judgment. Every\par
moment convinced him of the astonishing powers of her mind: But what\par
She gained in the opinion of the Man, She lost with interest in the\par
affection of the Lover. He regretted Rosario, the fond, the gentle, and\par
submissive: He grieved that Matilda preferred the virtues of his sex to\par
those of her own; and when He thought of her expressions respecting the\par
devoted Nun, He could not help blaming them as cruel and unfeminine.\par
Pity is a sentiment so natural, so appropriate to the female character,\par
that it is scarcely a merit for a Woman to possess it, but to be\par
without it is a grievous crime. Ambrosio could not easily forgive his\par
Mistress for being deficient in this amiable quality. However, though\par
he blamed her insensibility, He felt the truth of her observations; and\par
though He pitied sincerely the unfortunate Agnes, He resolved to drop\par
the idea of interposing in her behalf.\par
\par
Near an hour had elapsed, since Matilda descended into the Caverns;\par
Still She returned not. Ambrosio’s curiosity was excited. He drew near\par
the Staircase. He listened. All was silent, except that at intervals He\par
caught the sound of Matilda’s voice, as it wound along the\par
subterraneous passages, and was re-echoed by the Sepulchre’s vaulted\par
roofs. She was at too great a distance for him to distinguish her\par
words, and ere they reached him they were deadened into a low murmur.\par
He longed to penetrate into this mystery. He resolved to disobey her\par
injunctions and follow her into the Cavern. He advanced to the\par
Staircase; He had already descended some steps when his courage failed\par
him. He remembered Matilda’s menaces if He infringed her orders, and\par
his bosom was filled with a secret unaccountable awe. He returned up\par
the stairs, resumed his former station, and waited impatiently for the\par
conclusion of this adventure.\par
\par
Suddenly He was sensible of a violent shock: An earthquake rocked the\par
ground. The Columns which supported the roof under which He stood were\par
so strongly shaken, that every moment menaced him with its fall, and at\par
the same moment He heard a loud and tremendous burst of thunder. It\par
ceased, and his eyes being fixed upon the Staircase, He saw a bright\par
column of light flash along the Caverns beneath. It was seen but for an\par
instant. No sooner did it disappear, than all was once more quiet and\par
obscure. Profound Darkness again surrounded him, and the silence of\par
night was only broken by the whirring Bat, as She flitted slowly by\par
him.\par
\par
With every instant Ambrosio’s amazement increased. Another hour\par
elapsed, after which the same light again appeared and was lost again\par
as suddenly. It was accompanied by a strain of sweet but solemn Music,\par
which as it stole through the Vaults below, inspired the Monk with\par
mingled delight and terror. It had not long been hushed, when He heard\par
Matilda’s steps upon the Staircase. She ascended from the Cavern; The\par
most lively joy animated her beautiful features.\par
\par
“Did you see any thing?” She asked.\par
\par
“Twice I saw a column of light flash up the Staircase.”\par
\par
“Nothing else?”\par
\par
“Nothing.”\par
\par
“The Morning is on the point of breaking. Let us retire to the Abbey,\par
lest daylight should betray us.”\par
\par
With a light step She hastened from the burying-ground. She regained\par
her Cell, and the curious Abbot still accompanied her. She closed the\par
door, and disembarrassed herself of her Lamp and Basket.\par
\par
“I have succeeded!” She cried, throwing herself upon his bosom:\par
“Succeeded beyond my fondest hopes! I shall live, Ambrosio, shall live\par
for you! The step which I shuddered at taking proves to me a source of\par
joys inexpressible! Oh! that I dared communicate those joys to you! Oh!\par
that I were permitted to share with you my power, and raise you as high\par
above the level of your sex, as one bold deed has exalted me above\par
mine!”\par
\par
“And what prevents you, Matilda?” interrupted the Friar; “Why is your\par
business in the Cavern made a secret? Do you think me undeserving of\par
your confidence? Matilda, I must doubt the truth of your affection,\par
while you have joys in which I am forbidden to share.”\par
\par
“You reproach me with injustice. I grieve sincerely that I am obliged\par
to conceal from you my happiness. But I am not to blame: The fault lies\par
not in me, but in yourself, my Ambrosio! You are still too much the\par
Monk. Your mind is enslaved by the prejudices of Education; And\par
Superstition might make you shudder at the idea of that which\par
experience has taught me to prize and value. At present you are unfit\par
to be trusted with a secret of such importance: But the strength of\par
your judgment; and the curiosity which I rejoice to see sparkling in\par
your eyes, makes me hope that you will one day deserve my confidence.\par
Till that period arrives, restrain your impatience. Remember that you\par
have given me your solemn oath never to enquire into this night’s\par
adventures. I insist upon your keeping this oath: For though” She added\par
smiling, while She sealed his lips with a wanton kiss; “Though I\par
forgive your breaking your vows to heaven, I expect you to keep your\par
vows to me.”\par
\par
The Friar returned the embrace which had set his blood on fire. The\par
luxurious and unbounded excesses of the former night were renewed, and\par
they separated not till the Bell rang for Matins.\par
\par
The same pleasures were frequently repeated. The Monks rejoiced in the\par
feigned Rosario’s unexpected recovery, and none of them suspected his\par
real sex. The Abbot possessed his Mistress in tranquillity, and\par
perceiving his frailty unsuspected, abandoned himself to his passions\par
in full security. Shame and remorse no longer tormented him. Frequent\par
repetitions made him familiar with sin, and his bosom became proof\par
against the stings of Conscience. In these sentiments He was encouraged\par
by Matilda; But She soon was aware that She had satiated her Lover by\par
the unbounded freedom of her caresses. Her charms becoming accustomed\par
to him, they ceased to excite the same desires which at first they had\par
inspired. The delirium of passion being past, He had leisure to observe\par
every trifling defect: Where none were to be found, Satiety made him\par
fancy them. The Monk was glutted with the fullness of pleasure: A Week\par
had scarcely elapsed before He was wearied of his Paramour: His warm\par
constitution still made him seek in her arms the gratification of his\par
lust: But when the moment of passion was over, He quitted her with\par
disgust, and his humour, naturally inconstant, made him sigh\par
impatiently for variety.\par
\par
Possession, which cloys Man, only increases the affection of Woman.\par
Matilda with every succeeding day grew more attached to the Friar.\par
Since He had obtained her favours, He was become dearer to her than\par
ever, and She felt grateful to him for the pleasures in which they had\par
equally been Sharers. Unfortunately as her passion grew ardent,\par
Ambrosio’s grew cold; The very marks of her fondness excited his\par
disgust, and its excess served to extinguish the flame which already\par
burned but feebly in his bosom. Matilda could not but remark that her\par
society seemed to him daily less agreeable: He was inattentive while\par
She spoke: her musical talents, which She possessed in perfection, had\par
lost the power of amusing him; Or if He deigned to praise them, his\par
compliments were evidently forced and cold. He no longer gazed upon her\par
with affection, or applauded her sentiments with a Lover’s partiality.\par
This Matilda well perceived, and redoubled her efforts to revive those\par
sentiments which He once had felt. She could not but fail, since He\par
considered as importunities the pains which She took to please him, and\par
was disgusted by the very means which She used to recall the Wanderer.\par
Still, however, their illicit Commerce continued: But it was clear that\par
He was led to her arms, not by love, but the cravings of brutal\par
appetite. His constitution made a Woman necessary to him, and Matilda\par
was the only one with whom He could indulge his passions safely: In\par
spite of her beauty, He gazed upon every other Female with more desire;\par
But fearing that his Hypocrisy should be made public, He confined his\par
inclinations to his own breast.\par
\par
It was by no means his nature to be timid: But his education had\par
impressed his mind with fear so strongly, that apprehension was now\par
become part of his character. Had his Youth been passed in the world,\par
He would have shown himself possessed of many brilliant and manly\par
qualities. He was naturally enterprizing, firm, and fearless: He had a\par
Warrior’s heart, and He might have shone with splendour at the head of\par
an Army. There was no want of generosity in his nature: The Wretched\par
never failed to find in him a compassionate Auditor: His abilities were\par
quick and shining, and his judgment, vast, solid, and decisive. With\par
such qualifications He would have been an ornament to his Country: That\par
He possessed them, He had given proofs in his earliest infancy, and his\par
Parents had beheld his dawning virtues with the fondest delight and\par
admiration. Unfortunately, while yet a Child He was deprived of those\par
Parents. He fell into the power of a Relation whose only wish about him\par
was never to hear of him more; For that purpose He gave him in charge\par
to his Friend, the former Superior of the Capuchins. The Abbot, a very\par
Monk, used all his endeavours to persuade the Boy that happiness\par
existed not without the walls of a Convent. He succeeded fully. To\par
deserve admittance into the order of St. Francis was Ambrosio’s highest\par
ambition. His Instructors carefully repressed those virtues whose\par
grandeur and disinterestedness were ill-suited to the Cloister. Instead\par
of universal benevolence, He adopted a selfish partiality for his own\par
particular establishment: He was taught to consider compassion for the\par
errors of Others as a crime of the blackest dye: The noble frankness of\par
his temper was exchanged for servile humility; and in order to break\par
his natural spirit, the Monks terrified his young mind by placing\par
before him all the horrors with which Superstition could furnish them:\par
They painted to him the torments of the Damned in colours the most\par
dark, terrible, and fantastic, and threatened him at the slightest\par
fault with eternal perdition. No wonder that his imagination constantly\par
dwelling upon these fearful objects should have rendered his character\par
timid and apprehensive. Add to this, that his long absence from the\par
great world, and total unacquaintance with the common dangers of life,\par
made him form of them an idea far more dismal than the reality. While\par
the Monks were busied in rooting out his virtues and narrowing his\par
sentiments, they allowed every vice which had fallen to his share to\par
arrive at full perfection. He was suffered to be proud, vain,\par
ambitious, and disdainful: He was jealous of his Equals, and despised\par
all merit but his own: He was implacable when offended, and cruel in\par
his revenge. Still in spite of the pains taken to pervert them, his\par
natural good qualities would occasionally break through the gloom cast\par
over them so carefully:\par
\par
At such times the contest for superiority between his real and acquired\par
character was striking and unaccountable to those unacquainted with his\par
original disposition. He pronounced the most severe sentences upon\par
Offenders, which, the moment after, Compassion induced him to mitigate:\par
He undertook the most daring enterprizes, which the fear of their\par
consequences soon obliged him to abandon: His inborn genius darted a\par
brilliant light upon subjects the most obscure; and almost\par
instantaneously his Superstition replunged them in darkness more\par
profound than that from which they had just been rescued. His Brother\par
Monks, regarding him as a Superior Being, remarked not this\par
contradiction in their Idol’s conduct. They were persuaded that what He\par
did must be right, and supposed him to have good reasons for changing\par
his resolutions. The fact was, that the different sentiments with which\par
Education and Nature had inspired him were combating in his bosom: It\par
remained for his passions, which as yet no opportunity had called into\par
play, to decide the victory. Unfortunately his passions were the very\par
worst Judges, to whom He could possibly have applied. His monastic\par
seclusion had till now been in his favour, since it gave him no room\par
for discovering his bad qualities. The superiority of his talents\par
raised him too far above his Companions to permit his being jealous of\par
them: His exemplary piety, persuasive eloquence, and pleasing manners\par
had secured him universal Esteem, and consequently He had no injuries\par
to revenge: His Ambition was justified by his acknowledged merit, and\par
his pride considered as no more than proper confidence. He never saw,\par
much less conversed with, the other sex: He was ignorant of the\par
pleasures in Woman’s power to bestow, and if He read in the course of\par
his studies\par
\par
“That men were fond, he smiled, and wondered how!”\par
\par
\par
For a time, spare diet, frequent watching, and severe penance cooled\par
and represt the natural warmth of his constitution: But no sooner did\par
opportunity present itself, no sooner did He catch a glimpse of joys to\par
which He was still a Stranger, than Religion’s barriers were too feeble\par
to resist the overwhelming torrent of his desires. All impediments\par
yielded before the force of his temperament, warm, sanguine, and\par
voluptuous in the excess.\par
\par
As yet his other passions lay dormant; But they only needed to be once\par
awakened, to display themselves with violence as great and\par
irresistible.\par
\par
He continued to be the admiration of Madrid. The Enthusiasm created by\par
his eloquence seemed rather to increase than diminish.\par
\par
Every Thursday, which was the only day when He appeared in public, the\par
Capuchin Cathedral was crowded with Auditors, and his discourse was\par
always received with the same approbation. He was named Confessor to\par
all the chief families in Madrid; and no one was counted fashionable\par
who was injoined penance by any other than Ambrosio. In his resolution\par
of never stirring out of his Convent, He still persisted. This\par
circumstance created a still greater opinion of his sanctity and\par
self-denial. Above all, the Women sang forth his praises loudly, less\par
influenced by devotion than by his noble countenance, majestic air, and\par
well-turned, graceful figure. The Abbey door was thronged with\par
Carriages from morning to night; and the noblest and fairest Dames of\par
Madrid confessed to the Abbot their secret peccadilloes.\par
\par
The eyes of the luxurious Friar devoured their charms: Had his\par
Penitents consulted those Interpreters, He would have needed no other\par
means of expressing his desires. For his misfortune, they were so\par
strongly persuaded of his continence, that the possibility of his\par
harbouring indecent thoughts never once entered their imaginations. The\par
climate’s heat, ’tis well known, operates with no small influence upon\par
the constitutions of the Spanish Ladies: But the most abandoned would\par
have thought it an easier task to inspire with passion the marble\par
Statue of St. Francis than the cold and rigid heart of the immaculate\par
Ambrosio.\par
\par
On his part, the Friar was little acquainted with the depravity of the\par
world; He suspected not that but few of his Penitents would have\par
rejected his addresses. Yet had He been better instructed on this head,\par
the danger attending such an attempt would have sealed up his lips in\par
silence. He knew that it would be difficult for a Woman to keep a\par
secret so strange and so important as his frailty; and He even trembled\par
lest Matilda should betray him. Anxious to preserve a reputation which\par
was infinitely dear to him, He saw all the risque of committing it to\par
the power of some vain giddy Female; and as the Beauties of Madrid\par
affected only his senses without touching his heart, He forgot them as\par
soon as they were out of his sight. The danger of discovery, the fear\par
of being repulsed, the loss of reputation, all these considerations\par
counselled him to stifle his desires: And though He now felt for it the\par
most perfect indifference, He was necessitated to confine himself to\par
Matilda’s person.\par
\par
One morning, the confluence of Penitents was greater than usual. He was\par
detained in the Confessional Chair till a late hour. At length the\par
crowd was dispatched, and He prepared to quit the Chapel, when two\par
Females entered and drew near him with humility. They threw up their\par
veils, and the youngest entreated him to listen to her for a few\par
moments. The melody of her voice, of that voice to which no Man ever\par
listened without interest, immediately caught Ambrosio’s attention. He\par
stopped. The Petitioner seemed bowed down with affliction: Her cheeks\par
were pale, her eyes dimmed with tears, and her hair fell in disorder\par
over her face and bosom. Still her countenance was so sweet, so\par
innocent, so heavenly, as might have charmed an heart less susceptible,\par
than that which panted in the Abbot’s breast. With more than usual\par
softness of manner He desired her to proceed, and heard her speak as\par
follows with an emotion which increased every moment.\par
\par
“Reverend Father, you see an Unfortunate, threatened with the loss of\par
her dearest, of almost her only Friend! My Mother, my excellent Mother\par
lies upon the bed of sickness. A sudden and dreadful malady seized her\par
last night; and so rapid has been its progress, that the Physicians\par
despair of her life. Human aid fails me; Nothing remains for me but to\par
implore the mercy of Heaven. Father, all Madrid rings with the report\par
of your piety and virtue. Deign to remember my Mother in your prayers:\par
Perhaps they may prevail on the Almighty to spare her; and should that\par
be the case, I engage myself every Thursday in the next three Months to\par
illuminate the Shrine of St. Francis in his honour.”\par
\par
“So!” thought the Monk; “Here we have a second Vincentio della Ronda.\par
Rosario’s adventure began thus,” and He wished secretly that this might\par
have the same conclusion.\par
\par
He acceded to the request. The Petitioner returned him thanks with\par
every mark of gratitude, and then continued.\par
\par
“I have yet another favour to ask. We are Strangers in Madrid; My\par
Mother needs a Confessor, and knows not to whom She should apply. We\par
understand that you never quit the Abbey, and Alas! my poor Mother is\par
unable to come hither! If you would have the goodness, reverend Father,\par
to name a proper person, whose wise and pious consolations may soften\par
the agonies of my Parent’s deathbed, you will confer an everlasting\par
favour upon hearts not ungrateful.”\par
\par
With this petition also the Monk complied. Indeed, what petition would\par
He have refused, if urged in such enchanting accents? The suppliant was\par
so interesting! Her voice was so sweet, so harmonious! Her very tears\par
became her, and her affliction seemed to add new lustre to her charms.\par
He promised to send to her a Confessor that same Evening, and begged\par
her to leave her address. The Companion presented him with a Card on\par
which it was written, and then withdrew with the fair Petitioner, who\par
pronounced before her departure a thousand benedictions on the Abbot’s\par
goodness. His eyes followed her out of the Chapel. It was not till She\par
was out of sight that He examined the Card, on which He read the\par
following words.\par
\par
“Donna Elvira Dalfa, Strada di San Iago, four doors from the Palace\par
d’Albornos.”\par
\par
The Suppliant was no other than Antonia, and Leonella was her\par
Companion. The Latter had not consented without difficulty to accompany\par
her Niece to the Abbey: Ambrosio had inspired her with such awe that\par
She trembled at the very sight of him. Her fears had conquered even her\par
natural loquacity, and while in his presence She uttered not a single\par
syllable.\par
\par
The Monk retired to his Cell, whither He was pursued by Antonia’s\par
image. He felt a thousand new emotions springing in his bosom, and He\par
trembled to examine into the cause which gave them birth. They were\par
totally different from those inspired by Matilda, when She first\par
declared her sex and her affection. He felt not the provocation of\par
lust; No voluptuous desires rioted in his bosom; Nor did a burning\par
imagination picture to him the charms which Modesty had veiled from his\par
eyes. On the contrary, what He now felt was a mingled sentiment of\par
tenderness, admiration, and respect. A soft and delicious melancholy\par
infused itself into his soul, and He would not have exchanged it for\par
the most lively transports of joy. Society now disgusted him: He\par
delighted in solitude, which permitted his indulging the visions of\par
Fancy: His thoughts were all gentle, sad, and soothing, and the whole\par
wide world presented him with no other object than Antonia.\par
\par
“Happy Man!” He exclaimed in his romantic enthusiasm; “Happy Man, who\par
is destined to possess the heart of that lovely Girl! What delicacy in\par
her features! What elegance in her form! How enchanting was the timid\par
innocence of her eyes, and how different from the wanton expression,\par
the wild luxurious fire which sparkles in Matilda’s! Oh! sweeter must\par
one kiss be snatched from the rosy lips of the First, than all the full\par
and lustful favours bestowed so freely by the Second. Matilda gluts me\par
with enjoyment even to loathing, forces me to her arms, apes the\par
Harlot, and glories in her prostitution. Disgusting! Did She know the\par
inexpressible charm of Modesty, how irresistibly it enthralls the heart\par
of Man, how firmly it chains him to the Throne of Beauty, She never\par
would have thrown it off. What would be too dear a price for this\par
lovely Girl’s affections? What would I refuse to sacrifice, could I be\par
released from my vows, and permitted to declare my love in the sight of\par
earth and heaven? While I strove to inspire her with tenderness, with\par
friendship and esteem, how tranquil and undisturbed would the hours\par
roll away! Gracious God! To see her blue downcast eyes beam upon mine\par
with timid fondness! To sit for days, for years listening to that\par
gentle voice! To acquire the right of obliging her, and hear the\par
artless expressions of her gratitude! To watch the emotions of her\par
spotless heart! To encourage each dawning virtue! To share in her joy\par
when happy, to kiss away her tears when distrest, and to see her fly to\par
my arms for comfort and support! Yes; If there is perfect bliss on\par
earth, ’tis his lot alone, who becomes that Angel’s Husband.”\par
\par
While his fancy coined these ideas, He paced his Cell with a disordered\par
air. His eyes were fixed upon vacancy: His head reclined upon his\par
shoulder; A tear rolled down his cheek, while He reflected that the\par
vision of happiness for him could never be realized.\par
\par
“She is lost to me!” He continued; “By marriage She cannot be mine: And\par
to seduce such innocence, to use the confidence reposed in me to work\par
her ruin.... Oh! it would be a crime, blacker than yet the world ever\par
witnessed! Fear not, lovely Girl! Your virtue runs no risque from me.\par
Not for Indies would I make that gentle bosom know the tortures of\par
remorse.”\par
\par
Again He paced his chamber hastily. Then stopping, his eye fell upon\par
the picture of his once-admired Madona. He tore it with indignation\par
from the wall: He threw it on the ground, and spurned it from him with\par
his foot.\par
\par
“The Prostitute!”\par
\par
Unfortunate Matilda! Her Paramour forgot that for his sake alone She\par
had forfeited her claim to virtue; and his only reason for despising\par
her was that She had loved him much too well.\par
\par
He threw himself into a Chair which stood near the Table. He saw the\par
card with Elvira’s address. He took it up, and it brought to his\par
recollection his promise respecting a Confessor. He passed a few\par
minutes in doubt: But Antonia’s Empire over him was already too much\par
decided to permit his making a long resistance to the idea which struck\par
him. He resolved to be the Confessor himself. He could leave the Abbey\par
unobserved without difficulty: By wrapping up his head in his Cowl He\par
hoped to pass through the Streets without being recognised: By taking\par
these precautions, and by recommending secrecy to Elvira’s family, He\par
doubted not to keep Madrid in ignorance that He had broken his vow\par
never to see the outside of the Abbey walls. Matilda was the only\par
person whose vigilance He dreaded: But by informing her at the\par
Refectory that during the whole of that day, Business would confine him\par
to his Cell, He thought himself secure from her wakeful jealousy.\par
Accordingly, at the hours when the Spaniards are generally taking their\par
Siesta, He ventured to quit the Abbey by a private door, the Key of\par
which was in his possession. The Cowl of his habit was thrown over his\par
face: From the heat of the weather the Streets were almost totally\par
deserted: The Monk met with few people, found the Strada di San Iago,\par
and arrived without accident at Donna Elvira’s door. He rang, was\par
admitted, and immediately ushered into an upper apartment.\par
\par
It was here that He ran the greatest risque of a discovery. Had\par
Leonella been at home, She would have recognized him directly: Her\par
communicative disposition would never have permitted her to rest till\par
all Madrid was informed that Ambrosio had ventured out of the Abbey,\par
and visited her Sister. Fortune here stood the Monk’s Friend. On\par
Leonella’s return home, She found a letter instructing her that a\par
Cousin was just dead, who had left what little He possessed between\par
Herself and Elvira. To secure this bequest She was obliged to set out\par
for Cordova without losing a moment. Amidst all her foibles her heart\par
was truly warm and affectionate, and She was unwilling to quit her\par
Sister in so dangerous a state. But Elvira insisted upon her taking the\par
journey, conscious that in her Daughter’s forlorn situation no increase\par
of fortune, however trifling, ought to be neglected. Accordingly,\par
Leonella left Madrid, sincerely grieved at her Sister’s illness, and\par
giving some few sighs to the memory of the amiable but inconstant Don\par
Christoval. She was fully persuaded that at first She had made a\par
terrible breach in his heart: But hearing nothing more of him, She\par
supposed that He had quitted the pursuit, disgusted by the lowness of\par
her origin, and knowing upon other terms than marriage He had nothing\par
to hope from such a Dragon of Virtue as She professed herself; Or else,\par
that being naturally capricious and changeable, the remembrance of her\par
charms had been effaced from the Condé’s heart by those of some newer\par
Beauty. Whatever was the cause of her losing him, She lamented it\par
sorely. She strove in vain, as She assured every body who was kind\par
enough to listen to her, to tear his image from her too susceptible\par
heart. She affected the airs of a lovesick Virgin, and carried them all\par
to the most ridiculous excess. She heaved lamentable sighs, walked with\par
her arms folded, uttered long soliloquies, and her discourse generally\par
turned upon some forsaken Maid who expired of a broken heart! Her fiery\par
locks were always ornamented with a garland of willow; Every evening\par
She was seen straying upon the Banks of a rivulet by Moonlight; and She\par
declared herself a violent Admirer of murmuring Streams and\par
Nightingales;\par
\par
“Of lonely haunts, and twilight Groves,\par
“Places which pale Passion loves!”\par
\par
\par
Such was the state of Leonella’s mind, when obliged to quit Madrid.\par
Elvira was out of patience at all these follies, and endeavoured at\par
persuading her to act like a reasonable Woman. Her advice was thrown\par
away: Leonella assured her at parting that nothing could make her\par
forget the perfidious Don Christoval. In this point She was fortunately\par
mistaken. An honest Youth of Cordova, Journeyman to an Apothecary,\par
found that her fortune would be sufficient to set him up in a genteel\par
Shop of his own: In consequence of this reflection He avowed himself\par
her Admirer. Leonella was not inflexible. The ardour of his sighs\par
melted her heart, and She soon consented to make him the happiest of\par
Mankind. She wrote to inform her Sister of her marriage; But, for\par
reasons which will be explained hereafter, Elvira never answered her\par
letter.\par
\par
Ambrosio was conducted into the Antichamber to that where Elvira was\par
reposing. The Female Domestic who had admitted him left him alone while\par
She announced his arrival to her Mistress. Antonia, who had been by her\par
Mother’s Bedside, immediately came to him.\par
\par
“Pardon me, Father,” said She, advancing towards him; when recognizing\par
his features, She stopped suddenly, and uttered a cry of joy. “Is it\par
possible!” She continued;\par
\par
“Do not my eyes deceive me? Has the worthy Ambrosio broken through his\par
resolution, that He may soften the agonies of the best of Women? What\par
pleasure will this visit give my Mother! Let me not delay for a moment\par
the comfort which your piety and wisdom will afford her.”\par
\par
Thus saying, She opened the chamber door, presented to her Mother her\par
distinguished Visitor, and having placed an armed-chair by the side of\par
the Bed, withdrew into another department.\par
\par
Elvira was highly gratified by this visit: Her expectations had been\par
raised high by general report, but She found them far exceeded.\par
Ambrosio, endowed by nature with powers of pleasing, exerted them to\par
the utmost while conversing with Antonia’s Mother. With persuasive\par
eloquence He calmed every fear, and dissipated every scruple: He bad\par
her reflect on the infinite mercy of her Judge, despoiled Death of his\par
darts and terrors, and taught her to view without shrinking the abyss\par
of eternity, on whose brink She then stood. Elvira was absorbed in\par
attention and delight: While She listened to his exhortations,\par
confidence and comfort stole insensibly into her mind. She unbosomed to\par
him without hesitation her cares and apprehensions. The latter\par
respecting a future life He had already quieted: And He now removed the\par
former, which She felt for the concerns of this. She trembled for\par
Antonia. She had none to whose care She could recommend her, save to\par
the Marquis de las Cisternas and her Sister Leonella. The protection of\par
the One was very uncertain; and as to the Other, though fond of her\par
Niece, Leonella was so thoughtless and vain as to make her an improper\par
person to have the sole direction of a Girl so young and ignorant of\par
the World. The Friar no sooner learnt the cause of her alarms than He\par
begged her to make herself easy upon that head. He doubted not being\par
able to secure for Antonia a safe refuge in the House of one of his\par
Penitents, the Marchioness of Villa-Franca: This was a Lady of\par
acknowledged virtue, remarkable for strict principles and extensive\par
charity. Should accident deprive her of this resource, He engaged to\par
procure Antonia a reception in some respectable Convent: That is to\par
say, in quality of boarder; for Elvira had declared herself no Friend\par
to a monastic life, and the Monk was either candid or complaisant\par
enough to allow that her disapprobation was not unfounded.\par
\par
These proofs of the interest which He felt for her completely won\par
Elvira’s heart. In thanking him She exhausted every expression which\par
Gratitude could furnish, and protested that now She should resign\par
herself with tranquillity to the Grave. Ambrosio rose to take leave: He\par
promised to return the next day at the same hour, but requested that\par
his visits might be kept secret.\par
\par
“I am unwilling” said He, “that my breaking through a rule imposed by\par
necessity should be generally known. Had I not resolved never to quit\par
my Convent, except upon circumstances as urgent as that which has\par
conducted me to your door, I should be frequently summoned upon\par
insignificant occasions: That time would be engrossed by the Curious,\par
the Unoccupied, and the fanciful, which I now pass at the Bedside of\par
the Sick, in comforting the expiring Penitent, and clearing the passage\par
to Eternity from Thorns.”\par
\par
Elvira commended equally his prudence and compassion, promising to\par
conceal carefully the honour of his visits. The Monk then gave her his\par
benediction, and retired from the chamber.\par
\par
In the Antiroom He found Antonia: He could not refuse himself the\par
pleasure of passing a few moments in her society. He bad her take\par
comfort, for that her Mother seemed composed and tranquil, and He hoped\par
that She might yet do well. He enquired who attended her, and engaged\par
to send the Physician of his Convent to see her, one of the most\par
skilful in Madrid. He then launched out in Elvira’s commendation,\par
praised her purity and fortitude of mind, and declared that She had\par
inspired him with the highest esteem and reverence. Antonia’s innocent\par
heart swelled with gratitude: Joy danced in her eyes, where a tear\par
still sparkled. The hopes which He gave her of her Mother’s recovery,\par
the lively interest which He seemed to feel for her, and the flattering\par
way in which She was mentioned by him, added to the report of his\par
judgment and virtue, and to the impression made upon her by his\par
eloquence, confirmed the favourable opinion with which his first\par
appearance had inspired Antonia. She replied with diffidence, but\par
without restraint: She feared not to relate to him all her little\par
sorrows, all her little fears and anxieties; and She thanked him for\par
his goodness with all the genuine warmth which favours kindle in a\par
young and innocent heart. Such alone know how to estimate benefits at\par
their full value. They who are conscious of Mankind’s perfidy and\par
selfishness, ever receive an obligation with apprehension and distrust:\par
They suspect that some secret motive must lurk behind it: They express\par
their thanks with restraint and caution, and fear to praise a kind\par
action to its full extent, aware that some future day a return may be\par
required. Not so Antonia; She thought the world was composed only of\par
those who resembled her, and that vice existed, was to her still a\par
secret. The Monk had been of service to her; He said that He wished her\par
well; She was grateful for his kindness, and thought that no terms were\par
strong enough to be the vehicle of her thanks. With what delight did\par
Ambrosio listen to the declaration of her artless gratitude! The\par
natural grace of her manners, the unequalled sweetness of her voice,\par
her modest vivacity, her unstudied elegance, her expressive\par
countenance, and intelligent eyes united to inspire him with pleasure\par
and admiration, While the solidity and correctness of her remarks\par
received additional beauty from the unaffected simplicity of the\par
language in which they were conveyed.\par
\par
Ambrosio was at length obliged to tear himself from this conversation\par
which possessed for him but too many charms. He repeated to Antonia his\par
wishes that his visits should not be made known, which desire She\par
promised to observe. He then quitted the House, while his Enchantress\par
hastened to her Mother, ignorant of the mischief which her Beauty had\par
caused. She was eager to know Elvira’s opinion of the Man whom She had\par
praised in such enthusiastic terms, and was delighted to find it\par
equally favourable, if not even more so, than her own.\par
\par
“Even before He spoke,” said Elvira, “I was prejudiced in his favour:\par
The fervour of his exhortations, dignity of his manner, and closeness\par
of his reasoning, were very far from inducing me to alter my opinion.\par
His fine and full-toned voice struck me particularly; But surely,\par
Antonia, I have heard it before. It seemed perfectly familiar to my\par
ear. Either I must have known the Abbot in former times, or his voice\par
bears a wonderful resemblance to that of some other, to whom I have\par
often listened.\par
\par
There were certain tones which touched my very heart, and made me feel\par
sensations so singular, that I strive in vain to account for them.”\par
\par
“My dearest Mother, it produced the same effect upon me: Yet certainly\par
neither of us ever heard his voice till we came to Madrid. I suspect\par
that what we attribute to his voice, really proceeds from his pleasant\par
manners, which forbid our considering him as a Stranger. I know not\par
why, but I feel more at my ease while conversing with him than I\par
usually do with people who are unknown to me. I feared not to repeat to\par
him all my childish thoughts; and somehow I felt confident that He\par
would hear my folly with indulgence. Oh! I was not deceived in him! He\par
listened to me with such an air of kindness and attention! He answered\par
me with such gentleness, such condescension! He did not call me an\par
Infant, and treat me with contempt, as our cross old Confessor at the\par
Castle used to do. I verily believe that if I had lived in Murcia a\par
thousand years, I never should have liked that fat old Father Dominic!”\par
\par
“I confess that Father Dominic had not the most pleasing manners in the\par
world; But He was honest, friendly, and well-meaning.”\par
\par
“Ah! my dear Mother, those qualities are so common!”\par
\par
“God grant, my Child, that Experience may not teach you to think them\par
rare and precious: I have found them but too much so! But tell me,\par
Antonia; Why is it impossible for me to have seen the Abbot before?”\par
\par
“Because since the moment when He entered the Abbey, He has never been\par
on the outside of its walls. He told me just now, that from his\par
ignorance of the Streets, He had some difficulty to find the Strada di\par
San Iago, though so near the Abbey.”\par
\par
“All this is possible, and still I may have seen him BEFORE He entered\par
the Abbey: In order to come out, it was rather necessary that He should\par
first go in.”\par
\par
“Holy Virgin! As you say, that is very true.—Oh! But might He not have\par
been born in the Abbey?”\par
\par
Elvira smiled.\par
\par
“Why, not very easily.”\par
\par
“Stay, Stay! Now I recollect how it was. He was put into the Abbey\par
quite a Child; The common People say that He fell from heaven, and was\par
sent as a present to the Capuchins by the Virgin.”\par
\par
“That was very kind of her. And so He fell from heaven, Antonia?\par
\par
He must have had a terrible tumble.”\par
\par
“Many do not credit this, and I fancy, my dear Mother, that I must\par
number you among the Unbelievers. Indeed, as our Landlady told my Aunt,\par
the general idea is that his Parents, being poor and unable to maintain\par
him, left him just born at the Abbey door. The late Superior from pure\par
charity had him educated in the Convent, and He proved to be a model of\par
virtue, and piety, and learning, and I know not what else besides: In\par
consequence, He was first received as a Brother of the order, and not\par
long ago was chosen Abbot. However, whether this account or the other\par
is the true one, at least all agree that when the Monks took him under\par
their care, He could not speak: Therefore, you could not have heard his\par
voice before He entered the Monastery, because at that time He had no\par
voice at all.”\par
\par
“Upon my word, Antonia, you argue very closely! Your conclusions are\par
infallible! I did not suspect you of being so able a Logician.”\par
\par
“Ah! You are mocking me! But so much the better. It delights me to see\par
you in spirits: Besides you seem tranquil and easy, and I hope that you\par
will have no more convulsions. Oh! I was sure the Abbot’s visit would\par
do you good!”\par
\par
“It has indeed done me good, my Child. He has quieted my mind upon some\par
points which agitated me, and I already feel the effects of his\par
attention. My eyes grow heavy, and I think I can sleep a little. Draw\par
the curtains, my Antonia: But if I should not wake before midnight, do\par
not sit up with me, I charge you.”\par
\par
Antonia promised to obey her, and having received her blessing drew the\par
curtains of the Bed. She then seated herself in silence at her\par
embroidery frame, and beguiled the hours with building Castles in the\par
air. Her spirits were enlivened by the evident change for the better in\par
Elvira, and her fancy presented her with visions bright and pleasing.\par
In these dreams Ambrosio made no despicable figure. She thought of him\par
with joy and gratitude; But for every idea which fell to the Friar’s\par
share, at least two were unconsciously bestowed upon Lorenzo. Thus\par
passed the time, till the Bell in the neighbouring Steeple of the\par
Capuchin Cathedral announced the hour of midnight: Antonia remembered\par
her Mother’s injunctions, and obeyed them, though with reluctance. She\par
undrew the curtains with caution. Elvira was enjoying a profound and\par
quiet slumber; Her cheek glowed with health’s returning colours: A\par
smile declared that her dreams were pleasant, and as Antonia bent over\par
her, She fancied that She heard her name pronounced. She kissed her\par
Mother’s forehead softly, and retired to her chamber. There She knelt\par
before a Statue of St. Rosolia, her Patroness; She recommended herself\par
to the protection of heaven, and as had been her custom from infancy,\par
concluded her devotions by chaunting the following Stanzas.\par
\par
MIDNIGHT HYMN\par
\par
\par
Now all is hushed; The solemn chime\par
No longer swells the nightly gale:\par
Thy awful presence, Hour sublime,\par
With spotless heart once more I hail.\par
\par
’Tis now the moment still and dread,\par
When Sorcerers use their baleful power;\par
When Graves give up their buried dead\par
To profit by the sanctioned hour:\par
\par
From guilt and guilty thoughts secure,\par
To duty and devotion true,\par
With bosom light and conscience pure,\par
Repose, thy gentle aid I woo.\par
\par
Good Angels, take my thanks, that still\par
The snares of vice I view with scorn;\par
Thanks, that to-night as free from ill\par
I sleep, as when I woke at morn.\par
\par
Yet may not my unconscious breast\par
Harbour some guilt to me unknown?\par
Some wish impure, which unreprest\par
You blush to see, and I to own?\par
\par
If such there be, in gentle dream\par
Instruct my feet to shun the snare;\par
Bid truth upon my errors beam,\par
And deign to make me still your care.\par
\par
Chase from my peaceful bed away\par
The witching Spell, a foe to rest,\par
The nightly Goblin, wanton Fay,\par
The Ghost in pain, and Fiend unblest:\par
\par
Let not the Tempter in mine ear\par
Pour lessons of unhallowed joy;\par
Let not the Night-mare, wandering near\par
My Couch, the calm of sleep destroy;\par
\par
Let not some horrid dream affright\par
With strange fantastic forms mine eyes;\par
But rather bid some vision bright\par
Display the bliss of yonder skies.\par
\par
Show me the crystal Domes of Heaven,\par
The worlds of light where Angels lie;\par
Shew me the lot to Mortals given,\par
Who guiltless live, who guiltless die.\par
\par
Then show me how a seat to gain\par
Amidst those blissful realms of\par
Air; Teach me to shun each guilty stain,\par
And guide me to the good and fair.\par
\par
So every morn and night, my Voice\par
To heaven the grateful strain shall raise;\par
In You as Guardian Powers rejoice,\par
Good Angels, and exalt your praise:\par
\par
So will I strive with zealous fire\par
Each vice to shun, each fault correct;\par
Will love the lessons you inspire,\par
And Prize the virtues you protect.\par
\par
Then when at length by high command\par
My body seeks the Grave’s repose,\par
When Death draws nigh with friendly hand\par
My failing Pilgrim eyes to close;\par
\par
Pleased that my soul has ’scaped the wreck,\par
Sighless will I my life resign,\par
And yield to God my Spirit back,\par
As pure as when it first was mine.\par
\par
\par
Having finished her usual devotions, Antonia retired to bed. Sleep soon\par
stole over her senses; and for several hours She enjoyed that calm\par
repose which innocence alone can know, and for which many a Monarch\par
with pleasure would exchange his Crown.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER VII.\par
\par
\par
——Ah! how dark\par
These long-extended realms and rueful wastes;\par
Where nought but silence reigns, and night, dark night,\par
Dark as was Chaos ere the Infant Sun\par
Was rolled together, or had tried its beams\par
Athwart the gloom profound!\par
The sickly Taper\par
By glimmering through thy low-browed misty vaults,\par
Furred round with mouldy damps, and ropy slime,\par
Lets fall a supernumerary horror,\par
And only serves to make\par
Thy night more irksome!\par
\par
BLAIR.\par
\par
Returned undiscovered to the Abbey, Ambrosio’s mind was filled with the\par
most pleasing images. He was wilfully blind to the danger of exposing\par
himself to Antonia’s charms: He only remembered the pleasure which her\par
society had afforded him, and rejoiced in the prospect of that pleasure\par
being repeated. He failed not to profit by Elvira’s indisposition to\par
obtain a sight of her Daughter every day. At first He bounded his\par
wishes to inspire Antonia with friendship: But no sooner was He\par
convinced that She felt that sentiment in its fullest extent, than his\par
aim became more decided, and his attentions assumed a warmer colour.\par
The innocent familiarity with which She treated him, encouraged his\par
desires: Grown used to her modesty, it no longer commanded the same\par
respect and awe: He still admired it, but it only made him more anxious\par
to deprive her of that quality which formed her principal charm. Warmth\par
of passion, and natural penetration, of which latter unfortunately both\par
for himself and Antonia He possessed an ample share, supplied a\par
knowledge of the arts of seduction. He easily distinguished the\par
emotions which were favourable to his designs, and seized every means\par
with avidity of infusing corruption into Antonia’s bosom. This He found\par
no easy matter. Extreme simplicity prevented her from perceiving the\par
aim to which the Monk’s insinuations tended; But the excellent morals\par
which She owed to Elvira’s care, the solidity and correctness of her\par
understanding, and a strong sense of what was right implanted in her\par
heart by Nature, made her feel that his precepts must be faulty. By a\par
few simple words She frequently overthrew the whole bulk of his\par
sophistical arguments, and made him conscious how weak they were when\par
opposed to Virtue and Truth. On such occasion He took refuge in his\par
eloquence; He overpowered her with a torrent of Philosophical\par
paradoxes, to which, not understanding them, it was impossible for her\par
to reply; And thus though He did not convince her that his reasoning\par
was just, He at least prevented her from discovering it to be false. He\par
perceived that her respect for his judgment augmented daily, and\par
doubted not with time to bring her to the point desired.\par
\par
He was not unconscious that his attempts were highly criminal: He saw\par
clearly the baseness of seducing the innocent Girl: But his passion was\par
too violent to permit his abandoning his design. He resolved to pursue\par
it, let the consequences be what they might. He depended upon finding\par
Antonia in some unguarded moment; And seeing no other Man admitted into\par
her society, nor hearing any mentioned either by her or by Elvira, He\par
imagined that her young heart was still unoccupied. While He waited for\par
the opportunity of satisfying his unwarrantable lust, every day\par
increased his coldness for Matilda. Not a little was this occasioned by\par
the consciousness of his faults to her. To hide them from her He was\par
not sufficiently master of himself: Yet He dreaded lest, in a transport\par
of jealous rage, She should betray the secret on which his character\par
and even his life depended. Matilda could not but remark his\par
indifference: He was conscious that She remarked it, and fearing her\par
reproaches, shunned her studiously. Yet when He could not avoid her,\par
her mildness might have convinced him that He had nothing to dread from\par
her resentment. She had resumed the character of the gentle interesting\par
Rosario: She taxed him not with ingratitude; But her eyes filled with\par
involuntary tears, and the soft melancholy of her countenance and voice\par
uttered complaints far more touching than words could have conveyed.\par
Ambrosio was not unmoved by her sorrow; But unable to remove its cause,\par
He forbore to show that it affected him. As her conduct convinced him\par
that He needed not fear her vengeance, He continued to neglect her, and\par
avoided her company with care. Matilda saw that She in vain attempted\par
to regain his affections: Yet She stifled the impulse of resentment,\par
and continued to treat her inconstant Lover with her former fondness\par
and attention.\par
\par
By degrees Elvira’s constitution recovered itself. She was no longer\par
troubled with convulsions, and Antonia ceased to tremble for her\par
Mother. Ambrosio beheld this reestablishment with displeasure. He saw\par
that Elvira’s knowledge of the world would not be the Dupe of his\par
sanctified demeanour, and that She would easily perceive his views upon\par
her Daughter. He resolved therefore, before She quitted her chamber, to\par
try the extent of his influence over the innocent Antonia.\par
\par
One evening, when He had found Elvira almost perfectly restored to\par
health, He quitted her earlier than was his usual custom. Not finding\par
Antonia in the Antichamber, He ventured to follow her to her own. It\par
was only separated from her Mother’s by a Closet, in which Flora, the\par
Waiting-Woman, generally slept. Antonia sat upon a Sopha with her back\par
towards the door, and read attentively. She heard not his approach,\par
till He had seated himself by her. She started, and welcomed him with a\par
look of pleasure: Then rising, She would have conducted him to the\par
sitting-room; But Ambrosio taking her hand, obliged her by gentle\par
violence to resume her place. She complied without difficulty: She knew\par
not that there was more impropriety in conversing with him in one room\par
than another. She thought herself equally secure of his principles and\par
her own, and having replaced herself upon the Sopha, She began to\par
prattle to him with her usual ease and vivacity.\par
\par
He examined the Book which She had been reading, and had now placed\par
upon the Table. It was the Bible.\par
\par
“How!” said the Friar to himself; “Antonia reads the Bible, and is\par
still so ignorant?”\par
\par
But, upon a further inspection, He found that Elvira had made exactly\par
the same remark. That prudent Mother, while She admired the beauties of\par
the sacred writings, was convinced that, unrestricted, no reading more\par
improper could be permitted a young Woman. Many of the narratives can\par
only tend to excite ideas the worst calculated for a female breast:\par
Every thing is called plainly and roundly by its name; and the annals\par
of a Brothel would scarcely furnish a greater choice of indecent\par
expressions. Yet this is the Book which young Women are recommended to\par
study; which is put into the hands of Children, able to comprehend\par
little more than those passages of which they had better remain\par
ignorant; and which but too frequently inculcates the first rudiments\par
of vice, and gives the first alarm to the still sleeping passions. Of\par
this was Elvira so fully convinced, that She would have preferred\par
putting into her Daughter’s hands “_Amadis de Gaul_,” or “_The Valiant\par
Champion, Tirante the White;_” and would sooner have authorised her\par
studying the lewd exploits of “_Don Galaor_,” or the lascivious jokes\par
of the “_Damsel Plazer di mi vida_.” She had in consequence made two\par
resolutions respecting the Bible. The first was that Antonia should not\par
read it till She was of an age to feel its beauties, and profit by its\par
morality: The second, that it should be copied out with her own hand,\par
and all improper passages either altered or omitted. She had adhered to\par
this determination, and such was the Bible which Antonia was reading:\par
It had been lately delivered to her, and She perused it with an\par
avidity, with a delight that was inexpressible. Ambrosio perceived his\par
mistake, and replaced the Book upon the Table.\par
\par
Antonia spoke of her Mother’s health with all the enthusiastic joy of a\par
youthful heart.\par
\par
“I admire your filial affection,” said the Abbot; “It proves the\par
excellence and sensibility of your character; It promises a treasure to\par
him whom Heaven has destined to possess your affections. The Breast, so\par
capable of fondness for a Parent, what will it feel for a Lover? Nay,\par
perhaps, what feels it for one even now? Tell me, my lovely Daughter;\par
Have you known what it is to love? Answer me with sincerity: Forget my\par
habit, and consider me only as a Friend.”\par
\par
“What it is to love?” said She, repeating his question; “Oh! yes,\par
undoubtedly; I have loved many, many People.”\par
\par
“That is not what I mean. The love of which I speak can be felt only\par
for one. Have you never seen the Man whom you wished to be your\par
Husband?”\par
\par
“Oh! No, indeed!”\par
\par
This was an untruth, but She was unconscious of its falsehood: She knew\par
not the nature of her sentiments for Lorenzo; and never having seen him\par
since his first visit to Elvira, with every day his Image grew less\par
feebly impressed upon her bosom. Besides, She thought of an Husband\par
with all a Virgin’s terror, and negatived the Friar’s demand without a\par
moment’s hesitation.\par
\par
“And do you not long to see that Man, Antonia? Do you feel no void in\par
your heart which you fain would have filled up? Do you heave no sighs\par
for the absence of some one dear to you, but who that some one is, you\par
know not? Perceive you not that what formerly could please, has charms\par
for you no longer? That a thousand new wishes, new ideas, new\par
sensations, have sprang in your bosom, only to be felt, never to be\par
described? Or while you fill every other heart with passion, is it\par
possible that your own remains insensible and cold? It cannot be! That\par
melting eye, that blushing cheek, that enchanting voluptuous melancholy\par
which at times overspreads your features, all these marks belye your\par
words. You love, Antonia, and in vain would hide it from me.”\par
\par
“Father, you amaze me! What is this love of which you speak? I neither\par
know its nature, nor if I felt it, why I should conceal the sentiment.”\par
\par
“Have you seen no Man, Antonia, whom though never seen before, you\par
seemed long to have sought? Whose form, though a Stranger’s, was\par
familiar to your eyes? The sound of whose voice soothed you, pleased\par
you, penetrated to your very soul? In whose presence you rejoiced, for\par
whose absence you lamented? With whom your heart seemed to expand, and\par
in whose bosom with confidence unbounded you reposed the cares of your\par
own? Have you not felt all this, Antonia?”\par
\par
“Certainly I have: The first time that I saw you, I felt it.”\par
\par
Ambrosio started. Scarcely dared He credit his hearing.\par
\par
“Me, Antonia?” He cried, his eyes sparkling with delight and\par
impatience, while He seized her hand, and pressed it rapturously to his\par
lips. “Me, Antonia? You felt these sentiments for me?”\par
\par
“Even with more strength than you have described. The very moment that\par
I beheld you, I felt so pleased, so interested! I waited so eagerly to\par
catch the sound of your voice, and when I heard it, it seemed so sweet!\par
It spoke to me a language till then so unknown! Methought, it told me a\par
thousand things which I wished to hear! It seemed as if I had long\par
known you; as if I had a right to your friendship, your advice, and\par
your protection.\par
\par
I wept when you departed, and longed for the time which should restore\par
you to my sight.”\par
\par
“Antonia! my charming Antonia!” exclaimed the Monk, and caught her to\par
his bosom; “Can I believe my senses? Repeat it to me, my sweet Girl!\par
Tell me again that you love me, that you love me truly and tenderly!”\par
\par
“Indeed, I do: Let my Mother be excepted, and the world holds no one\par
more dear to me!”\par
\par
At this frank avowal Ambrosio no longer possessed himself; Wild with\par
desire, He clasped the blushing Trembler in his arms. He fastened his\par
lips greedily upon hers, sucked in her pure delicious breath, violated\par
with his bold hand the treasures of her bosom, and wound around him her\par
soft and yielding limbs. Startled, alarmed, and confused at his action,\par
surprize at first deprived her of the power of resistance. At length\par
recovering herself, She strove to escape from his embrace.\par
\par
“Father! .... Ambrosio!” She cried; “Release me, for God’s sake!”\par
\par
But the licentious Monk heeded not her prayers: He persisted in his\par
design, and proceeded to take still greater liberties. Antonia prayed,\par
wept, and struggled: Terrified to the extreme, though at what She knew\par
not, She exerted all her strength to repulse the Friar, and was on the\par
point of shrieking for assistance when the chamber door was suddenly\par
thrown open. Ambrosio had just sufficient presence of mind to be\par
sensible of his danger. Reluctantly He quitted his prey, and started\par
hastily from the Couch. Antonia uttered an exclamation of joy, flew\par
towards the door, and found herself clasped in the arms of her Mother.\par
\par
Alarmed at some of the Abbot’s speeches, which Antonia had innocently\par
repeated, Elvira resolved to ascertain the truth of her suspicions. She\par
had known enough of Mankind not to be imposed upon by the Monk’s\par
reputed virtue. She reflected on several circumstances, which though\par
trifling, on being put together seemed to authorize her fears. His\par
frequent visits, which as far as She could see, were confined to her\par
family; His evident emotion, whenever She spoke of Antonia; His being\par
in the full prime and heat of Manhood; and above all, his pernicious\par
philosophy communicated to her by Antonia, and which accorded but ill\par
with his conversation in her presence, all these circumstances inspired\par
her with doubts respecting the purity of Ambrosio’s friendship. In\par
consequence, She resolved, when He should next be alone with Antonia,\par
to endeavour at surprizing him. Her plan had succeeded. ’Tis true, that\par
when She entered the room, He had already abandoned his prey; But the\par
disorder of her Daughter’s dress, and the shame and confusion stamped\par
upon the Friar’s countenance, sufficed to prove that her suspicions\par
were but too well-founded. However, She was too prudent to make those\par
suspicions known. She judged that to unmask the Imposter would be no\par
easy matter, the public being so much prejudiced in his favour: and\par
having but few Friends, She thought it dangerous to make herself so\par
powerful an Enemy. She affected therefore not to remark his agitation,\par
seated herself tranquilly upon the Sopha, assigned some trifling reason\par
for having quitted her room unexpectedly, and conversed on various\par
subjects with seeming confidence and ease.\par
\par
Reassured by her behaviour, the Monk began to recover himself. He\par
strove to answer Elvira without appearing embarrassed: But He was still\par
too great a novice in dissimulation, and He felt that He must look\par
confused and awkward. He soon broke off the conversation, and rose to\par
depart. What was his vexation, when on taking leave, Elvira told him in\par
polite terms, that being now perfectly reestablished, She thought it an\par
injustice to deprive Others of his company, who might be more in need\par
of it! She assured him of her eternal gratitude, for the benefit which\par
during her illness She had derived from his society and exhortations:\par
And She lamented that her domestic affairs, as well as the multitude of\par
business which his situation must of necessity impose upon him, would\par
in future deprive her of the pleasure of his visits. Though delivered\par
in the mildest language this hint was too plain to be mistaken. Still,\par
He was preparing to put in a remonstrance when an expressive look from\par
Elvira stopped him short. He dared not press her to receive him, for\par
her manner convinced him that He was discovered: He submitted without\par
reply, took an hasty leave, and retired to the Abbey, his heart filled\par
with rage and shame, with bitterness and disappointment.\par
\par
Antonia’s mind felt relieved by his departure; Yet She could not help\par
lamenting that She was never to see him more. Elvira also felt a secret\par
sorrow; She had received too much pleasure from thinking him her\par
Friend, not to regret the necessity of changing her opinion: But her\par
mind was too much accustomed to the fallacy of worldly friendships to\par
permit her present disappointment to weigh upon it long. She now\par
endeavoured to make her Daughter aware of the risque which She had ran:\par
But She was obliged to treat the subject with caution, lest in removing\par
the bandage of ignorance, the veil of innocence should be rent away.\par
She therefore contented herself with warning Antonia to be upon her\par
guard, and ordering her, should the Abbot persist in his visits, never\par
to receive them but in company. With this injunction Antonia promised\par
to comply.\par
\par
Ambrosio hastened to his Cell. He closed the door after him, and threw\par
himself upon the bed in despair. The impulse of desire, the stings of\par
disappointment, the shame of detection, and the fear of being publicly\par
unmasked, rendered his bosom a scene of the most horrible confusion. He\par
knew not what course to pursue. Debarred the presence of Antonia, He\par
had no hopes of satisfying that passion which was now become a part of\par
his existence. He reflected that his secret was in a Woman’s power: He\par
trembled with apprehension when He beheld the precipice before him, and\par
with rage, when He thought that had it not been for Elvira, He should\par
now have possessed the object of his desires. With the direct\par
imprecations He vowed vengeance against her; He swore that, cost what\par
it would, He still would possess Antonia. Starting from the Bed, He\par
paced the chamber with disordered steps, howled with impotent fury,\par
dashed himself violently against the walls, and indulged all the\par
transports of rage and madness.\par
\par
He was still under the influence of this storm of passions when He\par
heard a gentle knock at the door of his Cell. Conscious that his voice\par
must have been heard, He dared not refuse admittance to the Importuner:\par
He strove to compose himself, and to hide his agitation. Having in some\par
degree succeeded, He drew back the bolt: The door opened, and Matilda\par
appeared.\par
\par
At this precise moment there was no one with whose presence He could\par
better have dispensed. He had not sufficient command over himself to\par
conceal his vexation. He started back, and frowned.\par
\par
“I am busy,” said He in a stern and hasty tone; “Leave me!”\par
\par
Matilda heeded him not: She again fastened the door, and then advanced\par
towards him with an air gentle and supplicating.\par
\par
“Forgive me, Ambrosio,” said She; “For your own sake I must not obey\par
you. Fear no complaints from me; I come not to reproach you with your\par
ingratitude. I pardon you from my heart, and since your love can no\par
longer be mine, I request the next best gift, your confidence and\par
friendship. We cannot force our inclinations; The little beauty which\par
you once saw in me has perished with its novelty, and if it can no\par
longer excite desire, mine is the fault, not yours. But why persist in\par
shunning me? Why such anxiety to fly my presence? You have sorrows, but\par
will not permit me to share them; You have disappointments, but will\par
not accept my comfort; You have wishes, but forbid my aiding your\par
pursuits. ’Tis of this which I complain, not of your indifference to my\par
person. I have given up the claims of the Mistress, but nothing shall\par
prevail on me to give up those of the Friend.”\par
\par
Her mildness had an instantaneous effect upon Ambrosio’s feelings.\par
\par
“Generous Matilda!” He replied, taking her hand, “How far do you rise\par
superior to the foibles of your sex! Yes, I accept your offer. I have\par
need of an adviser, and a Confident: In you I find every needful\par
quality united. But to aid my pursuits .... Ah! Matilda, it lies not in\par
your power!”\par
\par
“It lies in no one’s power but mine. Ambrosio, your secret is none to\par
me; Your every step, your every action has been observed by my\par
attentive eye. You love.”\par
\par
“Matilda!”\par
\par
“Why conceal it from me? Fear not the little jealousy which taints the\par
generality of Women: My soul disdains so despicable a passion. You\par
love, Ambrosio; Antonia Dalfa is the object of your flame. I know every\par
circumstance respecting your passion: Every conversation has been\par
repeated to me. I have been informed of your attempt to enjoy Antonia’s\par
person, your disappointment, and dismission from Elvira’s House. You\par
now despair of possessing your Mistress; But I come to revive your\par
hopes, and point out the road to success.”\par
\par
“To success? Oh! impossible!”\par
\par
“To them who dare nothing is impossible. Rely upon me, and you may yet\par
be happy. The time is come, Ambrosio, when regard for your comfort and\par
tranquillity compels me to reveal a part of my History, with which you\par
are still unacquainted. Listen, and do not interrupt me: Should my\par
confession disgust you, remember that in making it my sole aim is to\par
satisfy your wishes, and restore that peace to your heart which at\par
present has abandoned it. I formerly mentioned that my Guardian was a\par
Man of uncommon knowledge: He took pains to instil that knowledge into\par
my infant mind. Among the various sciences which curiosity had induced\par
him to explore, He neglected not that which by most is esteemed\par
impious, and by many chimerical. I speak of those arts which relate to\par
the world of Spirits. His deep researches into causes and effects, his\par
unwearied application to the study of natural philosophy, his profound\par
and unlimited knowledge of the properties and virtues of every gem\par
which enriches the deep, of every herb which the earth produces, at\par
length procured him the distinction which He had sought so long, so\par
earnestly. His curiosity was fully slaked, his ambition amply\par
gratified. He gave laws to the elements; He could reverse the order of\par
nature; His eye read the mandates of futurity, and the infernal Spirits\par
were submissive to his commands. Why shrink you from me? I understand\par
that enquiring look. Your suspicions are right, though your terrors are\par
unfounded. My Guardian concealed not from me his most precious\par
acquisition. Yet, had I never seen _you_, I should never have exerted\par
my power. Like you I shuddered at the thoughts of Magic: Like you I had\par
formed a terrible idea of the consequences of raising a daemon. To\par
preserve that life which your love had taught me to prize, I had\par
recourse to means which I trembled at employing. You remember that\par
night which I past in St. Clare’s Sepulchre? Then was it that,\par
surrounded by mouldering bodies, I dared to perform those mystic rites\par
which summoned to my aid a fallen Angel. Judge what must have been my\par
joy at discovering that my terrors were imaginary: I saw the Dæmon\par
obedient to my orders, I saw him trembling at my frown, and found that,\par
instead of selling my soul to a Master, my courage had purchased for\par
myself a slave.”\par
\par
“Rash Matilda! What have you done? You have doomed yourself to endless\par
perdition; You have bartered for momentary power eternal happiness! If\par
on witchcraft depends the fruition of my desires, I renounce your aid\par
most absolutely. The consequences are too horrible: I doat upon\par
Antonia, but am not so blinded by lust as to sacrifice for her\par
enjoyment my existence both in this world and the next.”\par
\par
“Ridiculous prejudices! Oh! blush, Ambrosio, blush at being subjected\par
to their dominion. Where is the risque of accepting my offers? What\par
should induce my persuading you to this step, except the wish of\par
restoring you to happiness and quiet. If there is danger, it must fall\par
upon me: It is I who invoke the ministry of the Spirits; Mine therefore\par
will be the crime, and yours the profit. But danger there is none: The\par
Enemy of Mankind is my Slave, not my Sovereign. Is there no difference\par
between giving and receiving laws, between serving and commanding?\par
Awake from your idle dreams, Ambrosio! Throw from you these terrors so\par
ill-suited to a soul like yours; Leave them for common Men, and dare to\par
be happy! Accompany me this night to St. Clare’s Sepulchre, witness my\par
incantations, and Antonia is your own.”\par
\par
“To obtain her by such means I neither can, or will. Cease then to\par
persuade me, for I dare not employ Hell’s agency.\par
\par
“You DARE not? How have you deceived me! That mind which I esteemed so\par
great and valiant, proves to be feeble, puerile, and grovelling, a\par
slave to vulgar errors, and weaker than a Woman’s.”\par
\par
“What? Though conscious of the danger, wilfully shall I expose myself\par
to the Seducer’s arts? Shall I renounce for ever my title to salvation?\par
Shall my eyes seek a sight which I know will blast them? No, no,\par
Matilda; I will not ally myself with God’s Enemy.”\par
\par
“Are you then God’s Friend at present? Have you not broken your\par
engagements with him, renounced his service, and abandoned yourself to\par
the impulse of your passions? Are you not planning the destruction of\par
innocence, the ruin of a Creature whom He formed in the mould of\par
Angels? If not of Dæmons, whose aid would you invoke to forward this\par
laudable design? Will the Seraphims protect it, conduct Antonia to your\par
arms, and sanction with their ministry your illicit pleasures? Absurd!\par
But I am not deceived, Ambrosio! It is not virtue which makes you\par
reject my offer: You WOULD accept it, but you dare not. ’Tis not the\par
crime which holds your hand, but the punishment; ’Tis not respect for\par
God which restrains you, but the terror of his vengeance! Fain would\par
you offend him in secret, but you tremble to profess yourself his Foe.\par
Now shame on the coward soul, which wants the courage either to be a\par
firm Friend or open Enemy!”\par
\par
“To look upon guilt with horror, Matilda, is in itself a merit: In this\par
respect I glory to confess myself a Coward. Though my passions have\par
made me deviate from her laws, I still feel in my heart an innate love\par
of virtue. But it ill becomes you to tax me with my perjury: You, who\par
first seduced me to violate my vows; You, who first rouzed my sleeping\par
vices, made me feel the weight of Religion’s chains, and bad me be\par
convinced that guilt had pleasures. Yet though my principles have\par
yielded to the force of temperament, I still have sufficient grace to\par
shudder at Sorcery, and avoid a crime so monstrous, so unpardonable!”\par
\par
“Unpardonable, say you? Where then is your constant boast of the\par
Almighty’s infinite mercy? Has He of late set bounds to it? Receives He\par
no longer a Sinner with joy? You injure him, Ambrosio; You will always\par
have time to repent, and He have goodness to forgive. Afford him a\par
glorious opportunity to exert that goodness: The greater your crime,\par
the greater his merit in pardoning. Away then with these childish\par
scruples: Be persuaded to your good, and follow me to the Sepulchre.”\par
\par
“Oh! cease, Matilda! That scoffing tone, that bold and impious\par
language, is horrible in every mouth, but most so in a Woman’s. Let us\par
drop a conversation which excites no other sentiments than horror and\par
disgust. I will not follow you to the Sepulchre, or accept the services\par
of your infernal Agents. Antonia shall be mine, but mine by human\par
means.”\par
\par
“Then yours She will never be! You are banished her presence; Her\par
Mother has opened her eyes to your designs, and She is now upon her\par
guard against them. Nay more, She loves another. A Youth of\par
distinguished merit possesses her heart, and unless you interfere, a\par
few days will make her his Bride. This intelligence was brought me by\par
my invisible Servants, to whom I had recourse on first perceiving your\par
indifference. They watched your every action, related to me all that\par
past at Elvira’s, and inspired me with the idea of favouring your\par
designs. Their reports have been my only comfort. Though you shunned my\par
presence, all your proceedings were known to me: Nay, I was constantly\par
with you in some degree, thanks to this precious gift!”\par
\par
With these words She drew from beneath her habit a mirror of polished\par
steel, the borders of which were marked with various strange and\par
unknown characters.\par
\par
“Amidst all my sorrows, amidst all my regrets for your coldness, I was\par
sustained from despair by the virtues of this Talisman. On pronouncing\par
certain words, the Person appears in it on whom the Observer’s thoughts\par
are bent: thus though _I_ was exiled from _your_ sight, you, Ambrosio,\par
were ever present to mine.”\par
\par
The Friar’s curiosity was excited strongly.\par
\par
“What you relate is incredible! Matilda, are you not amusing yourself\par
with my credulity?”\par
\par
“Be your own eyes the Judge.”\par
\par
She put the Mirror into his hand. Curiosity induced him to take it, and\par
Love, to wish that Antonia might appear. Matilda pronounced the magic\par
words. Immediately, a thick smoke rose from the characters traced upon\par
the borders, and spread itself over the surface. It dispersed again\par
gradually; A confused mixture of colours and images presented\par
themselves to the Friar’s eyes, which at length arranging themselves in\par
their proper places, He beheld in miniature Antonia’s lovely form.\par
\par
The scene was a small closet belonging to her apartment. She was\par
undressing to bathe herself. The long tresses of her hair were already\par
bound up. The amorous Monk had full opportunity to observe the\par
voluptuous contours and admirable symmetry of her person. She threw off\par
her last garment, and advancing to the Bath prepared for her, She put\par
her foot into the water. It struck cold, and She drew it back again.\par
Though unconscious of being observed, an inbred sense of modesty\par
induced her to veil her charms; and She stood hesitating upon the\par
brink, in the attitude of the Venus de Medicis. At this moment a tame\par
Linnet flew towards her, nestled its head between her breasts, and\par
nibbled them in wanton play. The smiling Antonia strove in vain to\par
shake off the Bird, and at length raised her hands to drive it from its\par
delightful harbour. Ambrosio could bear no more: His desires were\par
worked up to phrenzy.\par
\par
“I yield!” He cried, dashing the mirror upon the ground: “Matilda, I\par
follow you! Do with me what you will!”\par
\par
She waited not to hear his consent repeated. It was already midnight.\par
She flew to her Cell, and soon returned with her little basket and the\par
Key of the Cemetery, which had remained in her possession since her\par
first visit to the Vaults. She gave the Monk no time for reflection.\par
\par
“Come!” She said, and took his hand; “Follow me, and witness the\par
effects of your resolve!”\par
\par
This said, She drew him hastily along. They passed into the\par
Burying-ground unobserved, opened the door of the Sepulchre, and found\par
themselves at the head of the subterraneous Staircase. As yet the beams\par
of the full Moon had guided their steps, but that resource now failed\par
them. Matilda had neglected to provide herself with a Lamp. Still\par
holding Ambrosio’s hand She descended the marble steps; But the\par
profound obscurity with which they were overspread obliged them to walk\par
slow and cautiously.\par
\par
“You tremble!” said Matilda to her Companion; “Fear not; The destined\par
spot is near.”\par
\par
They reached the foot of the Staircase, and continued to proceed,\par
feeling their way along the Walls. On turning a corner suddenly, they\par
descried faint gleams of light which seemed burning at a distance.\par
Thither they bent their steps: The rays proceeded from a small\par
sepulchral Lamp which flamed unceasingly before the Statue of St.\par
Clare. It tinged with dim and cheerless beams the massy Columns which\par
supported the Roof, but was too feeble to dissipate the thick gloom in\par
which the Vaults above were buried.\par
\par
Matilda took the Lamp.\par
\par
“Wait for me!” said She to the Friar; “In a few moments I am here\par
again.”\par
\par
With these words She hastened into one of the passages which branched\par
in various directions from this spot, and formed a sort of Labyrinth.\par
Ambrosio was now left alone: Darkness the most profound surrounded him,\par
and encouraged the doubts which began to revive in his bosom. He had\par
been hurried away by the delirium of the moment: The shame of betraying\par
his terrors, while in Matilda’s presence, had induced him to repress\par
them; But now that he was abandoned to himself, they resumed their\par
former ascendancy. He trembled at the scene which He was soon to\par
witness. He knew not how far the delusions of Magic might operate upon\par
his mind, and possibly might force him to some deed whose commission\par
would make the breach between himself and Heaven irreparable. In this\par
fearful dilemma, He would have implored God’s assistance, but was\par
conscious that He had forfeited all claim to such protection. Gladly\par
would He have returned to the Abbey; But as He had past through\par
innumerable Caverns and winding passages, the attempt of regaining the\par
Stairs was hopeless. His fate was determined: No possibility of escape\par
presented itself: He therefore combated his apprehensions, and called\par
every argument to his succour, which might enable him to support the\par
trying scene with fortitude. He reflected that Antonia would be the\par
reward of his daring: He inflamed his imagination by enumerating her\par
charms. He persuaded himself that (as Matilda had observed), He always\par
should have time sufficient for repentance, and that as He employed\par
_her_ assistance, not that of the Dæmons, the crime of Sorcery could\par
not be laid to his charge. He had read much respecting witchcraft: He\par
understood that unless a formal Act was signed renouncing his claim to\par
salvation, Satan would have no power over him. He was fully determined\par
not to execute any such act, whatever threats might be used, or\par
advantages held out to him.\par
\par
Such were his meditations while waiting for Matilda. They were\par
interrupted by a low murmur which seemed at no great distance from him.\par
He was startled. He listened. Some minutes past in silence, after which\par
the murmur was repeated. It appeared to be the groaning of one in pain.\par
In any other situation, this circumstance would only have excited his\par
attention and curiosity:\par
\par
In the present, his predominant sensation was that of terror. His\par
imagination totally engrossed by the ideas of sorcery and Spirits, He\par
fancied that some unquiet Ghost was wandering near him; or else that\par
Matilda had fallen a Victim to her presumption, and was perishing under\par
the cruel fangs of the Dæmons. The noise seemed not to approach, but\par
continued to be heard at intervals. Sometimes it became more audible,\par
doubtless as the sufferings of the person who uttered the groans became\par
more acute and insupportable. Ambrosio now and then thought that He\par
could distinguish accents; and once in particular He was almost\par
convinced that He heard a faint voice exclaim,\par
\par
“God! Oh! God! No hope! No succour!”\par
\par
Yet deeper groans followed these words. They died away gradually, and\par
universal silence again prevailed.\par
\par
“What can this mean?” thought the bewildered Monk.\par
\par
At that moment an idea which flashed into his mind, almost petrified\par
him with horror. He started, and shuddered at himself.\par
\par
“Should it be possible!” He groaned involuntarily; “Should it but be\par
possible, Oh! what a Monster am I!”\par
\par
He wished to resolve his doubts, and to repair his fault, if it were\par
not too late already: But these generous and compassionate sentiments\par
were soon put to flight by the return of Matilda. He forgot the\par
groaning Sufferer, and remembered nothing but the danger and\par
embarrassment of his own situation. The light of the returning Lamp\par
gilded the walls, and in a few moments after Matilda stood beside him.\par
She had quitted her religious habit: She was now cloathed in a long\par
sable Robe, on which was traced in gold embroidery a variety of unknown\par
characters: It was fastened by a girdle of precious stones, in which\par
was fixed a poignard. Her neck and arms were uncovered. In her hand She\par
bore a golden wand. Her hair was loose and flowed wildly upon her\par
shoulders; Her eyes sparkled with terrific expression; and her whole\par
Demeanour was calculated to inspire the beholder with awe and\par
admiration.\par
\par
“Follow me!” She said to the Monk in a low and solemn voice; “All is\par
ready!”\par
\par
His limbs trembled, while He obeyed her. She led him through various\par
narrow passages; and on every side as they past along, the beams of the\par
Lamp displayed none but the most revolting objects; Skulls, Bones,\par
Graves, and Images whose eyes seemed to glare on them with horror and\par
surprize. At length they reached a spacious Cavern, whose lofty roof\par
the eye sought in vain to discover. A profound obscurity hovered\par
through the void. Damp vapours struck cold to the Friar’s heart; and He\par
listened sadly to the blast while it howled along the lonely Vaults.\par
Here Matilda stopped. She turned to Ambrosio. His cheeks and lips were\par
pale with apprehension. By a glance of mingled scorn and anger She\par
reproved his pusillanimity, but She spoke not. She placed the Lamp upon\par
the ground, near the Basket. She motioned that Ambrosio should be\par
silent, and began the mysterious rites. She drew a circle round him,\par
another round herself, and then taking a small Phial from the Basket,\par
poured a few drops upon the ground before her. She bent over the place,\par
muttered some indistinct sentences, and immediately a pale sulphurous\par
flame arose from the ground. It increased by degrees, and at length\par
spread its waves over the whole surface, the circles alone excepted in\par
which stood Matilda and the Monk. It then ascended the huge Columns of\par
unhewn stone, glided along the roof, and formed the Cavern into an\par
immense chamber totally covered with blue trembling fire. It emitted no\par
heat: On the contrary, the extreme chillness of the place seemed to\par
augment with every moment. Matilda continued her incantations: At\par
intervals She took various articles from the Basket, the nature and\par
name of most of which were unknown to the Friar: But among the few\par
which He distinguished, He particularly observed three human fingers,\par
and an Agnus Dei which She broke in pieces. She threw them all into the\par
flames which burned before her, and they were instantly consumed.\par
\par
The Monk beheld her with anxious curiosity. Suddenly She uttered a loud\par
and piercing shriek. She appeared to be seized with an access of\par
delirium; She tore her hair, beat her bosom, used the most frantic\par
gestures, and drawing the poignard from her girdle plunged it into her\par
left arm. The blood gushed out plentifully, and as She stood on the\par
brink of the circle, She took care that it should fall on the outside.\par
The flames retired from the spot on which the blood was pouring. A\par
volume of dark clouds rose slowly from the ensanguined earth, and\par
ascended gradually, till it reached the vault of the Cavern. At the\par
same time a clap of thunder was heard: The echo pealed fearfully along\par
the subterraneous passages, and the ground shook beneath the feet of\par
the Enchantress.\par
\par
It was now that Ambrosio repented of his rashness. The solemn\par
singularity of the charm had prepared him for something strange and\par
horrible. He waited with fear for the Spirit’s appearance, whose coming\par
was announced by thunder and earthquakes. He looked wildly round him,\par
expecting that some dreadful Apparition would meet his eyes, the sight\par
of which would drive him mad. A cold shivering seized his body, and He\par
sank upon one knee, unable to support himself.\par
\par
“He comes!” exclaimed Matilda in a joyful accent.\par
\par
Ambrosio started, and expected the Dæmon with terror. What was his\par
surprize, when the Thunder ceasing to roll, a full strain of melodious\par
Music sounded in the air. At the same time the cloud dispersed, and He\par
beheld a Figure more beautiful than Fancy’s pencil ever drew. It was a\par
Youth seemingly scarce eighteen, the perfection of whose form and face\par
was unrivalled. He was perfectly naked: A bright Star sparkled upon his\par
forehead; Two crimson wings extended themselves from his shoulders; and\par
his silken locks were confined by a band of many-coloured fires, which\par
played round his head, formed themselves into a variety of figures, and\par
shone with a brilliance far surpassing that of precious Stones.\par
Circlets of Diamonds were fastened round his arms and ankles, and in\par
his right hand He bore a silver branch, imitating Myrtle. His form\par
shone with dazzling glory: He was surrounded by clouds of rose-coloured\par
light, and at the moment that He appeared, a refreshing air breathed\par
perfumes through the Cavern. Enchanted at a vision so contrary to his\par
expectations, Ambrosio gazed upon the Spirit with delight and wonder:\par
Yet however beautiful the Figure, He could not but remark a wildness in\par
the Dæmon’s eyes, and a mysterious melancholy impressed upon his\par
features, betraying the Fallen Angel, and inspiring the Spectators with\par
secret awe.\par
\par
The Music ceased. Matilda addressed herself to the Spirit: She spoke in\par
a language unintelligible to the Monk, and was answered in the same.\par
She seemed to insist upon something which the Dæmon was unwilling to\par
grant. He frequently darted upon Ambrosio angry glances, and at such\par
times the Friar’s heart sank within him. Matilda appeared to grow\par
incensed. She spoke in a loud and commanding tone, and her gestures\par
declared that She was threatening him with her vengeance. Her menaces\par
had the desired effect: The Spirit sank upon his knee, and with a\par
submissive air presented to her the branch of Myrtle. No sooner had She\par
received it, than the Music was again heard; A thick cloud spread\par
itself over the Apparition; The blue flames disappeared, and total\par
obscurity reigned through the Cave. The Abbot moved not from his place:\par
His faculties were all bound up in pleasure, anxiety, and surprize. At\par
length the darkness dispersing, He perceived Matilda standing near him\par
in her religious habit, with the Myrtle in her hand. No traces of the\par
incantation, and the Vaults were only illuminated by the faint rays of\par
the sepulchral Lamp.\par
\par
“I have succeeded,” said Matilda, “though with more difficulty than I\par
expected. Lucifer, whom I summoned to my assistance, was at first\par
unwilling to obey my commands: To enforce his compliance I was\par
constrained to have recourse to my strongest charms. They have produced\par
the desired effect, but I have engaged never more to invoke his agency\par
in your favour. Beware then, how you employ an opportunity which never\par
will return. My magic arts will now be of no use to you: In future you\par
can only hope for supernatural aid by invoking the Dæmons yourself, and\par
accepting the conditions of their service. This you will never do: You\par
want strength of mind to force them to obedience, and unless you pay\par
their established price, they will not be your voluntary Servants. In\par
this one instance they consent to obey you: I offer you the means of\par
enjoying your Mistress, and be careful not to lose the opportunity.\par
Receive this constellated Myrtle: While you bear this in your hand,\par
every door will fly open to you. It will procure you access tomorrow\par
night to Antonia’s chamber: Then breathe upon it thrice, pronounce her\par
name, and place it upon her pillow. A death-like slumber will\par
immediately seize upon her, and deprive her of the power of resisting\par
your attempts. Sleep will hold her till break of Morning. In this state\par
you may satisfy your desires without danger of being discovered; since\par
when daylight shall dispel the effects of the enchantment, Antonia will\par
perceive her dishonour, but be ignorant of the Ravisher. Be happy then,\par
my Ambrosio, and let this service convince you that my friendship is\par
disinterested and pure. The night must be near expiring: Let us return\par
to the Abbey, lest our absence should create surprize.”\par
\par
The Abbot received the talisman with silent gratitude. His ideas were\par
too much bewildered by the adventures of the night to permit his\par
expressing his thanks audibly, or indeed as yet to feel the whole value\par
of her present. Matilda took up her Lamp and Basket, and guided her\par
Companion from the mysterious Cavern. She restored the Lamp to its\par
former place, and continued her route in darkness, till She reached the\par
foot of the Staircase. The first beams of the rising Sun darting down\par
it facilitated the ascent. Matilda and the Abbot hastened out of the\par
Sepulchre, closed the door after them, and soon regained the Abbey’s\par
western Cloister. No one met them, and they retired unobserved to their\par
respective Cells.\par
\par
The confusion of Ambrosio’s mind now began to appease. He rejoiced in\par
the fortunate issue of his adventure, and reflecting upon the virtues\par
of the Myrtle, looked upon Antonia as already in his power. Imagination\par
retraced to him those secret charms betrayed to him by the Enchanted\par
Mirror, and He waited with impatience for the approach of midnight.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER VIII.\par
\par
\par
The crickets sing, and Man’s o’er-laboured sense\par
Repairs itself by rest: Our Tarquin thus\par
Did softly press the rushes, ere He wakened\par
The chastity He wounded—Cytherea,\par
How bravely thou becom’st thy bed! Fresh Lily!\par
And whiter than the sheets!\par
\par
CYMBELINE.\par
\par
All the researches of the Marquis de las Cisternas proved vain: Agnes\par
was lost to him for ever. Despair produced so violent an effect upon\par
his constitution, that the consequence was a long and severe illness.\par
This prevented him from visiting Elvira as He had intended; and She\par
being ignorant of the cause of his neglect, it gave her no trifling\par
uneasiness. His Sister’s death had prevented Lorenzo from communicating\par
to his Uncle his designs respecting Antonia: The injunctions of her\par
Mother forbad his presenting himself to her without the Duke’s consent;\par
and as She heard no more of him or his proposals, Elvira conjectured\par
that He had either met with a better match, or had been commanded to\par
give up all thoughts of her Daughter. Every day made her more uneasy\par
respecting Antonia’s fate: While She retained the Abbot’s protection,\par
She bore with fortitude the disappointment of her hopes with regard to\par
Lorenzo and the Marquis. That resource now failed her. She was\par
convinced that Ambrosio had meditated her Daughter’s ruin: And when She\par
reflected that her death would leave Antonia friendless and unprotected\par
in a world so base, so perfidious and depraved, her heart swelled with\par
the bitterness of apprehension. At such times She would sit for hours\par
gazing upon the lovely Girl; and seeming to listen to her innocent\par
prattle, while in reality her thoughts dwelt upon the sorrows into\par
which a moment would suffice to plunge her. Then She would clasp her in\par
her arms suddenly, lean her head upon her Daughter’s bosom, and bedew\par
it with her tears.\par
\par
An event was in preparation which, had She known it, would have\par
relieved her from her inquietude. Lorenzo now waited only for a\par
favourable opportunity to inform the Duke of his intended marriage:\par
However, a circumstance which occurred at this period, obliged him to\par
delay his explanation for a few days longer.\par
\par
Don Raymond’s malady seemed to gain ground. Lorenzo was constantly at\par
his bedside, and treated him with a tenderness truly fraternal. Both\par
the cause and effects of the disorder were highly afflicting to the\par
Brother of Agnes: yet Theodore’s grief was scarcely less sincere. That\par
amiable Boy quitted not his Master for a moment, and put every means in\par
practice to console and alleviate his sufferings. The Marquis had\par
conceived so rooted an affection for his deceased Mistress, that it was\par
evident to all that He never could survive her loss: Nothing could have\par
prevented him from sinking under his grief but the persuasion of her\par
being still alive, and in need of his assistance. Though convinced of\par
its falsehood, his Attendants encouraged him in a belief which formed\par
his only comfort. He was assured daily that fresh perquisitions were\par
making respecting the fate of Agnes: Stories were invented recounting\par
the various attempts made to get admittance into the Convent; and\par
circumstances were related which, though they did not promise her\par
absolute recovery, at least were sufficient to keep his hopes alive.\par
The Marquis constantly fell into the most terrible excess of passion\par
when informed of the failure of these supposed attempts. Still He would\par
not credit that the succeeding ones would have the same fate, but\par
flattered himself that the next would prove more fortunate.\par
\par
Theodore was the only one who exerted himself to realize his Master’s\par
Chimoeras. He was eternally busied in planning schemes for entering the\par
Convent, or at least of obtaining from the Nuns some intelligence of\par
Agnes. To execute these schemes was the only inducement which could\par
prevail on him to quit Don Raymond. He became a very Proteus, changing\par
his shape every day; but all his metamorphoses were to very little\par
purpose: He regularly returned to the Palace de las Cisternas without\par
any intelligence to confirm his Master’s hopes. One day He took it into\par
his head to disguise himself as a Beggar. He put a patch over his left\par
eye, took his Guitar in hand, and posted himself at the Gate of the\par
Convent.\par
\par
“If Agnes is really confined in the Convent,” thought He, “and hears my\par
voice, She will recollect it, and possibly may find means to let me\par
know that She is here.”\par
\par
With this idea He mingled with a crowd of Beggars who assembled daily\par
at the Gate of St. Clare to receive Soup, which the Nuns were\par
accustomed to distribute at twelve o’clock. All were provided with jugs\par
or bowls to carry it away; But as Theodore had no utensil of this kind,\par
He begged leave to eat his portion at the Convent door. This was\par
granted without difficulty: His sweet voice, and in spite of his\par
patched eye, his engaging countenance, won the heart of the good old\par
Porteress, who, aided by a Lay-Sister, was busied in serving to each\par
his Mess. Theodore was bad to stay till the Others should depart, and\par
promised that his request should then be granted. The Youth desired no\par
better, since it was not to eat Soup that He presented himself at the\par
Convent. He thanked the Porteress for her permission, retired from the\par
Door, and seating himself upon a large stone, amused himself in tuning\par
his Guitar while the Beggars were served.\par
\par
As soon as the Crowd was gone, Theodore was beckoned to the Gate, and\par
desired to come in. He obeyed with infinite readiness, but affected\par
great respect at passing the hallowed Threshold, and to be much daunted\par
by the presence of the Reverend Ladies. His feigned timidity flattered\par
the vanity of the Nuns, who endeavoured to reassure him. The Porteress\par
took him into her awn little Parlour: In the meanwhile, the Lay-Sister\par
went to the Kitchen, and soon returned with a double portion of Soup,\par
of better quality than what was given to the Beggars. His Hostess added\par
some fruits and confections from her own private store, and Both\par
encouraged the Youth to dine heartily. To all these attentions He\par
replied with much seeming gratitude, and abundance of blessings upon\par
his benefactresses. While He ate, the Nuns admired the delicacy of his\par
features, the beauty of his hair, and the sweetness and grace which\par
accompanied all his actions. They lamented to each other in whispers,\par
that so charming a Youth should be exposed to the seductions of the\par
World, and agreed, that He would be a worthy Pillar of the Catholic\par
Church. They concluded their conference by resolving that Heaven would\par
be rendered a real service if they entreated the Prioress to intercede\par
with Ambrosio for the Beggar’s admission into the order of Capuchins.\par
\par
This being determined, the Porteress, who was a person of great\par
influence in the Convent, posted away in all haste to the Domina’s\par
Cell. Here She made so flaming a narrative of Theodore’s merits that\par
the old Lady grew curious to see him. Accordingly, the Porteress was\par
commissioned to convey him to the Parlour grate. In the interim, the\par
supposed Beggar was sifting the Lay-Sister with respect to the fate of\par
Agnes: Her evidence only corroborated the Domina’s assertions. She said\par
that Agnes had been taken ill on returning from confession, had never\par
quitted her bed from that moment, and that She had herself been present\par
at the Funeral. She even attested having seen her dead body, and\par
assisted with her own hands in adjusting it upon the Bier. This account\par
discouraged Theodore: Yet as He had pushed the adventure so far, He\par
resolved to witness its conclusion.\par
\par
The Porteress now returned, and ordered him to follow her. He obeyed,\par
and was conducted into the Parlour, where the Lady Prioress was already\par
posted at the Grate. The Nuns surrounded her, who all flocked with\par
eagerness to a scene which promised some diversion. Theodore saluted\par
them with profound respect, and his presence had the power to smooth\par
for a moment even the stern brow of the Superior. She asked several\par
questions respecting his Parents, his religion, and what had reduced\par
him to a state of Beggary. To these demands his answers were perfectly\par
satisfactory and perfectly false. He was then asked his opinion of a\par
monastic life: He replied in terms of high estimation and respect for\par
it. Upon this, the Prioress told him that his obtaining an entrance\par
into a religious order was not impossible; that her recommendation\par
would not permit his poverty to be an obstacle, and that if She found\par
him deserving it, He might depend in future upon her protection.\par
Theodore assured her that to merit her favour would be his highest\par
ambition; and having ordered him to return next day, when She would\par
talk with him further, the Domina quitted the Parlour.\par
\par
The Nuns, whom respect for the Superior had till then kept silent, now\par
crowded all together to the Grate, and assailed the Youth with a\par
multitude of questions. He had already examined each with attention:\par
Alas! Agnes was not amongst them. The Nuns heaped question upon\par
question so thickly that it was scarcely possible for him to reply. One\par
asked where He was born, since his accent declared him to be a\par
Foreigner: Another wanted to know, why He wore a patch upon his left\par
eye: Sister Helena enquired whether He had not a Sister like him,\par
because She should like such a Companion; and Sister Rachael was fully\par
persuaded that the Brother would be the pleasanter Companion of the\par
Two. Theodore amused himself with retailing to the credulous Nuns for\par
truths all the strange stories which his imagination could invent. He\par
related to them his supposed adventures, and penetrated every Auditor\par
with astonishment, while He talked of Giants, Savages, Ship-wrecks, and\par
Islands inhabited\par
\par
“By anthropophagi, and men whose heads\par
Do grow beneath their shoulders,”\par
\par
\par
with many other circumstances to the full as remarkable. He said, that\par
He was born in Terra Incognita, was educated at an Hottentot\par
University, and had past two years among the Americans of Silesia.\par
\par
“For what regards the loss of my eye” said He, “it was a just\par
punishment upon me for disrespect to the Virgin, when I made my second\par
pilgrimage to Loretto. I stood near the Altar in the miraculous Chapel:\par
The Monks were proceeding to array the Statue in her best apparel. The\par
Pilgrims were ordered to close their eyes during this ceremony: But\par
though by nature extremely religious, curiosity was too powerful. At\par
the moment ..... I shall penetrate you with horror, reverend Ladies,\par
when I reveal my crime! .... At the moment that the Monks were changing\par
her shift, I ventured to open my left eye, and gave a little peep\par
towards the Statue. That look was my last! The Glory which surrounded\par
the Virgin was too great to be supported. I hastily shut my\par
sacrilegious eye, and never have been able to unclose it since!”\par
\par
At the relation of this miracle the Nuns all crossed themselves, and\par
promised to intercede with the blessed Virgin for the recovery of his\par
sight. They expressed their wonder at the extent of his travels, and at\par
the strange adventures which He had met with at so early an age. They\par
now remarked his Guitar, and enquired whether he was an adept in Music.\par
He replied with modesty that it was not for him to decide upon his\par
talents, but requested permission to appeal to them as Judges. This was\par
granted without difficulty.\par
\par
“But at least,” said the old Porteress, “take care not to sing any\par
thing profane.”\par
\par
“You may depend upon my discretion,” replied Theodore: “You shall hear\par
how dangerous it is for young Women to abandon themselves to their\par
passions, illustrated by the adventure of a Damsel who fell suddenly in\par
love with an unknown Knight.”\par
\par
“But is the adventure true?” enquired the Porteress.\par
\par
“Every word of it. It happened in Denmark, and the Heroine was thought\par
so beautiful that She was known by no other name but that of ‘the\par
lovely Maid’.”\par
\par
“In Denmark, say you?” mumbled an old Nun; “Are not the People all\par
Blacks in Denmark?”\par
\par
“By no means, reverend Lady; They are of a delicate pea-green with\par
flame-coloured hair and whiskers.”\par
\par
“Mother of God! Pea-green?” exclaimed Sister Helena; “Oh! ’tis\par
impossible!”\par
\par
“Impossible?” said the Porteress with a look of contempt and\par
exultation: “Not at all: When I was a young Woman, I remember seeing\par
several of them myself.”\par
\par
Theodore now put his instrument in proper order. He had read the story\par
of a King of England whose prison was discovered by a Minstrel; and He\par
hoped that the same scheme would enable him to discover Agnes, should\par
She be in the Convent. He chose a Ballad which She had taught him\par
herself in the Castle of Lindenberg: She might possibly catch the\par
sound, and He hoped to hear her replying to some of the Stanzas. His\par
Guitar was now in tune, and He prepared to strike it.\par
\par
“But before I begin,” said He “it is necessary to inform you, Ladies,\par
that this same Denmark is terribly infested by Sorcerers, Witches, and\par
Evil Spirits. Every element possesses its appropriate Dæmons. The Woods\par
are haunted by a malignant power, called ‘the Erl- or Oak-King:’ He it\par
is who blights the Trees, spoils the Harvest, and commands the Imps and\par
Goblins: He appears in the form of an old Man of majestic figure, with\par
a golden Crown and long white beard: His principal amusement is to\par
entice young Children from their Parents, and as soon as He gets them\par
into his Cave, He tears them into a thousand pieces—The Rivers are\par
governed by another Fiend, called ‘the Water-King:’ His province is to\par
agitate the deep, occasion ship-wrecks, and drag the drowning Sailors\par
beneath the waves: He wears the appearance of a Warrior, and employs\par
himself in luring young Virgins into his snare: What He does with them,\par
when He catches them in the water, Reverend Ladies, I leave for you to\par
imagine—‘The Fire-King’ seems to be a Man all formed of flames: He\par
raises the Meteors and wandering lights which beguile Travellers into\par
ponds and marshes, and He directs the lightning where it may do most\par
mischief—The last of these elementary Dæmons is called ‘the\par
Cloud-King;’ His figure is that of a beautiful Youth, and He is\par
distinguished by two large sable Wings: Though his outside is so\par
enchanting, He is not a bit better disposed than the Others: He is\par
continually employed in raising Storms, tearing up Forests by the\par
roots, and blowing Castles and Convents about the ears of their\par
Inhabitants. The First has a Daughter, who is Queen of the Elves and\par
Fairies; The Second has a Mother, who is a powerful Enchantress:\par
Neither of these Ladies are worth more than the Gentlemen: I do not\par
remember to have heard any family assigned to the two other Dæmons, but\par
at present I have no business with any of them except the Fiend of the\par
Waters. He is the Hero of my Ballad; but I thought it necessary before\par
I began, to give you some account of his proceedings—”\par
\par
Theodore then played a short symphony; After which, stretching his\par
voice to its utmost extent to facilitate its reaching the ear of Agnes,\par
He sang the following Stanzas.\par
\par
THE WATER-KING\par
A DANISH BALLAD\par
\par
\par
With gentle murmur flowed the tide,\par
While by the fragrant flowery side\par
The lovely Maid with carols gay\par
To Mary’s church pursued her way.\par
\par
The Water-Fiend’s malignant eye\par
Along the Banks beheld her hie;\par
Straight to his Mother-witch he sped,\par
And thus in suppliant accents said:\par
\par
“Oh! Mother! Mother! now advise,\par
How I may yonder Maid surprize:\par
Oh! Mother! Mother! Now explain,\par
How I may yonder Maid obtain.”\par
\par
The Witch She gave him armour white;\par
She formed him like a gallant Knight;\par
Of water clear next made her hand\par
A Steed, whose housings were of sand.\par
\par
The Water-King then swift He went;\par
To Mary’s Church his steps He bent:\par
He bound his Courser to the Door,\par
And paced the Church-yard three times four.\par
\par
His Courser to the door bound He,\par
And paced the Church-yard four time three:\par
Then hastened up the Aisle, where all\par
The People flocked, both great and small.\par
\par
The Priest said, as the Knight drew near,\par
“And wherefore comes the white Chief here?”\par
The lovely Maid She smiled aside;\par
“Oh! would I were the white Chief’s Bride!”\par
\par
He stept o’er Benches one and two;\par
“Oh! lovely Maid, I die for You!”\par
He stept o’er Benches two and three;\par
“Oh! lovely Maiden, go with me!”\par
\par
Then sweet She smiled, the lovely Maid,\par
And while She gave her hand, She said,\par
“Betide me joy, betide me woe,\par
O’er Hill, o’er dale, with thee I go.”\par
\par
The Priest their hands together joins:\par
They dance, while clear the moon-beam shines;\par
And little thinks the Maiden bright,\par
Her Partner is the Water-spright.\par
\par
Oh! had some spirit deigned to sing,\par
“Your Partner is the Water-King!”\par
The Maid had fear and hate confest,\par
And cursed the hand which then She prest.\par
\par
But nothing giving cause to think,\par
How near She strayed to danger’s brink,\par
Still on She went, and hand in hand\par
The Lovers reached the yellow sand.\par
\par
“Ascend this Steed with me, my Dear;\par
We needs must cross the streamlet here;\par
Ride boldly in; It is not deep;\par
The winds are hushed, the billows sleep.”\par
\par
Thus spoke the Water-King. The Maid\par
Her Traitor-Bride-groom’s wish obeyed:\par
And soon She saw the Courser lave\par
Delighted in his parent wave.\par
\par
“Stop! Stop! my Love! The waters blue\par
E’en now my shrinking foot bedew!”\par
“Oh! lay aside your fears, sweet Heart!\par
We now have reached the deepest part.”\par
\par
“Stop! Stop! my Love! For now I see\par
The waters rise above my knee.”\par
“Oh! lay aside your fears, sweet Heart!\par
We now have reached the deepest part.”\par
\par
“Stop! Stop! for God’s sake, stop! For Oh!\par
The waters o’er my bosom flow!”—\par
Scarce was the word pronounced, when Knight\par
And Courser vanished from her sight.\par
\par
She shrieks, but shrieks in vain; for high\par
The wild winds rising dull the cry;\par
The Fiend exults; The Billows dash,\par
And o’er their hapless Victim wash.\par
\par
Three times while struggling with the stream,\par
The lovely Maid was heard to scream;\par
But when the Tempest’s rage was o’er,\par
The lovely Maid was seen no more.\par
\par
Warned by this Tale, ye Damsels fair,\par
To whom you give your love beware!\par
Believe not every handsome Knight,\par
And dance not with the Water-Spright!\par
\par
\par
The Youth ceased to sing. The Nuns were delighted with the sweetness of\par
his voice and masterly manner of touching the Instrument: But however\par
acceptable this applause would have been at any other time, at present\par
it was insipid to Theodore. His artifice had not succeeded. He paused\par
in vain between the Stanzas: No voice replied to his, and He abandoned\par
the hope of equalling Blondel.\par
\par
The Convent Bell now warned the Nuns that it was time to assemble in\par
the Refectory. They were obliged to quit the Grate; They thanked the\par
Youth for the entertainment which his Music had afforded them, and\par
charged him to return the next day. This He promised: The Nuns, to give\par
him the greater inclination to keep his word, told him that He might\par
always depend upon the Convent for his meals, and each of them made him\par
some little present. One gave him a box of sweetmeats; Another, an\par
Agnus Dei; Some brought reliques of Saints, waxen Images, and\par
consecrated Crosses; and Others presented him with pieces of those\par
works in which the Religious excel, such as embroidery, artificial\par
flowers, lace, and needlework. All these He was advised to sell, in\par
order to put himself into better case; and He was assured that it would\par
be easy to dispose of them, since the Spaniards hold the performances\par
of the Nuns in high estimation. Having received these gifts with\par
seeming respect and gratitude, He remarked that, having no Basket, He\par
knew not how to convey them away. Several of the Nuns were hastening in\par
search of one, when they were stopped by the return of an elderly\par
Woman, whom Theodore had not till then observed: Her mild countenance,\par
and respectable air prejudiced him immediately in her favour.\par
\par
“Hah!” said the Porteress; “Here comes the Mother St. Ursula with a\par
Basket.”\par
\par
The Nun approached the Grate, and presented the Basket to Theodore: It\par
was of willow, lined with blue satin, and upon the four sides were\par
painted scenes from the legend of St. Genevieve.\par
\par
“Here is my gift,” said She, as She gave it into his hand; “Good Youth,\par
despise it not; Though its value seems insignificant, it has many\par
hidden virtues.”\par
\par
She accompanied these words with an expressive look. It was not lost\par
upon Theodore; In receiving the present, He drew as near the Grate as\par
possible.\par
\par
“Agnes!” She whispered in a voice scarcely intelligible. Theodore,\par
however, caught the sound: He concluded that some mystery was concealed\par
in the Basket, and his heart beat with impatience and joy. At this\par
moment the Domina returned. Her air was gloomy and frowning, and She\par
looked if possible more stern than ever.\par
\par
“Mother St. Ursula, I would speak with you in private.”\par
\par
The Nun changed colour, and was evidently disconcerted.\par
\par
“With me?” She replied in a faltering voice.\par
\par
The Domina motioned that She must follow her, and retired. The Mother\par
St. Ursula obeyed her; Soon after, the Refectory Bell ringing a second\par
time, the Nuns quitted the Grate, and Theodore was left at liberty to\par
carry off his prize. Delighted that at length He had obtained some\par
intelligence for the Marquis, He flew rather than ran, till He reached\par
the Hotel de las Cisternas. In a few minutes He stood by his Master’s\par
Bed with the Basket in his hand. Lorenzo was in the chamber,\par
endeavouring to reconcile his Friend to a misfortune which He felt\par
himself but too severely. Theodore related his adventure, and the hopes\par
which had been created by the Mother St. Ursula’s gift. The Marquis\par
started from his pillow: That fire which since the death of Agnes had\par
been extinguished, now revived in his bosom, and his eyes sparkled with\par
the eagerness of expectation. The emotions which Lorenzo’s countenance\par
betrayed, were scarcely weaker, and He waited with inexpressible\par
impatience for the solution of this mystery. Raymond caught the basket\par
from the hands of his Page: He emptied the contents upon the bed, and\par
examined them with minute attention. He hoped that a letter would be\par
found at the bottom; Nothing of the kind appeared. The search was\par
resumed, and still with no better success. At length Don Raymond\par
observed that one corner of the blue satin lining was unripped; He tore\par
it open hastily, and drew forth a small scrap of paper neither folded\par
or sealed. It was addressed to the Marquis de las Cisternas, and the\par
contents were as follows:\par
\par
“Having recognised your Page, I venture to send these few lines.\par
Procure an order from the Cardinal-Duke for seizing my Person, and that\par
of the Domina; But let it not be executed till Friday at midnight. It\par
is the Festival of St. Clare: There will be a procession of Nuns by\par
torch-light, and I shall be among them. Beware not to let your\par
intention be known: Should a syllable be dropt to excite the Domina’s\par
suspicions, you will never hear of me more. Be cautious, if you prize\par
the memory of Agnes, and wish to punish her Assassins. I have that to\par
tell, will freeze your blood with horror.\par
\par
\par
“ST. URSULA.”\par
\par
\par
No sooner had the Marquis read the note than He fell back upon his\par
pillow deprived of sense or motion. The hope failed him which till now\par
had supported his existence; and these lines convinced him but too\par
positively that Agnes was indeed no more. Lorenzo felt this\par
circumstance less forcibly, since it had always been his idea that his\par
Sister had perished by unfair means. When He found by the Mother St.\par
Ursula’s letter how true were his suspicions, the confirmation excited\par
no other sentiment in his bosom than a wish to punish the Murderers as\par
they deserved. It was no easy task to recall the Marquis to himself. As\par
soon as He recovered his speech, He broke out into execrations against\par
the Assassins of his Beloved, and vowed to take upon them a signal\par
vengeance. He continued to rave and torment himself with impotent\par
passion till his constitution, enfeebled by grief and illness, could\par
support itself no longer, and He relapsed into insensibility. His\par
melancholy situation sincerely affected Lorenzo, who would willingly\par
have remained in the apartment of his Friend; But other cares now\par
demanded his presence. It was necessary to procure the order for\par
seizing the Prioress of St. Clare. For this purpose, having committed\par
Raymond to the care of the best Physicians in Madrid, He quitted the\par
Hotel de las Cisternas, and bent his course towards the Palace of the\par
Cardinal-Duke.\par
\par
His disappointment was excessive, when He found that affairs of State\par
had obliged the Cardinal to set out for a distant Province.\par
\par
It wanted but five to Friday: Yet by travelling day and night, He hoped\par
to return in time for the Pilgrimage of St. Clare. In this He\par
succeeded. He found the Cardinal-Duke; and represented to him the\par
supposed culpability of the Prioress, as also the violent effects which\par
it had produced upon Don Raymond. He could have used no argument so\par
forcible as this last. Of all his Nephews, the Marquis was the only one\par
to whom the Cardinal-Duke was sincerely attached: He perfectly doated\par
upon him, and the Prioress could have committed no greater crime in his\par
eyes than to have endangered the life of the Marquis. Consequently, He\par
granted the order of arrest without difficulty: He also gave Lorenzo a\par
letter to a principal Officer of the Inquisition, desiring him to see\par
his mandate executed. Furnished with these papers, Medina hastened back\par
to Madrid, which He reached on the Friday a few hours before dark. He\par
found the Marquis somewhat easier, but so weak and exhausted that\par
without great exertion He could neither speak or more. Having past an\par
hour by his Bedside, Lorenzo left him to communicate his design to his\par
Uncle, as also to give Don Ramirez de Mello the Cardinal’s letter. The\par
First was petrified with horror when He learnt the fate of his unhappy\par
Niece: He encouraged Lorenzo to punish her Assassins, and engaged to\par
accompany him at night to St. Clare’s Convent. Don Ramirez promised his\par
firmest support, and selected a band of trusty Archers to prevent\par
opposition on the part of the Populace.\par
\par
But while Lorenzo was anxious to unmask one religious Hypocrite, He was\par
unconscious of the sorrows prepared for him by Another. Aided by\par
Matilda’s infernal Agents, Ambrosio had resolved upon the innocent\par
Antonia’s ruin. The moment destined to be so fatal to her arrived. She\par
had taken leave of her Mother for the night.\par
\par
As She kissed her, She felt an unusual despondency infuse itself into\par
her bosom. She left her, and returned to her instantly, threw herself\par
into her maternal arms, and bathed her cheek with tears: She felt\par
uneasy at quitting her, and a secret presentiment assured her that\par
never must they meet again. Elvira observed, and tried to laugh her out\par
of this childish prejudice: She chid her mildly for encouraging such\par
ungrounded sadness, and warned her how dangerous it was to encourage\par
such ideas.\par
\par
To all her remonstrances She received no other answer than,\par
\par
“Mother! Dear Mother! Oh! would to God, it were Morning!”\par
\par
Elvira, whose inquietude respecting her Daughter was a great obstacle\par
to her perfect reestablishment, was still labouring under the effects\par
of her late severe illness. She was this Evening more than usually\par
indisposed, and retired to bed before her accustomed hour. Antonia\par
withdrew from her Mother’s chamber with regret, and till the Door\par
closed, kept her eyes fixed upon her with melancholy expression. She\par
retired to her own apartment; Her heart was filled with bitterness: It\par
seemed to her that all her prospects were blasted, and the world\par
contained nothing for which it was worth existing. She sank into a\par
Chair, reclined her head upon her arm, and gazed upon the floor with a\par
vacant stare, while the most gloomy images floated before her fancy.\par
She was still in this state of insensibility when She was disturbed by\par
hearing a strain of soft Music breathed beneath her window. She rose,\par
drew near the Casement, and opened it to hear it more distinctly.\par
Having thrown her veil over her face, She ventured to look out. By the\par
light of the Moon She perceived several Men below with Guitars and\par
Lutes in their hands; and at a little distance from them stood Another\par
wrapped in his cloak, whose stature and appearance bore a strong\par
resemblance to Lorenzo’s. She was not deceived in this conjecture. It\par
was indeed Lorenzo himself, who bound by his word not to present\par
himself to Antonia without his Uncle’s consent, endeavoured by\par
occasional Serenades, to convince his Mistress that his attachment\par
still existed. His stratagem had not the desired effect. Antonia was\par
far from supposing that this nightly music was intended as a compliment\par
to her: She was too modest to think herself worthy such attentions; and\par
concluding them to be addressed to some neighbouring Lady, She grieved\par
to find that they were offered by Lorenzo.\par
\par
The air which was played, was plaintive and melodious. It accorded with\par
the state of Antonia’s mind, and She listened with pleasure. After a\par
symphony of some length, it was succeeded by the sound of voices, and\par
Antonia distinguished the following words.\par
\par
SERENADE\par
\par
\par
Chorus\par
\par
\par
Oh! Breathe in gentle strain, my Lyre!\par
’Tis here that Beauty loves to rest:\par
Describe the pangs of fond desire,\par
Which rend a faithful Lover’s breast.\par
\par
\par
Song\par
\par
\par
In every heart to find a Slave,\par
In every Soul to fix his reign,\par
In bonds to lead the wise and brave,\par
And make the Captives kiss his chain,\par
Such is the power of Love, and Oh!\par
I grieve so well Love’s power to know.\par
\par
In sighs to pass the live-long day,\par
To taste a short and broken sleep,\par
For one dear Object far away,\par
All others scorned, to watch and weep,\par
Such are the pains of Love, and Oh!\par
I grieve so well Love’s pains to know!\par
\par
To read consent in virgin eyes,\par
To press the lip ne’er prest till then\par
To hear the sigh of transport rise,\par
And kiss, and kiss, and kiss again,\par
Such are thy pleasures, Love, But Oh!\par
When shall my heart thy pleasures know?\par
\par
\par
Chorus\par
\par
\par
Now hush, my Lyre! My voice be still!\par
Sleep, gentle Maid! May fond desire\par
With amorous thoughts thy visions fill,\par
Though still my voice, and hushed my Lyre.\par
\par
\par
The Music ceased: The Performers dispersed, and silence prevailed\par
through the Street. Antonia quitted the window with regret: She as\par
usual recommended herself to the protection of St. Rosolia, said her\par
accustomed prayers, and retired to bed. Sleep was not long absent, and\par
his presence relieved her from her terrors and inquietude.\par
\par
It was almost two o’clock before the lustful Monk ventured to bend his\par
steps towards Antonia’s dwelling. It has been already mentioned that\par
the Abbey was at no great distance from the Strada di San Iago. He\par
reached the House unobserved. Here He stopped, and hesitated for a\par
moment. He reflected on the enormity of the crime, the consequences of\par
a discovery, and the probability, after what had passed, of Elvira’s\par
suspecting him to be her Daughter’s Ravisher: On the other hand it was\par
suggested that She could do no more than suspect; that no proofs of his\par
guilt could be produced; that it would seem impossible for the rape to\par
have been committed without Antonia’s knowing when, where, or by whom;\par
and finally, He believed that his fame was too firmly established to be\par
shaken by the unsupported accusations of two unknown Women. This latter\par
argument was perfectly false: He knew not how uncertain is the air of\par
popular applause, and that a moment suffices to make him today the\par
detestation of the world, who yesterday was its Idol. The result of the\par
Monk’s deliberations was that He should proceed in his enterprize. He\par
ascended the steps leading to the House. No sooner did He touch the\par
door with the silver Myrtle, than it flew open, and presented him with\par
a free passage. He entered, and the door closed after him of its own\par
accord.\par
\par
Guided by the moonbeams, He proceeded up the Staircase with slow and\par
cautious steps. He looked round him every moment with apprehension and\par
anxiety. He saw a Spy in every shadow, and heard a voice in every\par
murmur of the night breeze. Consciousness of the guilty business on\par
which He was employed appalled his heart, and rendered it more timid\par
than a Woman’s. Yet still He proceeded. He reached the door of\par
Antonia’s chamber. He stopped, and listened. All was hushed within. The\par
total silence persuaded him that his intended Victim was retired to\par
rest, and He ventured to lift up the Latch. The door was fastened, and\par
resisted his efforts: But no sooner was it touched by the Talisman,\par
than the Bolt flew back. The Ravisher stept on, and found himself in\par
the chamber, where slept the innocent Girl, unconscious how dangerous a\par
Visitor was drawing near her Couch. The door closed after him, and the\par
Bolt shot again into its fastening.\par
\par
Ambrosio advanced with precaution. He took care that not a board should\par
creak under his foot, and held in his breath as He approached the Bed.\par
His first attention was to perform the magic ceremony, as Matilda had\par
charged him: He breathed thrice upon the silver Myrtle, pronounced over\par
it Antonia’s name, and laid it upon her pillow. The effects which it\par
had already produced permitted not his doubting its success in\par
prolonging the slumbers of his devoted Mistress. No sooner was the\par
enchantment performed than He considered her to be absolutely in his\par
power, and his eyes flamed with lust and impatience. He now ventured to\par
cast a glance upon the sleeping Beauty. A single Lamp, burning before\par
the Statue of St. Rosolia, shed a faint light through the room, and\par
permitted him to examine all the charms of the lovely Object before\par
him. The heat of the weather had obliged her to throw off part of the\par
Bed-cloathes: Those which still covered her, Ambrosio’s insolent hand\par
hastened to remove. She lay with her cheek reclining upon one ivory\par
arm; The Other rested on the side of the Bed with graceful indolence. A\par
few tresses of her hair had escaped from beneath the Muslin which\par
confined the rest, and fell carelessly over her bosom, as it heaved\par
with slow and regular suspiration. The warm air had spread her cheek\par
with higher colour than usual. A smile inexpressibly sweet played round\par
her ripe and coral lips, from which every now and then escaped a gentle\par
sigh or an half-pronounced sentence. An air of enchanting innocence and\par
candour pervaded her whole form; and there was a sort of modesty in her\par
very nakedness which added fresh stings to the desires of the lustful\par
Monk.\par
\par
He remained for some moments devouring those charms with his eyes which\par
soon were to be subjected to his ill-regulated passions. Her mouth\par
half-opened seemed to solicit a kiss: He bent over her; he joined his\par
lips to hers, and drew in the fragrance of her breath with rapture.\par
This momentary pleasure increased his longing for still greater. His\par
desires were raised to that frantic height by which Brutes are\par
agitated. He resolved not to delay for one instant longer the\par
accomplishment of his wishes, and hastily proceeded to tear off those\par
garments which impeded the gratification of his lust.\par
\par
“Gracious God!” exclaimed a voice behind him; “Am I not deceived?\par
\par
Is not this an illusion?”\par
\par
Terror, confusion, and disappointment accompanied these words, as they\par
struck Ambrosio’s hearing. He started, and turned towards it. Elvira\par
stood at the door of the chamber, and regarded the Monk with looks of\par
surprize and detestation.\par
\par
A frightful dream had represented to her Antonia on the verge of a\par
precipice. She saw her trembling on the brink: Every moment seemed to\par
threaten her fall, and She heard her exclaim with shrieks, “Save me,\par
Mother! Save me!—Yet a moment, and it will be too late!” Elvira woke in\par
terror. The vision had made too strong an impression upon her mind, to\par
permit her resting till assured of her Daughter’s safety. She hastily\par
started from her Bed, threw on a loose night-gown, and passing through\par
the Closet in which slept the Waiting-woman, She reached Antonia’s\par
chamber just in time to rescue her from the grasp of the Ravisher.\par
\par
His shame and her amazement seemed to have petrified into Statues both\par
Elvira and the Monk: They remained gazing upon each other in silence.\par
The Lady was the first to recover herself.\par
\par
“It is no dream!” She cried; “It is really Ambrosio, who stands before\par
me! It is the Man whom Madrid esteems a Saint, that I find at this late\par
hour near the Couch of my unhappy Child! Monster of Hypocrisy! I\par
already suspected your designs, but forbore your accusation in pity to\par
human frailty. Silence would now be criminal: The whole City shall be\par
informed of your incontinence. I will unmask you, Villain, and convince\par
the Church what a Viper She cherishes in her bosom.”\par
\par
Pale and confused the baffled Culprit stood trembling before her.\par
\par
He would fain have extenuated his offence, but could find no apology\par
for his conduct: He could produce nothing but broken sentences, and\par
excuses which contradicted each other. Elvira was too justly incensed\par
to grant the pardon which He requested. She protested that She would\par
raise the neighbourhood, and make him an example to all future\par
Hypocrites. Then hastening to the Bed, She called to Antonia to wake;\par
and finding that her voice had no effect, She took her arm, and raised\par
her forcibly from the pillow. The charm operated too powerfully.\par
Antonia remained insensible, and on being released by her Mother, sank\par
back upon the pillow.\par
\par
“This slumber cannot be natural!” cried the amazed Elvira, whose\par
indignation increased with every moment. “Some mystery is concealed in\par
it; But tremble, Hypocrite; all your villainy shall soon be unravelled!\par
Help! Help!” She exclaimed aloud; “Within there! Flora! Flora!”\par
\par
“Hear me for one moment, Lady!” cried the Monk, restored to himself by\par
the urgency of the danger; “By all that is sacred and holy, I swear\par
that your Daughter’s honour is still unviolated. Forgive my\par
transgression! Spare me the shame of a discovery, and permit me to\par
regain the Abbey undisturbed. Grant me this request in mercy! I promise\par
not only that Antonia shall be secure from me in future, but that the\par
rest of my life shall prove .....”\par
\par
Elvira interrupted him abruptly.\par
\par
“Antonia secure from you? _I_ will secure her! You shall betray no\par
longer the confidence of Parents! Your iniquity shall be unveiled to\par
the public eye: All Madrid shall shudder at your perfidy, your\par
hypocrisy and incontinence. What Ho! there! Flora! Flora, I say!”\par
\par
While She spoke thus, the remembrance of Agnes struck upon his mind.\par
Thus had She sued to him for mercy, and thus had He refused her prayer!\par
It was now his turn to suffer, and He could not but acknowledge that\par
his punishment was just. In the meanwhile Elvira continued to call\par
Flora to her assistance; but her voice was so choaked with passion that\par
the Servant, who was buried in profound slumber, was insensible to all\par
her cries: Elvira dared not go towards the Closet in which Flora slept,\par
lest the Monk should take that opportunity to escape. Such indeed was\par
his intention: He trusted that could He reach the Abbey unobserved by\par
any other than Elvira, her single testimony would not suffice to ruin a\par
reputation so well established as his was in Madrid. With this idea He\par
gathered up such garments as He had already thrown off, and hastened\par
towards the Door. Elvira was aware of his design; She followed him, and\par
ere He could draw back the bolt, seized him by the arm, and detained\par
him.\par
\par
“Attempt not to fly!” said She; “You quit not this room without\par
Witnesses of your guilt.”\par
\par
Ambrosio struggled in vain to disengage himself. Elvira quitted not her\par
hold, but redoubled her cries for succour. The Friar’s danger grew more\par
urgent. He expected every moment to hear people assembling at her\par
voice; And worked up to madness by the approach of ruin, He adopted a\par
resolution equally desperate and savage. Turning round suddenly, with\par
one hand He grasped Elvira’s throat so as to prevent her continuing her\par
clamour, and with the other, dashing her violently upon the ground, He\par
dragged her towards the Bed. Confused by this unexpected attack, She\par
scarcely had power to strive at forcing herself from his grasp: While\par
the Monk, snatching the pillow from beneath her Daughter’s head,\par
covering with it Elvira’s face, and pressing his knee upon her stomach\par
with all his strength, endeavoured to put an end to her existence. He\par
succeeded but too well. Her natural strength increased by the excess of\par
anguish, long did the Sufferer struggle to disengage herself, but in\par
vain. The Monk continued to kneel upon her breast, witnessed without\par
mercy the convulsive trembling of her limbs beneath him, and sustained\par
with inhuman firmness the spectacle of her agonies, when soul and body\par
were on the point of separating. Those agonies at length were over. She\par
ceased to struggle for life. The Monk took off the pillow, and gazed\par
upon her. Her face was covered with a frightful blackness:\par
\par
Her limbs moved no more; The blood was chilled in her veins; Her heart\par
had forgotten to beat, and her hands were stiff and frozen.\par
\par
Ambrosio beheld before him that once noble and majestic form, now\par
become a Corse, cold, senseless and disgusting.\par
\par
This horrible act was no sooner perpetrated, than the Friar beheld the\par
enormity of his crime. A cold dew flowed over his limbs; his eyes\par
closed; He staggered to a chair, and sank into it almost as lifeless as\par
the Unfortunate who lay extended at his feet. From this state He was\par
rouzed by the necessity of flight, and the danger of being found in\par
Antonia’s apartment. He had no desire to profit by the execution of his\par
crime. Antonia now appeared to him an object of disgust. A deadly cold\par
had usurped the place of that warmth which glowed in his bosom: No\par
ideas offered themselves to his mind but those of death and guilt, of\par
present shame and future punishment. Agitated by remorse and fear He\par
prepared for flight: Yet his terrors did not so compleatly master his\par
recollection, as to prevent his taking the precautions necessary for\par
his safety. He replaced the pillow upon the bed, gathered up his\par
garments, and with the fatal Talisman in his hand, bent his unsteady\par
steps towards the door. Bewildered by fear, He fancied that his flight\par
was opposed by Legions of Phantoms; Whereever He turned, the disfigured\par
Corse seemed to lie in his passage, and it was long before He succeeded\par
in reaching the door. The enchanted Myrtle produced its former effect.\par
The door opened, and He hastened down the staircase. He entered the\par
Abbey unobserved, and having shut himself into his Cell, He abandoned\par
his soul to the tortures of unavailing remorse, and terrors of\par
impending detection.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER IX.\par
\par
\par
Tell us, ye Dead, will none of you in pity\par
To those you left behind disclose the secret?\par
O! That some courteous Ghost would blab it out,\par
What ’tis you are, and we must shortly be.\par
I’ve heard that Souls departed have sometimes\par
Fore-warned Men of their deaths:\par
’Twas kindly done\par
To knock, and give the alarum.\par
\par
BLAIR.\par
\par
Ambrosio shuddered at himself, when He reflected on his rapid advances\par
in iniquity. The enormous crime which He had just committed filled him\par
with real horror. The murdered Elvira was continually before his eyes,\par
and his guilt was already punished by the agonies of his conscience.\par
Time, however, considerably weakened these impressions: One day passed\par
away, another followed it, and still not the least suspicion was thrown\par
upon him. Impunity reconciled him to his guilt: He began to resume his\par
spirits; and as his fears of detection died away, He paid less\par
attention to the reproaches of remorse. Matilda exerted herself to\par
quiet his alarms. At the first intelligence of Elvira’s death, She\par
seemed greatly affected, and joined the Monk in deploring the unhappy\par
catastrophe of his adventure: But when She found his agitation to be\par
somewhat calmed, and himself better disposed to listen to her\par
arguments, She proceeded to mention his offence in milder terms, and\par
convince him that He was not so highly culpable as He appeared to\par
consider himself. She represented that He had only availed himself of\par
the rights which Nature allows to every one, those of\par
self-preservation: That either Elvira or himself must have perished,\par
and that her inflexibility and resolution to ruin him had deservedly\par
marked her out for the Victim. She next stated, that as He had before\par
rendered himself suspected to Elvira, it was a fortunate event for him\par
that her lips were closed by death; since without this last adventure,\par
her suspicions if made public might have produced very disagreeable\par
consequences. He had therefore freed himself from an Enemy, to whom the\par
errors of his conduct were sufficiently known to make her dangerous,\par
and who was the greatest obstacle to his designs upon Antonia. Those\par
designs She encouraged him not to abandon. She assured him that, no\par
longer protected by her Mother’s watchful eye, the Daughter would fall\par
an easy conquest; and by praising and enumerating Antonia’s charms, She\par
strove to rekindle the desires of the Monk. In this endeavour She\par
succeeded but too well.\par
\par
As if the crimes into which his passion had seduced him had only\par
increased its violence, He longed more eagerly than ever to enjoy\par
Antonia. The same success in concealing his present guilt, He trusted\par
would attend his future. He was deaf to the murmurs of conscience, and\par
resolved to satisfy his desires at any price. He waited only for an\par
opportunity of repeating his former enterprize; But to procure that\par
opportunity by the same means was now impracticable. In the first\par
transports of despair He had dashed the enchanted Myrtle into a\par
thousand pieces: Matilda told him plainly that He must expect no\par
further assistance from the infernal Powers unless He was willing to\par
subscribe to their established conditions. This Ambrosio was determined\par
not to do: He persuaded himself that however great might be his\par
iniquity, so long as he preserved his claim to salvation, He need not\par
despair of pardon. He therefore resolutely refused to enter into any\par
bond or compact with the Fiends; and Matilda finding him obstinate upon\par
this point, forbore to press him further. She exerted her invention to\par
discover some means of putting Antonia into the Abbot’s power: Nor was\par
it long before that means presented itself.\par
\par
While her ruin was thus meditating, the unhappy Girl herself suffered\par
severely from the loss of her Mother. Every morning on waking, it was\par
her first care to hasten to Elvira’s chamber. On that which followed\par
Ambrosio’s fatal visit, She woke later than was her usual custom: Of\par
this She was convinced by the Abbey Chimes. She started from her bed,\par
threw on a few loose garments hastily, and was speeding to enquire how\par
her Mother had passed the night, when her foot struck against something\par
which lay in her passage. She looked down. What was her horror at\par
recognizing Elvira’s livid Corse! She uttered a loud shriek, and threw\par
herself upon the floor. She clasped the inanimate form to her bosom,\par
felt that it was dead-cold, and with a movement of disgust, of which\par
She was not the Mistress, let it fall again from her arms. The cry had\par
alarmed Flora, who hastened to her assistance. The sight which She\par
beheld penetrated her with horror; but her alarm was more audible than\par
Antonia’s. She made the House ring with her lamentations, while her\par
Mistress, almost suffocated with grief, could only mark her distress by\par
sobs and groans. Flora’s shrieks soon reached the ears of the Hostess,\par
whose terror and surprize were excessive on learning the cause of this\par
disturbance. A Physician was immediately sent for: But on the first\par
moment of beholding the Corse, He declared that Elvira’s recovery was\par
beyond the power of art. He proceeded therefore to give his assistance\par
to Antonia, who by this time was truly in need of it. She was conveyed\par
to bed, while the Landlady busied herself in giving orders for Elvira’s\par
Burial. Dame Jacintha was a plain good kind of Woman, charitable,\par
generous, and devout: But her intellects were weak, and She was a\par
Miserable Slave to fear and superstition. She shuddered at the idea of\par
passing the night in the same House with a dead Body: She was persuaded\par
that Elvira’s Ghost would appear to her, and no less certain that such\par
a visit would kill her with fright. From this persuasion, She resolved\par
to pass the night at a Neighbour’s, and insisted that the Funeral\par
should take place the next day. St. Clare’s Cemetery being the nearest,\par
it was determined that Elvira should be buried there. Dame Jacintha\par
engaged to defray every expence attending the burial. She knew not in\par
what circumstances Antonia was left, but from the sparing manner in\par
which the Family had lived, She concluded them to be indifferent.\par
\par
Consequently, She entertained very little hope of ever being\par
recompensed; But this consideration prevented her not from taking care\par
that the Interment was performed with decency, and from showing the\par
unfortunate Antonia all possible respect.\par
\par
Nobody dies of mere grief; Of this Antonia was an instance. Aided by\par
her youth and healthy constitution, She shook off the malady which her\par
Mother’s death had occasioned; But it was not so easy to remove the\par
disease of her mind. Her eyes were constantly filled with tears: Every\par
trifle affected her, and She evidently nourished in her bosom a\par
profound and rooted melancholy. The slightest mention of Elvira, the\par
most trivial circumstance recalling that beloved Parent to her memory,\par
was sufficient to throw her into serious agitation. How much would her\par
grief have been increased, had She known the agonies which terminated\par
her Mother’s existence! But of this no one entertained the least\par
suspicion. Elvira was subject to strong convulsions: It was supposed\par
that, aware of their approach, She had dragged herself to her\par
Daughter’s chamber in hopes of assistance; that a sudden access of her\par
fits had seized her, too violent to be resisted by her already\par
enfeebled state of health; and that She had expired ere She had time to\par
reach the medicine which generally relieved her, and which stood upon a\par
shelf in Antonia’s room. This idea was firmly credited by the few\par
people, who interested themselves about Elvira: Her Death was esteemed\par
a natural event, and soon forgotten by all save by her, who had but too\par
much reason to deplore her loss.\par
\par
In truth Antonia’s situation was sufficiently embarrassing and\par
unpleasant. She was alone in the midst of a dissipated and expensive\par
City; She was ill provided with money, and worse with Friends. Her aunt\par
Leonella was still at Cordova, and She knew not her direction. Of the\par
Marquis de las Cisternas She heard no news: As to Lorenzo, She had long\par
given up the idea of possessing any interest in his bosom. She knew not\par
to whom She could address herself in her present dilemma. She wished to\par
consult Ambrosio; But She remembered her Mother’s injunctions to shun\par
him as much as possible, and the last conversation which Elvira had\par
held with her upon the subject had given her sufficient lights\par
respecting his designs to put her upon her guard against him in future.\par
Still all her Mother’s warnings could not make her change her good\par
opinion of the Friar. She continued to feel that his friendship and\par
society were requisite to her happiness: She looked upon his failings\par
with a partial eye, and could not persuade herself that He really had\par
intended her ruin. However, Elvira had positively commanded her to drop\par
his acquaintance, and She had too much respect for her orders to\par
disobey them.\par
\par
At length She resolved to address herself for advice and protection to\par
the Marquis de las Cisternas, as being her nearest Relation. She wrote\par
to him, briefly stating her desolate situation; She besought him to\par
compassionate his Brother’s Child, to continue to her Elvira’s pension,\par
and to authorise her retiring to his old Castle in Murcia, which till\par
now had been her retreat. Having sealed her letter, She gave it to the\par
trusty Flora, who immediately set out to execute her commission. But\par
Antonia was born under an unlucky Star. Had She made her application to\par
the Marquis but one day sooner, received as his Niece and placed at the\par
head of his Family, She would have escaped all the misfortunes with\par
which She was now threatened. Raymond had always intended to execute\par
this plan: But first, his hopes of making the proposal to Elvira\par
through the lips of Agnes, and afterwards, his disappointment at losing\par
his intended Bride, as well as the severe illness which for some time\par
had confined him to his Bed, made him defer from day to day the giving\par
an Asylum in his House to his Brother’s Widow. He had commissioned\par
Lorenzo to supply her liberally with money: But Elvira, unwilling to\par
receive obligations from that Nobleman, had assured him that She needed\par
no immediate pecuniary assistance. Consequently, the Marquis did not\par
imagine that a trifling delay on his part could create any\par
embarrassment; and the distress and agitation of his mind might well\par
excuse his negligence.\par
\par
Had He been informed that Elvira’s death had left her Daughter\par
Friendless and unprotected, He would doubtless have taken such\par
measures, as would have ensured her from every danger: But Antonia was\par
not destined to be so fortunate. The day on which She sent her letter\par
to the Palace de las Cisternas was that following Lorenzo’s departure\par
from Madrid. The Marquis was in the first paroxysms of despair at the\par
conviction that Agnes was indeed no more: He was delirious, and his\par
life being in danger, no one was suffered to approach him. Flora was\par
informed that He was incapable of attending to Letters, and that\par
probably a few hours would decide his fate. With this unsatisfactory\par
answer She was obliged to return to her Mistress, who now found herself\par
plunged into greater difficulties than ever.\par
\par
Flora and Dame Jacintha exerted themselves to console her. The Latter\par
begged her to make herself easy, for that as long as She chose to stay\par
with her, She would treat her like her own Child. Antonia, finding that\par
the good Woman had taken a real affection for her, was somewhat\par
comforted by thinking that She had at least one Friend in the World. A\par
Letter was now brought to her, directed to Elvira. She recognized\par
Leonella’s writing, and opening it with joy, found a detailed account\par
of her Aunt’s adventures at Cordova. She informed her Sister that She\par
had recovered her Legacy, had lost her heart, and had received in\par
exchange that of the most amiable of Apothecaries, past, present, and\par
to come. She added that She should be at Madrid on the Tuesday night,\par
and meant to have the pleasure of presenting her Caro Sposo in form.\par
Though her nuptials were far from pleasing Antonia, Leonella’s speedy\par
return gave her Niece much delight. She rejoiced in thinking that She\par
should once more be under a Relation’s care. She could not but judge it\par
to be highly improper, for a young Woman to be living among absolute\par
Strangers, with no one to regulate her conduct, or protect her from the\par
insults to which, in her defenceless situation, She was exposed. She\par
therefore looked forward with impatience to the Tuesday night.\par
\par
It arrived. Antonia listened anxiously to the Carriages, as they rolled\par
along the Street. None of them stopped, and it grew late without\par
Leonella’s appearing. Still, Antonia resolved to sit up till her Aunt’s\par
arrival, and in spite of all her remonstrances, Dame Jacintha and Flora\par
insisted upon doing the same. The hours passed on slow and tediously.\par
Lorenzo’s departure from Madrid had put a stop to the nightly\par
Serenades: She hoped in vain to hear the usual sound of Guitars beneath\par
her window. She took up her own, and struck a few chords: But Music\par
that evening had lost its charms for her, and She soon replaced the\par
Instrument in its case. She seated herself at her embroidery frame, but\par
nothing went right: The silks were missing, the thread snapped every\par
moment, and the needles were so expert at falling that they seemed to\par
be animated. At length a flake of wax fell from the Taper which stood\par
near her upon a favourite wreath of Violets: This compleatly\par
discomposed her; She threw down her needle, and quitted the frame. It\par
was decreed that for that night nothing should have the power of\par
amusing her. She was the prey of Ennui, and employed herself in making\par
fruitless wishes for the arrival of her Aunt.\par
\par
As She walked with a listless air up and down the chamber, the Door\par
caught her eye conducting to that which had been her Mother’s. She\par
remembered that Elvira’s little Library was arranged there, and thought\par
that She might possibly find in it some Book to amuse her till Leonella\par
should arrive. Accordingly She took her Taper from the table, passed\par
through the little Closet, and entered the adjoining apartment. As She\par
looked around her, the sight of this room brought to her recollection a\par
thousand painful ideas. It was the first time of her entering it since\par
her Mother’s death. The total silence prevailing through the chamber,\par
the Bed despoiled of its furniture, the cheerless hearth where stood an\par
extinguished Lamp, and a few dying Plants in the window which, since\par
Elvira’s loss, had been neglected, inspired Antonia with a melancholy\par
awe. The gloom of night gave strength to this sensation. She placed her\par
light upon the Table, and sank into a large chair, in which She had\par
seen her Mother seated a thousand and a thousand times. She was never\par
to see her seated there again! Tears unbidden streamed down her cheek,\par
and She abandoned herself to the sadness which grew deeper with every\par
moment.\par
\par
Ashamed of her weakness, She at length rose from her seat: She\par
proceeded to seek for what had brought her to this melancholy scene.\par
The small collection of Books was arranged upon several shelves in\par
order. Antonia examined them without finding any thing likely to\par
interest her, till She put her hand upon a volume of old Spanish\par
Ballads. She read a few Stanzas of one of them: They excited her\par
curiosity. She took down the Book, and seated herself to peruse it with\par
more ease. She trimmed the Taper, which now drew towards its end, and\par
then read the following Ballad.\par
\par
ALONZO THE BRAVE, AND FAIR IMOGINE\par
\par
\par
A Warrior so bold, and a Virgin so bright\par
    Conversed, as They sat on the green:\par
They gazed on each other with tender delight;\par
Alonzo the Brave was the name of the Knight,\par
    The Maid’s was the Fair Imogine.\par
\par
“And Oh!” said the Youth, “since to-morrow I go\par
    To fight in a far distant land,\par
Your tears for my absence soon leaving to flow,\par
Some Other will court you, and you will bestow\par
    On a wealthier Suitor your hand.”\par
\par
“Oh! hush these suspicions,” Fair Imogine said,\par
    “Offensive to Love and to me!\par
For if ye be living, or if ye be dead,\par
I swear by the Virgin, that none in your stead\par
    Shall Husband of Imogine be.\par
\par
“If e’er I by lust or by wealth led aside\par
    Forget my Alonzo the Brave,\par
God grant, that to punish my falsehood and pride\par
Your Ghost at the Marriage may sit by my side,\par
May tax me with perjury, claim me as Bride,\par
    And bear me away to the Grave!”\par
\par
To Palestine hastened the Hero so bold;\par
    His Love, She lamented him sore:\par
But scarce had a twelve-month elapsed, when behold,\par
A Baron all covered with jewels and gold\par
    Arrived at Fair Imogine’s door.\par
\par
His treasure, his presents, his spacious domain\par
    Soon made her untrue to her vows:\par
He dazzled her eyes; He bewildered her brain;\par
He caught her affections so light and so vain,\par
    And carried her home as his Spouse.\par
\par
And now had the Marriage been blest by the Priest;\par
    The revelry now was begun:\par
The Tables, they groaned with the weight of the Feast;\par
Nor yet had the laughter and merriment ceased,\par
    When the Bell of the Castle told,—“One!”\par
\par
Then first with amazement Fair Imogine found\par
    That a Stranger was placed by her side: His air was terrific;\par
He uttered no sound; He spoke not, He moved not,\par
He looked not around,\par
    But earnestly gazed on the Bride.\par
\par
His vizor was closed, and gigantic his height;\par
    His armour was sable to view:\par
All pleasure and laughter were hushed at his sight;\par
The Dogs as They eyed him drew back in affright,\par
    The Lights in the chamber burned blue!\par
\par
His presence all bosoms appeared to dismay;\par
    The Guests sat in silence and fear.\par
At length spoke the Bride, while She trembled;\par
“I pray, Sir Knight, that your Helmet aside you would lay,\par
    And deign to partake of our chear.”\par
\par
The Lady is silent: The Stranger complies.\par
    His vizor lie slowly unclosed:\par
Oh! God! what a sight met Fair Imogine’s eyes!\par
What words can express her dismay and surprize,\par
    When a Skeleton’s head was exposed.\par
\par
All present then uttered a terrified shout;\par
    All turned with disgust from the scene.\par
The worms, They crept in, and the worms, They crept out,\par
And sported his eyes and his temples about,\par
    While the Spectre addressed Imogine.\par
\par
“Behold me, Thou false one! Behold me!” He cried;\par
    “Remember Alonzo the Brave!\par
God grants, that to punish thy falsehood and pride\par
My Ghost at thy marriage should sit by thy side,\par
Should tax thee with perjury, claim thee as Bride\par
    And bear thee away to the Grave!”\par
\par
Thus saying, his arms round the Lady He wound,\par
    While loudly She shrieked in dismay;\par
Then sank with his prey through the wide-yawning ground:\par
Nor ever again was Fair Imogine found,\par
    Or the Spectre who bore her away.\par
\par
Not long lived the Baron; and none since that time\par
    To inhabit the Castle presume:\par
For Chronicles tell, that by order sublime\par
There Imogine suffers the pain of her crime,\par
    And mourns her deplorable doom.\par
\par
At midnight four times in each year does her Spright\par
    When Mortals in slumber are bound,\par
Arrayed in her bridal apparel of white,\par
Appear in the Hall with the Skeleton-Knight,\par
    And shriek, as He whirls her around.\par
\par
While They drink out of skulls newly torn from the grave,\par
    Dancing round them the Spectres are seen:\par
Their liquor is blood, and this horrible Stave\par
They howl.—“To the health of Alonzo the Brave,\par
    And his Consort, the False Imogine!”\par
\par
\par
The perusal of this story was ill-calculated to dispel Antonia’s\par
melancholy. She had naturally a strong inclination to the marvellous;\par
and her Nurse, who believed firmly in Apparitions, had related to her\par
when an Infant so many horrible adventures of this kind, that all\par
Elvira’s attempts had failed to eradicate their impressions from her\par
Daughter’s mind. Antonia still nourished a superstitious prejudice in\par
her bosom: She was often susceptible of terrors which, when She\par
discovered their natural and insignificant cause, made her blush at her\par
own weakness. With such a turn of mind, the adventure which She had\par
just been reading sufficed to give her apprehensions the alarm. The\par
hour and the scene combined to authorize them. It was the dead of\par
night: She was alone, and in the chamber once occupied by her deceased\par
Mother. The weather was comfortless and stormy: The wind howled around\par
the House, the doors rattled in their frames, and the heavy rain\par
pattered against the windows. No other sound was heard. The Taper, now\par
burnt down to the socket, sometimes flaring upwards shot a gleam of\par
light through the room, then sinking again seemed upon the point of\par
expiring. Antonia’s heart throbbed with agitation: Her eyes wandered\par
fearfully over the objects around her, as the trembling flame\par
illuminated them at intervals. She attempted to rise from her seat; But\par
her limbs trembled so violently that She was unable to proceed. She\par
then called Flora, who was in a room at no great distance: But\par
agitation choaked her voice, and her cries died away in hollow murmurs.\par
\par
She passed some minutes in this situation, after which her terrors\par
began to diminish. She strove to recover herself, and acquire strength\par
enough to quit the room: Suddenly She fancied, that She heard a low\par
sigh drawn near her. This idea brought back her former weakness. She\par
had already raised herself from her seat, and was on the point of\par
taking the Lamp from the Table. The imaginary noise stopped her: She\par
drew back her hand, and supported herself upon the back of a Chair. She\par
listened anxiously, but nothing more was heard.\par
\par
“Gracious God!” She said to herself; “What could be that sound? Was I\par
deceived, or did I really hear it?”\par
\par
Her reflections were interrupted by a noise at the door scarcely\par
audible: It seemed as if somebody was whispering. Antonia’s alarm\par
increased: Yet the Bolt She knew to be fastened, and this idea in some\par
degree reassured her. Presently the Latch was lifted up softly, and the\par
Door moved with caution backwards and forwards. Excess of terror now\par
supplied Antonia with that strength, of which She had till then been\par
deprived. She started from her place and made towards the Closet door,\par
whence She might soon have reached the chamber where She expected to\par
find Flora and Dame Jacintha. Scarcely had She reached the middle of\par
the room when the Latch was lifted up a second time. An involuntary\par
movement obliged her to turn her head. Slowly and gradually the Door\par
turned upon its hinges, and standing upon the Threshold She beheld a\par
tall thin Figure, wrapped in a white shroud which covered it from head\par
to foot.\par
\par
This vision arrested her feet: She remained as if petrified in the\par
middle of the apartment. The Stranger with measured and solemn steps\par
drew near the Table. The dying Taper darted a blue and melancholy flame\par
as the Figure advanced towards it. Over the Table was fixed a small\par
Clock; The hand of it was upon the stroke of three. The Figure stopped\par
opposite to the Clock: It raised its right arm, and pointed to the\par
hour, at the same time looking earnestly upon Antonia, who waited for\par
the conclusion of this scene, motionless and silent.\par
\par
The figure remained in this posture for some moments. The clock struck.\par
When the sound had ceased, the Stranger advanced yet a few steps nearer\par
Antonia.\par
\par
“Yet three days,” said a voice faint, hollow, and sepulchral; “Yet\par
three days, and we meet again!”\par
\par
Antonia shuddered at the words.\par
\par
“We meet again?” She pronounced at length with difficulty: “Where shall\par
we meet? Whom shall I meet?”\par
\par
The figure pointed to the ground with one hand, and with the other\par
raised the Linen which covered its face.\par
\par
“Almighty God! My Mother!”\par
\par
Antonia shrieked, and fell lifeless upon the floor.\par
\par
Dame Jacintha who was at work in a neighbouring chamber, was alarmed by\par
the cry: Flora was just gone down stairs to fetch fresh oil for the\par
Lamp, by which they had been sitting. Jacintha therefore hastened alone\par
to Antonia’s assistance, and great was her amazement to find her\par
extended upon the floor. She raised her in her arms, conveyed her to\par
her apartment, and placed her upon the Bed still senseless. She then\par
proceeded to bathe her temples, chafe her hands, and use all possible\par
means of bringing her to herself. With some difficulty She succeeded.\par
Antonia opened her eyes, and looked round her wildly.\par
\par
“Where is She?” She cried in a trembling voice; “Is She gone? Am I\par
safe? Speak to me! Comfort me! Oh! speak to me for God’s sake!”\par
\par
“Safe from whom, my Child?” replied the astonished Jacintha; “What\par
alarms you? Of whom are you afraid?”\par
\par
“In three days! She told me that we should meet in three days! I heard\par
her say it! I saw her, Jacintha, I saw her but this moment!”\par
\par
She threw herself upon Jacintha’s bosom.\par
\par
“You saw her? Saw whom?”\par
\par
“My Mother’s Ghost!”\par
\par
“Christ Jesus!” cried Jacintha, and starting from the Bed, let fall\par
Antonia upon the pillow, and fled in consternation out of the room.\par
\par
As She hastened down stairs, She met Flora ascending them.\par
\par
“Go to your Mistress, Flora,” said She; “Here are rare doings! Oh! I am\par
the most unfortunate Woman alive! My House is filled with Ghosts and\par
dead Bodies, and the Lord knows what besides; Yet I am sure, nobody\par
likes such company less than I do. But go your way to Donna Antonia,\par
Flora, and let me go mine.”\par
\par
Thus saying, She continued her course to the Street door, which She\par
opened, and without allowing herself time to throw on her veil, She\par
made the best of her way to the Capuchin Abbey. In the meanwhile, Flora\par
hastened to her Lady’s chamber, equally surprized and alarmed at\par
Jacintha’s consternation. She found Antonia lying upon the bed\par
insensible. She used the same means for her recovery that Jacintha had\par
already employed; But finding that her Mistress only recovered from one\par
fit to fall into another, She sent in all haste for a Physician. While\par
expecting his arrival, She undrest Antonia, and conveyed her to Bed.\par
\par
Heedless of the storm, terrified almost out of her senses, Jacintha ran\par
through the Streets, and stopped not till She reached the Gate of the\par
Abbey. She rang loudly at the bell, and as soon as the Porter appeared,\par
She desired permission to speak to the Superior. Ambrosio was then\par
conferring with Matilda upon the means of procuring access to Antonia.\par
The cause of Elvira’s death remaining unknown, He was convinced that\par
crimes were not so swiftly followed by punishment, as his Instructors\par
the Monks had taught him, and as till then He had himself believed.\par
This persuasion made him resolve upon Antonia’s ruin, for the enjoyment\par
of whose person dangers and difficulties only seemed to have increased\par
his passion. The Monk had already made one attempt to gain admission to\par
her presence; But Flora had refused him in such a manner as to convince\par
him that all future endeavours must be vain. Elvira had confided her\par
suspicions to that trusty Servant: She had desired her never to leave\par
Ambrosio alone with her Daughter, and if possible to prevent their\par
meeting altogether. Flora promised to obey her, and had executed her\par
orders to the very letter. Ambrosio’s visit had been rejected that\par
morning, though Antonia was ignorant of it. He saw that to obtain a\par
sight of his Mistress by open means was out of the question; and both\par
Himself and Matilda had consumed the night, in endeavouring to invent\par
some plan, whose event might be more successful. Such was their\par
employment, when a Lay-Brother entered the Abbot’s Cell, and informed\par
him that a Woman calling herself Jacintha Zuniga requested audience for\par
a few minutes.\par
\par
Ambrosio was by no means disposed to grant the petition of his Visitor.\par
He refused it positively, and bad the Lay-Brother tell the Stranger to\par
return the next day. Matilda interrupted him.\par
\par
“See this Woman,” said She in a low voice; “I have my reasons.”\par
\par
The Abbot obeyed her, and signified that He would go to the Parlour\par
immediately. With this answer the Lay-Brother withdrew. As soon as they\par
were alone Ambrosio enquired why Matilda wished him to see this\par
Jacintha.\par
\par
“She is Antonia’s Hostess,” replied Matilda; “She may possibly be of\par
use to you: but let us examine her, and learn what brings her hither.”\par
\par
They proceeded together to the Parlour, where Jacintha was already\par
waiting for the Abbot. She had conceived a great opinion of his piety\par
and virtue; and supposing him to have much influence over the Devil,\par
thought that it must be an easy matter for him to lay Elvira’s Ghost in\par
the Red Sea. Filled with this persuasion She had hastened to the Abbey.\par
As soon as She saw the Monk enter the Parlour, She dropped upon her\par
knees, and began her story as follows.\par
\par
“Oh! Reverend Father! Such an accident! Such an adventure! I know not\par
what course to take, and unless you can help me, I shall certainly go\par
distracted. Well, to be sure, never was Woman so unfortunate, as\par
myself! All in my power to keep clear of such abomination have I done,\par
and yet that all is too little. What signifies my telling my beads four\par
times a day, and observing every fast prescribed by the Calendar? What\par
signifies my having made three Pilgrimages to St. James of Compostella,\par
and purchased as many pardons from the Pope as would buy off Cain’s\par
punishment? Nothing prospers with me! All goes wrong, and God only\par
knows, whether any thing will ever go right again! Why now, be your\par
Holiness the Judge. My Lodger dies in convulsions; Out of pure kindness\par
I bury her at my own expence; (Not that she is any relation of mine, or\par
that I shall be benefited a single pistole by her death: I got nothing\par
by it, and therefore you know, reverend Father, that her living or\par
dying was just the same to me. But that is nothing to the purpose; To\par
return to what I was saying,) I took care of her funeral, had every\par
thing performed decently and properly, and put myself to expence\par
enough, God knows! And how do you think the Lady repays me for my\par
kindness? Why truly by refusing to sleep quietly in her comfortable\par
deal Coffin, as a peaceable well-disposed Spirit ought to do, and\par
coming to plague me, who never wish to set eyes on her again. Forsooth,\par
it well becomes her to go racketing about my House at midnight, popping\par
into her Daughter’s room through the Keyhole, and frightening the poor\par
Child out of her wits! Though She be a Ghost, She might be more civil\par
than to bolt into a Person’s House, who likes her company so little.\par
But as for me, reverend Father, the plain state of the case is this: If\par
She walks into my House, I must walk out of it, for I cannot abide such\par
Visitors, not I! Thus you see, your Sanctity, that without your\par
assistance I am ruined and undone for ever. I shall be obliged to quit\par
my House; Nobody will take it, when ’tis known that She haunts it, and\par
then I shall find myself in a fine situation! Miserable Woman that I\par
am! What shall I do! What will become of me!”\par
\par
Here She wept bitterly, wrung her hands, and begged to know the Abbot’s\par
opinion of her case.\par
\par
“In truth, good Woman,” replied He, “It will be difficult for me to\par
relieve you without knowing what is the matter with you. You have\par
forgotten to tell me what has happened, and what it is you want.”\par
\par
“Let me die” cried Jacintha, “but your Sanctity is in the right! This\par
then is the fact stated briefly. A lodger of mine is lately dead, a\par
very good sort of Woman that I must needs say for her as far as my\par
knowledge of her went, though that was not a great way:\par
\par
She kept me too much at a distance; for indeed She was given to be upon\par
the high ropes, and whenever I ventured to speak to her, She had a look\par
with her which always made me feel a little queerish, God forgive me\par
for saying so. However, though She was more stately than needful, and\par
affected to look down upon me (Though if I am well informed, I come of\par
as good Parents as She could do for her ears, for her Father was a\par
Shoe-maker at Cordova, and Mine was an Hatter at Madrid, aye, and a\par
very creditable Hatter too, let me tell you,) Yet for all her pride,\par
She was a quiet well-behaved Body, and I never wish to have a better\par
Lodger. This makes me wonder the more at her not sleeping quietly in\par
her Grave: But there is no trusting to people in this world! For my\par
part, I never saw her do amiss, except on the Friday before her death.\par
To be sure, I was then much scandalized by seeing her eat the wing of a\par
Chicken! ‘How, Madona Flora!’ quoth I; (Flora, may it please your\par
Reverence, is the name of the waiting Maid)—‘How, Madona Flora!’ quoth\par
I; ‘Does your Mistress eat flesh upon Fridays? Well! Well! See the\par
event, and then remember that Dame Jacintha warned you of it!’ These\par
were my very words, but Alas! I might as well have held my tongue!\par
Nobody minded me; and Flora, who is somewhat pert and snappish, (More\par
is the pity, say I) told me that there was no more harm in eating a\par
Chicken than the egg from which it came. Nay, She even declared that if\par
her Lady added a slice of bacon, She would not be an inch nearer\par
Damnation, God protect us! A poor ignorant sinful soul! I protest to\par
your Holiness, I trembled to hear her utter such blasphemies, and\par
expected every moment to see the ground open and swallow her up,\par
Chicken and all! For you must know, worshipful Father, that while She\par
talked thus, She held the plate in her hand, on which lay the identical\par
roast Fowl. And a fine Bird it was, that I must say for it! Done to a\par
turn, for I superintended the cooking of it myself: It was a little\par
Gallician of my own raising, may it please your Holiness, and the flesh\par
was as white as an egg-shell, as indeed Donna Elvira told me herself.\par
‘Dame Jacintha,’ said She, very good-humouredly, though to say the\par
truth, She was always very polite to me .....”\par
\par
Here Ambrosio’s patience failed him. Eager to know Jacintha’s business\par
in which Antonia seemed to be concerned, He was almost distracted while\par
listening to the rambling of this prosing old Woman. He interrupted\par
her, and protested that if She did not immediately tell her story and\par
have done with it, He should quit the Parlour, and leave her to get out\par
of her difficulties by herself. This threat had the desired effect.\par
Jacintha related her business in as few words as She could manage; But\par
her account was still so prolix that Ambrosio had need of his patience\par
to bear him to the conclusion.\par
\par
“And so, your Reverence,” said She, after relating Elvira’s death and\par
burial, with all their circumstances; “And so, your Reverence, upon\par
hearing the shriek, I put away my work, and away posted I to Donna\par
Antonia’s chamber. Finding nobody there, I past on to the next; But I\par
must own, I was a little timorous at going in, for this was the very\par
room where Donna Elvira used to sleep. However, in I went, and sure\par
enough, there lay the young Lady at full length upon the floor, as cold\par
as a stone, and as white as a sheet. I was surprized at this, as your\par
Holiness may well suppose; But Oh me! how I shook when I saw a great\par
tall figure at my elbow whose head touched the ceiling! The face was\par
Donna Elvira’s, I must confess; But out of its mouth came clouds of\par
fire, its arms were loaded with heavy chains which it rattled\par
piteously, and every hair on its head was a Serpent as big as my arm!\par
At this I was frightened enough, and began to say my Ave-Maria: But the\par
Ghost interrupting me uttered three loud groans, and roared out in a\par
terrible voice, ‘Oh! That Chicken’s wing! My poor soul suffers for it!’\par
As soon as She had said this, the Ground opened, the Spectre sank down,\par
I heard a clap of thunder, and the room was filled with a smell of\par
brimstone. When I recovered from my fright, and had brought Donna\par
Antonia to herself, who told me that She had cried out upon seeing her\par
Mother’s Ghost, (And well might She cry, poor Soul! Had I been in her\par
place, I should have cried ten times louder) it directly came into my\par
head, that if any one had power to quiet this Spectre, it must be your\par
Reverence. So hither I came in all diligence, to beg that you will\par
sprinkle my House with holy water, and lay the Apparition in the Red\par
Sea.”\par
\par
Ambrosio stared at this strange story, which He could not credit.\par
\par
“Did Donna Antonia also see the Ghost?” said He.\par
\par
“As plain as I see you, Reverend Father!”\par
\par
Ambrosio paused for a moment. Here was an opportunity offered him of\par
gaining access to Antonia, but He hesitated to employ it. The\par
reputation which He enjoyed in Madrid was still dear to him; and since\par
He had lost the reality of virtue, it appeared as if its semblance was\par
become more valuable. He was conscious that publicly to break through\par
the rule never to quit the Abbey precincts, would derogate much from\par
his supposed austerity. In visiting Elvira, He had always taken care to\par
keep his features concealed from the Domestics. Except by the Lady, her\par
Daughter, and the faithful Flora, He was known in the Family by no\par
other name than that of Father Jerome. Should He comply with Jacintha’s\par
request, and accompany her to her House, He knew that the violation of\par
his rule could not be kept a secret. However, his eagerness to see\par
Antonia obtained the victory: He even hoped, that the singularity of\par
this adventure would justify him in the eyes of Madrid: But whatever\par
might be the consequences, He resolved to profit by the opportunity\par
which chance had presented to him. An expressive look from Matilda\par
confirmed him in this resolution.\par
\par
“Good Woman,” said He to Jacintha, “what you tell me is so\par
extraordinary that I can scarcely credit your assertions. However, I\par
will comply with your request. Tomorrow after Matins you may expect me\par
at your House: I will then examine into what I can do for you, and if\par
it is in my power, will free you from this unwelcome Visitor. Now then\par
go home, and peace be with you!”\par
\par
“Home?” exclaimed Jacintha; “I go home? Not I by my troth! except under\par
your protection, I set no foot of mine within the threshold. God help\par
me, the Ghost may meet me upon the Stairs, and whisk me away with her\par
to the devil! Oh! That I had accepted young Melchior Basco’s offer!\par
Then I should have had somebody to protect me; But now I am a lone\par
Woman, and meet with nothing but crosses and misfortunes! Thank Heaven,\par
it is not yet too late to repent! There is Simon Gonzalez will have me\par
any day of the week, and if I live till daybreak, I will marry him out\par
of hand: An Husband I will have, that is determined, for now this Ghost\par
is once in my House, I shall be frightened out of my wits to sleep\par
alone. But for God’s sake, reverend Father, come with me now. I shall\par
have no rest till the House is purified, or the poor young Lady either.\par
The dear Girl! She is in a piteous taking: I left her in strong\par
convulsions, and I doubt, She will not easily recover her fright.”\par
\par
The Friar started, and interrupted her hastily.\par
\par
“In convulsions, say you? Antonia in convulsions? Lead on, good Woman!\par
I follow you this moment!”\par
\par
Jacintha insisted upon his stopping to furnish himself with the vessel\par
of holy water: With this request He complied. Thinking herself safe\par
under his protection should a Legion of Ghosts attack her, the old\par
Woman returned the Monk a profusion of thanks, and they departed\par
together for the Strada di San Iago.\par
\par
So strong an impression had the Spectre made upon Antonia, that for the\par
first two or three hours the Physician declared her life to be in\par
danger. The fits at length becoming less frequent induced him to alter\par
his opinion. He said that to keep her quiet was all that was necessary;\par
and He ordered a medicine to be prepared which would tranquillize her\par
nerves, and procure her that repose which at present She much wanted.\par
The sight of Ambrosio, who now appeared with Jacintha at her Bedside,\par
contributed essentially to compose her ruffled spirits. Elvira had not\par
sufficiently explained herself upon the nature of his designs, to make\par
a Girl so ignorant of the world as her Daughter aware how dangerous was\par
his acquaintance. At this moment, when penetrated with horror at the\par
scene which had just past, and dreading to contemplate the Ghost’s\par
prediction, her mind had need of all the succours of friendship and\par
religion, Antonia regarded the Abbot with an eye doubly partial. That\par
strong prepossession in his favour still existed which She had felt for\par
him at first sight: She fancied, yet knew not wherefore, that his\par
presence was a safeguard to her from every danger, insult, or\par
misfortune.\par
\par
She thanked him gratefully for his visit, and related to him the\par
adventure, which had alarmed her so seriously.\par
\par
The Abbot strove to reassure her, and convince her that the whole had\par
been a deception of her overheated fancy. The solitude in which She had\par
passed the Evening, the gloom of night, the Book which She had been\par
reading, and the Room in which She sat, were all calculated to place\par
before her such a vision. He treated the idea of Ghosts with ridicule,\par
and produced strong arguments to prove the fallacy of such a system.\par
His conversation tranquillized and comforted her, but did not convince\par
her. She could not believe that the Spectre had been a mere creature of\par
her imagination; Every circumstance was impressed upon her mind too\par
forcibly, to permit her flattering herself with such an idea. She\par
persisted in asserting that She had really seen her Mother’s Ghost, had\par
heard the period of her dissolution announced and declared that She\par
never should quit her bed alive. Ambrosio advised her against\par
encouraging these sentiments, and then quitted her chamber, having\par
promised to repeat his visit on the morrow. Antonia received this\par
assurance with every mark of joy: But the Monk easily perceived that He\par
was not equally acceptable to her Attendant. Flora obeyed Elvira’s\par
injunctions with the most scrupulous observance. She examined every\par
circumstance with an anxious eye likely in the least to prejudice her\par
young Mistress, to whom She had been attached for many years. She was a\par
Native of Cuba, had followed Elvira to Spain, and loved the young\par
Antonia with a Mother’s affection. Flora quitted not the room for a\par
moment while the Abbot remained there: She watched his every word, his\par
every look, his every action. He saw that her suspicious eye was always\par
fixed upon him, and conscious that his designs would not bear\par
inspection so minute, He felt frequently confused and disconcerted. He\par
was aware that She doubted the purity of his intentions; that She would\par
never leave him alone with Antonia, and his Mistress defended by the\par
presence of this vigilant Observer, He despaired of finding the means\par
to gratify his passion.\par
\par
As He quitted the House, Jacintha met him, and begged that some Masses\par
might be sung for the repose of Elvira’s soul, which She doubted not\par
was suffering in Purgatory. He promised not to forget her request; But\par
He perfectly gained the old Woman’s heart by engaging to watch during\par
the whole of the approaching night in the haunted chamber. Jacintha\par
could find no terms sufficiently strong to express her gratitude, and\par
the Monk departed loaded with her benedictions.\par
\par
It was broad day when He returned to the Abbey. His first care was to\par
communicate what had past to his Confident. He felt too sincere a\par
passion for Antonia to have heard unmoved the prediction of her speedy\par
death, and He shuddered at the idea of losing an object so dear to him.\par
Upon this head Matilda reassured him. She confirmed the arguments which\par
Himself had already used: She declared Antonia to have been deceived by\par
the wandering of her brain, by the Spleen which opprest her at the\par
moment, and by the natural turn of her mind to superstition, and the\par
marvellous. As to Jacintha’s account, the absurdity refuted itself; The\par
Abbot hesitated not to believe that She had fabricated the whole story,\par
either confused by terror, or hoping to make him comply more readily\par
with her request. Having overruled the Monk’s apprehensions, Matilda\par
continued thus.\par
\par
“The prediction and the Ghost are equally false; But it must be your\par
care, Ambrosio, to verify the first. Antonia within three days must\par
indeed be dead to the world; But She must live for you.\par
\par
Her present illness, and this fancy which She has taken into her head,\par
will colour a plan which I have long meditated, but which was\par
impracticable without your procuring access to Antonia. She shall be\par
yours, not for a single night, but for ever. All the vigilance of her\par
Duenna shall not avail her: You shall riot unrestrained in the charms\par
of your Mistress. This very day must the scheme be put in execution,\par
for you have no time to lose. The Nephew of the Duke of Medina Celi\par
prepares to demand Antonia for his Bride: In a few days She will be\par
removed to the Palace of her Relation, the Marquis de las Cisternas,\par
and there She will be secure from your attempts. Thus during your\par
absence have I been informed by my Spies, who are ever employed in\par
bringing me intelligence for your service. Now then listen to me. There\par
is a juice extracted from certain herbs, known but to few, which brings\par
on the Person who drinks it the exact image of Death. Let this be\par
administered to Antonia: You may easily find means to pour a few drops\par
into her medicine. The effect will be throwing her into strong\par
convulsions for an hour: After which her blood will gradually cease to\par
flow, and heart to beat; A mortal paleness will spread itself over her\par
features, and She will appear a Corse to every eye. She has no Friends\par
about her: You may charge yourself unsuspected with the superintendence\par
of her funeral, and cause her to be buried in the Vaults of St. Clare.\par
Their solitude and easy access render these Caverns favourable to your\par
designs. Give Antonia the soporific draught this Evening: Eight and\par
forty hours after She has drank it, Life will revive to her bosom. She\par
will then be absolutely in your power: She will find all resistance\par
unavailing, and necessity will compel her to receive you in her arms.”\par
\par
“Antonia will be in my power!” exclaimed the Monk; “Matilda, you\par
transport me! At length then, happiness will be mine, and that\par
happiness will be Matilda’s gift, will be the gift of friendship!\par
\par
I shall clasp Antonia in my arms, far from every prying eye, from every\par
tormenting Intruder! I shall sigh out my soul upon her bosom; Shall\par
teach her young heart the first rudiments of pleasure, and revel\par
uncontrouled in the endless variety of her charms! And shall this\par
delight indeed by mine? Shall I give the reins to my desires, and\par
gratify every wild tumultuous wish? Oh! Matilda, how can I express to\par
you my gratitude?”\par
\par
“By profiting by my counsels. Ambrosio, I live but to serve you:\par
\par
Your interest and happiness are equally mine. Be your person Antonia’s,\par
but to your friendship and your heart I still assert my claim.\par
Contributing to yours forms now my only pleasure. Should my exertions\par
procure the gratification of your wishes, I shall consider my trouble\par
to be amply repaid. But let us lose no time. The liquor of which I\par
spoke is only to be found in St. Clare’s Laboratory. Hasten then to the\par
Prioress; Request of her admission to the Laboratory, and it will not\par
be denied. There is a Closet at the lower end of the great Room, filled\par
with liquids of different colours and qualities. The Bottle in question\par
stands by itself upon the third shelf on the left. It contains a\par
greenish liquor: Fill a small phial with it when you are unobserved,\par
and Antonia is your own.”\par
\par
The Monk hesitated not to adopt this infamous plan. His desires, but\par
too violent before, had acquired fresh vigour from the sight of\par
Antonia. As He sat by her bedside, accident had discovered to him some\par
of those charms which till then had been concealed from him: He found\par
them even more perfect, than his ardent imagination had pictured them.\par
Sometimes her white and polished arm was displayed in arranging the\par
pillow: Sometimes a sudden movement discovered part of her swelling\par
bosom: But whereever the new-found charm presented itself, there rested\par
the Friar’s gloting eyes. Scarcely could He master himself sufficiently\par
to conceal his desires from Antonia and her vigilant Duenna. Inflamed\par
by the remembrance of these beauties, He entered into Matilda’s scheme\par
without hesitation.\par
\par
No sooner were Matins over than He bent his course towards the Convent\par
of St. Clare: His arrival threw the whole Sisterhood into the utmost\par
amazement. The Prioress was sensible of the honour done her Convent by\par
his paying it his first visit, and strove to express her gratitude by\par
every possible attention. He was paraded through the Garden, shown all\par
the reliques of Saints and Martyrs, and treated with as much respect\par
and distinction as had He been the Pope himself. On his part, Ambrosio\par
received the Domina’s civilities very graciously, and strove to remove\par
her surprize at his having broken through his resolution. He stated,\par
that among his penitents, illness prevented many from quitting their\par
Houses. These were exactly the People who most needed his advice and\par
the comforts of Religion: Many representations had been made to him\par
upon this account, and though highly repugnant to his own wishes, He\par
had found it absolutely necessary for the service of heaven to change\par
his determination, and quit his beloved retirement. The Prioress\par
applauded his zeal in his profession and his charity towards Mankind:\par
She declared that Madrid was happy in possessing a Man so perfect and\par
irreproachable. In such discourse, the Friar at length reached the\par
Laboratory. He found the Closet: The Bottle stood in the place which\par
Matilda had described, and the Monk seized an opportunity to fill his\par
phial unobserved with the soporific liquor. Then having partaken of a\par
Collation in the Refectory, He retired from the Convent pleased with\par
the success of his visit, and leaving the Nuns delighted by the honour\par
conferred upon them.\par
\par
He waited till Evening before He took the road to Antonia’s dwelling.\par
Jacintha welcomed him with transport, and besought him not to forget\par
his promise to pass the night in the haunted Chamber: That promise He\par
now repeated. He found Antonia tolerably well, but still harping upon\par
the Ghost’s prediction. Flora moved not from her Lady’s Bed, and by\par
symptoms yet stronger than on the former night testified her dislike to\par
the Abbot’s presence. Still Ambrosio affected not to observe them. The\par
Physician arrived, while He was conversing with Antonia. It was dark\par
already; Lights were called for, and Flora was compelled to descend for\par
them herself. However, as She left a third Person in the room, and\par
expected to be absent but a few minutes, She believed that She risqued\par
nothing in quitting her post. No sooner had She left the room, than\par
Ambrosio moved towards the Table, on which stood Antonia’s medicine: It\par
was placed in a recess of the window. The Physician seated in an\par
armed-chair, and employed in questioning his Patient, paid no attention\par
to the proceedings of the Monk. Ambrosio seized the opportunity: He\par
drew out the fatal Phial, and let a few drops fall into the medicine.\par
He then hastily left the Table, and returned to the seat which He had\par
quitted. When Flora made her appearance with lights, every thing seemed\par
to be exactly as She had left it.\par
\par
The Physician declared that Antonia might quit her chamber the next day\par
with perfect safety. He recommended her following the same prescription\par
which, on the night before, had procured her a refreshing sleep: Flora\par
replied that the draught stood ready upon the Table: He advised the\par
Patient to take it without delay, and then retired. Flora poured the\par
medicine into a Cup and presented it to her Mistress. At that moment\par
Ambrosio’s courage failed him. Might not Matilda have deceived him?\par
Might not Jealousy have persuaded her to destroy her Rival, and\par
substitute poison in the room of an opiate? This idea appeared so\par
reasonable that He was on the point of preventing her from swallowing\par
the medicine. His resolution was adopted too late: The Cup was already\par
emptied, and Antonia restored it into Flora’s hands. No remedy was now\par
to be found: Ambrosio could only expect the moment impatiently,\par
destined to decide upon Antonia’s life or death, upon his own happiness\par
or despair.\par
\par
Dreading to create suspicion by his stay, or betray himself by his\par
mind’s agitation, He took leave of his Victim, and withdrew from the\par
room. Antonia parted from him with less cordiality than on the former\par
night. Flora had represented to her Mistress that to admit his visits\par
was to disobey her Mother’s orders: She described to her his emotion on\par
entering the room, and the fire which sparkled in his eyes while He\par
gazed upon her. This had escaped Antonia’s observation, but not her\par
Attendant’s; Who explaining the Monk’s designs and their probable\par
consequences in terms much clearer than Elvira’s, though not quite so\par
delicate, had succeeded in alarming her young Lady, and persuading her\par
to treat him more distantly than She had done hitherto. The idea of\par
obeying her Mother’s will at once determined Antonia. Though She\par
grieved at losing his society, She conquered herself sufficiently to\par
receive the Monk with some degree of reserve and coldness. She thanked\par
him with respect and gratitude for his former visits, but did not\par
invite his repeating them in future. It now was not the Friar’s\par
interest to solicit admission to her presence, and He took leave of her\par
as if not designing to return. Fully persuaded that the acquaintance\par
which She dreaded was now at an end, Flora was so much worked upon by\par
his easy compliance that She began to doubt the justice of her\par
suspicions. As She lighted him down Stairs, She thanked him for having\par
endeavoured to root out from Antonia’s mind her superstitious terrors\par
of the Spectre’s prediction: She added, that as He seemed interested in\par
Donna Antonia’s welfare, should any change take place in her situation,\par
She would be careful to let him know it. The Monk in replying took\par
pains to raise his voice, hoping that Jacintha would hear it. In this\par
He succeeded; As He reached the foot of the Stairs with his\par
Conductress, the Landlady failed not to make her appearance.\par
\par
“Why surely you are not going away, reverend Father?” cried She; “Did\par
you not promise to pass the night in the haunted Chamber? Christ Jesus!\par
I shall be left alone with the Ghost, and a fine pickle I shall be in\par
by morning! Do all I could, say all I could, that obstinate old Brute,\par
Simon Gonzalez, refused to marry me today; And before tomorrow comes, I\par
suppose, I shall be torn to pieces, by the Ghosts, and Goblins, and\par
Devils, and what not! For God’s sake, your Holiness, do not leave me in\par
such a woeful condition! On my bended knees I beseech you to keep your\par
promise: Watch this night in the haunted chamber; Lay the Apparition in\par
the Red Sea, and Jacintha remembers you in her prayers to the last day\par
of her existence!”\par
\par
This request Ambrosio expected and desired; Yet He affected to raise\par
objections, and to seem unwilling to keep his word. He told Jacintha\par
that the Ghost existed nowhere but in her own brain, and that her\par
insisting upon his staying all night in the House was ridiculous and\par
useless. Jacintha was obstinate: She was not to be convinced, and\par
pressed him so urgently not to leave her a prey to the Devil, that at\par
length He granted her request. All this show of resistance imposed not\par
upon Flora, who was naturally of a suspicious temper. She suspected the\par
Monk to be acting a part very contrary to his own inclinations, and\par
that He wished for no better than to remain where He was. She even went\par
so far as to believe that Jacintha was in his interest; and the poor\par
old Woman was immediately set down, as no better than a Procuress.\par
While She applauded herself for having penetrated into this plot\par
against her Lady’s honour, She resolved in secret to render it\par
fruitless.\par
\par
“So then,” said She to the Abbot with a look half-satirical and half\par
indignant; “So then you mean to stay here tonight? Do so, in God’s\par
name! Nobody will prevent you. Sit up to watch for the Ghost’s arrival:\par
I shall sit up too, and the Lord grant that I may see nothing worse\par
than a Ghost! I quit not Donna Antonia’s Bedside during this blessed\par
night: Let me see any one dare to enter the room, and be He mortal or\par
immortal, be He Ghost, Devil, or Man, I warrant his repenting that ever\par
He crossed the threshold!”\par
\par
This hint was sufficiently strong, and Ambrosio understood its meaning.\par
But instead of showing that He perceived her suspicions; He replied\par
mildly that He approved the Duenna’s precautions, and advised her to\par
persevere in her intention. This, She assured him faithfully that He\par
might depend upon her doing. Jacintha then conducted him into the\par
chamber where the Ghost had appeared, and Flora returned to her Lady’s.\par
\par
Jacintha opened the door of the haunted room with a trembling hand: She\par
ventured to peep in; But the wealth of India would not have tempted her\par
to cross the threshold. She gave the Taper to the Monk, wished him well\par
through the adventure, and hastened to be gone. Ambrosio entered. He\par
bolted the door, placed the light upon the Table, and seated himself in\par
the Chair which on the former night had sustained Antonia. In spite of\par
Matilda’s assurances that the Spectre was a mere creation of fancy, his\par
mind was impressed with a certain mysterious horror. He in vain\par
endeavoured to shake it off. The silence of the night, the story of the\par
Apparition, the chamber wainscotted with dark oak pannells, the\par
recollection which it brought with it of the murdered Elvira, and his\par
incertitude respecting the nature of the drops given by him to Antonia,\par
made him feel uneasy at his present situation. But He thought much less\par
of the Spectre, than of the poison. Should He have destroyed the only\par
object which rendered life dear to him; Should the Ghost’s prediction\par
prove true; Should Antonia in three days be no more, and He the\par
wretched cause of her death ...... The supposition was too horrible to\par
dwell upon. He drove away these dreadful images, and as often they\par
presented themselves again before him. Matilda had assured him that the\par
effects of the Opiate would be speedy. He listened with fear, yet with\par
eagerness, expecting to hear some disturbance in the adjoining chamber.\par
All was still silent. He concluded that the drops had not begun to\par
operate. Great was the stake, for which He now played: A moment would\par
suffice to decide upon his misery or happiness. Matilda had taught him\par
the means of ascertaining that life was not extinct for ever: Upon this\par
assay depended all his hopes. With every instant his impatience\par
redoubled; His terrors grew more lively, his anxiety more awake. Unable\par
to bear this state of incertitude, He endeavoured to divert it by\par
substituting the thoughts of Others to his own. The Books, as was\par
before mentioned, were ranged upon shelves near the Table: This stood\par
exactly opposite to the Bed, which was placed in an Alcove near the\par
Closet door. Ambrosio took down a Volume, and seated himself by the\par
Table: But his attention wandered from the Pages before him. Antonia’s\par
image and that of the murdered Elvira persisted to force themselves\par
before his imagination. Still He continued to read, though his eyes ran\par
over the characters without his mind being conscious of their import.\par
Such was his occupation, when He fancied that He heard a footstep. He\par
turned his head, but nobody was to be seen.\par
\par
He resumed his Book; But in a few minutes after the same sound was\par
repeated, and followed by a rustling noise close behind him. He now\par
started from his seat, and looking round him, perceived the Closet door\par
standing half-unclosed. On his first entering the room He had tried to\par
open it, but found it bolted on the inside.\par
\par
“How is this?” said He to himself; “How comes this door unfastened?”\par
\par
He advanced towards it: He pushed it open, and looked into the closet:\par
No one was there. While He stood irresolute, He thought that He\par
distinguished a groaning in the adjacent chamber: It was Antonia’s, and\par
He supposed that the drops began to take effect: But upon listening\par
more attentively, He found the noise to be caused by Jacintha, who had\par
fallen asleep by the Lady’s Bedside, and was snoring most lustily.\par
Ambrosio drew back, and returned to the other room, musing upon the\par
sudden opening of the Closet door, for which He strove in vain to\par
account.\par
\par
He paced the chamber up and down in silence. At length He stopped, and\par
the Bed attracted his attention. The curtain of the Recess was but\par
half-drawn. He sighed involuntarily.\par
\par
“That Bed,” said He in a low voice, “That Bed was Elvira’s! There has\par
She past many a quiet night, for She was good and innocent. How sound\par
must have been her sleep! And yet now She sleeps sounder! Does She\par
indeed sleep? Oh! God grant that She may! What if She rose from her\par
Grave at this sad and silent hour? What if She broke the bonds of the\par
Tomb, and glided angrily before my blasted eyes? Oh! I never could\par
support the sight! Again to see her form distorted by dying agonies,\par
her blood-swollen veins, her livid countenance, her eyes bursting from\par
their sockets with pain! To hear her speak of future punishment, menace\par
me with Heaven’s vengeance, tax me with the crimes I have committed,\par
with those I am going to commit ..... Great God! What is that?”\par
\par
As He uttered these words, his eyes which were fixed upon the Bed, saw\par
the curtain shaken gently backwards and forwards. The Apparition was\par
recalled to his mind, and He almost fancied that He beheld Elvira’s\par
visionary form reclining upon the Bed. A few moments consideration\par
sufficed to reassure him.\par
\par
“It was only the wind,” said He, recovering himself.\par
\par
Again He paced the chamber; But an involuntary movement of awe and\par
inquietude constantly led his eye towards the Alcove. He drew near it\par
with irresolution. He paused before He ascended the few steps which led\par
to it. He put out his hand thrice to remove the curtain, and as often\par
drew it back.\par
\par
“Absurd terrors!” He cried at length, ashamed of his own weakness——\par
\par
Hastily he mounted the steps; When a Figure drest in white started from\par
the Alcove, and gliding by him, made with precipitation towards the\par
Closet. Madness and despair now supplied the Monk with that courage, of\par
which He had till then been destitute. He flew down the steps, pursued\par
the Apparition, and attempted to grasp it.\par
\par
“Ghost, or Devil, I hold you!” He exclaimed, and seized the Spectre by\par
the arm.\par
\par
“Oh! Christ Jesus!” cried a shrill voice; “Holy Father, how you gripe\par
me! I protest that I meant no harm!”\par
\par
This address, as well as the arm which He held, convinced the Abbot\par
that the supposed Ghost was substantial flesh and blood. He drew the\par
Intruder towards the Table, and holding up the light, discovered the\par
features of ...... Madona Flora!\par
\par
Incensed at having been betrayed by this trifling cause into fears so\par
ridiculous, He asked her sternly, what business had brought her to that\par
chamber. Flora, ashamed at being found out, and terrified at the\par
severity of Ambrosio’s looks, fell upon her knees, and promised to make\par
a full confession.\par
\par
“I protest, reverend Father,” said She, “that I am quite grieved at\par
having disturbed you: Nothing was further from my intention. I meant to\par
get out of the room as quietly as I got in; and had you been ignorant\par
that I watched you, you know, it would have been the same thing as if I\par
had not watched you at all. To be sure, I did very wrong in being a Spy\par
upon you, that I cannot deny; But Lord! your Reverence, how can a poor\par
weak Woman resist curiosity? Mine was so strong to know what you were\par
doing, that I could not but try to get a little peep, without any body\par
knowing any thing about it. So with that I left old Dame Jacintha\par
sitting by my Lady’s Bed, and I ventured to steal into the Closet.\par
Being unwilling to interrupt you, I contented myself at first with\par
putting my eye to the Keyhole; But as I could see nothing by this\par
means, I undrew the bolt, and while your back was turned to the Alcove,\par
I whipt me in softly and silently. Here I lay snug behind the curtain,\par
till your Reverence found me out, and seized me ere I had time to\par
regain the Closet door. This is the whole truth, I assure you, Holy\par
Father, and I beg your pardon a thousand times for my impertinence.”\par
\par
During this speech the Abbot had time to recollect himself: He was\par
satisfied with reading the penitent Spy a lecture upon the dangers of\par
curiosity, and the meanness of the action in which She had been just\par
discovered. Flora declared herself fully persuaded that She had done\par
wrong; She promised never to be guilty of the same fault again, and was\par
retiring very humble and contrite to Antonia’s chamber, when the Closet\par
door was suddenly thrown open, and in rushed Jacintha pale and out of\par
breath.\par
\par
“Oh! Father! Father!” She cried in a voice almost choaked with terror;\par
“What shall I do! What shall I do! Here is a fine piece of work!\par
Nothing but misfortunes! Nothing but dead people, and dying people! Oh!\par
I shall go distracted! I shall go distracted!”\par
\par
“Speak! Speak!” cried Flora and the Monk at the same time; “What has\par
happened? What is the matter?”\par
\par
“Oh! I shall have another Corse in my House! Some Witch has certainly\par
cast a spell upon it, upon me, and upon all about me! Poor Donna\par
Antonia! There She lies in just such convulsions, as killed her Mother!\par
The Ghost told her true! I am sure, the Ghost has told her true!”\par
\par
Flora ran, or rather flew to her Lady’s chamber: Ambrosio followed her,\par
his bosom trembling with hope and apprehension. They found Antonia as\par
Jacintha had described, torn by racking convulsions from which they in\par
vain endeavoured to relieve her. The Monk dispatched Jacintha to the\par
Abbey in all haste, and commissioned her to bring Father Pablos back\par
with her, without losing a moment.\par
\par
“I will go for him,” replied Jacintha, “and tell him to come hither;\par
But as to bringing him myself, I shall do no such thing. I am sure that\par
the House is bewitched, and burn me if ever I set foot in it again.”\par
\par
With this resolution She set out for the Monastery, and delivered to\par
Father Pablos the Abbot’s orders. She then betook herself to the House\par
of old Simon Gonzalez, whom She resolved never to quit, till She had\par
made him her Husband, and his dwelling her own.\par
\par
Father Pablos had no sooner beheld Antonia, than He pronounced her\par
incurable. The convulsions continued for an hour: During that time her\par
agonies were much milder than those which her groans created in the\par
Abbot’s heart. Her every pang seemed a dagger in his bosom, and He\par
cursed himself a thousand times for having adopted so barbarous a\par
project. The hour being expired, by degrees the Fits became less\par
frequent, and Antonia less agitated. She felt that her dissolution was\par
approaching, and that nothing could save her.\par
\par
“Worthy Ambrosio,” She said in a feeble voice, while She pressed his\par
hand to her lips; “I am now at liberty to express, how grateful is my\par
heart for your attention and kindness. I am upon the bed of death; Yet\par
an hour, and I shall be no more. I may therefore acknowledge without\par
restraint, that to relinquish your society was very painful to me: But\par
such was the will of a Parent, and I dared not disobey. I die without\par
repugnance: There are few, who will lament my leaving them; There are\par
few, whom I lament to leave. Among those few, I lament for none more\par
than for yourself; But we shall meet again, Ambrosio! We shall one day\par
meet in heaven: There shall our friendship be renewed, and my Mother\par
shall view it with pleasure!”\par
\par
She paused. The Abbot shuddered when She mentioned Elvira: Antonia\par
imputed his emotion to pity and concern for her.\par
\par
“You are grieved for me, Father,” She continued; “Ah! sigh not for my\par
loss. I have no crimes to repent, at least none of which I am\par
conscious, and I restore my soul without fear to him from whom I\par
received it. I have but few requests to make: Yet let me hope that what\par
few I have shall be granted. Let a solemn Mass be said for my soul’s\par
repose, and another for that of my beloved Mother. Not that I doubt her\par
resting in her Grave: I am now convinced that my reason wandered, and\par
the falsehood of the Ghost’s prediction is sufficient to prove my\par
error. But every one has some failing: My Mother may have had hers,\par
though I knew them not: I therefore wish a Mass to be celebrated for\par
her repose, and the expence may be defrayed by the little wealth of\par
which I am possessed. Whatever may then remain, I bequeath to my Aunt\par
Leonella. When I am dead, let the Marquis de las Cisternas know that\par
his Brother’s unhappy family can no longer importune him. But\par
disappointment makes me unjust: They tell me that He is ill, and\par
perhaps had it been in his power, He wished to have protected me. Tell\par
him then, Father, only that I am dead, and that if He had any faults to\par
me, I forgave him from my heart. This done, I have nothing more to ask\par
for, than your prayers: Promise to remember my requests, and I shall\par
resign my life without a pang or sorrow.”\par
\par
Ambrosio engaged to comply with her desires, and proceeded to give her\par
absolution. Every moment announced the approach of Antonia’s fate: Her\par
sight failed; Her heart beat sluggishly; Her fingers stiffened, and\par
grew cold, and at two in the morning She expired without a groan. As\par
soon as the breath had forsaken her body, Father Pablos retired,\par
sincerely affected at the melancholy scene. On her part, Flora gave way\par
to the most unbridled sorrow.\par
\par
Far different concerns employed Ambrosio: He sought for the pulse whose\par
throbbing, so Matilda had assured him, would prove Antonia’s death but\par
temporal. He found it; He pressed it; It palpitated beneath his hand,\par
and his heart was filled with ecstacy. However, He carefully concealed\par
his satisfaction at the success of his plan. He assumed a melancholy\par
air, and addressing himself to Flora, warned her against abandoning\par
herself to fruitless sorrow. Her tears were too sincere to permit her\par
listening to his counsels, and She continued to weep unceasingly.\par
\par
The Friar withdrew, first promising to give orders himself about the\par
Funeral, which, out of consideration for Jacintha as He pretended,\par
should take place with all expedition. Plunged in grief for the loss of\par
her beloved Mistress, Flora scarcely attended to what He said. Ambrosio\par
hastened to command the Burial. He obtained permission from the\par
Prioress, that the Corse should be deposited in St. Clare’s Sepulchre:\par
and on the Friday Morning, every proper and needful ceremony being\par
performed, Antonia’s body was committed to the Tomb.\par
\par
On the same day Leonella arrived at Madrid, intending to present her\par
young Husband to Elvira. Various circumstances had obliged her to defer\par
her journey from Tuesday to Friday, and She had no opportunity of\par
making this alteration in her plans known to her Sister. As her heart\par
was truly affectionate, and as She had ever entertained a sincere\par
regard for Elvira and her Daughter, her surprize at hearing of their\par
sudden and melancholy fate was fully equalled by her sorrow and\par
disappointment. Ambrosio sent to inform her of Antonia’s bequest: At\par
her solication, He promised, as soon as Elvira’s trifling debts were\par
discharged, to transmit to her the remainder. This being settled, no\par
other business detained Leonella in Madrid, and She returned to Cordova\par
with all diligence.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER X.\par
\par
\par
Oh! could I worship aught beneath the skies\par
That earth hath seen or fancy could devise,\par
Thine altar, sacred Liberty, should stand,\par
Built by no mercenary vulgar hand,\par
With fragrant turf, and flowers as wild and fair,\par
As ever dressed a bank, or scented summer air.\par
\par
COWPER.\par
\par
His whole attention bent upon bringing to justice the Assassins of his\par
Sister, Lorenzo little thought how severely his interest was suffering\par
in another quarter. As was before mentioned, He returned not to Madrid\par
till the evening of that day on which Antonia was buried. Signifying to\par
the Grand Inquisitor the order of the Cardinal-Duke (a ceremony not to\par
be neglected, when a Member of the Church was to be arrested publicly)\par
communicating his design to his Uncle and Don Ramirez, and assembling a\par
troop of Attendants sufficiently to prevent opposition, furnished him\par
with full occupation during the few hours preceding midnight.\par
Consequently, He had no opportunity to enquire about his Mistress, and\par
was perfectly ignorant both of her death and her Mother’s.\par
\par
The Marquis was by no means out of danger: His delirium was gone, but\par
had left him so much exhausted that the Physicians declined pronouncing\par
upon the consequences likely to ensue. As for Raymond himself, He\par
wished for nothing more earnestly than to join Agnes in the grave.\par
Existence was hateful to him: He saw nothing in the world deserving his\par
attention; and He hoped to hear that Agnes was revenged, and himself\par
given over in the same moment.\par
\par
Followed by Raymond’s ardent prayers for success, Lorenzo was at the\par
Gates of St. Clare a full hour before the time appointed by the Mother\par
St. Ursula. He was accompanied by his Uncle, by Don Ramirez de Mello,\par
and a party of chosen Archers. Though in considerable numbers their\par
appearance created no surprize: A great Crowd was already assembled\par
before the Convent doors, in order to witness the Procession. It was\par
naturally supposed that Lorenzo and his Attendants were conducted\par
thither by the same design. The Duke of Medina being recognised, the\par
People drew back, and made way for his party to advance. Lorenzo placed\par
himself opposite to the great Gate, through which the Pilgrims were to\par
pass. Convinced that the Prioress could not escape him, He waited\par
patiently for her appearance, which She was expected to make exactly at\par
Midnight.\par
\par
The Nuns were employed in religious duties established in honour of St.\par
Clare, and to which no Prophane was ever admitted. The Chapel windows\par
were illuminated. As they stood on the outside, the Auditors heard the\par
full swell of the organ, accompanied by a chorus of female voices, rise\par
upon the stillness of the night. This died away, and was succeeded by a\par
single strain of harmony: It was the voice of her who was destined to\par
sustain in the procession the character of St. Clare. For this office\par
the most beautiful Virgin of Madrid was always selected, and She upon\par
whom the choice fell esteemed it as the highest of honours. While\par
listening to the Music, whose melody distance only seemed to render\par
sweeter, the Audience was wrapped up in profound attention. Universal\par
silence prevailed through the Crowd, and every heart was filled with\par
reverence for religion. Every heart but Lorenzo’s. Conscious that among\par
those who chaunted the praises of their God so sweetly, there were some\par
who cloaked with devotion the foulest sins, their hymns inspired him\par
with detestation at their Hypocrisy. He had long observed with\par
disapprobation and contempt the superstition which governed Madrid’s\par
Inhabitants. His good sense had pointed out to him the artifices of the\par
Monks, and the gross absurdity of their miracles, wonders, and\par
supposititious reliques. He blushed to see his Countrymen the Dupes of\par
deceptions so ridiculous, and only wished for an opportunity to free\par
them from their monkish fetters. That opportunity, so long desired in\par
vain, was at length presented to him. He resolved not to let it slip,\par
but to set before the People in glaring colours how enormous were the\par
abuses but too frequently practised in Monasteries, and how unjustly\par
public esteem was bestowed indiscriminately upon all who wore a\par
religious habit. He longed for the moment destined to unmask the\par
Hypocrites, and convince his Countrymen that a sanctified exterior does\par
not always hide a virtuous heart.\par
\par
The service lasted, till Midnight was announced by the Convent Bell.\par
That sound being heard, the Music ceased: The voices died away softly,\par
and soon after the lights disappeared from the Chapel windows.\par
Lorenzo’s heart beat high, when He found the execution of his plan to\par
be at hand. From the natural superstition of the People He had prepared\par
himself for some resistance. But He trusted that the Mother St. Ursula\par
would bring good reasons to justify his proceeding. He had force with\par
him to repel the first impulse of the Populace, till his arguments\par
should be heard: His only fear was lest the Domina, suspecting his\par
design, should have spirited away the Nun on whose deposition every\par
thing depended. Unless the Mother St. Ursula should be present, He\par
could only accuse the Prioress upon suspicion; and this reflection gave\par
him some little apprehension for the success of his enterprize. The\par
tranquillity which seemed to reign through the Convent in some degree\par
re-assured him: Still He expected the moment eagerly, when the presence\par
of his Ally should deprive him of the power of doubting.\par
\par
The Abbey of Capuchins was only separated from the Convent by the\par
Garden and Cemetery. The Monks had been invited to assist at the\par
Pilgrimage. They now arrived, marching two by two with lighted Torches\par
in their hands, and chaunting Hymns in honour of St. Clare. Father\par
Pablos was at their head, the Abbot having excused himself from\par
attending. The people made way for the holy Train, and the Friars\par
placed themselves in ranks on either side of the great Gates. A few\par
minutes sufficed to arrange the order of the Procession. This being\par
settled, the Convent doors were thrown open, and again the female\par
Chorus sounded in full melody. First appeared a Band of Choristers: As\par
soon as they had passed, the Monks fell in two by two, and followed\par
with steps slow and measured. Next came the Novices; They bore no\par
Tapers, as did the Professed, but moved on with eyes bent downwards,\par
and seemed to be occupied by telling their Beads. To them succeeded a\par
young and lovely Girl, who represented St. Lucia: She held a golden\par
bason in which were two eyes: Her own were covered by a velvet bandage,\par
and She was conducted by another Nun habited as an Angel. She was\par
followed by St. Catherine, a palm-branch in one hand, a flaming Sword\par
in the other: She was robed in white, and her brow was ornamented with\par
a sparkling Diadem. After her appeared St. Genevieve, surrounded by a\par
number of Imps, who putting themselves into grotesque attitudes,\par
drawing her by the robe, and sporting round her with antic gestures,\par
endeavoured to distract her attention from the Book, on which her eyes\par
were constantly fixed. These merry Devils greatly entertained the\par
Spectators, who testified their pleasure by repeated bursts of\par
Laughter. The Prioress had been careful to select a Nun whose\par
disposition was naturally solemn and saturnine. She had every reason to\par
be satisfied with her choice: The drolleries of the Imps were entirely\par
thrown away, and St. Genevieve moved on without discomposing a muscle.\par
\par
Each of these Saints was separated from the Other by a band of\par
Choristers, exalting her praise in their Hymns, but declaring her to be\par
very much inferior to St. Clare, the Convent’s avowed Patroness. These\par
having passed, a long train of Nuns appeared, bearing like the\par
Choristers each a burning Taper. Next came the reliques of St. Clare,\par
inclosed in vases equally precious for their materials and workmanship:\par
But they attracted not Lorenzo’s attention. The Nun who bore the heart\par
occupied him entirely. According to Theodore’s description, He doubted\par
not her being the Mother St. Ursula. She seemed to look round with\par
anxiety. As He stood foremost in the rank by which the procession past,\par
her eye caught Lorenzo’s. A flush of joy overspread her till then\par
pallid cheek. She turned to her Companion eagerly.\par
\par
“We are safe!” He heard her whisper; “’tis her Brother!”\par
\par
His heart being now at ease, Lorenzo gazed with tranquillity upon the\par
remainder of the show. Now appeared its most brilliant ornament. It was\par
a Machine fashioned like a throne, rich with jewels and dazzling with\par
light. It rolled onwards upon concealed wheels, and was guided by\par
several lovely Children, dressed as Seraphs. The summit was covered\par
with silver clouds, upon which reclined the most beautiful form that\par
eyes ever witnessed. It was a Damsel representing St. Clare: Her dress\par
was of inestimable price, and round her head a wreath of Diamonds\par
formed an artificial glory: But all these ornaments yielded to the\par
lustre of her charms. As She advanced, a murmur of delight ran through\par
the Crowd. Even Lorenzo confessed secretly, that He never beheld more\par
perfect beauty, and had not his heart been Antonia’s, it must have\par
fallen a sacrifice to this enchanting Girl. As it was, He considered\par
her only as a fine Statue: She obtained from him no tribute save cold\par
admiration, and when She had passed him, He thought of her no more.\par
\par
“Who is She?” asked a By-stander in Lorenzo’s hearing.\par
\par
“One whose beauty you must often have heard celebrated. Her name is\par
Virginia de Villa-Franca: She is a Pensioner of St. Clare’s Convent, a\par
Relation of the Prioress, and has been selected with justice as the\par
ornament of the Procession.”\par
\par
The Throne moved onwards. It was followed by the Prioress herself: She\par
marched at the head of the remaining Nuns with a devout and sanctified\par
air, and closed the procession. She moved on slowly: Her eyes were\par
raised to heaven: Her countenance calm and tranquil seemed abstracted\par
from all sublunary things, and no feature betrayed her secret pride at\par
displaying the pomp and opulence of her Convent. She passed along,\par
accompanied by the prayers and benedictions of the Populace: But how\par
great was the general confusion and surprize, when Don Ramirez starting\par
forward, challenged her as his Prisoner.\par
\par
For a moment amazement held the Domina silent and immoveable: But no\par
sooner did She recover herself, than She exclaimed against sacrilege\par
and impiety, and called the People to rescue a Daughter of the Church.\par
They were eagerly preparing to obey her; when Don Ramirez, protected by\par
the Archers from their rage, commanded them to forbear, and threatened\par
them with the severest vengeance of the Inquisition. At that dreaded\par
word every arm fell, every sword shrunk back into its scabbard. The\par
Prioress herself turned pale, and trembled. The general silence\par
convinced her that She had nothing to hope but from innocence, and She\par
besought Don Ramirez in a faultering voice, to inform her of what crime\par
She was accused.\par
\par
“That you shall know in time,” replied He; “But first I must secure the\par
Mother St. Ursula.”\par
\par
“The Mother St. Ursula?” repeated the Domina faintly.\par
\par
At this moment casting her eyes round, She saw near her Lorenzo and the\par
Duke, who had followed Don Ramirez.\par
\par
“Ah! great God!” She cried, clasping her hands together with a frantic\par
air; “I am betrayed!”\par
\par
“Betrayed?” replied St. Ursula, who now arrived conducted by some of\par
the Archers, and followed by the Nun her Companion in the procession:\par
“Not betrayed, but discovered. In me recognise your Accuser: You know\par
not how well I am instructed in your guilt!—Segnor!” She continued,\par
turning to Don Ramirez; “I commit myself to your custody. I charge the\par
Prioress of St. Clare with murder, and stake my life for the justice of\par
my accusation.”\par
\par
A general cry of surprize was uttered by the whole Audience, and an\par
explanation was demanded loudly. The trembling Nuns, terrified at the\par
noise and universal confusion, had dispersed, and fled different ways.\par
Some regained the Convent; Others sought refuge in the dwellings of\par
their Relations; and Many, only sensible of their present danger, and\par
anxious to escape from the tumult, ran through the Streets, and\par
wandered, they knew not whither. The lovely Virginia was one of the\par
first to fly: And in order that She might be better seen and heard, the\par
People desired that St. Ursula should harangue them from the vacant\par
Throne. The Nun complied; She ascended the glittering Machine, and then\par
addressed the surrounding multitude as follows.\par
\par
“However strange and unseemly may appear my conduct, when considered to\par
be adopted by a Female and a Nun, necessity will justify it most fully.\par
A secret, an horrible secret weighs heavy upon my soul: No rest can be\par
mine till I have revealed it to the world, and satisfied that innocent\par
blood which calls from the Grave for vengeance. Much have I dared to\par
gain this opportunity of lightening my conscience. Had I failed in my\par
attempt to reveal the crime, had the Domina but suspected that the\par
mystery was none to me, my ruin was inevitable. Angels who watch\par
unceasingly over those who deserve their favour, have enabled me to\par
escape detection: I am now at liberty to relate a Tale, whose\par
circumstances will freeze every honest soul with horror. Mine is the\par
task to rend the veil from Hypocrisy, and show misguided Parents to\par
what dangers the Woman is exposed, who falls under the sway of a\par
monastic Tyrant.\par
\par
“Among the Votaries of St. Clare, none was more lovely, none more\par
gentle, than Agnes de Medina. I knew her well; She entrusted to me\par
every secret of her heart; I was her Friend and Confident, and I loved\par
her with sincere affection. Nor was I singular in my attachment. Her\par
piety unfeigned, her willingness to oblige, and her angelic\par
disposition, rendered her the Darling of all that was estimable in the\par
Convent. The Prioress herself, proud, scrupulous and forbidding, could\par
not refuse Agnes that tribute of approbation which She bestowed upon no\par
one else. Every one has some fault: Alas! Agnes had her weakness! She\par
violated the laws of our order, and incurred the inveterate hate of the\par
unforgiving Domina. St. Clare’s rules are severe: But grown antiquated\par
and neglected, many of late years have either been forgotten, or\par
changed by universal consent into milder punishments. The penance,\par
adjudged to the crime of Agnes, was most cruel, most inhuman! The law\par
had been long exploded: Alas! It still existed, and the revengeful\par
Prioress now determined to revive it.\par
\par
This law decreed that the Offender should be plunged into a private\par
dungeon, expressly constituted to hide from the world for ever the\par
Victim of Cruelty and tyrannic superstition. In this dreadful abode She\par
was to lead a perpetual solitude, deprived of all society, and believed\par
to be dead by those whom affection might have prompted to attempt her\par
rescue. Thus was She to languish out the remainder of her days, with no\par
other food than bread and water, and no other comfort than the free\par
indulgence of her tears.”\par
\par
The indignation created by this account was so violent, as for some\par
moments to interrupt St. Ursula’s narrative. When the disturbance\par
ceased, and silence again prevailed through the Assembly, She continued\par
her discourse, while at every word the Domina’s countenance betrayed\par
her increasing terrors.\par
\par
“A council of the twelve elder nuns was called: I was of the number.\par
The Prioress in exaggerated colours described the offence of Agnes, and\par
scrupled not to propose the revival of this almost forgotten law. To\par
the shame of our sex be it spoken, that either so absolute was the\par
Domina’s will in the Convent, or so much had disappointment, solitude,\par
and self-denial hardened their hearts and soured their tempers that\par
this barbarous proposal was assented to by nine voices out of the\par
twelve. I was not one of the nine. Frequent opportunities had convinced\par
me of the virtues of Agnes, and I loved and pitied her most sincerely.\par
The Mothers Bertha and Cornelia joined my party: We made the strongest\par
opposition possible, and the Superior found herself compelled to change\par
her intention. In spite of the majority in her favour, She feared to\par
break with us openly. She knew that supported by the Medina family, our\par
forces would be too strong for her to cope with: And She also knew that\par
after being once imprisoned and supposed dead, should Agnes be\par
discovered, her ruin would be inevitable. She therefore gave up her\par
design, though which much reluctance. She demanded some days to reflect\par
upon a mode of punishment which might be agreeable to the whole\par
Community; and She promised, that as soon as her resolution was fixed,\par
the same Council should be again summoned. Two days passed away: On the\par
Evening of the Third it was announced that on the next day Agnes should\par
be examined; and that according to her behaviour on that occasion, her\par
punishment should be either strengthened or mitigated.\par
\par
“On the night preceding this examination, I stole to the Cell of Agnes\par
at an hour when I supposed the other Nuns to be buried in sleep. I\par
comforted her to the best of my power: I bad her take courage, told her\par
to rely upon the support of her friends, and taught her certain signs,\par
by which I might instruct her to answer the Domina’s questions by an\par
assent or negative. Conscious that her Enemy would strive to confuse,\par
embarrass, and daunt her, I feared her being ensnared into some\par
confession prejudicial to her interests. Being anxious to keep my visit\par
secret, I stayed with Agnes but a short time. I bad her not let her\par
spirits be cast down; I mingled my tears with those which streamed down\par
her cheek, embraced her fondly, and was on the point of retiring, when\par
I heard the sound of steps approaching the Cell. I started back. A\par
Curtain which veiled a large Crucifix offered me a retreat, and I\par
hastened to place myself behind it. The door opened. The Prioress\par
entered, followed by four other Nuns. They advanced towards the bed of\par
Agnes. The Superior reproached her with her errors in the bitterest\par
terms: She told her that She was a disgrace to the Convent, that She\par
was resolved to deliver the world and herself from such a Monster, and\par
commanded her to drink the contents of a Goblet now presented to her by\par
one of the Nuns. Aware of the fatal properties of the liquor, and\par
trembling to find herself upon the brink of Eternity, the unhappy Girl\par
strove to excite the Domina’s pity by the most affecting prayers.\par
\par
She sued for life in terms which might have melted the heart of a\par
Fiend: She promised to submit patiently to any punishment, to shame,\par
imprisonment, and torture, might She but be permitted to live! Oh!\par
might She but live another month, or week, or day! Her merciless Enemy\par
listened to her complaints unmoved: She told her that at first She\par
meant to have spared her life, and that if She had altered her\par
intention, She had to thank the opposition of her Friends. She\par
continued to insist upon her swallowing the poison: She bad her\par
recommend herself to the Almighty’s mercy, not to hers, and assured her\par
that in an hour She would be numbered with the Dead. Perceiving that it\par
was vain to implore this unfeeling Woman, She attempted to spring from\par
her bed, and call for assistance: She hoped, if She could not escape\par
the fate announced to her, at least to have witnesses of the violence\par
committed. The Prioress guessed her design. She seized her forcibly by\par
the arm, and pushed her back upon her pillow. At the same time drawing\par
a dagger, and placing it at the breast of the unfortunate Agnes, She\par
protested that if She uttered a single cry, or hesitated a single\par
moment to drink the poison, She would pierce her heart that instant.\par
Already half-dead with fear, She could make no further resistance. The\par
Nun approached with the fatal Goblet. The Domina obliged her to take\par
it, and swallow the contents. She drank, and the horrid deed was\par
accomplished. The Nuns then seated themselves round the Bed. They\par
answered her groans with reproaches; They interrupted with sarcasms the\par
prayers in which She recommended her parting soul to mercy: They\par
threatened her with heaven’s vengeance and eternal perdition: They bad\par
her despair of pardon, and strowed with yet sharper thorns Death’s\par
painful pillow. Such were the sufferings of this young Unfortunate,\par
till released by fate from the malice of her Tormentors. She expired in\par
horror of the past, in fears for the future; and her agonies were such\par
as must have amply gratified the hate and vengeance of her Enemies. As\par
soon as her Victim ceased to breathe, the Domina retired, and was\par
followed by her Accomplices.\par
\par
“It was now that I ventured from my concealment. I dared not to assist\par
my unhappy Friend, aware that without preserving her, I should only\par
have brought on myself the same destruction. Shocked and terrified\par
beyond expression at this horrid scene, scarcely had I sufficient\par
strength to regain my Cell. As I reached the door of that of Agnes, I\par
ventured to look towards the bed, on which lay her lifeless body, once\par
so lovely and so sweet! I breathed a prayer for her departed Spirit,\par
and vowed to revenge her death by the shame and punishment of her\par
Assassins. With danger and difficulty have I kept my oath. I unwarily\par
dropped some words at the funeral of Agnes, while thrown off my guard\par
by excessive grief, which alarmed the guilty conscience of the\par
Prioress. My every action was observed; My every step was traced. I was\par
constantly surrounded by the Superior’s spies. It was long before I\par
could find the means of conveying to the unhappy Girl’s Relations an\par
intimation of my secret. It was given out that Agnes had expired\par
suddenly: This account was credited not only by her Friends in Madrid,\par
but even by those within the Convent. The poison had left no marks upon\par
her body: No one suspected the true cause of her death, and it remained\par
unknown to all, save the Assassins and Myself.\par
\par
“I have no more to say: for what I have already said, I will answer\par
with my life. I repeat that the Prioress is a Murderess; that she has\par
driven from the world, perhaps from heaven, an Unfortunate whose\par
offence was light and venial; that She has abused the power intrusted\par
to her hands, and has been a Tyrant, a Barbarian, and an Hypocrite. I\par
also accuse the four Nuns, Violante, Camilla, Alix, and Mariana, as\par
being her Accomplices, and equally criminal.”\par
\par
Here St. Ursula ended her narrative. It created horror and surprize\par
throughout: But when She related the inhuman murder of Agnes, the\par
indignation of the Mob was so audibly testified, that it was scarcely\par
possible to hear the conclusion. This confusion increased with every\par
moment: At length a multitude of voices exclaimed that the Prioress\par
should be given up to their fury. To this Don Ramirez refused to\par
consent positively. Even Lorenzo bad the People remember that She had\par
undergone no trial, and advised them to leave her punishment to the\par
Inquisition. All representations were fruitless: The disturbance grew\par
still more violent, and the Populace more exasperated. In vain did\par
Ramirez attempt to convey his Prisoner out of the Throng. Wherever He\par
turned, a band of Rioters barred his passage, and demanded her being\par
delivered over to them more loudly than before. Ramirez ordered his\par
Attendants to cut their way through the multitude: Oppressed by\par
numbers, it was impossible for them to draw their swords. He threatened\par
the Mob with the vengeance of the Inquisition: But in this moment of\par
popular phrenzy even this dreadful name had lost its effect. Though\par
regret for his Sister made him look upon the Prioress with abhorrence,\par
Lorenzo could not help pitying a Woman in a situation so terrible: But\par
in spite of all his exertions, and those of the Duke, of Don Ramirez,\par
and the Archers, the People continued to press onwards. They forced a\par
passage through the Guards who protected their destined Victim, dragged\par
her from her shelter, and proceeded to take upon her a most summary and\par
cruel vengeance. Wild with terror, and scarcely knowing what She said,\par
the wretched Woman shrieked for a moment’s mercy: She protested that\par
She was innocent of the death of Agnes, and could clear herself from\par
the suspicion beyond the power of doubt. The Rioters heeded nothing but\par
the gratification of their barbarous vengeance. They refused to listen\par
to her: They showed her every sort of insult, loaded her with mud and\par
filth, and called her by the most opprobrious appellations. They tore\par
her one from another, and each new Tormentor was more savage than the\par
former. They stifled with howls and execrations her shrill cries for\par
mercy; and dragged her through the Streets, spurning her, trampling\par
her, and treating her with every species of cruelty which hate or\par
vindictive fury could invent. At length a Flint, aimed by some\par
well-directing hand, struck her full upon the temple. She sank upon the\par
ground bathed in blood, and in a few minutes terminated her miserable\par
existence. Yet though She no longer felt their insults, the Rioters\par
still exercised their impotent rage upon her lifeless body. They beat\par
it, trod upon it, and ill-used it, till it became no more than a mass\par
of flesh, unsightly, shapeless, and disgusting.\par
\par
Unable to prevent this shocking event, Lorenzo and his Friends had\par
beheld it with the utmost horror: But they were rouzed from their\par
compelled inactivity, on hearing that the Mob was attacking the Convent\par
of St. Clare. The incensed Populace, confounding the innocent with the\par
guilty, had resolved to sacrifice all the Nuns of that order to their\par
rage, and not to leave one stone of the building upon another. Alarmed\par
at this intelligence, they hastened to the Convent, resolved to defend\par
it if possible, or at least to rescue the Inhabitants from the fury of\par
the Rioters. Most of the Nuns had fled, but a few still remained in\par
their habitation. Their situation was truly dangerous. However, as they\par
had taken the precaution of fastening the inner Gates, with this\par
assistance Lorenzo hoped to repel the Mob, till Don Ramirez should\par
return to him with a more sufficient force.\par
\par
Having been conducted by the former disturbance to the distance of some\par
Streets from the Convent, He did not immediately reach it: When He\par
arrived, the throng surrounding it was so excessive as to prevent his\par
approaching the Gates. In the interim, the Populace besieged the\par
Building with persevering rage: They battered the walls, threw lighted\par
torches in at the windows, and swore that by break of day not a Nun of\par
St. Clare’s order should be left alive. Lorenzo had just succeeded in\par
piercing his way through the Crowd, when one of the Gates was forced\par
open. The Rioters poured into the interior part of the Building, where\par
they exercised their vengeance upon every thing which found itself in\par
their passage. They broke the furniture into pieces, tore down the\par
pictures, destroyed the reliques, and in their hatred of her Servant\par
forgot all respect to the Saint. Some employed themselves in searching\par
out the Nuns, Others in pulling down parts of the Convent, and Others\par
again in setting fire to the pictures and valuable furniture which it\par
contained. These Latter produced the most decisive desolation: Indeed\par
the consequences of their action were more sudden than themselves had\par
expected or wished. The Flames rising from the burning piles caught\par
part of the Building, which being old and dry, the conflagration spread\par
with rapidity from room to room. The Walls were soon shaken by the\par
devouring element: The Columns gave way: The Roofs came tumbling down\par
upon the Rioters, and crushed many of them beneath their weight.\par
Nothing was to be heard but shrieks and groans; The Convent was wrapped\par
in flames, and the whole presented a scene of devastation and horror.\par
\par
Lorenzo was shocked at having been the cause, however innocent, of this\par
frightful disturbance: He endeavoured to repair his fault by protecting\par
the helpless Inhabitants of the Convent. He entered it with the Mob,\par
and exerted himself to repress the prevailing Fury, till the sudden and\par
alarming progress of the flames compelled him to provide for his own\par
safety. The People now hurried out, as eagerly as they had before\par
thronged in; But their numbers clogging up the doorway, and the fire\par
gaining upon them rapidly, many of them perished ere they had time to\par
effect their escape. Lorenzo’s good fortune directed him to a small\par
door in a farther Aisle of the Chapel. The bolt was already undrawn: He\par
opened the door, and found himself at the foot of St. Clare’s\par
Sepulchre.\par
\par
Here he stopped to breathe. The Duke and some of his Attendants had\par
followed him, and thus were in security for the present. They now\par
consulted, what steps they should take to escape from this scene of\par
disturbance: But their deliberations were considerably interrupted by\par
the sight of volumes of fire rising from amidst the Convent’s massy\par
walls, by the noise of some heavy Arch tumbling down in ruins, or by\par
the mingled shrieks of the Nuns and Rioters, either suffocating in the\par
press, perishing in the flames, or crushed beneath the weight of the\par
falling Mansion.\par
\par
Lorenzo enquired, whither the Wicket led? He was answered, to the\par
Garden of the Capuchins, and it was resolved to explore an outlet upon\par
that side. Accordingly the Duke raised the Latch, and passed into the\par
adjoining Cemetery. The Attendants followed without ceremony. Lorenzo,\par
being the last, was also on the point of quitting the Colonnade, when\par
He saw the door of the Sepulchre opened softly. Someone looked out, but\par
on perceiving Strangers uttered a loud shriek, started back again, and\par
flew down the marble Stairs.\par
\par
“What can this mean?” cried Lorenzo; “Here is some mystery concealed.\par
Follow me without delay!”\par
\par
Thus saying, He hastened into the Sepulchre, and pursued the person who\par
continued to fly before him. The Duke knew not the cause of his\par
exclamation, but supposing that He had good reasons for it, he followed\par
him without hesitation. The Others did the same, and the whole Party\par
soon arrived at the foot of the Stairs.\par
\par
The upper door having been left open, the neighbouring flames darted\par
from above a sufficient light to enable Lorenzo’s catching a glance of\par
the Fugitive running through the long passages and distant Vaults: But\par
when a sudden turn deprived him of this assistance, total darkness\par
succeeded, and He could only trace the object of his enquiry by the\par
faint echo of retiring feet. The Pursuers were now compelled to proceed\par
with caution: As well as they could judge, the Fugitive also seemed to\par
slacken pace, for they heard the steps follow each other at longer\par
intervals. They at length were bewildered by the Labyrinth of passages,\par
and dispersed in various directions. Carried away by his eagerness to\par
clear up this mystery, and to penetrate into which He was impelled by a\par
movement secret and unaccountable, Lorenzo heeded not this circumstance\par
till He found himself in total solitude. The noise of footsteps had\par
ceased. All was silent around, and no clue offered itself to guide him\par
to the flying Person. He stopped to reflect on the means most likely to\par
aid his pursuit. He was persuaded that no common cause would have\par
induced the Fugitive to seek that dreary place at an hour so unusual:\par
The cry which He had heard, seemed uttered in a voice of terror, and He\par
was convinced that some mystery was attached to this event. After some\par
minutes past in hesitation He continued to proceed, feeling his way\par
along the walls of the passage. He had already past some time in this\par
slow progress, when He descried a spark of light glimmering at a\par
distance. Guided by this observation, and having drawn his sword, He\par
bent his steps towards the place, whence the beam seemed to be emitted.\par
\par
It proceeded from the Lamp which flamed before St. Clare’s Statue.\par
Before it stood several Females, their white Garments streaming in the\par
blast, as it howled along the vaulted dungeons. Curious to know what\par
had brought them together in this melancholy spot, Lorenzo drew near\par
with precaution. The Strangers seemed earnestly engaged in\par
conversation. They heard not Lorenzo’s steps, and He approached\par
unobserved, till He could hear their voices distinctly.\par
\par
“I protest,” continued She who was speaking when He arrived, and to\par
whom the rest were listening with great attention; “I protest, that I\par
saw them with my own eyes. I flew down the steps; They pursued me, and\par
I escaped falling into their hands with difficulty. Had it not been for\par
the Lamp, I should never have found you.”\par
\par
“And what could bring them hither?” said another in a trembling voice;\par
“Do you think that they were looking for us?”\par
\par
“God grant that my fears may be false,” rejoined the First; “But I\par
doubt they are Murderers! If they discover us, we are lost! As for me,\par
my fate is certain: My affinity to the Prioress will be a sufficient\par
crime to condemn me; and though till now these Vaults have afforded me\par
a retreat.......”\par
\par
Here looking up, her eye fell upon Lorenzo, who had continued to\par
approach softly.\par
\par
“The Murderers!” She cried—\par
\par
She started away from the Statue’s Pedestal on which She had been\par
seated, and attempted to escape by flight. Her Companions at the same\par
moment uttered a terrified scream, while Lorenzo arrested the Fugitive\par
by the arm. Frightened and desperate She sank upon her knees before\par
him.\par
\par
“Spare me!” She exclaimed; “For Christ’s sake, spare me! I am innocent,\par
indeed, I am!”\par
\par
While She spoke, her voice was almost choaked with fear. The beams of\par
the Lamp darting full upon her face which was unveiled, Lorenzo\par
recognized the beautiful Virginia de Villa-Franca. He hastened to raise\par
her from the ground, and besought her to take courage. He promised to\par
protect her from the Rioters, assured her that her retreat was still a\par
secret, and that She might depend upon his readiness to defend her to\par
the last drop of his blood. During this conversation, the Nuns had\par
thrown themselves into various attitudes: One knelt, and addressed\par
herself to heaven; Another hid her face in the lap of her Neighbour;\par
Some listened motionless with fear to the discourse of the supposed\par
Assassin; while Others embraced the Statue of St. Clare, and implored\par
her protection with frantic cries. On perceiving their mistake, they\par
crowded round Lorenzo and heaped benedictions on him by dozens. He\par
found that, on hearing the threats of the Mob, and terrified by the\par
cruelties which from the Convent Towers they had seen inflicted on the\par
Superior, many of the Pensioners and Nuns had taken refuge in the\par
Sepulchre. Among the former was to be reckoned the lovely Virginia.\par
Nearly related to the Prioress, She had more reason than the rest to\par
dread the Rioters, and now besought Lorenzo earnestly not to abandon\par
her to their rage. Her Companions, most of whom were Women of noble\par
family, made the same request, which He readily granted. He promised\par
not to quit them, till He had seen each of them safe in the arms of her\par
Relations: But He advised their deferring to quit the Sepulchre for\par
some time longer, when the popular fury should be somewhat calmed, and\par
the arrival of military force have dispersed the multitude.\par
\par
“Would to God!” cried Virginia, “That I were already safe in my\par
Mother’s embraces! How say you, Segnor; Will it be long, ere we may\par
leave this place? Every moment that I pass here, I pass in torture!”\par
\par
“I hope, not long,” said He; “But till you can proceed with security,\par
this Sepulchre will prove an impenetrable asylum. Here you run no\par
risque of a discovery, and I would advise your remaining quiet for the\par
next two or three hours.”\par
\par
“Two or three hours?” exclaimed Sister Helena; “If I stay another hour\par
in these vaults, I shall expire with fear! Not the wealth of worlds\par
should bribe me to undergo again what I have suffered since my coming\par
hither. Blessed Virgin! To be in this melancholy place in the middle of\par
night, surrounded by the mouldering bodies of my deceased Companions,\par
and expecting every moment to be torn in pieces by their Ghosts who\par
wander about me, and complain, and groan, and wail in accents that make\par
my blood run cold, ..... Christ Jesus! It is enough to drive me to\par
madness!”\par
\par
“Excuse me,” replied Lorenzo, “if I am surprized that while menaced by\par
real woes you are capable of yielding to imaginary dangers. These\par
terrors are puerile and groundless: Combat them, holy Sister; I have\par
promised to guard you from the Rioters, but against the attacks of\par
superstition you must depend for protection upon yourself. The idea of\par
Ghosts is ridiculous in the extreme; And if you continue to be swayed\par
by ideal terrors ...”\par
\par
“Ideal?” exclaimed the Nuns with one voice; “Why we heard it ourselves,\par
Segnor! Every one of us heard it! It was frequently repeated, and it\par
sounded every time more melancholy and deep. You will never persuade me\par
that we could all have been deceived. Not we, indeed; No, no; Had the\par
noise been merely created by fancy ....”\par
\par
“Hark! Hark!” interrupted Virginia in a voice of terror; “God preserve\par
us! There it is again!”\par
\par
The Nuns clasped their hands together, and sank upon their knees.\par
\par
Lorenzo looked round him eagerly, and was on the point of yielding to\par
the fears which already had possessed the Women. Universal silence\par
prevailed. He examined the Vault, but nothing was to be seen. He now\par
prepared to address the Nuns, and ridicule their childish\par
apprehensions, when his attention was arrested by a deep and long-drawn\par
groan.\par
\par
“What was that?” He cried, and started.\par
\par
“There, Segnor!” said Helena; “Now you must be convinced! You have\par
heard the noise yourself! Now judge, whether our terrors are imaginary.\par
Since we have been here, that groaning has been repeated almost every\par
five minutes. Doubtless, it proceeds from some Soul in pain, who wishes\par
to be prayed out of purgatory: But none of us here dares ask it the\par
question. As for me, were I to see an Apparition, the fright, I am very\par
certain, would kill me out of hand.”\par
\par
As She said this, a second groan was heard yet more distinctly. The\par
Nuns crossed themselves, and hastened to repeat their prayers against\par
evil Spirits. Lorenzo listened attentively. He even thought that He\par
could distinguish sounds, as of one speaking in complaint; But distance\par
rendered them inarticulate. The noise seemed to come from the midst of\par
the small Vault in which He and the Nuns then were, and which a\par
multitude of passages branching out in various directions, formed into\par
a sort of Star. Lorenzo’s curiosity which was ever awake, made him\par
anxious to solve this mystery. He desired that silence might be kept.\par
The Nuns obeyed him. All was hushed, till the general stillness was\par
again disturbed by the groaning, which was repeated several times\par
successively. He perceived it to be most audible, when upon following\par
the sound He was conducted close to the shrine of St. Clare:\par
\par
“The noise comes from hence,” said He; “Whose is this Statue?”\par
\par
Helena, to whom He addressed the question, paused for a moment.\par
Suddenly She clapped her hands together.\par
\par
“Aye!” cried she, “it must be so. I have discovered the meaning of\par
these groans.”\par
\par
The nuns crowded round her, and besought her eagerly to explain\par
herself. She gravely replied that for time immemorial the Statue had\par
been famous for performing miracles: From this She inferred that the\par
Saint was concerned at the conflagration of a Convent which She\par
protected, and expressed her grief by audible lamentations. Not having\par
equal faith in the miraculous Saint, Lorenzo did not think this\par
solution of the mystery quite so satisfactory, as the Nuns, who\par
subscribed to it without hesitation. In one point, ’tis true, that He\par
agreed with Helena.\par
\par
He suspected that the groans proceeded from the Statue: The more He\par
listened, the more was He confirmed in this idea. He drew nearer to the\par
Image, designing to inspect it more closely: But perceiving his\par
intention, the Nuns besought him for God’s sake to desist, since if He\par
touched the Statue, his death was inevitable.\par
\par
“And in what consists the danger?” said He.\par
\par
“Mother of God! In what?” replied Helena, ever eager to relate a\par
miraculous adventure; “If you had only heard the hundredth part of\par
those marvellous Stories about this Statue which the Domina used to\par
recount! She assured us often and often, that if we only dared to lay a\par
finger upon it, we might expect the most fatal consequences. Among\par
other things She told us that a Robber having entered these Vaults by\par
night, He observed yonder Ruby, whose value is inestimable. Do you see\par
it, Segnor? It sparkles upon the third finger of the hand, in which She\par
holds a crown of Thorns. This Jewel naturally excited the Villain’s\par
cupidity. He resolved to make himself Master of it. For this purpose He\par
ascended the Pedestal: He supported himself by grasping the Saint’s\par
right arm, and extended his own towards the Ring. What was his\par
surprize, when He saw the Statue’s hand raised in a posture of menace,\par
and heard her lips pronounce his eternal perdition! Penetrated with awe\par
and consternation, He desisted from his attempt, and prepared to quit\par
the Sepulchre. In this He also failed. Flight was denied him. He found\par
it impossible to disengage the hand, which rested upon the right arm of\par
the Statue. In vain did He struggle: He remained fixed to the Image,\par
till the insupportable and fiery anguish which darted itself through\par
his veins, compelled his shrieking for assistance.\par
\par
The Sepulchre was now filled with Spectators. The Villain confessed his\par
sacrilege, and was only released by the separation of his hand from his\par
body. It has remained ever since fastened to the Image. The Robber\par
turned Hermit, and led ever after an exemplary life: But yet the\par
Saint’s decree was performed, and Tradition says that He continues to\par
haunt this Sepulchre, and implore St. Clare’s pardon with groans and\par
lamentations. Now I think of it, those which we have just heard, may\par
very possibly have been uttered by the Ghost of this Sinner: But of\par
this I will not be positive. All that I can say is, that since that\par
time no one has ever dared to touch the Statue: Then do not be\par
foolhardy, good Segnor! For the love of heaven, give up your design,\par
nor expose yourself unnecessarily to certain destruction.”\par
\par
Not being convinced that his destruction would be so certain as Helena\par
seemed to think it, Lorenzo persisted in his resolution. The Nuns\par
besought him to desist in piteous terms, and even pointed out the\par
Robber’s hand, which in effect was still visible upon the arm of the\par
Statue. This proof, as they imagined, must convince him. It was very\par
far from doing so; and they were greatly scandalized when he declared\par
his suspicion that the dried and shrivelled fingers had been placed\par
there by order of the Prioress. In spite of their prayers and threats\par
He approached the Statue. He sprang over the iron Rails which defended\par
it, and the Saint underwent a thorough examination. The Image at first\par
appeared to be of Stone, but proved on further inspection to be formed\par
of no more solid materials than coloured Wood. He shook it, and\par
attempted to move it; But it appeared to be of a piece with the Base\par
which it stood upon. He examined it over and over: Still no clue guided\par
him to the solution of this mystery, for which the Nuns were become\par
equally solicitous, when they saw that He touched the Statue with\par
impunity. He paused, and listened: The groans were repeated at\par
intervals, and He was convinced of being in the spot nearest to them.\par
He mused upon this singular event, and ran over the Statue with\par
enquiring eyes. Suddenly they rested upon the shrivelled hand. It\par
struck him, that so particular an injunction was not given without\par
cause, not to touch the arm of the Image. He again ascended the\par
Pedestal; He examined the object of his attention, and discovered a\par
small knob of iron concealed between the Saint’s shoulder and what was\par
supposed to have been the hand of the Robber. This observation\par
delighted him. He applied his fingers to the knob, and pressed it down\par
forcibly. Immediately a rumbling noise was heard within the Statue, as\par
if a chain tightly stretched was flying back. Startled at the sound the\par
timid Nuns started away, prepared to hasten from the Vault at the first\par
appearance of danger. All remaining quiet and still, they again\par
gathered round Lorenzo, and beheld his proceedings with anxious\par
curiosity.\par
\par
Finding that nothing followed this discovery, He descended. As He took\par
his hand from the Saint, She trembled beneath his touch. This created\par
new terrors in the Spectators, who believed the Statue to be animated.\par
Lorenzo’s ideas upon the subject were widely different. He easily\par
comprehended that the noise which He had heard, was occasioned by his\par
having loosened a chain which attached the Image to its Pedestal. He\par
once more attempted to move it, and succeeded without much exertion. He\par
placed it upon the ground, and then perceived the Pedestal to be\par
hollow, and covered at the opening with an heavy iron grate.\par
\par
This excited such general curiosity that the Sisters forgot both their\par
real and imaginary dangers. Lorenzo proceeded to raise the Grate, in\par
which the Nuns assisted him to the utmost of their strength. The\par
attempt was accomplished with little difficulty. A deep abyss now\par
presented itself before them, whose thick obscurity the eye strove in\par
vain to pierce. The rays of the Lamp were too feeble to be of much\par
assistance. Nothing was discernible, save a flight of rough unshapen\par
steps which sank into the yawning Gulph and were soon lost in darkness.\par
The groans were heard no more; But All believed them to have ascended\par
from this Cavern. As He bent over it, Lorenzo fancied that He\par
distinguished something bright twinkling through the gloom. He gazed\par
attentively upon the spot where it showed itself, and was convinced\par
that He saw a small spark of light, now visible, now disappearing. He\par
communicated this circumstance to the Nuns: They also perceived the\par
spark; But when He declared his intention to descend into the Cave,\par
they united to oppose his resolution. All their remonstrances could not\par
prevail on him to alter it. None of them had courage enough to\par
accompany him; neither could He think of depriving them of the Lamp.\par
Alone therefore, and in darkness, He prepared to pursue his design,\par
while the Nuns were contented to offer up prayers for his success and\par
safety.\par
\par
The steps were so narrow and uneven, that to descend them was like\par
walking down the side of a precipice. The obscurity by which He was\par
surrounded rendered his footing insecure. He was obliged to proceed\par
with great caution, lest He should miss the steps and fall into the\par
Gulph below him. This He was several times on the point of doing.\par
However, He arrived sooner upon solid ground than He had expected: He\par
now found that the thick darkness and impenetrable mists which reigned\par
through the Cavern had deceived him into the belief of its being much\par
more profound than it proved upon inspection. He reached the foot of\par
the Stairs unhurt: He now stopped, and looked round for the spark which\par
had before caught his attention. He sought it in vain: All was dark and\par
gloomy. He listened for the groans; But his ear caught no sound, except\par
the distant murmur of the Nuns above, as in low voices they repeated\par
their Ave-Marias. He stood irresolute to which side He should address\par
his steps. At all events He determined to proceed: He did so, but\par
slowly, fearing lest instead of approaching, He should be retiring from\par
the object of his search. The groans seemed to announce one in pain, or\par
at least in sorrow, and He hoped to have the power of relieving the\par
Mourner’s calamities. A plaintive tone, sounding at no great distance,\par
at length reached his hearing; He bent his course joyfully towards it.\par
It became more audible as He advanced; and He soon beheld again the\par
spark of light, which a low projecting Wall had hitherto concealed from\par
him.\par
\par
It proceeded from a small lamp which was placed upon an heap of stones,\par
and whose faint and melancholy rays served rather to point out, than\par
dispell the horrors of a narrow gloomy dungeon formed in one side of\par
the Cavern; It also showed several other recesses of similar\par
construction, but whose depth was buried in obscurity. Coldly played\par
the light upon the damp walls, whose dew-stained surface gave back a\par
feeble reflection. A thick and pestilential fog clouded the height of\par
the vaulted dungeon. As Lorenzo advanced, He felt a piercing chillness\par
spread itself through his veins. The frequent groans still engaged him\par
to move forwards. He turned towards them, and by the Lamp’s glimmering\par
beams beheld in a corner of this loathsome abode, a Creature stretched\par
upon a bed of straw, so wretched, so emaciated, so pale, that He\par
doubted to think her Woman. She was half-naked: Her long dishevelled\par
hair fell in disorder over her face, and almost entirely concealed it.\par
One wasted Arm hung listlessly upon a tattered rug which covered her\par
convulsed and shivering limbs: The Other was wrapped round a small\par
bundle, and held it closely to her bosom. A large Rosary lay near her:\par
Opposite to her was a Crucifix, on which She bent her sunk eyes\par
fixedly, and by her side stood a Basket and a small Earthen Pitcher.\par
\par
Lorenzo stopped: He was petrified with horror. He gazed upon the\par
miserable Object with disgust and pity. He trembled at the spectacle;\par
He grew sick at heart: His strength failed him, and his limbs were\par
unable to support his weight. He was obliged to lean against the low\par
Wall which was near him, unable to go forward, or to address the\par
Sufferer. She cast her eyes towards the Staircase: The Wall concealed\par
Lorenzo, and She observed him not.\par
\par
“No one comes!” She at length murmured.\par
\par
As She spoke, her voice was hollow, and rattled in her throat: She\par
sighed bitterly.\par
\par
“No one comes!” She repeated; “No! They have forgotten me! They will\par
come no more!”\par
\par
She paused for a moment: Then continued mournfully.\par
\par
“Two days! Two long, long days, and yet no food! And yet no hope, no\par
comfort! Foolish Woman! How can I wish to lengthen a life so wretched!\par
Yet such a death! O! God! To perish by such a death! To linger out such\par
ages in torture! Till now, I knew not what it was to hunger! Hark! No.\par
No one comes! They will come no more!”\par
\par
She was silent. She shivered, and drew the rug over her naked\par
shoulders.\par
\par
“I am very cold! I am still unused to the damps of this dungeon!\par
\par
’Tis strange: But no matter. Colder shall I soon be, and yet not feel\par
it—I shall be cold, cold as Thou art!”\par
\par
She looked at the bundle which lay upon her breast. She bent over it,\par
and kissed it: Then drew back hastily, and shuddered with disgust.\par
\par
“It was once so sweet! It would have been so lovely, so like him! I\par
have lost it for ever! How a few days have changed it! I should not\par
know it again myself! Yet it is dear to me! God! how dear! I will\par
forget what it is: I will only remember what it was, and love it as\par
well, as when it was so sweet! so lovely! so like him! I thought that I\par
had wept away all my tears, but here is one still lingering.”\par
\par
She wiped her eyes with a tress of her hair. She put out her hand for\par
the Pitcher, and reached it with difficulty. She cast into it a look of\par
hopeless enquiry. She sighed, and replaced it upon the ground.\par
\par
“Quite a void! Not a drop! Not one drop left to cool my scorched-up\par
burning palate! Now would I give treasures for a draught of water! And\par
they are God’s Servants, who make me suffer thus! They think themselves\par
holy, while they torture me like Fiends! They are cruel and unfeeling;\par
And ’tis they who bid me repent; And ’tis they, who threaten me with\par
eternal perdition! Saviour, Saviour! You think not so!”\par
\par
She again fixed her eyes upon the Crucifix, took her Rosary, and while\par
She told her beads, the quick motion of her lips declared her to be\par
praying with fervency.\par
\par
While He listened to her melancholy accents, Lorenzo’s sensibility\par
became yet more violently affected. The first sight of such misery had\par
given a sensible shock to his feelings: But that being past, He now\par
advanced towards the Captive. She heard his steps, and uttering a cry\par
of joy, dropped the Rosary.\par
\par
“Hark! Hark! Hark!” She cried: “Some one comes!”\par
\par
She strove to raise herself, but her strength was unequal to the\par
attempt: She fell back, and as She sank again upon the bed of straw,\par
Lorenzo heard the rattling of heavy chains. He still approached, while\par
the Prisoner thus continued.\par
\par
“Is it you, Camilla? You are come then at last? Oh! it was time! I\par
thought that you had forsaken me; that I was doomed to perish of\par
hunger. Give me to drink, Camilla, for pity’s sake! I am faint with\par
long fasting, and grown so weak that I cannot raise myself from the\par
ground. Good Camilla, give me to drink, lest I expire before you!”\par
\par
Fearing that surprize in her enfeebled state might be fatal, Lorenzo\par
was at a loss how to address her.\par
\par
“It is not Camilla,” said He at length, speaking in a slow and gentle\par
voice.\par
\par
“Who is it then?” replied the Sufferer: “Alix, perhaps, or Violante. My\par
eyes are grown so dim and feeble that I cannot distinguish your\par
features. But whichever it is, if your breast is sensible of the least\par
compassion, if you are not more cruel than Wolves and Tigers, take pity\par
on my sufferings. You know that I am dying for want of sustenance. This\par
is the third day, since these lips have received nourishment. Do you\par
bring me food? Or come you only to announce my death, and learn how\par
long I have yet to exist in agony?”\par
\par
“You mistake my business,” replied Lorenzo; “I am no Emissary of the\par
cruel Prioress. I pity your sorrows, and come hither to relieve them.”\par
\par
“To relieve them?” repeated the Captive; “Said you, to relieve them?”\par
\par
At the same time starting from the ground, and supporting herself upon\par
her hands, She gazed upon the Stranger earnestly.\par
\par
“Great God! It is no illusion! A Man! Speak! Who are you? What brings\par
you hither? Come you to save me, to restore me to liberty, to life and\par
light? Oh! speak, speak quickly, lest I encourage an hope whose\par
disappointment will destroy me.”\par
\par
“Be calm!” replied Lorenzo in a voice soothing and compassionate; “The\par
Domina of whose cruelty you complain, has already paid the forfeit of\par
her offences: You have nothing more to fear from her.\par
\par
A few minutes will restore you to liberty, and the embraces of your\par
Friends from whom you have been secluded. You may rely upon my\par
protection. Give me your hand, and be not fearful. Let me conduct you\par
where you may receive those attentions which your feeble state\par
requires.”\par
\par
“Oh! Yes! Yes! Yes!” cried the Prisoner with an exulting shriek; “There\par
is a God then, and a just one! Joy! Joy! I shall once more breath the\par
fresh air, and view the light of the glorious sunbeams! I will go with\par
you! Stranger, I will go with you! Oh! Heaven will bless you for\par
pitying an Unfortunate! But this too must go with me,” She added\par
pointing to the small bundle which She still clasped to her bosom; “I\par
cannot part with this. I will bear it away: It shall convince the world\par
how dreadful are the abodes so falsely termed religious. Good Stranger,\par
lend me your hand to rise: I am faint with want, and sorrow, and\par
sickness, and my forces have quite forsaken me! So, that is well!”\par
\par
As Lorenzo stooped to raise her, the beams of the Lamp struck full upon\par
his face.\par
\par
“Almighty God!” She exclaimed; “Is it possible! That look! Those\par
features! Oh! Yes, it is, it is .....”\par
\par
She extended her arms to throw them round him; But her enfeebled frame\par
was unable to sustain the emotions which agitated her bosom. She\par
fainted, and again sank upon the bed of straw.\par
\par
Lorenzo was surprized at her last exclamation. He thought that He had\par
before heard such accents as her hollow voice had just formed, but\par
where He could not remember. He saw that in her dangerous situation\par
immediate physical aid was absolutely necessary, and He hastened to\par
convey her from the dungeon. He was at first prevented from doing so by\par
a strong chain fastened round the prisoner’s body, and fixing her to\par
the neighbouring Wall. However, his natural strength being aided by\par
anxiety to relieve the Unfortunate, He soon forced out the Staple to\par
which one end of the Chain was attached. Then taking the Captive in his\par
arms, He bent his course towards the Staircase. The rays of the Lamp\par
above, as well as the murmur of female voices, guided his steps. He\par
gained the Stairs, and in a few minutes after arrived at the\par
iron-grate.\par
\par
The nuns during his absence had been terribly tormented by curiosity\par
and apprehension: They were equally surprized and delighted on seeing\par
him suddenly emerge from the Cave. Every heart was filled with\par
compassion for the miserable Creature whom He bore in his arms. While\par
the Nuns, and Virginia in particular, employed themselves in striving\par
to recall her to her senses, Lorenzo related in few words the manner of\par
his finding her. He then observed to them that by this time the tumult\par
must have been quelled, and that He could now conduct them to their\par
Friends without danger. All were eager to quit the Sepulchre: Still to\par
prevent all possibility of ill-usage, they besought Lorenzo to venture\par
out first alone, and examine whether the Coast was clear. With this\par
request He complied. Helena offered to conduct him to the Staircase,\par
and they were on the point of departing, when a strong light flashed\par
from several passages upon the adjacent walls. At the same time Steps\par
were heard of people approaching hastily, and whose number seemed to be\par
considerable. The Nuns were greatly alarmed at this circumstance: They\par
supposed their retreat to be discovered, and the Rioters to be\par
advancing in pursuit of them. Hastily quitting the Prisoner who\par
remained insensible, they crowded round Lorenzo, and claimed his\par
promise to protect them. Virginia alone forgot her own danger by\par
striving to relieve the sorrows of Another. She supported the\par
Sufferer’s head upon her knees, bathing her temples with rose-water,\par
chafing her cold hands, and sprinkling her face with tears which were\par
drawn from her by compassion. The Strangers approaching nearer, Lorenzo\par
was enabled to dispel the fears of the Suppliants. His name, pronounced\par
by a number of voices among which He distinguished the Duke’s, pealed\par
along the Vaults, and convinced him that He was the object of their\par
search. He communicated this intelligence to the Nuns, who received it\par
with rapture. A few moments after confirmed his idea. Don Ramirez, as\par
well as the Duke, appeared, followed by Attendants with Torches. They\par
had been seeking him through the Vaults, in order to let him know that\par
the Mob was dispersed, and the riot entirely over. Lorenzo recounted\par
briefly his adventure in the Cavern, and explained how much the Unknown\par
was in want of medical assistance. He besought the Duke to take charge\par
of her, as well as of the Nuns and Pensioners.\par
\par
“As for me,” said He, “Other cares demand my attention. While you with\par
one half of the Archers convey these Ladies to their respective homes,\par
I wish the other half to be left with me. I will examine the Cavern\par
below, and pervade the most secret recesses of the Sepulchre. I cannot\par
rest till convinced that yonder wretched Victim was the only one\par
confined by Superstition in these vaults.”\par
\par
The Duke applauded his intention. Don Ramirez offered to assist him in\par
his enquiry, and his proposal was accepted with gratitude.\par
\par
The Nuns having made their acknowledgments to Lorenzo, committed\par
themselves to the care of his Uncle, and were conducted from the\par
Sepulchre. Virginia requested that the Unknown might be given to her in\par
charge, and promised to let Lorenzo know whenever She was sufficiently\par
recovered to accept his visits. In truth, She made this promise more\par
from consideration for herself than for either Lorenzo or the Captive.\par
She had witnessed his politeness, gentleness, and intrepidity with\par
sensible emotion. She wished earnestly to preserve his acquaintance;\par
and in addition to the sentiments of pity which the Prisoner excited,\par
She hoped that her attention to this Unfortunate would raise her a\par
degree in the esteem of Lorenzo. She had no occasion to trouble herself\par
upon this head. The kindness already displayed by her and the tender\par
concern which She had shown for the Sufferer had gained her an exalted\par
place in his good graces. While occupied in alleviating the Captive’s\par
sorrows, the nature of her employment adorned her with new charms, and\par
rendered her beauty a thousand times more interesting. Lorenzo viewed\par
her with admiration and delight: He considered her as a ministering\par
Angel descended to the aid of afflicted innocence; nor could his heart\par
have resisted her attractions, had it not been steeled by the\par
remembrance of Antonia.\par
\par
The duke now conveyed the nuns in safety to the dwellings of their\par
respective friends. The rescued Prisoner was still insensible and gave\par
no signs of life, except by occasional groans. She was borne upon a\par
sort of litter; Virginia, who was constantly by the side of it, was\par
apprehensive that exhausted by long abstinence, and shaken by the\par
sudden change from bonds and darkness to liberty and light, her frame\par
would never get the better of the shock. Lorenzo and Don Ramirez still\par
remained in the Sepulchre. After deliberating upon their proceedings,\par
it was resolved that to prevent losing time, the Archers should be\par
divided into two Bodies: That with one Don Ramirez should examine the\par
cavern, while Lorenzo with the other might penetrate into the further\par
Vaults. This being arranged, and his Followers being provided with\par
Torches, Don Ramirez advanced to the Cavern. He had already descended\par
some steps when He heard People approaching hastily from the interior\par
part of the Sepulchre. This surprized him, and He quitted the Cave\par
precipitately.\par
\par
“Do you hear footsteps?” said Lorenzo; “Let us bend our course towards\par
them. ’Tis from this side that they seem to proceed.”\par
\par
At that moment a loud and piercing shriek induced him to quicken his\par
steps.\par
\par
“Help! Help, for God’s sake! cried a voice, whose melodious tone\par
penetrated Lorenzo’s heart with terror.\par
\par
He flew towards the cry with the rapidity of lightning, and was\par
followed by Don Ramirez with equal swiftness.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XI.\par
\par
\par
Great Heaven! How frail thy creature Man is made!\par
How by himself insensibly betrayed!\par
In our own strength unhappily secure,\par
Too little cautious of the adverse power,\par
On pleasure’s flowery brink we idly stray,\par
Masters as yet of our returning way:\par
Till the strong gusts of raging passion rise,\par
Till the dire Tempest mingles earth and skies,\par
And swift into the boundless Ocean borne,\par
Our foolish confidence too late we mourn:\par
Round our devoted heads the billows beat,\par
And from our troubled view the lessening lands retreat.\par
\par
PRIOR.\par
\par
All this while, Ambrosio was unconscious of the dreadful scenes which\par
were passing so near. The execution of his designs upon Antonia\par
employed his every thought. Hitherto, He was satisfied with the success\par
of his plans. Antonia had drank the opiate, was buried in the vaults of\par
St. Clare, and absolutely in his disposal. Matilda, who was well\par
acquainted with the nature and effects of the soporific medicine, had\par
computed that it would not cease to operate till one in the Morning.\par
For that hour He waited with impatience. The Festival of St. Clare\par
presented him with a favourable opportunity of consummating his crime.\par
He was certain that the Friars and Nuns would be engaged in the\par
Procession, and that He had no cause to dread an interruption: From\par
appearing himself at the head of his Monks, He had desired to be\par
excused. He doubted not, that being beyond the reach of help, cut off\par
from all the world, and totally in his power, Antonia would comply with\par
his desires. The affection which She had ever exprest for him,\par
warranted this persuasion: But He resolved that should She prove\par
obstinate, no consideration whatever should prevent him from enjoying\par
her. Secure from a discovery, He shuddered not at the idea of employing\par
force: If He felt any repugnance, it arose not from a principle of\par
shame or compassion, but from his feeling for Antonia the most sincere\par
and ardent affection, and wishing to owe her favours to no one but\par
herself.\par
\par
The Monks quitted the Abbey at midnight. Matilda was among the\par
Choristers, and led the chaunt. Ambrosio was left by himself, and at\par
liberty to pursue his own inclinations. Convinced that no one remained\par
behind to watch his motions, or disturb his pleasures, He now hastened\par
to the Western Aisles. His heart beating with hope not unmingled with\par
anxiety, He crossed the Garden, unlocked the door which admitted him\par
into the Cemetery, and in a few minutes He stood before the Vaults.\par
Here He paused.\par
\par
He looked round him with suspicion, conscious that his business was\par
unfit for any other eye. As He stood in hesitation, He heard the\par
melancholy shriek of the screech-Owl: The wind rattled loudly against\par
the windows of the adjacent Convent, and as the current swept by him,\par
bore with it the faint notes of the chaunt of Choristers. He opened the\par
door cautiously, as if fearing to be overheard: He entered; and closed\par
it again after him. Guided by his Lamp, He threaded the long passages,\par
in whose windings Matilda had instructed him, and reached the private\par
Vault which contained his sleeping Mistress.\par
\par
Its entrance was by no means easy to discover: But this was no obstacle\par
to Ambrosio, who at the time of Antonia’s Funeral had observed it too\par
carefully to be deceived. He found the door, which was unfastened,\par
pushed it open, and descended into the dungeon. He approached the\par
humble Tomb in which Antonia reposed. He had provided himself with an\par
iron crow and a pick-axe; But this precaution was unnecessary. The\par
Grate was slightly fastened on the outside: He raised it, and placing\par
the Lamp upon its ridge, bent silently over the Tomb. By the side of\par
three putrid half-corrupted Bodies lay the sleeping Beauty. A lively\par
red, the forerunner of returning animation, had already spread itself\par
over her cheek; and as wrapped in her shroud She reclined upon her\par
funeral Bier, She seemed to smile at the Images of Death around her.\par
While He gazed upon their rotting bones and disgusting figures, who\par
perhaps were once as sweet and lovely, Ambrosio thought upon Elvira, by\par
him reduced to the same state. As the memory of that horrid act glanced\par
upon his mind, it was clouded with a gloomy horror. Yet it served but\par
to strengthen his resolution to destroy Antonia’s honour.\par
\par
“For your sake, Fatal Beauty!” murmured the Monk, while gazing on his\par
devoted prey; “For your sake, have I committed this murder, and sold\par
myself to eternal tortures. Now you are in my power: The produce of my\par
guilt will at least be mine. Hope not that your prayers breathed in\par
tones of unequalled melody, your bright eyes filled with tears, and\par
your hands lifted in supplication, as when seeking in penitence the\par
Virgin’s pardon; Hope not that your moving innocence, your beauteous\par
grief, or all your suppliant arts shall ransom you from my embraces.\par
Before the break of day, mine you must, and mine you shall be!”\par
\par
He lifted her still motionless from the Tomb: He seated himself upon a\par
bank of Stone, and supporting her in his arms, watched impatiently for\par
the symptoms of returning animation. Scarcely could He command his\par
passions sufficiently, to restrain himself from enjoying her while yet\par
insensible. His natural lust was increased in ardour by the\par
difficulties which had opposed his satisfying it: As also by his long\par
abstinence from Woman, since from the moment of resigning her claim to\par
his love, Matilda had exiled him from her arms for ever.\par
\par
“I am no Prostitute, Ambrosio;” Had She told him, when in the fullness\par
of his lust He demanded her favours with more than usual earnestness;\par
“I am now no more than your Friend, and will not be your Mistress.\par
Cease then to solicit my complying with desires, which insult me. While\par
your heart was mine, I gloried in your embraces: Those happy times are\par
past: My person is become indifferent to you, and ’tis necessity, not\par
love, which makes you seek my enjoyment. I cannot yield to a request so\par
humiliating to my pride.”\par
\par
Suddenly deprived of pleasures, the use of which had made them an\par
absolute want, the Monk felt this restraint severely. Naturally\par
addicted to the gratification of the senses, in the full vigour of\par
manhood, and heat of blood, He had suffered his temperament to acquire\par
such ascendency that his lust was become madness. Of his fondness for\par
Antonia, none but the grosser particles remained: He longed for the\par
possession of her person; and even the gloom of the vault, the\par
surrounding silence, and the resistance which He expected from her,\par
seemed to give a fresh edge to his fierce and unbridled desires.\par
\par
Gradually He felt the bosom which rested against his, glow with\par
returning warmth. Her heart throbbed again; Her blood flowed swifter,\par
and her lips moved. At length She opened her eyes, but still opprest\par
and bewildered by the effects of the strong opiate, She closed them\par
again immediately. Ambrosio watched her narrowly, nor permitted a\par
movement to escape him. Perceiving that She was fully restored to\par
existence, He caught her in rapture to his bosom, and closely pressed\par
his lips to hers. The suddenness of his action sufficed to dissipate\par
the fumes which obscured Antonia’s reason. She hastily raised herself,\par
and cast a wild look round her. The strange Images which presented\par
themselves on every side contributed to confuse her. She put her hand\par
to her head, as if to settle her disordered imagination. At length She\par
took it away, and threw her eyes through the dungeon a second time.\par
They fixed upon the Abbot’s face.\par
\par
“Where am I?” She said abruptly. “How came I here? Where is my Mother?\par
Methought, I saw her! Oh! a dream, a dreadful dreadful dream told me\par
...... But where am I? Let me go! I cannot stay here!”\par
\par
She attempted to rise, but the Monk prevented her.\par
\par
“Be calm, lovely Antonia!” He replied; “No danger is near you: Confide\par
in my protection. Why do you gaze on me so earnestly? Do you not know\par
me? Not know your Friend? Ambrosio?”\par
\par
“Ambrosio? My Friend? Oh! yes, yes; I remember ...... But why am I\par
here? Who has brought me? Why are you with me? Oh! Flora bad me beware\par
.....! Here are nothing but Graves, and Tombs, and Skeletons! This\par
place frightens me! Good Ambrosio take me away from it, for it recalls\par
my fearful dream! Methought I was dead, and laid in my grave! Good\par
Ambrosio, take me from hence. Will you not? Oh! will you not? Do not\par
look on me thus!\par
\par
Your flaming eyes terrify me! Spare me, Father! Oh! spare me for God’s\par
sake!”\par
\par
“Why these terrors, Antonia?” rejoined the Abbot, folding her in his\par
arms, and covering her bosom with kisses which She in vain struggled to\par
avoid: “What fear you from me, from one who adores you? What matters it\par
where you are? This Sepulchre seems to me Love’s bower; This gloom is\par
the friendly night of mystery which He spreads over our delights! Such\par
do I think it, and such must my Antonia. Yes, my sweet Girl! Yes! Your\par
veins shall glow with fire which circles in mine, and my transports\par
shall be doubled by your sharing them!”\par
\par
While He spoke thus, He repeated his embraces, and permitted himself\par
the most indecent liberties. Even Antonia’s ignorance was not proof\par
against the freedom of his behaviour. She was sensible of her danger,\par
forced herself from his arms, and her shroud being her only garment,\par
She wrapped it closely round her.\par
\par
“Unhand me, Father!” She cried, her honest indignation tempered by\par
alarm at her unprotected position; “Why have you brought me to this\par
place? Its appearance freezes me with horror! Convey me from hence, if\par
you have the least sense of pity and humanity! Let me return to the\par
House which I have quitted I know not how; But stay here one moment\par
longer, I neither will, or ought.”\par
\par
Though the Monk was somewhat startled by the resolute tone in which\par
this speech was delivered, it produced upon him no other effect than\par
surprize. He caught her hand, forced her upon his knee, and gazing upon\par
her with gloting eyes, He thus replied to her.\par
\par
“Compose yourself, Antonia. Resistance is unavailing, and I need\par
disavow my passion for you no longer. You are imagined dead: Society is\par
for ever lost to you. I possess you here alone; You are absolutely in\par
my power, and I burn with desires which I must either gratify or die:\par
But I would owe my happiness to yourself. My lovely Girl! My adorable\par
Antonia! Let me instruct you in joys to which you are still a Stranger,\par
and teach you to feel those pleasures in my arms which I must soon\par
enjoy in yours. Nay, this struggling is childish,” He continued, seeing\par
her repell his caresses, and endeavour to escape from his grasp; “No\par
aid is near: Neither heaven or earth shall save you from my embraces.\par
Yet why reject pleasures so sweet, so rapturous? No one observes us:\par
Our loves will be a secret to all the world: Love and opportunity\par
invite your giving loose to your passions. Yield to them, my Antonia!\par
Yield to them, my lovely Girl! Throw your arms thus fondly round me;\par
Join your lips thus closely to mine! Amidst all her gifts, has Nature\par
denied her most precious, the sensibility of Pleasure? Oh! impossible!\par
Every feature, look, and motion declares you formed to bless, and to be\par
blessed yourself! Turn not on me those supplicating eyes: Consult your\par
own charms; They will tell you that I am proof against entreaty. Can I\par
relinquish these limbs so white, so soft, so delicate; These swelling\par
breasts, round, full, and elastic! These lips fraught with such\par
inexhaustible sweetness? Can I relinquish these treasures, and leave\par
them to another’s enjoyment? No, Antonia; never, never! I swear it by\par
this kiss, and this! and this!”\par
\par
With every moment the Friar’s passion became more ardent, and Antonia’s\par
terror more intense. She struggled to disengage herself from his arms:\par
Her exertions were unsuccessful; and finding that Ambrosio’s conduct\par
became still freer, She shrieked for assistance with all her strength.\par
The aspect of the Vault, the pale glimmering of the Lamp, the\par
surrounding obscurity, the sight of the Tomb, and the objects of\par
mortality which met her eyes on either side, were ill-calculated to\par
inspire her with those emotions by which the Friar was agitated. Even\par
his caresses terrified her from their fury, and created no other\par
sentiment than fear. On the contrary, her alarm, her evident disgust,\par
and incessant opposition, seemed only to inflame the Monk’s desires,\par
and supply his brutality with additional strength. Antonia’s shrieks\par
were unheard: Yet She continued them, nor abandoned her endeavours to\par
escape, till exhausted and out of breath She sank from his arms upon\par
her knees, and once more had recourse to prayers and supplications.\par
This attempt had no better success than the former. On the contrary,\par
taking advantage of her situation, the Ravisher threw himself by her\par
side: He clasped her to his bosom almost lifeless with terror, and\par
faint with struggling. He stifled her cries with kisses, treated her\par
with the rudeness of an unprincipled Barbarian, proceeded from freedom\par
to freedom, and in the violence of his lustful delirium, wounded and\par
bruised her tender limbs. Heedless of her tears, cries and entreaties,\par
He gradually made himself Master of her person, and desisted not from\par
his prey, till He had accomplished his crime and the dishonour of\par
Antonia.\par
\par
Scarcely had He succeeded in his design than He shuddered at himself\par
and the means by which it was effected. The very excess of his former\par
eagerness to possess Antonia now contributed to inspire him with\par
disgust; and a secret impulse made him feel how base and unmanly was\par
the crime which He had just committed. He started hastily from her\par
arms. She, who so lately had been the object of his adoration, now\par
raised no other sentiment in his heart than aversion and rage. He\par
turned away from her; or if his eyes rested upon her figure\par
involuntarily, it was only to dart upon her looks of hate. The\par
Unfortunate had fainted ere the completion of her disgrace: She only\par
recovered life to be sensible of her misfortune. She remained stretched\par
upon the earth in silent despair: The tears chased each other slowly\par
down her cheeks, and her bosom heaved with frequent sobs. Oppressed\par
with grief, She continued for some time in this state of torpidity. At\par
length She rose with difficulty, and dragging her feeble steps towards\par
the door, prepared to quit the dungeon.\par
\par
The sound of her footsteps rouzed the Monk from his sullen apathy.\par
Starting from the Tomb against which He reclined, while his eyes\par
wandered over the images of corruption contained in it, He pursued the\par
Victim of his brutality, and soon overtook her. He seized her by the\par
arm, and violently forced her back into the dungeon.\par
\par
“Whither go you?” He cried in a stern voice; “Return this instant!”\par
\par
Antonia trembled at the fury of his countenance.\par
\par
“What, would you more?” She said with timidity: “Is not my ruin\par
compleated? Am I not undone, undone for ever? Is not your cruelty\par
contented, or have I yet more to suffer? Let me depart. Let me return\par
to my home, and weep unrestrained my shame and my affliction!”\par
\par
“Return to your home?” repeated the Monk, with bitter and contemptuous\par
mockery; Then suddenly his eyes flaming with passion, “What? That you\par
may denounce me to the world? That you may proclaim me an Hypocrite, a\par
Ravisher, a Betrayer, a Monster of cruelty, lust, and ingratitude? No,\par
no, no! I know well the whole weight of my offences; Well that your\par
complaints would be too just, and my crimes too notorious! You shall\par
not from hence to tell Madrid that I am a Villain; that my conscience\par
is loaded with sins which make me despair of Heaven’s pardon. Wretched\par
Girl, you must stay here with me! Here amidst these lonely Tombs, these\par
images of Death, these rotting loathsome corrupted bodies! Here shall\par
you stay, and witness my sufferings; witness what it is to die in the\par
horrors of despondency, and breathe the last groan in blasphemy and\par
curses! And who am I to thank for this? What seduced me into crimes,\par
whose bare remembrance makes me shudder? Fatal Witch! was it not thy\par
beauty? Have you not plunged my soul into infamy? Have you not made me\par
a perjured Hypocrite, a Ravisher, an Assassin! Nay, at this moment,\par
does not that angel look bid me despair of God’s forgiveness? Oh! when\par
I stand before his judgment-throne, that look will suffice to damn me!\par
You will tell my Judge that you were happy, till _I_ saw you; that you\par
were innocent, till _I_ polluted you! You will come with those tearful\par
eyes, those cheeks pale and ghastly, those hands lifted in\par
supplication, as when you sought from me that mercy which I gave not!\par
Then will my perdition be certain! Then will come your Mother’s Ghost,\par
and hurl me down into the dwellings of Fiends, and flames, and Furies,\par
and everlasting torments! And ’tis you, who will accuse me! ’Tis you,\par
who will cause my eternal anguish! You, wretched Girl! You! You!”\par
\par
As He thundered out these words, He violently grasped Antonia’s arm,\par
and spurned the earth with delirious fury.\par
\par
Supposing his brain to be turned, Antonia sank in terror upon her\par
knees: She lifted up her hands, and her voice almost died away, ere She\par
could give it utterance.\par
\par
“Spare me! Spare me!” She murmured with difficulty.\par
\par
“Silence!” cried the Friar madly, and dashed her upon the ground——\par
\par
He quitted her, and paced the dungeon with a wild and disordered air.\par
His eyes rolled fearfully: Antonia trembled whenever She met their\par
gaze. He seemed to meditate on something horrible, and She gave up all\par
hopes of escaping from the Sepulchre with life. Yet in harbouring this\par
idea, She did him injustice. Amidst the horror and disgust to which his\par
soul was a prey, pity for his Victim still held a place in it. The\par
storm of passion once over, He would have given worlds had He possest\par
them, to have restored to her that innocence of which his unbridled\par
lust had deprived her. Of the desires which had urged him to the crime,\par
no trace was left in his bosom: The wealth of India would not have\par
tempted him to a second enjoyment of her person. His nature seemed to\par
revolt at the very idea, and fain would He have wiped from his memory\par
the scene which had just past. As his gloomy rage abated, in proportion\par
did his compassion augment for Antonia. He stopped, and would have\par
spoken to her words of comfort; But He knew not from whence to draw\par
them, and remained gazing upon her with mournful wildness. Her\par
situation seemed so hopeless, so woebegone, as to baffle mortal power\par
to relieve her. What could He do for her? Her peace of mind was lost,\par
her honour irreparably ruined. She was cut off for ever from society,\par
nor dared He give her back to it. He was conscious that were She to\par
appear in the world again, his guilt would be revealed, and his\par
punishment inevitable. To one so laden with crimes, Death came armed\par
with double terrors. Yet should He restore Antonia to light, and stand\par
the chance of her betraying him, how miserable a prospect would present\par
itself before her. She could never hope to be creditably established;\par
She would be marked with infamy, and condemned to sorrow and solitude\par
for the remainder of her existence. What was the alternative? A\par
resolution far more terrible for Antonia, but which at least would\par
insure the Abbot’s safety. He determined to leave the world persuaded\par
of her death, and to retain her a captive in this gloomy prison: There\par
He proposed to visit her every night, to bring her food, to profess his\par
penitence, and mingle his tears with hers. The Monk felt that this\par
resolution was unjust and cruel; but it was his only means to prevent\par
Antonia from publishing his guilt and her own infamy. Should He release\par
her, He could not depend upon her silence: His offence was too flagrant\par
to permit his hoping for her forgiveness. Besides, her reappearing\par
would excite universal curiosity, and the violence of her affliction\par
would prevent her from concealing its cause. He determined therefore,\par
that Antonia should remain a Prisoner in the dungeon.\par
\par
He approached her with confusion painted on his countenance. He raised\par
her from the ground. Her hand trembled, as He took it, and He dropped\par
it again as if He had touched a Serpent. Nature seemed to recoil at the\par
touch. He felt himself at once repulsed from and attracted towards her,\par
yet could account for neither sentiment. There was something in her\par
look which penetrated him with horror; and though his understanding was\par
still ignorant of it, Conscience pointed out to him the whole extent of\par
his crime. In hurried accents yet the gentlest He could find, while his\par
eye was averted, and his voice scarcely audible, He strove to console\par
her under a misfortune which now could not be avoided. He declared\par
himself sincerely penitent, and that He would gladly shed a drop of his\par
blood, for every tear which his barbarity had forced from her. Wretched\par
and hopeless, Antonia listened to him in silent grief: But when He\par
announced her confinement in the Sepulchre, that dreadful doom to which\par
even death seemed preferable roused her from her insensibility at once.\par
To linger out a life of misery in a narrow loathsome Cell, known to\par
exist by no human Being save her Ravisher, surrounded by mouldering\par
Corses, breathing the pestilential air of corruption, never more to\par
behold the light, or drink the pure gale of heaven, the idea was more\par
terrible than She could support. It conquered even her abhorrence of\par
the Friar. Again She sank upon her knees: She besought his compassion\par
in terms the most pathetic and urgent. She promised, would He but\par
restore her to liberty, to conceal her injuries from the world; to\par
assign any reason for her reappearance which He might judge proper; and\par
in order to prevent the least suspicion from falling upon him, She\par
offered to quit Madrid immediately. Her entreaties were so urgent as to\par
make a considerable impression upon the Monk. He reflected that as her\par
person no longer excited his desires, He had no interest in keeping her\par
concealed as He had at first intended; that He was adding a fresh\par
injury to those which She had already suffered; and that if She adhered\par
to her promises, whether She was confined or at liberty, his life and\par
reputation were equally secure. On the other hand, He trembled lest in\par
her affliction Antonia should unintentionally break her engagement; or\par
that her excessive simplicity and ignorance of deceit should permit\par
some one more artful to surprize her secret. However well-founded were\par
these apprehensions, compassion, and a sincere wish to repair his fault\par
as much as possible solicited his complying with the prayers of his\par
Suppliant. The difficulty of colouring Antonia’s unexpected return to\par
life, after her supposed death and public interment, was the only point\par
which kept him irresolute. He was still pondering on the means of\par
removing this obstacle, when He heard the sound of feet approaching\par
with precipitation. The door of the Vault was thrown open, and Matilda\par
rushed in, evidently much confused and terrified.\par
\par
On seeing a Stranger enter, Antonia uttered a cry of joy: But her hopes\par
of receiving succour from him were soon dissipated. The supposed\par
Novice, without expressing the least surprize at finding a Woman alone\par
with the Monk, in so strange a place, and at so late an hour, addressed\par
him thus without losing a moment.\par
\par
“What is to be done, Ambrosio? We are lost, unless some speedy means is\par
found of dispelling the Rioters. Ambrosio, the Convent of St. Clare is\par
on fire; The Prioress has fallen a victim to the fury of the Mob.\par
Already is the Abbey menaced with a similar fate. Alarmed at the\par
threats of the People, the Monks seek for you everywhere. They imagine\par
that your authority alone will suffice to calm this disturbance. No one\par
knows what is become of you, and your absence creates universal\par
astonishment and despair. I profited by the confusion, and fled hither\par
to warn you of the danger.”\par
\par
“This will soon be remedied,” answered the Abbot; “I will hasten back\par
to my Cell: a trivial reason will account for my having been missed.”\par
\par
“Impossible!” rejoined Matilda: “The Sepulchre is filled with Archers.\par
Lorenzo de Medina, with several Officers of the Inquisition, searches\par
through the Vaults, and pervades every passage. You will be intercepted\par
in your flight; Your reasons for being at this late hour in the\par
Sepulchre will be examined; Antonia will be found, and then you are\par
undone for ever!”\par
\par
“Lorenzo de Medina? Officers of the Inquisition? What brings them here?\par
Seek they for me? Am I then suspected? Oh! speak, Matilda! Answer me,\par
in pity!”\par
\par
“As yet they do not think of you, but I fear that they will ere long.\par
Your only chance of escaping their notice rests upon the difficulty of\par
exploring this Vault. The door is artfully hidden:\par
\par
Haply it may not be observed, and we may remain concealed till the\par
search is over.”\par
\par
“But Antonia ..... Should the Inquisitors draw near, and her cries be\par
heard ....”\par
\par
“Thus I remove that danger!” interrupted Matilda.\par
\par
At the same time drawing a poignard, She rushed upon her devoted prey.\par
\par
“Hold! Hold!” cried Ambrosio, seizing her hand, and wresting from it\par
the already lifted weapon. “What would you do, cruel Woman? The\par
Unfortunate has already suffered but too much, thanks to your\par
pernicious consels! Would to God that I had never followed them! Would\par
to God that I had never seen your face!”\par
\par
Matilda darted upon him a look of scorn.\par
\par
“Absurd!” She exclaimed with an air of passion and majesty which\par
impressed the Monk with awe. “After robbing her of all that made it\par
dear, can you fear to deprive her of a life so miserable? But ’tis\par
well! Let her live to convince you of your folly. I abandon you to your\par
evil destiny! I disclaim your alliance! Who trembles to commit so\par
insignificant a crime, deserves not my protection. Hark! Hark!\par
Ambrosio; Hear you not the Archers? They come, and your destruction is\par
inevitable!”\par
\par
At this moment the Abbot heard the sound of distant voices. He flew to\par
close the door on whose concealment his safety depended, and which\par
Matilda had neglected to fasten. Ere He could reach it, He saw Antonia\par
glide suddenly by him, rush through the door, and fly towards the noise\par
with the swiftness of an arrow. She had listened attentively to\par
Matilda: She heard Lorenzo’s name mentioned, and resolved to risque\par
every thing to throw herself under his protection. The door was open.\par
The sounds convinced her that the Archers could be at no great\par
distance. She mustered up her little remaining strength, rushed by the\par
Monk ere He perceived her design, and bent her course rapidly towards\par
the voices. As soon as He recovered from his first surprize, the Abbot\par
failed not to pursue her. In vain did Antonia redouble her speed, and\par
stretch every nerve to the utmost. Her Enemy gained upon her every\par
moment: She heard his steps close after her, and felt the heat of his\par
breath glow upon her neck. He overtook her; He twisted his hand in the\par
ringlets of her streaming hair, and attempted to drag her back with him\par
to the dungeon. Antonia resisted with all her strength: She folded her\par
arms round a Pillar which supported the roof, and shrieked loudly for\par
assistance. In vain did the Monk strive to threaten her to silence.\par
\par
“Help!” She continued to exclaim; “Help! Help! for God’s sake!”\par
\par
Quickened by her cries, the sound of footsteps was heard approaching.\par
The Abbot expected every moment to see the Inquisitors arrive. Antonia\par
still resisted, and He now enforced her silence by means the most\par
horrible and inhuman. He still grasped Matilda’s dagger: Without\par
allowing himself a moment’s reflection, He raised it, and plunged it\par
twice in the bosom of Antonia! She shrieked, and sank upon the ground.\par
The Monk endeavoured to bear her away with him, but She still embraced\par
the Pillar firmly. At that instant the light of approaching Torches\par
flashed upon the Walls. Dreading a discovery, Ambrosio was compelled to\par
abandon his Victim, and hastily fled back to the Vault, where He had\par
left Matilda.\par
\par
He fled not unobserved. Don Ramirez happening to arrive the first,\par
perceived a Female bleeding upon the ground, and a Man flying from the\par
spot, whose confusion betrayed him for the Murderer. He instantly\par
pursued the Fugitive with some part of the Archers, while the Others\par
remained with Lorenzo to protect the wounded Stranger. They raised her,\par
and supported her in their arms. She had fainted from excess of pain,\par
but soon gave signs of returning life. She opened her eyes, and on\par
lifting up her head, the quantity of fair hair fell back which till\par
then had obscured her features.\par
\par
“God Almighty! It is Antonia!”\par
\par
Such was Lorenzo’s exclamation, while He snatched her from the\par
Attendant’s arms, and clasped her in his own.\par
\par
Though aimed by an uncertain hand, the poignard had answered but too\par
well the purpose of its Employer. The wounds were mortal, and Antonia\par
was conscious that She never could recover. Yet the few moments which\par
remained for her were moments of happiness. The concern exprest upon\par
Lorenzo’s countenance, the frantic fondness of his complaints, and his\par
earnest enquiries respecting her wounds, convinced her beyond a doubt\par
that his affections were her own. She would not be removed from the\par
Vaults, fearing lest motion should only hasten her death; and She was\par
unwilling to lose those moments which She past in receiving proofs of\par
Lorenzo’s love, and assuring him of her own. She told him that had She\par
still been undefiled She might have lamented the loss of life; But that\par
deprived of honour and branded with shame, Death was to her a blessing:\par
She could not have been his Wife, and that hope being denied her, She\par
resigned herself to the Grave without one sigh of regret. She bad him\par
take courage, conjured him not to abandon himself to fruitless sorrow,\par
and declared that She mourned to leave nothing in the whole world but\par
him. While every sweet accent increased rather than lightened Lorenzo’s\par
grief, She continued to converse with him till the moment of\par
dissolution. Her voice grew faint and scarcely audible; A thick cloud\par
spread itself over her eyes; Her heart beat slow and irregular, and\par
every instant seemed to announce that her fate was near at hand.\par
\par
She lay, her head reclining upon Lorenzo’s bosom, and her lips still\par
murmuring to him words of comfort. She was interrupted by the Convent\par
Bell, as tolling at a distance, it struck the hour. Suddenly Antonia’s\par
eyes sparkled with celestial brightness: Her frame seemed to have\par
received new strength and animation. She started from her Lover’s arms.\par
\par
“Three o’clock!” She cried; “Mother, I come!”\par
\par
She clasped her hands, and sank lifeless upon the ground. Lorenzo in\par
agony threw himself beside her: He tore his hair, beat his breast, and\par
refused to be separated from the Corse. At length his force being\par
exhausted, He suffered himself to be led from the Vault, and was\par
conveyed to the Palace de Medina scarcely more alive than the\par
unfortunate Antonia.\par
\par
In the meanwhile, though closely pursued, Ambrosio succeeded in\par
regaining the Vault. The Door was already fastened when Don Ramirez\par
arrived, and much time elapsed, ere the Fugitive’s retreat was\par
discovered. But nothing can resist perseverance. Though so artfully\par
concealed, the Door could not escape the vigilance of the Archers. They\par
forced it open, and entered the Vault to the infinite dismay of\par
Ambrosio and his Companion. The Monk’s confusion, his attempt to hide\par
himself, his rapid flight, and the blood sprinkled upon his cloaths,\par
left no room to doubt his being Antonia’s Murderer. But when He was\par
recognized for the immaculate Ambrosio, “The Man of Holiness,” the Idol\par
of Madrid, the faculties of the Spectators were chained up in surprize,\par
and scarcely could they persuade themselves that what they saw was no\par
vision. The Abbot strove not to vindicate himself, but preserved a\par
sullen silence. He was secured and bound. The same precaution was taken\par
with Matilda: Her Cowl being removed, the delicacy of her features and\par
profusion of her golden hair betrayed her sex, and this incident\par
created fresh amazement. The dagger was also found in the Tomb, where\par
the Monk had thrown it; and the dungeon having undergone a thorough\par
search, the two Culprits were conveyed to the prisons of the\par
Inquisition.\par
\par
Don Ramirez took care that the populace should remain ignorant both of\par
the crimes and profession of the Captives. He feared a repetition of\par
the riots which had followed the apprehending the Prioress of St.\par
Clare. He contented himself with stating to the Capuchins the guilt of\par
their Superior. To avoid the shame of a public accusation, and dreading\par
the popular fury from which they had already saved their Abbey with\par
much difficulty, the Monks readily permitted the Inquisitors to search\par
their Mansion without noise. No fresh discoveries were made. The\par
effects found in the Abbot’s and Matilda’s Cells were seized, and\par
carried to the Inquisition to be produced in evidence. Every thing else\par
remained in its former position, and order and tranquillity once more\par
prevailed through Madrid.\par
\par
St. Clare’s Convent was completely ruined by the united ravages of the\par
Mob and conflagration. Nothing remained of it but the principal Walls,\par
whose thickness and solidity had preserved them from the flames. The\par
Nuns who had belonged to it were obliged in consequence to disperse\par
themselves into other Societies: But the prejudice against them ran\par
high, and the Superiors were very unwilling to admit them. However,\par
most of them being related to Families the most distinguished for their\par
riches, birth and power, the several Convents were compelled to receive\par
them, though they did it with a very ill grace. This prejudice was\par
extremely false and unjustifiable: After a close investigation, it was\par
proved that All in the Convent were persuaded of the death of Agnes,\par
except the four Nuns whom St. Ursula had pointed out. These had fallen\par
Victims to the popular fury; as had also several who were perfectly\par
innocent and unconscious of the whole affair. Blinded by resentment,\par
the Mob had sacrificed every Nun who fell into their hands: They who\par
escaped were entirely indebted to the Duke de Medina’s prudence and\par
moderation. Of this they were conscious, and felt for that Nobleman a\par
proper sense of gratitude.\par
\par
Virginia was not the most sparing of her thanks: She wished equally to\par
make a proper return for his attentions, and to obtain the good graces\par
of Lorenzo’s Uncle. In this She easily succeeded.\par
\par
The Duke beheld her beauty with wonder and admiration; and while his\par
eyes were enchanted with her Form, the sweetness of her manners and her\par
tender concern for the suffering Nun prepossessed his heart in her\par
favour. This Virginia had discernment enough to perceive, and She\par
redoubled her attention to the Invalid. When He parted from her at the\par
door of her Father’s Palace, the Duke entreated permission to enquire\par
occasionally after her health. His request was readily granted:\par
Virginia assured him that the Marquis de Villa-Franca would be proud of\par
an opportunity to thank him in person for the protection afforded to\par
her. They now separated, He enchanted with her beauty and gentleness,\par
and She much pleased with him and more with his Nephew.\par
\par
On entering the Palace, Virginia’s first care was to summon the family\par
Physician, and take care of her unknown charge. Her Mother hastened to\par
share with her the charitable office. Alarmed by the riots, and\par
trembling for his Daughter’s safety, who was his only child, the\par
Marquis had flown to St. Clare’s Convent, and was still employed in\par
seeking her. Messengers were now dispatched on all sides to inform him\par
that He would find her safe at his Hotel, and desire him to hasten\par
thither immediately. His absence gave Virginia liberty to bestow her\par
whole attention upon her Patient; and though much disordered herself by\par
the adventures of the night, no persuasion could induce her to quit the\par
bedside of the Sufferer. Her constitution being much enfeebled by want\par
and sorrow, it was some time before the Stranger was restored to her\par
senses. She found great difficulty in swallowing the medicines\par
prescribed to her: But this obstacle being removed, She easily\par
conquered her disease which proceeded from nothing but weakness. The\par
attention which was paid her, the wholesome food to which She had been\par
long a Stranger, and her joy at being restored to liberty, to society,\par
and, as She dared to hope, to Love, all this combined to her speedy\par
re-establishment.\par
\par
From the first moment of knowing her, her melancholy situation, her\par
sufferings almost unparalleled had engaged the affections of her\par
amiable Hostess: Virginia felt for her the most lively interest; But\par
how was She delighted, when her Guest being sufficiently recovered to\par
relate her History, She recognized in the captive Nun the Sister of\par
Lorenzo!\par
\par
This victim of monastic cruelty was indeed no other than the\par
unfortunate Agnes. During her abode in the Convent, She had been well\par
known to Virginia: But her emaciated form, her features altered by\par
affliction, her death universally credited, and her overgrown and\par
matted hair which hung over her face and bosom in disorder at first had\par
prevented her being recollected. The Prioress had put every artifice in\par
practice to induce Virginia to take the veil; for the Heiress of\par
Villa-Franca would have been no despicable acquisition. Her seeming\par
kindness and unremitted attention so far succeeded that her young\par
Relation began to think seriously upon compliance. Better instructed in\par
the disgust and ennui of a monastic life, Agnes had penetrated the\par
designs of the Domina: She trembled for the innocent Girl, and\par
endeavoured to make her sensible of her error. She painted in their\par
true colours the numerous inconveniencies attached to a Convent, the\par
continued restraint, the low jealousies, the petty intrigues, the\par
servile court and gross flattery expected by the Superior. She then bad\par
Virginia reflect on the brilliant prospect which presented itself\par
before her: The Idol of her Parents, the admiration of Madrid, endowed\par
by nature and education with every perfection of person and mind, She\par
might look forward to an establishment the most fortunate. Her riches\par
furnished her with the means of exercising in their fullest extent,\par
charity and benevolence, those virtues so dear to her; and her stay in\par
the world would enable her discovering Objects worthy her protection,\par
which could not be done in the seclusion of a Convent.\par
\par
Her persuasions induced Virginia to lay aside all thoughts of the Veil:\par
But another argument, not used by Agnes, had more weight with her than\par
all the others put together. She had seen Lorenzo, when He visited his\par
Sister at the Grate. His Person pleased her, and her conversations with\par
Agnes generally used to terminate in some question about her Brother.\par
She, who doted upon Lorenzo, wished for no better than an opportunity\par
to trumpet out his praise. She spoke of him in terms of rapture; and to\par
convince her Auditor how just were his sentiments, how cultivated his\par
mind, and elegant his expressions, She showed her at different times\par
the letters which She received from him. She soon perceived that from\par
these communications the heart of her young Friend had imbibed\par
impressions, which She was far from intending to give, but was truly\par
happy to discover. She could not have wished her Brother a more\par
desirable union: Heiress of Villa-Franca, virtuous, affectionate,\par
beautiful, and accomplished, Virginia seemed calculated to make him\par
happy. She sounded her Brother upon the subject, though without\par
mentioning names or circumstances. He assured her in his answers that\par
his heart and hand were totally disengaged, and She thought that upon\par
these grounds She might proceed without danger. She in consequence\par
endeavoured to strengthen the dawning passion of her Friend. Lorenzo\par
was made the constant topic of her discourse; and the avidity with\par
which her Auditor listened, the sighs which frequently escaped from her\par
bosom, and the eagerness with which upon any digression She brought\par
back the conversation to the subject whence it had wandered, sufficed\par
to convince Agnes that her Brother’s addresses would be far from\par
disagreeable. She at length ventured to mention her wishes to the Duke:\par
Though a Stranger to the Lady herself, He knew enough of her situation\par
to think her worthy his Nephew’s hand. It was agreed between him and\par
his Niece, that She should insinuate the idea to Lorenzo, and She only\par
waited his return to Madrid to propose her Friend to him as his Bride.\par
The unfortunate events which took place in the interim, prevented her\par
from executing her design. Virginia wept her loss sincerely, both as a\par
Companion, and as the only Person to whom She could speak of Lorenzo.\par
Her passion continued to prey upon her heart in secret, and She had\par
almost determined to confess her sentiments to her Mother, when\par
accident once more threw their object in her way. The sight of him so\par
near her, his politeness, his compassion, his intrepidity, had combined\par
to give new ardour to her affection. When She now found her Friend and\par
Advocate restored to her, She looked upon her as a Gift from Heaven;\par
She ventured to cherish the hope of being united to Lorenzo, and\par
resolved to use with him his Sister’s influence.\par
\par
Supposing that before her death Agnes might possibly have made the\par
proposal, the Duke had placed all his Nephew’s hints of marriage to\par
Virginia’s account: Consequently, He gave them the most favourable\par
reception. On returning to his Hotel, the relation given him of\par
Antonia’s death, and Lorenzo’s behaviour on the occasion, made evident\par
his mistake. He lamented the circumstances; But the unhappy Girl being\par
effectually out of the way, He trusted that his designs would yet be\par
executed. ’Tis true that Lorenzo’s situation just then ill-suited him\par
for a Bridegroom. His hopes disappointed at the moment when He expected\par
to realize them, and the dreadful and sudden death of his Mistress had\par
affected him very severely. The Duke found him upon the Bed of\par
sickness. His Attendants expressed serious apprehensions for his life;\par
But the Uncle entertained not the same fears. He was of opinion, and\par
not unwisely, that “Men have died, and worms have eat them; but not for\par
Love!” He therefore flattered himself that however deep might be the\par
impression made upon his Nephew’s heart, Time and Virginia would be\par
able to efface it. He now hastened to the afflicted Youth, and\par
endeavoured to console him: He sympathised in his distress, but\par
encouraged him to resist the encroachments of despair. He allowed that\par
He could not but feel shocked at an event so terrible, nor could He\par
blame his sensibility; But He besought him not to torment himself with\par
vain regrets, and rather to struggle with affliction, and preserve his\par
life, if not for his own sake, at least for the sake of those who were\par
fondly attached to him. While He laboured thus to make Lorenzo forget\par
Antonia’s loss, the Duke paid his court assiduously to Virginia, and\par
seized every opportunity to advance his Nephew’s interest in her heart.\par
\par
It may easily be expected that Agnes was not long without enquiring\par
after Don Raymond. She was shocked to hear the wretched situation to\par
which grief had reduced him; Yet She could not help exulting secretly,\par
when She reflected, that his illness proved the sincerity of his love.\par
The Duke undertook the office himself, of announcing to the Invalid the\par
happiness which awaited him. Though He omitted no precaution to prepare\par
him for such an event, at this sudden change from despair to happiness\par
Raymond’s transports were so violent, as nearly to have proved fatal to\par
him. These once passed, the tranquillity of his mind, the assurance of\par
felicity, and above all the presence of Agnes, (Who was no sooner\par
reestablished by the care of Virginia and the Marchioness, than She\par
hastened to attend her Lover) soon enabled him to overcome the effects\par
of his late dreadful malady. The calm of his soul communicated itself\par
to his body, and He recovered with such rapidity as to create universal\par
surprize.\par
\par
No so Lorenzo. Antonia’s death accompanied with such terrible\par
circumstances weighed upon his mind heavily. He was worn down to a\par
shadow. Nothing could give him pleasure. He was persuaded with\par
difficulty to swallow nourishment sufficient for the support of life,\par
and a consumption was apprehended. The society of Agnes formed his only\par
comfort. Though accident had never permitted their being much together,\par
He entertained for her a sincere friendship and attachment. Perceiving\par
how necessary She was to him, She seldom quitted his chamber. She\par
listened to his complaints with unwearied attention, and soothed him by\par
the gentleness of her manners, and by sympathising with his distress.\par
She still inhabited the Palace de Villa-Franca, the Possessors of which\par
treated her with marked affection. The Duke had intimated to the\par
Marquis his wishes respecting Virginia. The match was unexceptionable:\par
Lorenzo was Heir to his Uncle’s immense property, and was distinguished\par
in Madrid for his agreeable person, extensive knowledge, and propriety\par
of conduct: Add to this, that the Marchioness had discovered how strong\par
was her Daughter’s prepossession in his favour.\par
\par
In consequence the Duke’s proposal was accepted without hesitation:\par
Every precaution was taken to induce Lorenzo’s seeing the Lady with\par
those sentiments which She so well merited to excite. In her visits to\par
her Brother Agnes was frequently accompanied by the Marchioness; and as\par
soon as He was able to move into his Antichamber, Virginia under her\par
mother’s protection was sometimes permitted to express her wishes for\par
his recovery. This She did with such delicacy, the manner in which She\par
mentioned Antonia was so tender and soothing, and when She lamented her\par
Rival’s melancholy fate, her bright eyes shone so beautiful through her\par
tears, that Lorenzo could not behold, or listen to her without emotion.\par
His Relations, as well as the Lady, perceived that with every day her\par
society seemed to give him fresh pleasure, and that He spoke of her in\par
terms of stronger admiration. However, they prudently kept their\par
observations to themselves. No word was dropped which might lead him to\par
suspect their designs. They continued their former conduct and\par
attention, and left Time to ripen into a warmer sentiment the\par
friendship which He already felt for Virginia.\par
\par
In the mean while, her visits became more frequent; and latterly there\par
was scarce a day, of which She did not pass some part by the side of\par
Lorenzo’s Couch. He gradually regained his strength, but the progress\par
of his recovery was slow and doubtful. One evening He seemed to be in\par
better spirits than usual: Agnes and her Lover, the Duke, Virginia, and\par
her Parents were sitting round him. He now for the first time entreated\par
his Sister to inform him how She had escaped the effects of the poison\par
which St. Ursula had seen her swallow. Fearful of recalling those\par
scenes to his mind in which Antonia had perished, She had hitherto\par
concealed from him the history of her sufferings. As He now started the\par
subject himself, and thinking that perhaps the narrative of her sorrows\par
might draw him from the contemplation of those on which He dwelt too\par
constantly, She immediately complied with his request. The rest of the\par
company had already heard her story; But the interest which all present\par
felt for its Heroine made them anxious to hear it repeated. The whole\par
society seconding Lorenzo’s entreaties, Agnes obeyed. She first\par
recounted the discovery which had taken place in the Abbey Chapel, the\par
Domina’s resentment, and the midnight scene of which St. Ursula had\par
been a concealed witness. Though the Nun had already described this\par
latter event, Agnes now related it more circumstantially and at large:\par
After which She proceeded in her narrative as follows.\par
\par
Conclusion of the History of Agnes de Medina\par
\par
My supposed death was attended with the greatest agonies. Those moments\par
which I believed my last, were embittered by the Domina’s assurances\par
that I could not escape perdition; and as my eyes closed, I heard her\par
rage exhale itself in curses on my offence. The horror of this\par
situation, of a death-bed from which hope was banished, of a sleep from\par
which I was only to wake to find myself the prey of flames and Furies,\par
was more dreadful than I can describe. When animation revived in me, my\par
soul was still impressed with these terrible ideas: I looked round with\par
fear, expecting to behold the Ministers of divine vengeance. For the\par
first hour, my senses were so bewildered, and my brain so dizzy, that I\par
strove in vain to arrange the strange images which floated in wild\par
confusion before me. If I endeavoured to raise myself from the ground,\par
the wandering of my head deceived me. Every thing around me seemed to\par
rock, and I sank once more upon the earth. My weak and dazzled eyes\par
were unable to bear a nearer approach to a gleam of light which I saw\par
trembling above me. I was compelled to close them again, and remain\par
motionless in the same posture.\par
\par
A full hour elapsed, before I was sufficiently myself to examine the\par
surrounding Objects. When I did examine them, what terror filled my\par
bosom I found myself extended upon a sort of wicker Couch: It had six\par
handles to it, which doubtless had served the Nuns to convey me to my\par
grave. I was covered with a linen cloth:\par
\par
Several faded flowers were strown over me: On one side lay a small\par
wooden Crucifix; On the other, a Rosary of large Beads. Four low narrow\par
walls confined me. The top was also covered, and in it was practised a\par
small grated Door: Through this was admitted the little air which\par
circulated in this miserable place. A faint glimmering of light which\par
streamed through the Bars, permitted me to distinguish the surrounding\par
horrors. I was opprest by a noisome suffocating smell; and perceiving\par
that the grated door was unfastened, I thought that I might possibly\par
effect my escape. As I raised myself with this design, my hand rested\par
upon something soft: I grasped it, and advanced it towards the light.\par
Almighty God! What was my disgust, my consternation! In spite of its\par
putridity, and the worms which preyed upon it, I perceived a corrupted\par
human head, and recognised the features of a Nun who had died some\par
months before!\par
\par
I threw it from me, and sank almost lifeless upon my Bier.\par
\par
When my strength returned, this circumstance, and the consciousness of\par
being surrounded by the loathsome and mouldering Bodies of my\par
Companions, increased my desire to escape from my fearful prison. I\par
again moved towards the light. The grated door was within my reach: I\par
lifted it without difficulty; Probably it had been left unclosed to\par
facilitate my quitting the dungeon. Aiding myself by the irregularity\par
of the Walls some of whose stones projected beyond the rest, I\par
contrived to ascend them, and drag myself out of my prison. I now found\par
Myself in a Vault tolerably spacious. Several Tombs, similar in\par
appearance to that whence I had just escaped, were ranged along the\par
sides in order, and seemed to be considerably sunk within the earth. A\par
sepulchral Lamp was suspended from the roof by an iron chain, and shed\par
a gloomy light through the dungeon. Emblems of Death were seen on every\par
side: Skulls, shoulder-blades, thigh-bones, and other leavings of\par
Mortality were scattered upon the dewy ground. Each Tomb was ornamented\par
with a large Crucifix, and in one corner stood a wooden Statue of St.\par
Clare. To these objects I at first paid no attention: A Door, the only\par
outlet from the Vault, had attracted my eyes. I hastened towards it,\par
having wrapped my winding-sheet closely round me. I pushed against the\par
door, and to my inexpressible terror found that it was fastened on the\par
outside.\par
\par
I guessed immediately that the Prioress, mistaking the nature of the\par
liquor which She had compelled me to drink, instead of poison had\par
administered a strong Opiate. From this I concluded that being to all\par
appearance dead I had received the rites of burial; and that deprived\par
of the power of making my existence known, it would be my fate to\par
expire of hunger. This idea penetrated me with horror, not merely for\par
my own sake, but that of the innocent Creature, who still lived within\par
my bosom. I again endeavoured to open the door, but it resisted all my\par
efforts. I stretched my voice to the extent of its compass, and\par
shrieked for aid: I was remote from the hearing of every one: No\par
friendly voice replied to mine. A profound and melancholy silence\par
prevailed through the Vault, and I despaired of liberty. My long\par
abstinence from food now began to torment me. The tortures which hunger\par
inflicted on me, were the most painful and insupportable: Yet they\par
seemed to increase with every hour which past over my head. Sometimes I\par
threw myself upon the ground, and rolled upon it wild and desperate:\par
Sometimes starting up, I returned to the door, again strove to force it\par
open, and repeated my fruitless cries for succour. Often was I on the\par
point of striking my temple against the sharp corner of some Monument,\par
dashing out my brains, and thus terminating my woes at once; But still\par
the remembrance of my Baby vanquished my resolution: I trembled at a\par
deed which equally endangered my Child’s existence and my own. Then\par
would I vent my anguish in loud exclamations and passionate complaints;\par
and then again my strength failing me, silent and hopeless I would sit\par
me down upon the base of St. Clare’s Statue, fold my arms, and abandon\par
myself to sullen despair. Thus passed several wretched hours. Death\par
advanced towards me with rapid strides, and I expected that every\par
succeeding moment would be that of my dissolution. Suddenly a\par
neighbouring Tomb caught my eye: A Basket stood upon it, which till\par
then I had not observed. I started from my seat: I made towards it as\par
swiftly as my exhausted frame would permit. How eagerly did I seize the\par
Basket, on finding it to contain a loaf of coarse bread and a small\par
bottle of water.\par
\par
I threw myself with avidity upon these humble aliments. They had to all\par
appearance been placed in the Vault for several days; The bread was\par
hard, and the water tainted; Yet never did I taste food to me so\par
delicious. When the cravings of appetite were satisfied, I busied\par
myself with conjectures upon this new circumstance: I debated whether\par
the Basket had been placed there with a view to my necessity. Hope\par
answered my doubts in the affirmative. Yet who could guess me to be in\par
need of such assistance? If my existence was known, why was I detained\par
in this gloomy Vault? If I was kept a Prisoner, what meant the ceremony\par
of committing me to the Tomb? Or if I was doomed to perish with hunger,\par
to whose pity was I indebted for provisions placed within my reach? A\par
Friend would not have kept my dreadful punishment a secret; Neither did\par
it seem probable that an Enemy would have taken pains to supply me with\par
the means of existence. Upon the whole I was inclined to think that the\par
Domina’s designs upon my life had been discovered by some one of my\par
Partizans in the Convent, who had found means to substitute an opiate\par
for poison: That She had furnished me with food to support me, till She\par
could effect my delivery: And that She was then employed in giving\par
intelligence to my Relations of my danger, and pointing out a way to\par
release me from captivity. Yet why then was the quality of my\par
provisions so coarse? How could my Friend have entered the Vault\par
without the Domina’s knowledge? And if She had entered, why was the\par
Door fastened so carefully? These reflections staggered me: Yet still\par
this idea was the most favourable to my hopes, and I dwelt upon it in\par
preference.\par
\par
My meditations were interrupted by the sound of distant footsteps. They\par
approached, but slowly. Rays of light now darted through the crevices\par
of the Door. Uncertain whether the Persons who advanced came to relieve\par
me, or were conducted by some other motive to the Vault, I failed not\par
to attract their notice by loud cries for help. Still the sounds drew\par
near: The light grew stronger: At length with inexpressible pleasure I\par
heard the Key turning in the Lock. Persuaded that my deliverance was at\par
hand, I flew towards the Door with a shriek of joy. It opened: But all\par
my hopes of escape died away, when the Prioress appeared followed by\par
the same four Nuns, who had been witnesses of my supposed death. They\par
bore torches in their hands, and gazed upon me in fearful silence.\par
\par
I started back in terror. The Domina descended into the Vault, as did\par
also her Companions. She bent upon me a stern resentful eye, but\par
expressed no surprize at finding me still living. She took the seat\par
which I had just quitted: The door was again closed, and the Nuns\par
ranged themselves behind their Superior, while the glare of their\par
torches, dimmed by the vapours and dampness of the Vault, gilded with\par
cold beams the surrounding Monuments. For some moments all preserved a\par
dead and solemn silence. I stood at some distance from the Prioress. At\par
length She beckoned me to advance. Trembling at the severity of her\par
aspect my strength scarce sufficed me to obey her. I drew near, but my\par
limbs were unable to support their burthen. I sank upon my knees; I\par
clasped my hands, and lifted them up to her for mercy, but had no power\par
to articulate a syllable.\par
\par
She gazed upon me with angry eyes.\par
\par
“Do I see a Penitent, or a Criminal?” She said at length; “Are those\par
hands raised in contrition for your crimes, or in fear of meeting their\par
punishment? Do those tears acknowledge the justice of your doom, or\par
only solicit mitigation of your sufferings? I fear me, ’tis the\par
latter!”\par
\par
She paused, but kept her eye still fixt upon mine.\par
\par
“Take courage;” She continued: “I wish not for your death, but your\par
repentance. The draught which I administered, was no poison, but an\par
opiate. My intention in deceiving you was to make you feel the agonies\par
of a guilty conscience, had Death overtaken you suddenly while your\par
crimes were still unrepented. You have suffered those agonies: I have\par
brought you to be familiar with the sharpness of death, and I trust\par
that your momentary anguish will prove to you an eternal benefit. It is\par
not my design to destroy your immortal soul; or bid you seek the grave,\par
burthened with the weight of sins unexpiated. No, Daughter, far from\par
it: I will purify you with wholesome chastisement, and furnish you with\par
full leisure for contrition and remorse. Hear then my sentence; The\par
ill-judged zeal of your Friends delayed its execution, but cannot now\par
prevent it. All Madrid believes you to be no more; Your Relations are\par
thoroughly persuaded of your death, and the Nuns your Partizans have\par
assisted at your funeral. Your existence can never be suspected; I have\par
taken such precautions, as must render it an impenetrable mystery. Then\par
abandon all thoughts of a World from which you are eternally separated,\par
and employ the few hours which are allowed you, in preparing for the\par
next.”\par
\par
This exordium led me to expect something terrible. I trembled, and\par
would have spoken to deprecate her wrath: but a motion of the Domina\par
commanded me to be silent. She proceeded.\par
\par
“Though of late years unjustly neglected, and now opposed by many of\par
our misguided Sisters, (whom Heaven convert!) it is my intention to\par
revive the laws of our order in their full force. That against\par
incontinence is severe, but no more than so monstrous an offence\par
demands: Submit to it, Daughter, without resistance; You will find the\par
benefit of patience and resignation in a better life than this. Listen\par
then to the sentence of St. Clare. Beneath these Vaults there exist\par
Prisons, intended to receive such criminals as yourself: Artfully is\par
their entrance concealed, and She who enters them, must resign all\par
hopes of liberty. Thither must you now be conveyed. Food shall be\par
supplied you, but not sufficient for the indulgence of appetite: You\par
shall have just enough to keep together body and soul, and its quality\par
shall be the simplest and coarsest. Weep, Daughter, weep, and moisten\par
your bread with your tears: God knows that you have ample cause for\par
sorrow! Chained down in one of these secret dungeons, shut out from the\par
world and light for ever, with no comfort but religion, no society but\par
repentance, thus must you groan away the remainder of your days. Such\par
are St. Clare’s orders; Submit to them without repining. Follow me!”\par
\par
Thunderstruck at this barbarous decree, my little remaining strength\par
abandoned me. I answered only by falling at her feet, and bathing them\par
with tears. The Domina, unmoved by my affliction, rose from her seat\par
with a stately air. She repeated her commands in an absolute tone: But\par
my excessive faintness made me unable to obey her. Mariana and Alix\par
raised me from the ground, and carried me forwards in their arms. The\par
Prioress moved on, leaning upon Violante, and Camilla preceded her with\par
a Torch. Thus passed our sad procession along the passages, in silence\par
only broken by my sighs and groans. We stopped before the principal\par
shrine of St. Clare. The Statue was removed from its Pedestal, though\par
how I knew not. The Nuns afterwards raised an iron grate till then\par
concealed by the Image, and let it fall on the other side with a loud\par
crash. The awful sound, repeated by the vaults above, and Caverns below\par
me, rouzed me from the despondent apathy in which I had been plunged. I\par
looked before me: An abyss presented itself to my affrighted eyes, and\par
a steep and narrow Staircase, whither my Conductors were leading me. I\par
shrieked, and started back. I implored compassion, rent the air with my\par
cries, and summoned both heaven and earth to my assistance. In vain! I\par
was hurried down the Staircase, and forced into one of the Cells which\par
lined the Cavern’s sides.\par
\par
My blood ran cold, as I gazed upon this melancholy abode. The cold\par
vapours hovering in the air, the walls green with damp, the bed of\par
Straw so forlorn and comfortless, the Chain destined to bind me for\par
ever to my prison, and the Reptiles of every description which as the\par
torches advanced towards them, I descried hurrying to their retreats,\par
struck my heart with terrors almost too exquisite for nature to bear.\par
Driven by despair to madness, I burst suddenly from the Nuns who held\par
me: I threw myself upon my knees before the Prioress, and besought her\par
mercy in the most passionate and frantic terms.\par
\par
“If not on me,” said I, “look at least with pity on that innocent\par
Being, whose life is attached to mine! Great is my crime, but let not\par
my Child suffer for it! My Baby has committed no fault: Oh! spare me\par
for the sake of my unborn Offspring, whom ere it tastes life your\par
severity dooms to destruction!”\par
\par
The Prioress drew back haughtily: She forced her habit from my grasp,\par
as if my touch had been contagious.\par
\par
“What?” She exclaimed with an exasperated air; “What? Dare you plead\par
for the produce of your shame? Shall a Creature be permitted to live,\par
conceived in guilt so monstrous? Abandoned Woman, speak for him no\par
more! Better that the Wretch should perish than live: Begotten in\par
perjury, incontinence, and pollution, It cannot fail to prove a Prodigy\par
of vice. Hear me, thou Guilty! Expect no mercy from me either for\par
yourself, or Brat. Rather pray that Death may seize you before you\par
produce it; Or if it must see the light, that its eyes may immediately\par
be closed again for ever! No aid shall be given you in your labour;\par
Bring your Offspring into the world yourself, Feed it yourself, Nurse\par
it yourself, Bury it yourself: God grant that the latter may happen\par
soon, lest you receive comfort from the fruit of your iniquity!”\par
\par
This inhuman speech, the threats which it contained, the dreadful\par
sufferings foretold to me by the Domina, and her prayers for my\par
Infant’s death, on whom though unborn I already doated, were more than\par
my exhausted frame could support. Uttering a deep groan, I fell\par
senseless at the feet of my unrelenting Enemy. I know not how long I\par
remained in this situation; But I imagine that some time must have\par
elapsed before my recovery, since it sufficed the Prioress and her Nuns\par
to quit the Cavern. When my senses returned, I found myself in silence\par
and solitude. I heard not even the retiring footsteps of my\par
Persecutors. All was hushed, and all was dreadful! I had been thrown\par
upon the bed of Straw: The heavy Chain which I had already eyed with\par
terror, was wound around my waist, and fastened me to the Wall. A Lamp\par
glimmering with dull, melancholy rays through my dungeon, permitted my\par
distinguishing all its horrors: It was separated from the Cavern by a\par
low and irregular Wall of Stone: A large Chasm was left open in it\par
which formed the entrance, for door there was none. A leaden Crucifix\par
was in front of my straw Couch. A tattered rug lay near me, as did also\par
a Chaplet of Beads; and not far from me stood a pitcher of water, and a\par
wicker Basket containing a small loaf, and a bottle of oil to supply my\par
Lamp.\par
\par
With a despondent eye did I examine this scene of suffering: When I\par
reflected that I was doomed to pass in it the remainder of my days, my\par
heart was rent with bitter anguish. I had once been taught to look\par
forward to a lot so different! At one time my prospects had appeared so\par
bright, so flattering! Now all was lost to me. Friends, comfort,\par
society, happiness, in one moment I was deprived of all! Dead to the\par
world, Dead to pleasure, I lived to nothing but the sense of misery.\par
How fair did that world seem to me, from which I was for ever excluded!\par
How many loved objects did it contain, whom I never should behold\par
again! As I threw a look of terror round my prison, as I shrunk from\par
the cutting wind which howled through my subterraneous dwelling, the\par
change seemed so striking, so abrupt, that I doubted its reality.\par
\par
That the Duke de Medina’s Niece, that the destined Bride of the Marquis\par
de las Cisternas, One bred up in affluence, related to the noblest\par
families in Spain, and rich in a multitude of affectionate Friends,\par
that She should in one moment become a Captive, separated from the\par
world for ever, weighed down with chains, and reduced to support life\par
with the coarsest aliments, appeared a change so sudden and incredible,\par
that I believed myself the sport of some frightful vision. Its\par
continuance convinced me of my mistake with but too much certainty.\par
Every morning my hopes were disappointed. At length I abandoned all\par
idea of escaping: I resigned myself to my fate, and only expected\par
Liberty when She came the Companion of Death.\par
\par
My mental anguish, and the dreadful scenes in which I had been an\par
Actress, advanced the period of my labour. In solitude and misery,\par
abandoned by all, unassisted by Art, uncomforted by Friendship, with\par
pangs which if witnessed would have touched the hardest heart, was I\par
delivered of my wretched burthen. It came alive into the world; But I\par
knew not how to treat it, or by what means to preserve its existence. I\par
could only bathe it with tears, warm it in my bosom, and offer up\par
prayers for its safety. I was soon deprived of this mournful\par
employment: The want of proper attendance, my ignorance how to nurse\par
it, the bitter cold of the dungeon, and the unwholesome air which\par
inflated its lungs, terminated my sweet Babe’s short and painful\par
existence. It expired in a few hours after its birth, and I witnessed\par
its death with agonies which beggar all description.\par
\par
But my grief was unavailing. My Infant was no more; nor could all my\par
sighs impart to its little tender frame the breath of a moment. I rent\par
my winding-sheet, and wrapped in it my lovely Child. I placed it on my\par
bosom, its soft arm folded round my neck, and its pale cold cheek\par
resting upon mine. Thus did its lifeless limbs repose, while I covered\par
it with kisses, talked to it, wept, and moaned over it without\par
remission, day or night. Camilla entered my prison regularly once every\par
twenty-four hours, to bring me food. In spite of her flinty nature, She\par
could not behold this spectacle unmoved. She feared that grief so\par
excessive would at length turn my brain, and in truth I was not always\par
in my proper senses. From a principle of compassion She urged me to\par
permit the Corse to be buried: But to this I never would consent. I\par
vowed not to part with it while I had life: Its presence was my only\par
comfort, and no persuasion could induce me to give it up. It soon\par
became a mass of putridity, and to every eye was a loathsome and\par
disgusting Object; To every eye but a Mother’s. In vain did human\par
feelings bid me recoil from this emblem of mortality with repugnance: I\par
withstood, and vanquished that repugnance. I persisted in holding my\par
Infant to my bosom, in lamenting it, loving it, adoring it! Hour after\par
hour have I passed upon my sorry Couch, contemplating what had once\par
been my Child: I endeavoured to retrace its features through the livid\par
corruption, with which they were overspread: During my confinement this\par
sad occupation was my only delight; and at that time Worlds should not\par
have bribed me to give it up. Even when released from my prison, I\par
brought away my Child in my arms. The representations of my two kind\par
Friends,‘—(Here She took the hands of the Marchioness and Virginia, and\par
pressed them alternately to her lips)—’at length persuaded me to resign\par
my unhappy Infant to the Grave. Yet I parted from it with reluctance:\par
However, reason at length prevailed; I suffered it to be taken from me,\par
and it now reposes in consecrated ground.\par
\par
I before mentioned that regularly once a day Camilla brought me food.\par
She sought not to embitter my sorrows with reproach: She bad me, ’tis\par
true, resign all hopes of liberty and worldly happiness; But She\par
encouraged me to bear with patience my temporary distress, and advised\par
me to draw comfort from religion.\par
\par
My situation evidently affected her more than She ventured to express:\par
But She believed that to extenuate my fault would make me less anxious\par
to repent it. Often while her lips painted the enormity of my guilt in\par
glaring colours, her eyes betrayed, how sensible She was to my\par
sufferings. In fact I am certain that none of my Tormentors, (for the\par
three other Nuns entered my prison occasionally) were so much actuated\par
by the spirit of oppressive cruelty as by the idea that to afflict my\par
body was the only way to preserve my soul. Nay, even this persuasion\par
might not have had such weight with them, and they might have thought\par
my punishment too severe, had not their good dispositions been represt\par
by blind obedience to their Superior. Her resentment existed in full\par
force. My project of elopement having been discovered by the Abbot of\par
the Capuchins, She supposed herself lowered in his opinion by my\par
disgrace, and in consequence her hate was inveterate. She told the Nuns\par
to whose custody I was committed that my fault was of the most heinous\par
nature, that no sufferings could equal the offence, and that nothing\par
could save me from eternal perdition but punishing my guilt with the\par
utmost severity. The Superior’s word is an oracle to but too many of a\par
Convent’s Inhabitants. The Nuns believed whatever the Prioress chose to\par
assert: Though contradicted by reason and charity, they hesitated not\par
to admit the truth of her arguments. They followed her injunctions to\par
the very letter, and were fully persuaded that to treat me with lenity,\par
or to show the least pity for my woes, would be a direct means to\par
destroy my chance for salvation.\par
\par
Camilla, being most employed about me, was particularly charged by the\par
Prioress to treat me with harshness. In compliance with these orders,\par
She frequently strove to convince me, how just was my punishment, and\par
how enormous was my crime: She bad me think myself too happy in saving\par
my soul by mortifying my body, and even threatened me sometimes with\par
eternal perdition. Yet as I before observed, She always concluded by\par
words of encouragement and comfort; and though uttered by Camilla’s\par
lips, I easily recognised the Domina’s expressions. Once, and once\par
only, the Prioress visited me in my dungeon. She then treated me with\par
the most unrelenting cruelty: She loaded me with reproaches, taunted me\par
with my frailty, and when I implored her mercy, told me to ask it of\par
heaven, since I deserved none on earth. She even gazed upon my lifeless\par
Infant without emotion; and when She left me, I heard her charge\par
Camilla to increase the hardships of my Captivity. Unfeeling Woman! But\par
let me check my resentment: She has expiated her errors by her sad and\par
unexpected death. Peace be with her; and may her crimes be forgiven in\par
heaven, as I forgive her my sufferings on earth!\par
\par
Thus did I drag on a miserable existence. Far from growing familiar\par
with my prison, I beheld it every moment with new horror. The cold\par
seemed more piercing and bitter, the air more thick and pestilential.\par
My frame became weak, feverish, and emaciated. I was unable to rise\par
from the bed of Straw, and exercise my limbs in the narrow limits, to\par
which the length of my chain permitted me to move. Though exhausted,\par
faint, and weary, I trembled to profit by the approach of Sleep: My\par
slumbers were constantly interrupted by some obnoxious Insect crawling\par
over me.\par
\par
Sometimes I felt the bloated Toad, hideous and pampered with the\par
poisonous vapours of the dungeon, dragging his loathsome length along\par
my bosom: Sometimes the quick cold Lizard rouzed me leaving his slimy\par
track upon my face, and entangling itself in the tresses of my wild and\par
matted hair: Often have I at waking found my fingers ringed with the\par
long worms which bred in the corrupted flesh of my Infant. At such\par
times I shrieked with terror and disgust, and while I shook off the\par
reptile, trembled with all a Woman’s weakness.\par
\par
Such was my situation, when Camilla was suddenly taken ill. A dangerous\par
fever, supposed to be infectious, confined her to her bed. Every one\par
except the Lay-Sister appointed to nurse her, avoided her with caution,\par
and feared to catch the disease. She was perfectly delirious, and by no\par
means capable of attending to me. The Domina and the Nuns admitted to\par
the mystery, had latterly given me over entirely to Camilla’s care: In\par
consequence, they busied themselves no more about me; and occupied by\par
preparing for the approaching Festival, it is more than probable that I\par
never once entered into their thoughts. Of the reason of Camilla’s\par
negligence, I have been informed since my release by the Mother St.\par
Ursula; At that time I was very far from suspecting its cause. On the\par
contrary, I waited for my Gaoler’s appearance at first with impatience,\par
and afterwards with despair. One day passed away; Another followed it;\par
The Third arrived. Still no Camilla! Still no food! I knew the lapse of\par
time by the wasting of my Lamp, to supply which fortunately a week’s\par
supply of Oil had been left me. I supposed, either that the Nuns had\par
forgotten me, or that the Domina had ordered them to let me perish. The\par
latter idea seemed the most probable; Yet so natural is the love of\par
life, that I trembled to find it true. Though embittered by every\par
species of misery, my existence was still dear to me, and I dreaded to\par
lose it. Every succeeding minute proved to me that I must abandon all\par
hopes of relief. I was become an absolute skeleton: My eyes already\par
failed me, and my limbs were beginning to stiffen. I could only express\par
my anguish, and the pangs of that hunger which gnawed my heart-strings,\par
by frequent groans, whose melancholy sound the vaulted roof of the\par
dungeon re-echoed. I resigned myself to my fate: I already expected the\par
moment of dissolution, when my Guardian Angel, when my beloved Brother\par
arrived in time to save me. My sight grown dim and feeble at first\par
refused to recognize him; and when I did distinguish his features, the\par
sudden burst of rapture was too much for me to bear. I was overpowered\par
by the swell of joy at once more beholding a Friend, and that a Friend\par
so dear to me. Nature could not support my emotions, and took her\par
refuge in insensibility.\par
\par
You already know, what are my obligations to the Family of\par
Villa-Franca: But what you cannot know is the extent of my gratitude,\par
boundless as the excellence of my Benefactors. Lorenzo! Raymond! Names\par
so dear to me! Teach me to bear with fortitude this sudden transition\par
from misery to bliss. So lately a Captive, opprest with chains,\par
perishing with hunger, suffering every inconvenience of cold and want,\par
hidden from the light, excluded from society, hopeless, neglected, and\par
as I feared, forgotten; Now restored to life and liberty, enjoying all\par
the comforts of affluence and ease, surrounded by those who are most\par
loved by me, and on the point of becoming his Bride who has long been\par
wedded to my heart, my happiness is so exquisite, so perfect, that\par
scarcely can my brain sustain the weight. One only wish remains\par
ungratified: It is to see my Brother in his former health, and to know\par
that Antonia’s memory is buried in her grave.\par
\par
Granted this prayer, I have nothing more to desire. I trust, that my\par
past sufferings have purchased from heaven the pardon of my momentary\par
weakness. That I have offended, offended greatly and grievously, I am\par
fully conscious; But let not my Husband, because He once conquered my\par
virtue, doubt the propriety of my future conduct. I have been frail and\par
full of error: But I yielded not to the warmth of constitution;\par
Raymond, affection for you betrayed me. I was too confident of my\par
strength; But I depended no less on your honour than my own. I had\par
vowed never to see you more: Had it not been for the consequences of\par
that unguarded moment, my resolution had been kept. Fate willed it\par
otherwise, and I cannot but rejoice at its decree. Still my conduct has\par
been highly blameable, and while I attempt to justify myself, I blush\par
at recollecting my imprudence. Let me then dismiss the ungrateful\par
subject; First assuring you, Raymond, that you shall have no cause to\par
repent our union, and that the more culpable have been the errors of\par
your Mistress, the more exemplary shall be the conduct of your Wife.\par
\par
\par
Here Agnes ceased, and the Marquis replied to her address in terms\par
equally sincere and affectionate. Lorenzo expressed his satisfaction at\par
the prospect of being so closely connected with a Man for whom He had\par
ever entertained the highest esteem. The Pope’s Bull had fully and\par
effectually released Agnes from her religious engagements: The marriage\par
was therefore celebrated as soon as the needful preparations had been\par
made, for the Marquis wished to have the ceremony performed with all\par
possible splendour and publicity. This being over, and the Bride having\par
received the compliments of Madrid, She departed with Don Raymond for\par
his Castle in Andalusia: Lorenzo accompanied them, as did also the\par
Marchioness de Villa-Franca and her lovely Daughter. It is needless to\par
say that Theodore was of the party, and would be impossible to describe\par
his joy at his Master’s marriage. Previous to his departure, the\par
Marquis, to atone in some measure for his past neglect, made some\par
enquiries relative to Elvira. Finding that She as well as her Daughter\par
had received many services from Leonella and Jacintha, He showed his\par
respect to the memory of his Sister-in-law by making the two Women\par
handsome presents. Lorenzo followed his example—Leonella was highly\par
flattered by the attentions of Noblemen so distinguished, and Jacintha\par
blessed the hour on which her House was bewitched.\par
\par
On her side, Agnes failed not to reward her Convent Friends. The worthy\par
Mother St. Ursula, to whom She owed her liberty, was named at her\par
request Superintendent of “The Ladies of Charity:” This was one of the\par
best and most opulent Societies throughout Spain. Bertha and Cornelia\par
not choosing to quit their Friend, were appointed to principal charges\par
in the same establishment. As to the Nuns who had aided the Domina in\par
persecuting Agnes, Camilla being confined by illness to her bed, had\par
perished in the flames which consumed St. Clare’s Convent. Mariana,\par
Alix, and Violante, as well as two more, had fallen victims to the\par
popular rage. The three Others who in Council had supported the\par
Domina’s sentence, were severely reprimanded, and banished to religious\par
Houses in obscure and distant Provinces: Here they languished away a\par
few years, ashamed of their former weakness, and shunned by their\par
Companions with aversion and contempt.\par
\par
Nor was the fidelity of Flora permitted to go unrewarded. Her wishes\par
being consulted, She declared herself impatient to revisit her native\par
land. In consequence, a passage was procured for her to Cuba, where She\par
arrived in safety, loaded with the presents of Raymond and Lorenzo.\par
\par
The debts of gratitude discharged, Agnes was at liberty to pursue her\par
favourite plan. Lodged in the same House, Lorenzo and Virginia were\par
eternally together. The more He saw of her, the more was He convinced\par
of her merit. On her part, She laid herself out to please, and not to\par
succeed was for her impossible.\par
\par
Lorenzo witnessed with admiration her beautiful person, elegant\par
manners, innumerable talents, and sweet disposition: He was also much\par
flattered by her prejudice in his favour, which She had not sufficient\par
art to conceal. However, his sentiments partook not of that ardent\par
character which had marked his affection for Antonia. The image of that\par
lovely and unfortunate Girl still lived in his heart, and baffled all\par
Virginia’s efforts to displace it. Still when the Duke proposed to him\par
the match, which He wished to earnestly to take place, his Nephew did\par
not reject the offer. The urgent supplications of his Friends, and the\par
Lady’s merit conquered his repugnance to entering into new engagements.\par
He proposed himself to the Marquis de Villa-Franca, and was accepted\par
with joy and gratitude. Virginia became his Wife, nor did She ever give\par
him cause to repent his choice. His esteem increased for her daily. Her\par
unremitted endeavours to please him could not but succeed. His\par
affection assumed stronger and warmer colours. Antonia’s image was\par
gradually effaced from his bosom; and Virginia became sole Mistress of\par
that heart, which She well deserved to possess without a Partner.\par
\par
The remaining years of Raymond and Agnes, of Lorenzo and Virginia, were\par
happy as can be those allotted to Mortals, born to be the prey of\par
grief, and sport of disappointment. The exquisite sorrows with which\par
they had been afflicted, made them think lightly of every succeeding\par
woe. They had felt the sharpest darts in misfortune’s quiver; Those\par
which remained appeared blunt in comparison. Having weathered Fate’s\par
heaviest Storms, they looked calmly upon its terrors: or if ever they\par
felt Affliction’s casual gales, they seemed to them gentle as Zephyrs\par
which breathe over summer-seas.\par
\par
\par
\par
\par
CHAPTER XII.\par
\par
\par
——He was a fell despightful Fiend:\par
Hell holds none worse in baleful bower below:\par
By pride, and wit, and rage, and rancor keened;\par
Of Man alike, if good or bad the Foe.\par
\par
THOMSON.\par
\par
On the day following Antonia’s death, all Madrid was a scene of\par
consternation and amazement. An Archer who had witnessed the adventure\par
in the Sepulchre had indiscreetly related the circumstances of the\par
murder: He had also named the Perpetrator. The confusion was without\par
example which this intelligence raised among the Devotees. Most of them\par
disbelieved it, and went themselves to the Abbey to ascertain the fact.\par
Anxious to avoid the shame to which their Superior’s ill-conduct\par
exposed the whole Brotherhood, the Monks assured the Visitors that\par
Ambrosio was prevented from receiving them as usual by nothing but\par
illness. This attempt was unsuccessful: The same excuse being repeated\par
day after day, the Archer’s story gradually obtained confidence. His\par
Partizans abandoned him: No one entertained a doubt of his guilt; and\par
they who before had been the warmest in his praise were now the most\par
vociferous in his condemnation.\par
\par
While his innocence or guilt was debated in Madrid with the utmost\par
acrimony, Ambrosio was a prey to the pangs of conscious villainy, and\par
the terrors of punishment impending over him. When He looked back to\par
the eminence on which He had lately stood, universally honoured and\par
respected, at peace with the world and with himself, scarcely could He\par
believe that He was indeed the culprit whose crimes and whose fate He\par
trembled to envisage. But a few weeks had elapsed, since He was pure\par
and virtuous, courted by the wisest and noblest in Madrid, and regarded\par
by the People with a reverence that approached idolatry: He now saw\par
himself stained with the most loathed and monstrous sins, the object of\par
universal execration, a Prisoner of the Holy Office, and probably\par
doomed to perish in tortures the most severe. He could not hope to\par
deceive his Judges: The proofs of his guilt were too strong. His being\par
in the Sepulchre at so late an hour, his confusion at the discovery,\par
the dagger which in his first alarm He owned had been concealed by him,\par
and the blood which had spirted upon his habit from Antonia’s wound,\par
sufficiently marked him out for the Assassin. He waited with agony for\par
the day of examination: He had no resource to comfort him in his\par
distress. Religion could not inspire him with fortitude: If He read the\par
Books of morality which were put into his hands, He saw in them nothing\par
but the enormity of his offences; If he attempted to pray, He\par
recollected that He deserved not heaven’s protection, and believed his\par
crimes so monstrous as to baffle even God’s infinite goodness. For\par
every other Sinner He thought there might be hope, but for him there\par
could be none. Shuddering at the past, anguished by the present, and\par
dreading the future, thus passed He the few days preceding that which\par
was marked for his Trial.\par
\par
That day arrived. At nine in the morning his prison door was unlocked,\par
and his Gaoler entering, commanded him to follow him. He obeyed with\par
trembling. He was conducted into a spacious Hall, hung with black\par
cloth. At the Table sat three grave, stern-looking Men, also habited in\par
black: One was the Grand Inquisitor, whom the importance of this cause\par
had induced to examine into it himself. At a smaller table at a little\par
distance sat the Secretary, provided with all necessary implements for\par
writing. Ambrosio was beckoned to advance, and take his station at the\par
lower end of the Table. As his eye glanced downwards, He perceived\par
various iron instruments lying scattered upon the floor. Their forms\par
were unknown to him, but apprehension immediately guessed them to be\par
engines of torture. He turned pale, and with difficulty prevented\par
himself from sinking upon the ground.\par
\par
Profound silence prevailed, except when the Inquisitors whispered a few\par
words among themselves mysteriously. Near an hour past away, and with\par
every second of it Ambrosio’s fears grew more poignant. At length a\par
small Door, opposite to that by which He had entered the Hall, grated\par
heavily upon its hinges. An Officer appeared, and was immediately\par
followed by the beautiful Matilda. Her hair hung about her face wildly;\par
Her cheeks were pale, and her eyes sunk and hollow. She threw a\par
melancholy look upon Ambrosio: He replied by one of aversion and\par
reproach. She was placed opposite to him. A Bell then sounded thrice.\par
It was the signal for opening the Court, and the Inquisitors entered\par
upon their office.\par
\par
In these trials neither the accusation is mentioned, or the name of the\par
Accuser. The Prisoners are only asked, whether they will confess: If\par
they reply that having no crime they can make no confession, they are\par
put to the torture without delay. This is repeated at intervals, either\par
till the suspected avow themselves culpable, or the perseverance of the\par
examinants is worn out and exhausted: But without a direct\par
acknowledgment of their guilt, the Inquisition never pronounces the\par
final doom of its Prisoners.\par
\par
In general much time is suffered to elapse without their being\par
questioned: But Ambrosio’s trial had been hastened, on account of a\par
solemn Auto da Fe which would take place in a few days, and in which\par
the Inquisitors meant this distinguished Culprit to perform a part, and\par
give a striking testimony of their vigilance.\par
\par
The Abbot was not merely accused of rape and murder: The crime of\par
Sorcery was laid to his charge, as well as to Matilda’s. She had been\par
seized as an Accomplice in Antonia’s assassination. On searching her\par
Cell, various suspicious books and instruments were found which\par
justified the accusation brought against her. To criminate the Monk,\par
the constellated Mirror was produced, which Matilda had accidentally\par
left in his chamber. The strange figures engraved upon it caught the\par
attention of Don Ramirez, while searching the Abbot’s Cell: In\par
consequence, He carried it away with him. It was shown to the Grand\par
Inquisitor, who having considered it for some time, took off a small\par
golden Cross which hung at his girdle, and laid it upon the Mirror.\par
Instantly a loud noise was heard, resembling a clap of thunder, and the\par
steel shivered into a thousand pieces. This circumstance confirmed the\par
suspicion of the Monk’s having dealt in Magic: It was even supposed\par
that his former influence over the minds of the People was entirely to\par
be ascribed to witchcraft.\par
\par
Determined to make him confess not only the crimes which He had\par
committed, but those also of which He was innocent, the Inquisitors\par
began their examination. Though dreading the tortures, as He dreaded\par
death still more which would consign him to eternal torments, the Abbot\par
asserted his purity in a voice bold and resolute. Matilda followed his\par
example, but spoke with fear and trembling. Having in vain exhorted him\par
to confess, the Inquisitors ordered the Monk to be put to the question.\par
The Decree was immediately executed. Ambrosio suffered the most\par
excruciating pangs that ever were invented by human cruelty: Yet so\par
dreadful is Death when guilt accompanies it, that He had sufficient\par
fortitude to persist in his disavowal. His agonies were redoubled in\par
consequence: Nor was He released till fainting from excess of pain,\par
insensibility rescued him from the hands of his Tormentors.\par
\par
Matilda was next ordered to the torture: But terrified by the sight of\par
the Friar’s sufferings, her courage totally deserted her. She sank upon\par
her knees, acknowledged her corresponding with infernal Spirits, and\par
that She had witnessed the Monk’s assassination of Antonia: But as to\par
the crime of Sorcery, She declared herself the sole criminal, and\par
Ambrosio perfectly innocent. The latter assertion met with no credit.\par
The Abbot had recovered his senses in time to hear the confession of\par
his Accomplice: But He was too much enfeebled by what He had already\par
undergone to be capable at that time of sustaining new torments.\par
\par
He was commanded back to his Cell, but first informed that as soon as\par
He had gained strength sufficient, He must prepare himself for a second\par
examination. The Inquisitors hoped that He would then be less hardened\par
and obstinate. To Matilda it was announced that She must expiate her\par
crime in fire on the approaching Auto da Fe. All her tears and\par
entreaties could procure no mitigation of her doom, and She was dragged\par
by force from the Hall of Trial.\par
\par
Returned to his dungeon, the sufferings of Ambrosio’s body were far\par
more supportable than those of his mind. His dislocated limbs, the\par
nails torn from his hands and feet, and his fingers mashed and broken\par
by the pressure of screws, were far surpassed in anguish by the\par
agitation of his soul and vehemence of his terrors. He saw that, guilty\par
or innocent, his Judges were bent upon condemning him: The remembrance\par
of what his denial had already cost him terrified him at the idea of\par
being again applied to the question, and almost engaged him to confess\par
his crimes. Then again the consequences of his confession flashed\par
before him, and rendered him once more irresolute. His death would be\par
inevitable, and that a death the most dreadful: He had listened to\par
Matilda’s doom, and doubted not that a similar was reserved for him. He\par
shuddered at the approaching Auto da Fe, at the idea of perishing in\par
flames, and only escaping from indurable torments to pass into others\par
more subtile and ever-lasting! With affright did He bend his mind’s eye\par
on the space beyond the grave; nor could hide from himself how justly\par
he ought to dread Heaven’s vengeance. In this Labyrinth of terrors,\par
fain would He have taken his refuge in the gloom of Atheism: Fain would\par
He have denied the soul’s immortality; have persuaded himself that when\par
his eyes once closed, they would never more open, and that the same\par
moment would annihilate his soul and body. Even this resource was\par
refused to him. To permit his being blind to the fallacy of this\par
belief, his knowledge was too extensive, his understanding too solid\par
and just. He could not help feeling the existence of a God. Those\par
truths, once his comfort, now presented themselves before him in the\par
clearest light; But they only served to drive him to distraction. They\par
destroyed his ill-grounded hopes of escaping punishment; and dispelled\par
by the irresistible brightness of Truth and convinction, Philosophy’s\par
deceitful vapours faded away like a dream.\par
\par
In anguish almost too great for mortal frame to bear, He expected the\par
time when He was again to be examined. He busied himself in planning\par
ineffectual schemes for escaping both present and future punishment. Of\par
the first there was no possibility; Of the second Despair made him\par
neglect the only means. While Reason forced him to acknowledge a God’s\par
existence, Conscience made him doubt the infinity of his goodness. He\par
disbelieved that a Sinner like him could find mercy. He had not been\par
deceived into error: Ignorance could furnish him with no excuse. He had\par
seen vice in her true colours; Before He committed his crimes, He had\par
computed every scruple of their weight; and yet he had committed them.\par
\par
“Pardon?” He would cry in an access of phrenzy “Oh! there can be none\par
for me!”\par
\par
Persuaded of this, instead of humbling himself in penitence, of\par
deploring his guilt, and employing his few remaining hours in\par
deprecating Heaven’s wrath, He abandoned himself to the transports of\par
desperate rage; He sorrowed for the punishment of his crimes, not their\par
commission; and exhaled his bosom’s anguish in idle sighs, in vain\par
lamentations, in blasphemy and despair. As the few beams of day which\par
pierced through the bars of his prison window gradually disappeared,\par
and their place was supplied by the pale and glimmering Lamp, He felt\par
his terrors redouble, and his ideas become more gloomy, more solemn,\par
more despondent. He dreaded the approach of sleep: No sooner did his\par
eyes close, wearied with tears and watching, than the dreadful visions\par
seemed to be realised on which his mind had dwelt during the day. He\par
found himself in sulphurous realms and burning Caverns, surrounded by\par
Fiends appointed his Tormentors, and who drove him through a variety of\par
tortures, each of which was more dreadful than the former. Amidst these\par
dismal scenes wandered the Ghosts of Elvira and her Daughter. They\par
reproached him with their deaths, recounted his crimes to the Dæmons,\par
and urged them to inflict torments of cruelty yet more refined. Such\par
were the pictures which floated before his eyes in sleep: They vanished\par
not till his repose was disturbed by excess of agony. Then would He\par
start from the ground on which He had stretched himself, his brows\par
running down with cold sweat, his eyes wild and phrenzied; and He only\par
exchanged the terrible certainty for surmizes scarcely more\par
supportable. He paced his dungeon with disordered steps; He gazed with\par
terror upon the surrounding darkness, and often did He cry,\par
\par
“Oh! fearful is night to the Guilty!”\par
\par
The day of his second examination was at hand. He had been compelled to\par
swallow cordials, whose virtues were calculated to restore his bodily\par
strength, and enable him to support the question longer. On the night\par
preceding this dreaded day, his fears for the morrow permitted him not\par
to sleep. His terrors were so violent, as nearly to annihilate his\par
mental powers. He sat like one stupefied near the Table on which his\par
Lamp was burning dimly. Despair chained up his faculties in Idiotism,\par
and He remained for some hours, unable to speak or move, or indeed to\par
think.\par
\par
“Look up, Ambrosio!” said a Voice in accents well-known to him—\par
\par
The Monk started, and raised his melancholy eyes. Matilda stood before\par
him. She had quitted her religious habit. She now wore a female dress,\par
at once elegant and splendid: A profusion of diamonds blazed upon her\par
robes, and her hair was confined by a coronet of Roses. In her right\par
hand She held a small Book: A lively expression of pleasure beamed upon\par
her countenance; But still it was mingled with a wild imperious majesty\par
which inspired the Monk with awe, and represt in some measure his\par
transports at seeing her.\par
\par
“You here, Matilda?” He at length exclaimed; “How have you gained\par
entrance? Where are your Chains? What means this magnificence, and the\par
joy which sparkles in your eyes? Have our Judges relented? Is there a\par
chance of my escaping? Answer me for pity, and tell me, what I have to\par
hope, or fear.”\par
\par
“Ambrosio!” She replied with an air of commanding dignity; “I have\par
baffled the Inquisition’s fury. I am free: A few moments will place\par
kingdoms between these dungeons and me. Yet I purchase my liberty at a\par
dear, at a dreadful price! Dare you pay the same, Ambrosio? Dare you\par
spring without fear over the bounds which separate Men from Angels?—You\par
are silent.—You look upon me with eyes of suspicion and alarm—I read\par
your thoughts and confess their justice. Yes, Ambrosio; I have\par
sacrificed all for life and liberty. I am no longer a candidate for\par
heaven! I have renounced God’s service, and am enlisted beneath the\par
banners of his Foes. The deed is past recall: Yet were it in my power\par
to go back, I would not. Oh! my Friend, to expire in such torments! To\par
die amidst curses and execrations! To bear the insults of an\par
exasperated Mob! To be exposed to all the mortifications of shame and\par
infamy! Who can reflect without horror on such a doom? Let me then\par
exult in my exchange. I have sold distant and uncertain happiness for\par
present and secure: I have preserved a life which otherwise I had lost\par
in torture; and I have obtained the power of procuring every bliss\par
which can make that life delicious! The Infernal Spirits obey me as\par
their Sovereign: By their aid shall my days be past in every refinement\par
of luxury and voluptuousness. I will enjoy unrestrained the\par
gratification of my senses: Every passion shall be indulged, even to\par
satiety; Then will I bid my Servants invent new pleasures, to revive\par
and stimulate my glutted appetites! I go impatient to exercise my\par
newly-gained dominion. I pant to be at liberty. Nothing should hold me\par
one moment longer in this abhorred abode, but the hope of persuading\par
you to follow my example. Ambrosio, I still love you: Our mutual guilt\par
and danger have rendered you dearer to me than ever, and I would fain\par
save you from impending destruction. Summon then your resolution to\par
your aid; and renounce for immediate and certain benefits the hopes of\par
a salvation, difficult to obtain, and perhaps altogether erroneous.\par
Shake off the prejudice of vulgar souls; Abandon a God who has\par
abandoned you, and raise yourself to the level of superior Beings!”\par
\par
She paused for the Monk’s reply: He shuddered, while He gave it.\par
\par
“Matilda!” He said after a long silence in a low and unsteady voice;\par
“What price gave you for liberty?”\par
\par
She answered him firm and dauntless.\par
\par
“Ambrosio, it was my Soul!”\par
\par
“Wretched Woman, what have you done? Pass but a few years, and how\par
dreadful will be your sufferings!”\par
\par
“Weak Man, pass but this night, and how dreadful will be your own! Do\par
you remember what you have already endured? Tomorrow you must bear\par
torments doubly exquisite. Do you remember the horrors of a fiery\par
punishment? In two days you must be led a Victim to the Stake! What\par
then will become of you? Still dare you hope for pardon? Still are you\par
beguiled with visions of salvation? Think upon your crimes! Think upon\par
your lust, your perjury, inhumanity, and hypocrisy! Think upon the\par
innocent blood which cries to the Throne of God for vengeance, and then\par
hope for mercy! Then dream of heaven, and sigh for worlds of light, and\par
realms of peace and pleasure! Absurd! Open your eyes, Ambrosio, and be\par
prudent. Hell is your lot; You are doomed to eternal perdition; Nought\par
lies beyond your grave but a gulph of devouring flames. And will you\par
then speed towards that Hell? Will you clasp that perdition in your\par
arms, ere ’tis needful? Will you plunge into those flames while you\par
still have the power to shun them? ’Tis a Madman’s action. No, no,\par
Ambrosio: Let us for awhile fly from divine vengeance. Be advised by\par
me; Purchase by one moment’s courage the bliss of years; Enjoy the\par
present, and forget that a future lags behind.”\par
\par
“Matilda, your counsels are dangerous: I dare not, I will not follow\par
them. I must not give up my claim to salvation. Monstrous are my\par
crimes; But God is merciful, and I will not despair of pardon.”\par
\par
“Is such your resolution? I have no more to say. I speed to joy and\par
liberty, and abandon you to death and eternal torments.”\par
\par
“Yet stay one moment, Matilda! You command the infernal Dæmons:\par
\par
You can force open these prison doors; You can release me from these\par
chains which weigh me down. Save me, I conjure you, and bear me from\par
these fearful abodes!”\par
\par
“You ask the only boon beyond my power to bestow. I am forbidden to\par
assist a Churchman and a Partizan of God: Renounce those titles, and\par
command me.”\par
\par
“I will not sell my soul to perdition.”\par
\par
“Persist in your obstinacy, till you find yourself at the Stake: Then\par
will you repent your error, and sigh for escape when the moment is gone\par
by. I quit you. Yet ere the hour of death arrives should wisdom\par
enlighten you, listen to the means of repairing your present fault. I\par
leave with you this Book. Read the four first lines of the seventh page\par
backwards: The Spirit whom you have already once beheld will\par
immediately appear to you. If you are wise, we shall meet again: If\par
not, farewell for ever!”\par
\par
She let the Book fall upon the ground. A cloud of blue fire wrapped\par
itself round her: She waved her hand to Ambrosio, and disappeared. The\par
momentary glare which the flames poured through the dungeon, on\par
dissipating suddenly, seemed to have increased its natural gloom. The\par
solitary Lamp scarcely gave light sufficient to guide the Monk to a\par
Chair. He threw himself into his seat, folded his arms, and leaning his\par
head upon the table, sank into reflections perplexing and unconnected.\par
\par
He was still in this attitude when the opening of the prison door\par
rouzed him from his stupor. He was summoned to appear before the Grand\par
Inquisitor. He rose, and followed his Gaoler with painful steps. He was\par
led into the same Hall, placed before the same Examiners, and was again\par
interrogated whether He would confess. He replied as before, that\par
having no crimes, He could acknowledge none: But when the Executioners\par
prepared to put him to the question, when He saw the engines of\par
torture, and remembered the pangs which they had already inflicted, his\par
resolution failed him entirely. Forgetting the consequences, and only\par
anxious to escape the terrors of the present moment, He made an ample\par
confession. He disclosed every circumstance of his guilt, and owned not\par
merely the crimes with which He was charged, but those of which He had\par
never been suspected. Being interrogated as to Matilda’s flight which\par
had created much confusion, He confessed that She had sold herself to\par
Satan, and that She was indebted to Sorcery for her escape. He still\par
assured his Judges that for his own part He had never entered into any\par
compact with the infernal Spirits; But the threat of being tortured\par
made him declare himself to be a Sorcerer, and Heretic, and whatever\par
other title the Inquisitors chose to fix upon him. In consequence of\par
this avowal, his sentence was immediately pronounced. He was ordered to\par
prepare himself to perish in the Auto da Fe, which was to be solemnized\par
at twelve o’clock that night. This hour was chosen from the idea that\par
the horror of the flames being heightened by the gloom of midnight, the\par
execution would have a greater effect upon the mind of the People.\par
\par
Ambrosio rather dead than alive was left alone in his dungeon. The\par
moment in which this terrible decree was pronounced had nearly proved\par
that of his dissolution. He looked forward to the morrow with despair,\par
and his terrors increased with the approach of midnight. Sometimes He\par
was buried in gloomy silence: At others He raved with delirious\par
passion, wrung his hands, and cursed the hour when He first beheld the\par
light. In one of these moments his eye rested upon Matilda’s mysterious\par
gift. His transports of rage were instantly suspended. He looked\par
earnestly at the Book; He took it up, but immediately threw it from him\par
with horror. He walked rapidly up and down his dungeon: Then stopped,\par
and again fixed his eyes on the spot where the Book had fallen. He\par
reflected that here at least was a resource from the fate which He\par
dreaded. He stooped, and took it up a second time.\par
\par
He remained for some time trembling and irresolute: He longed to try\par
the charm, yet feared its consequences. The recollection of his\par
sentence at length fixed his indecision. He opened the Volume; but his\par
agitation was so great that He at first sought in vain for the page\par
mentioned by Matilda. Ashamed of himself, He called all his courage to\par
his aid. He turned to the seventh leaf. He began to read it aloud; But\par
his eyes frequently wandered from the Book, while He anxiously cast\par
them round in search of the Spirit, whom He wished, yet dreaded to\par
behold. Still He persisted in his design; and with a voice unassured\par
and frequent interruptions, He contrived to finish the four first lines\par
of the page.\par
\par
They were in a language, whose import was totally unknown to him.\par
\par
Scarce had He pronounced the last word when the effects of the charm\par
were evident. A loud burst of Thunder was heard; The prison shook to\par
its very foundations; A blaze of lightning flashed through the Cell;\par
and in the next moment, borne upon sulphurous whirl-winds, Lucifer\par
stood before him a second time. But He came not as when at Matilda’s\par
summons He borrowed the Seraph’s form to deceive Ambrosio. He appeared\par
in all that ugliness which since his fall from heaven had been his\par
portion: His blasted limbs still bore marks of the Almighty’s thunder:\par
A swarthy darkness spread itself over his gigantic form: His hands and\par
feet were armed with long Talons: Fury glared in his eyes, which might\par
have struck the bravest heart with terror: Over his huge shoulders\par
waved two enormous sable wings; and his hair was supplied by living\par
snakes, which twined themselves round his brows with frightful\par
hissings. In one hand He held a roll of parchment, and in the other an\par
iron pen. Still the lightning flashed around him, and the Thunder with\par
repeated bursts, seemed to announce the dissolution of Nature.\par
\par
Terrified at an Apparition so different from what He had expected,\par
Ambrosio remained gazing upon the Fiend, deprived of the power of\par
utterance. The Thunder had ceased to roll: Universal silence reigned\par
through the dungeon.\par
\par
“For what am I summoned hither?” said the dæmon, in a voice which\par
_sulphurous fogs had damped to hoarseness_.\par
\par
At the sound Nature seemed to tremble: A violent earthquake rocked the\par
ground, accompanied by a fresh burst of Thunder, louder and more\par
appalling than the first.\par
\par
Ambrosio was long unable to answer the Dæmon’s demand.\par
\par
“I am condemned to die;” He said with a faint voice, his blood running\par
cold, while He gazed upon his dreadful Visitor. “Save me! Bear me from\par
hence!”\par
\par
“Shall the reward of my services be paid me? Dare you embrace my cause?\par
Will you be mine, body and soul? Are you prepared to renounce him who\par
made you, and him who died for you? Answer but ‘Yes’ and Lucifer is\par
your Slave.”\par
\par
“Will no less price content you? Can nothing satisfy you but my eternal\par
ruin? Spirit, you ask too much. Yet convey me from this dungeon: Be my\par
Servant for one hour, and I will be yours for a thousand years. Will\par
not this offer suffice?”\par
\par
“It will not. I must have your soul; must have it mine, and mine for\par
ever.”\par
\par
“Insatiate Dæmon, I will not doom myself to endless torments. I will\par
not give up my hopes of being one day pardoned.”\par
\par
“You will not? On what Chimaera rest then your hopes? Short-sighted\par
Mortal! Miserable Wretch! Are you not guilty? Are you not infamous in\par
the eyes of Men and Angels. Can such enormous sins be forgiven? Hope\par
you to escape my power? Your fate is already pronounced. The Eternal\par
has abandoned you; Mine you are marked in the book of destiny, and mine\par
you must and shall be!”\par
\par
“Fiend, ’tis false! Infinite is the Almighty’s mercy, and the Penitent\par
shall meet his forgiveness. My crimes are monstrous, but I will not\par
despair of pardon: Haply, when they have received due chastisement....”\par
\par
“Chastisement? Was Purgatory meant for guilt like yours? Hope you that\par
your offences shall be bought off by prayers of superstitious dotards\par
and droning Monks? Ambrosio, be wise! Mine you must be: You are doomed\par
to flames, but may shun them for the present. Sign this parchment: I\par
will bear you from hence, and you may pass your remaining years in\par
bliss and liberty. Enjoy your existence: Indulge in every pleasure to\par
which appetite may lead you: But from the moment that it quits your\par
body, remember that your soul belongs to me, and that I will not be\par
defrauded of my right.”\par
\par
The Monk was silent; But his looks declared that the Tempter’s words\par
were not thrown away. He reflected on the conditions proposed with\par
horror: On the other hand, He believed himself doomed to perdition and\par
that, by refusing the Dæmon’s succour, He only hastened tortures which\par
He never could escape. The Fiend saw that his resolution was shaken: He\par
renewed his instances, and endeavoured to fix the Abbot’s indecision.\par
He described the agonies of death in the most terrific colours; and He\par
worked so powerfully upon Ambrosio’s despair and fears that He\par
prevailed upon him to receive the Parchment. He then struck the iron\par
Pen which He held into a vein of the Monk’s left hand. It pierced deep,\par
and was instantly filled with blood; Yet Ambrosio felt no pain from the\par
wound. The Pen was put into his hand: It trembled. The Wretch placed\par
the Parchment on the Table before him, and prepared to sign it.\par
Suddenly He held his hand: He started away hastily, and threw the Pen\par
upon the table.\par
\par
“What am I doing?” He cried—Then turning to the Fiend with a desperate\par
air, “Leave me! Begone! I will not sign the Parchment.”\par
\par
“Fool!” exclaimed the disappointed Dæmon, darting looks so furious as\par
penetrated the Friar’s soul with horror; “Thus am I trifled with? Go\par
then! Rave in agony, expire in tortures, and then learn the extent of\par
the Eternal’s mercy! But beware how you make me again your mock! Call\par
me no more till resolved to accept my offers! Summon me a second time\par
to dismiss me thus idly, and these Talons shall rend you into a\par
thousand pieces! Speak yet again; Will you sign the Parchment?”\par
\par
“I will not! Leave me! Away!”\par
\par
Instantly the Thunder was heard to roll horribly: Once more the earth\par
trembled with violence: The Dungeon resounded with loud shrieks, and\par
the Dæmon fled with blasphemy and curses.\par
\par
At first, the Monk rejoiced at having resisted the Seducer’s arts, and\par
obtained a triumph over Mankind’s Enemy: But as the hour of punishment\par
drew near, his former terrors revived in his heart. Their momentary\par
repose seemed to have given them fresh vigour. The nearer that the time\par
approached, the more did He dread appearing before the Throne of God.\par
He shuddered to think how soon He must be plunged into eternity; How\par
soon meet the eyes of his Creator, whom He had so grievously offended.\par
The Bell announced midnight: It was the signal for being led to the\par
Stake! As He listened to the first stroke, the blood ceased to\par
circulate in the Abbot’s veins: He heard death and torture murmured in\par
each succeeding sound. He expected to see the Archers entering his\par
prison; and as the Bell forbore to toll, he seized the magic volume in\par
a fit of despair. He opened it, turned hastily to the seventh page, and\par
as if fearing to allow himself a moment’s thought ran over the fatal\par
lines with rapidity. Accompanied by his former terrors, Lucifer again\par
stood before the Trembler.\par
\par
“You have summoned me,” said the Fiend; “Are you determined to be wise?\par
Will you accept my conditions? You know them already. Renounce your\par
claim to salvation, make over to me your soul, and I bear you from this\par
dungeon instantly. Yet is it time. Resolve, or it will be too late.\par
Will you sign the Parchment?”\par
\par
“I must!—Fate urges me! I accept your conditions.”\par
\par
“Sign the Parchment!” replied the Dæmon in an exulting tone.\par
\par
The Contract and the bloody Pen still lay upon the Table. Ambrosio drew\par
near it. He prepared to sign his name. A moment’s reflection made him\par
hesitate.\par
\par
“Hark!” cried the Tempter; “They come! Be quick! Sign the Parchment,\par
and I bear you from hence this moment.”\par
\par
In effect, the Archers were heard approaching, appointed to lead\par
Ambrosio to the Stake. The sound encouraged the Monk in his resolution.\par
\par
“What is the import of this writing?” said He.\par
\par
“It makes your soul over to me for ever, and without reserve.”\par
\par
“What am I to receive in exchange?”\par
\par
“My protection, and release from this dungeon. Sign it, and this\par
instant I bear you away.”\par
\par
Ambrosio took up the Pen; He set it to the Parchment. Again his courage\par
failed him: He felt a pang of terror at his heart, and once more threw\par
the Pen upon the Table.\par
\par
“Weak and Puerile!” cried the exasperated Fiend: “Away with this folly!\par
Sign the writing this instant, or I sacrifice you to my rage!”\par
\par
At this moment the bolt of the outward Door was drawn back. The\par
Prisoner heard the rattling of Chains; The heavy Bar fell; The Archers\par
were on the point of entering. Worked up to phrenzy by the urgent\par
danger, shrinking from the approach of death, terrified by the Dæmon’s\par
threats, and seeing no other means to escape destruction, the wretched\par
Monk complied. He signed the fatal contract, and gave it hastily into\par
the evil Spirit’s hands, whose eyes, as He received the gift, glared\par
with malicious rapture.\par
\par
“Take it!” said the God-abandoned; “Now then save me! Snatch me from\par
hence!”\par
\par
“Hold! Do you freely and absolutely renounce your Creator and his Son?”\par
\par
“I do! I do!”\par
\par
“Do you make over your soul to me for ever?”\par
\par
“For ever!”\par
\par
“Without reserve or subterfuge? Without future appeal to the divine\par
mercy?”\par
\par
The last Chain fell from the door of the prison: The key was heard\par
turning in the Lock: Already the iron door grated heavily upon its\par
rusty hinges.\par
\par
“I am yours for ever and irrevocably!” cried the Monk wild with terror:\par
“I abandon all claim to salvation! I own no power but yours! Hark!\par
Hark! They come! Oh! save me! Bear me away!”\par
\par
“I have triumphed! You are mine past reprieve, and I fulfil my\par
promise.”\par
\par
While He spoke, the Door unclosed. Instantly the Dæmon grasped one of\par
Ambrosio’s arms, spread his broad pinions, and sprang with him into the\par
air. The roof opened as they soared upwards, and closed again when they\par
had quitted the Dungeon.\par
\par
In the meanwhile, the Gaoler was thrown into the utmost surprize by the\par
disappearance of his Prisoner. Though neither He nor the Archers were\par
in time to witness the Monk’s escape, a sulphurous smell prevailing\par
through the prison sufficiently informed them by whose aid He had been\par
liberated. They hastened to make their report to the Grand Inquisitor.\par
The story, how a Sorcerer had been carried away by the Devil, was soon\par
noised about Madrid; and for some days the whole City was employed in\par
discussing the subject. Gradually it ceased to be the topic of\par
conversation: Other adventures arose whose novelty engaged universal\par
attention; and Ambrosio was soon forgotten as totally, as if He never\par
had existed. While this was passing, the Monk supported by his infernal\par
guide, traversed the air with the rapidity of an arrow, and a few\par
moments placed him upon a Precipice’s brink, the steepest in Sierra\par
Morena.\par
\par
Though rescued from the Inquisition, Ambrosio as yet was insensible of\par
the blessings of liberty. The damning contract weighed heavy upon his\par
mind; and the scenes in which He had been a principal actor had left\par
behind them such impressions as rendered his heart the seat of anarchy\par
and confusion. The Objects now before his eyes, and which the full Moon\par
sailing through clouds permitted him to examine, were ill-calculated to\par
inspire that calm, of which He stood so much in need. The disorder of\par
his imagination was increased by the wildness of the surrounding\par
scenery; By the gloomy Caverns and steep rocks, rising above each\par
other, and dividing the passing clouds; solitary clusters of Trees\par
scattered here and there, among whose thick-twined branches the wind of\par
night sighed hoarsely and mournfully; the shrill cry of mountain\par
Eagles, who had built their nests among these lonely Desarts; the\par
stunning roar of torrents, as swelled by late rains they rushed\par
violently down tremendous precipices; and the dark waters of a silent\par
sluggish stream which faintly reflected the moonbeams, and bathed the\par
Rock’s base on which Ambrosio stood. The Abbot cast round him a look of\par
terror. His infernal Conductor was still by his side, and eyed him with\par
a look of mingled malice, exultation, and contempt.\par
\par
“Whither have you brought me?” said the Monk at length in an hollow\par
trembling voice: “Why am I placed in this melancholy scene? Bear me\par
from it quickly! Carry me to Matilda!”\par
\par
The Fiend replied not, but continued to gaze upon him in silence.\par
\par
Ambrosio could not sustain his glance; He turned away his eyes, while\par
thus spoke the Dæmon:\par
\par
“I have him then in my power! This model of piety! This being without\par
reproach! This Mortal who placed his puny virtues on a level with those\par
of Angels. He is mine! Irrevocably, eternally mine! Companions of my\par
sufferings! Denizens of hell! How grateful will be my present!”\par
\par
He paused; then addressed himself to the Monk——\par
\par
“Carry you to Matilda?” He continued, repeating Ambrosio’s words:\par
\par
“Wretch! you shall soon be with her! You well deserve a place near her,\par
for hell boasts no miscreant more guilty than yourself.\par
\par
Hark, Ambrosio, while I unveil your crimes! You have shed the blood of\par
two innocents; Antonia and Elvira perished by your hand. That Antonia\par
whom you violated, was your Sister! That Elvira whom you murdered, gave\par
you birth! Tremble, abandoned Hypocrite! Inhuman Parricide! Incestuous\par
Ravisher! Tremble at the extent of your offences! And you it was who\par
thought yourself proof against temptation, absolved from human\par
frailties, and free from error and vice! Is pride then a virtue? Is\par
inhumanity no fault? Know, vain Man! That I long have marked you for my\par
prey: I watched the movements of your heart; I saw that you were\par
virtuous from vanity, not principle, and I seized the fit moment of\par
seduction. I observed your blind idolatry of the Madona’s picture. I\par
bad a subordinate but crafty spirit assume a similar form, and you\par
eagerly yielded to the blandishments of Matilda. Your pride was\par
gratified by her flattery; Your lust only needed an opportunity to\par
break forth; You ran into the snare blindly, and scrupled not to commit\par
a crime which you blamed in another with unfeeling severity. It was I\par
who threw Matilda in your way; It was I who gave you entrance to\par
Antonia’s chamber; It was I who caused the dagger to be given you which\par
pierced your Sister’s bosom; and it was I who warned Elvira in dreams\par
of your designs upon her Daughter, and thus, by preventing your\par
profiting by her sleep, compelled you to add rape as well as incest to\par
the catalogue of your crimes. Hear, hear, Ambrosio! Had you resisted me\par
one minute longer, you had saved your body and soul. The guards whom\par
you heard at your prison door came to signify your pardon. But I had\par
already triumphed: My plots had already succeeded. Scarcely could I\par
propose crimes so quick as you performed them. You are mine, and Heaven\par
itself cannot rescue you from my power. Hope not that your penitence\par
will make void our contract. Here is your bond signed with your blood;\par
You have given up your claim to mercy, and nothing can restore to you\par
the rights which you have foolishly resigned. Believe you that your\par
secret thoughts escaped me? No, no, I read them all! You trusted that\par
you should still have time for repentance. I saw your artifice, knew\par
its falsity, and rejoiced in deceiving the deceiver! You are mine\par
beyond reprieve: I burn to possess my right, and alive you quit not\par
these mountains.”\par
\par
During the Dæmon’s speech, Ambrosio had been stupefied by terror and\par
surprize. This last declaration rouzed him.\par
\par
“Not quit these mountains alive?” He exclaimed: “Perfidious, what mean\par
you? Have you forgotten our contract?”\par
\par
The Fiend answered by a malicious laugh:\par
\par
“Our contract? Have I not performed my part? What more did I promise\par
than to save you from your prison? Have I not done so? Are you not safe\par
from the Inquisition—safe from all but from me? Fool that you were to\par
confide yourself to a Devil! Why did you not stipulate for life, and\par
power, and pleasure? Then all would have been granted: Now, your\par
reflections come too late. Miscreant, prepare for death; You have not\par
many hours to live!”\par
\par
On hearing this sentence, dreadful were the feelings of the devoted\par
Wretch! He sank upon his knees, and raised his hands towards heaven.\par
The Fiend read his intention and prevented it—\par
\par
“What?” He cried, darting at him a look of fury: “Dare you still\par
implore the Eternal’s mercy? Would you feign penitence, and again act\par
an Hypocrite’s part? Villain, resign your hopes of pardon. Thus I\par
secure my prey!”\par
\par
As he said this, darting his talons into the monk’s shaven crown, he\par
sprang with him from the rock. The caves and mountains rang with\par
Ambrosio’s shrieks. The dæmon continued to soar aloft, till reaching a\par
dreadful height, He released the sufferer. Headlong fell the Monk\par
through the airy waste; The sharp point of a rock received him; and He\par
rolled from precipice to precipice, till bruised and mangled He rested\par
on the river’s banks. Life still existed in his miserable frame: He\par
attempted in vain to raise himself; His broken and dislocated limbs\par
refused to perform their office, nor was He able to quit the spot where\par
He had first fallen. The Sun now rose above the horizon; Its scorching\par
beams darted full upon the head of the expiring Sinner. Myriads of\par
insects were called forth by the warmth; They drank the blood which\par
trickled from Ambrosio’s wounds; He had no power to drive them from\par
him, and they fastened upon his sores, darted their stings into his\par
body, covered him with their multitudes, and inflicted on him tortures\par
the most exquisite and insupportable. The Eagles of the rock tore his\par
flesh piecemeal, and dug out his eyeballs with their crooked beaks. A\par
burning thirst tormented him; He heard the river’s murmur as it rolled\par
beside him, but strove in vain to drag himself towards the sound.\par
Blind, maimed, helpless, and despairing, venting his rage in blasphemy\par
and curses, execrating his existence, yet dreading the arrival of death\par
destined to yield him up to greater torments, six miserable days did\par
the Villain languish. On the Seventh a violent storm arose: The winds\par
in fury rent up rocks and forests: The sky was now black with clouds,\par
now sheeted with fire: The rain fell in torrents; It swelled the\par
stream; The waves overflowed their banks; They reached the spot where\par
Ambrosio lay, and when they abated carried with them into the river the\par
corse of the despairing monk.\par
\par
\par
Haughty Lady, why shrunk you back when yon poor frail-one drew near?\par
Was the air infected by her errors? Was your purity soiled by her\par
passing breath? Ah! Lady, smooth that insulting brow: stifle the\par
reproach just bursting from your scornful lip: wound not a soul, that\par
bleeds already! She has suffered, suffers still. Her air is gay, but\par
her heart is broken; her dress sparkles, but her bosom groans.\par
\par
Lady, to look with mercy on the conduct of others, is a virtue no less\par
than to look with severity on your own.\par
\par
FINIS.\par
